      "description": "You Belong Here | University Human Resources Skip to main content NC State Home University Human Resources Menu Close About Contact Us Departments Directory News Project Management Office Reporting and Dashboards Work at NC State Browse Open Jobs You Belong Here Applicant Support and Information Executive Search Services International Employment New Employee Onboarding University Temporary Services Employee Resources HRNow Calendars and Closings Employee Relations Employment Records and Employment Verification Flexible Work HR Forms Performance Management Benefits and Wellness Benefit Plans Benefits Online Orientation Separating Employee Benefits Child Care Disability Plans Faculty and Staff Assistance Program Leave Programs Life Events Retirement Information Wellness WolfPerks Workers’ Compensation Career Development Awards and Recognition Employee Career Development Program Employee Tuition Assistance LinkedIn Learning Professional Development Team Development and Retreats HR Professionals HRNow Career Development for HR Professionals Classification and Compensation Dual Employment HR Concierge Service HR Exchange HR Forward University Payroll Give Now About About Contact Us Departments Directory News Project Management Office Reporting and Dashboards Work at NC State Work at NC State Browse Open Jobs You Belong Here Applicant Support and Information Executive Search Services International Employment New Employee Onboarding University Temporary Services Employee Resources Employee Resources HRNow Calendars and Closings Employee Relations Employment Records and Employment Verification Flexible Work HR Forms Performance Management Benefits and Wellness Benefits and Wellness Benefit Plans Benefits Online Orientation Separating Employee Benefits Child Care Disability Plans Faculty and Staff Assistance Program Leave Programs Life Events Retirement Information Wellness WolfPerks Workers’ Compensation Career Development Career Development Awards and Recognition Employee Career Development Program Employee Tuition Assistance LinkedIn Learning Professional Development Team Development and Retreats HR Professionals HR Professionals HRNow Career Development for HR Professionals Classification and Compensation Dual Employment HR Concierge Service HR Exchange HR Forward University Payroll Give Now You Belong Here Our employee value proposition includes purpose, community, lifelong learning and support for your holistic well-being. At NC State, our employee value proposition aims to create an ecosystem of meaningful work opportunities that align with Wolfpack 2030: Powering the Extraordinary , NC State’s strategic plan, and that provide an accepting and dynamic culture, opportunities for personal growth and professional development, and programs to support your busy life. Simply put, we want you to achieve your highest potential at work. The Foundation of Your Career Starts Here Click on the blocks below to learn more about why you belong at NC State. Your Career Building Blocks Your Purpose, Impact and Passion NC State is a preeminent land-grant university that is globally recognized for its real-world impact through innovation, research and creativity, thanks to our world-renowned faculty, exceptional staff and deep network of partners and colleagues throughout the state, across the nation and around the world. Here, you’ll find a workforce that’s committed to making a positive difference for our students and the communities we serve as we collaboratively tackle the grand challenges of our time. Your Community and Culture NC State’s main campus is situated in the heart of Raleigh — one of the fastest-growing regions in the country — and has employees located in every county of North Carolina and with the Eastern Band of the Cherokee Indians, so our work is truly boundary-spanning. NC State’s commitment to powering (and empowering) the extraordinary fosters belonging and pride in our Pack. Regardless of where you work, here you’ll find a dynamic community that’s committed to our land-grant mission: leveraging cutting-edge research and technology to help our communities prosper. Your Development and Lifelong Learning At NC State, you’ll find programs and benefits that support you now and throughout your career with the Pack. Regardless of your role, there are targeted professional development and training opportunities focused on your growth, success, and lifelong learning. Helping you maximize your potential and recognizing your efforts is a top priority. Your Life, Health and Well-Being At NC State, we know your ability to balance the requirements of work and the demands of life is a top priority. As a member of our community, you’ll have access to find many great perks designed to support you and help you and your needs. Our Mission, Vision and Values Learn more about our university’s core values and guiding principals. Mission Driven As a research-extensive land-grant university, North Carolina State University is dedicated to excellent teaching, the creation and application of knowledge, and engagement with public and private partners. By uniting our strength in science and technology with a commitment to excellence in a comprehensive range of disciplines, NC State promotes an integrated approach to problem-solving that transforms lives and provides leadership for social, economic, and technological development across North Carolina and around the world. A Clear Vision As a preeminent public research university, NC State University will be increasingly recognized nationally and internationally for its innovation in education, research, scholarship and engagement that solves the world’s most critical challenges. NC State will be known as a welcoming and supportive community that has a transformative impact on society and advances the greater good. Values to Work By Consistent with our mission and vision, NC State affirms these core values that direct our actions and align our decision-making with the best interests of the Wolfpack community: Excellence Representation Integrity Sustainability Community Freedom Collaboration Join the Pack! At NC State, your work can change the world. Come join a university that creates economic, societal and intellectual prosperity. Search open positions University Human Resources Home 2711 Sullivan Drive Administrative Services Building II Raleigh, North Carolina 27695 Phone: 919.515.2135 (HR Service Center) Fax: 919.515.7543 Campus Map Office of Finance and Administration Emergency Information Privacy Statement Accessibility University Policies Contact Us Jobs UHR Intranet © 2026 NC State University. All rights reserved. Accessibility Privacy Resources Close smart search Find NC State websites, locations and people Search all of ncsu.edu TOP LINKS MyPack Portal Access vital records, resources and information for students, families, faculty and staff. University Libraries Our campus libraries are your destinations for studying, research and expanding your mind. Academic Calendar Look up important dates and university holidays for the upcoming academic year and beyond. Majors and Careers Explore our 100-plus degree programs and see where an NC State education can take you."
    },
    "https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/all_jobs.atom": {
      "title": "North Carolina State University: All Jobs",
      "description": "tag:jobs.ncsu.edu,2005:/postings/all_jobs North Carolina State University: All Jobs 2026-08-03T16:33:36-04:00 https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233478 2026-08-03T16:33:36-04:00 2026-08-03T16:33:36-04:00 Temporary IT Intern - My Open Math <div>The successful candidate will serve as the primary liaison between the AGI Math Lecturer and the MathGPT development team to support the successful implementation and ongoing use of the MathGPT platform within the mathematics curriculum.<br>10</div><div>Prior to the start of the Fall 2026 semester, the individual will be responsible for integrating the MAA 102 curriculum into MathGPT, importing course homework assignments into the platform, testing the system from the student perspective to evaluate functionality, usability, and the quality of instructional feedback, and providing training to the Math Lecturer on managing and updating course content within the platform.<br><br></div><div>During the Fall 2026 semester, the successful candidate will provide ongoing technical and operational support on an as-needed basis. Responsibilities include responding to urgent faculty inquiries, troubleshooting significant issues affecting students, coordinating with the MathGPT development team to resolve technical concerns, and helping ensure the continued effectiveness and reliability of the platform in support of student learning and the mathematics program.</div> 110201 - Agricultural Institute - Admin https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223301 2025-09-25T11:36:16-04:00 2025-09-25T11:36:17-04:00 Facilities Enhancement Technician <div>We are seeking enthusiastic individuals to Join our Pack as a <strong>temporary Facilities Enhancement Technician</strong>. The Student Centers Facilities Enhancement team offers meaningful opportunities for employment across our retail and hospitality units. We offer flexible schedules and opportunities for learning, growth, and promotion. Dedicated and experienced professional staff are available to provide on-the-job training and assistance so you become a successful member of the team.<br><br>The primary purpose of this position is to clean and perform routine housekeeping duties in the NC State Student Centers. This position requires a high degree of flexibility to respond to and coordinate with the needs of clients, occupants, and requests. Communication with the team, others and supervisor is critical for success.<br><strong><br></strong>Duties and responsibilities include, but are not limited to:</div><ul><li>Use standard housekeeping equipment, tools and supplies to accomplish basic cleaning tasks such as vacuuming, mopping, scrubbing, dusting, disinfecting, and replenishing to complete assigned responsibilities and job tasks.</li><li>Responsible for reporting discrepancies found within the building in addition to responding to emergencies and inclement weather.</li><li>Complete assigned projects.</li><li>Inventory of assigned area.</li><li>Move and reset furniture.</li><li>Perform proper routine maintenance on all equipment.</li><li>May be required to work during adverse weather conditions to participate in snow/ice removal under the direction of a supervisor.</li><li>Other duties as assigned to ensure NC State Student Centers business needs are met.</li></ul><div><br>Excellent performance during this time may provide an opportunity to renew employment and/or advance.<br><br>This is an hourly position with a 10-11 month assignment with a mandatory 32-day break at the end of 11 months.</div> 477001 - Student Centers Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234265 2026-08-27T12:43:59-04:00 2026-08-27T12:44:00-04:00 Research Technician <div>The Research Technician position is a multi-disciplinary agricultural research field position in support of all research station operations. This position will assist with all tasks related to the establishment, maintenance, and disposition of field crops, vegetables, fruit, and tobacco.<br><br><strong>This includes, but is not limited to:</strong></div><ul><li>Application of treatments to research subjects according to protocol, including pesticides, fertilizers, and irrigation.</li><li>Operation of a wide variety of agricultural equipment to prepare fields, apply treatments, and harvest crops.</li><li>Assist research personnel with plot layout, implementation of treatments, and collection and recording of data.</li><li>Hand planting and harvest.</li><li>Working with various types of irrigation equipment, including laying solid set pipe, drip, and linear irrigation systems.</li></ul><div><br></div><div>This position will also assist with the maintenance of equipment, grounds, buildings, field plots, and other tasks. The validity of a research test depends upon the quality of work in the Research Technician position.<br><br></div><div><strong>This position is located in Kinston, NC.</strong></div> 115007 - Lower Coastal Plain Tobacco Research https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228392 2026-03-12T23:56:39-04:00 2026-03-12T23:56:40-04:00 Temporary Assistant Cook - Reidsville, NC <div>The Assistant Cook will support the Food Service Director in daily planning and preparation of meals for approximately 2000 annual visitors of the Betsy-Jeff Penn 4-H Educational Center. This position will also assist with ensuring kitchen operations remain compliant with state and local health requirements. Qualified individuals will have at least one year of previous kitchen experience, thrive in a fast paced environment, possess excellent written and verbal communication skills, and be currently certified as a ServSafe Manager (or have ability to obtain certification within 3 months of start date).<br><br>Primary Responsibilities</div><ul><li>Possess basic food knowledge and cooking skills</li><li>Have the ability to wash, chop, peel, cut, and or mix ingredients for salad/fruit bar</li><li>Prepare the assigned meal for the day while being aware of allergens and cross-contamination</li><li>Availability to work the open and/or close kitchen shift as needed</li><li>Clean and sanitize dishes, utensils, work areas, and equipment</li><li>Maintain dining room cleanliness after meals, taking out trash as needed</li><li>Receive deliveries using FIFO method (first in, first out) to rotate stock in the cooler, freezer, and dry storage. Help maintain inventory levels for ordering</li><li>Other duties as deemed necessary by the Food Service Director</li></ul><div><br>The Assistant Cook position is a physically demanding role, requiring the ability to lift up to 50lbs unassisted and stand/walk for extended periods of time. Additionally, applicants must be capable of safely performing tasks such as washing, chopping, peeling and cutting of various ingredients.<br><br><strong>Work Schedule:<br></strong>Varies depending on the need of the facility. Flexibility to work both open and close as determined by the Food Service Director 20</div> 112412 - Betsy-Jeff Penn 4-H Camp https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234699 2026-09-04T09:10:45-04:00 2026-09-04T09:10:46-04:00 Utilities Distribution Technician <div>Join NC State University’s <strong>Utilities and Engineering</strong> team as a <strong>Utilities Distribution Technician</strong> and play a hands-on role in maintaining the essential infrastructure that keeps our campus operating safely and reliably. This position supports NC State’s <strong>Main, Centennial, and CBC campuses</strong>, performing skilled mechanical and piping work, diagnostics, maintenance, and repairs on critical utility systems, including <strong>steam tunnels, pump stations, heat exchangers, pressure-reducing valves (PRVs), and grease traps</strong>.<br><br></div><div>You’ll have the opportunity to work on a variety of projects, from routine maintenance and repairs to <strong>new construction, renovations, and welding projects</strong>, while helping ensure reliable utility services for research, academic, and animal hospital facilities. If you enjoy troubleshooting, hands-on work, and solving mechanical challenges that make a meaningful impact, <strong>we invite you to apply and become part of the NC State Facilities team!</strong></div><div><br></div><div><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li><strong>Diagnose and repair equipment</strong> such as fans, pumps, receivers, steam lines, condensate lines, and pressure-reducing stations.</li><li><strong>Inspect, maintain, and repair steam and hot water systems, including components like traps, strainers, expansion joints, and piping.</strong></li><li>Utilize welding technologies for torch work, including welding, brazing, soldering, and cutting, and fabricate parts from blueprints, sketches, and verbal instructions.</li><li>Install, operate, maintain, and rebuild mechanical pumps, PRVs, and associated piping and equipment.</li><li><strong>Cut, thread, solder, and assemble piping; connect valves and test gas, water, sanitary, and storm lines; eliminate leaks and replace faulty fittings.</strong></li><li>Test and certify backflow preventers, conduct fire hydrant flow tests, and perform inspections of wastewater collection systems, including grease traps and SSOs.</li><li><strong>Conduct annual inspections of wastewater distribution systems</strong> and report findings to the Environmental Engineer and shop supervisor.</li><li>Align motors and pumps, set frames, and install related equipment such as fans, PRVs, and turbines for steam generation and control.</li><li>Serve as backup ORC for wastewater collection system compliance, ensuring adherence to permit requirements.</li><li>Anticipate, identify, and address customer service needs by balancing multiple priorities, ensuring repairs or solutions meet expectations, and fostering positive relationships with internal and external stakeholders, including vendors and subcontractors, while proactively developing options, implementing solutions, and verifying customer satisfaction.</li></ul> 445050 - Utilities and Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231143 2026-06-02T08:39:12-04:00 2026-06-02T08:39:13-04:00 Assistant Professor: Digital Technology, Machine Learning and AI in Agriculture Communications and Education <div>We are seeking candidates that will help define what’s next for agricultural education and communication. This tenure-track faculty position within the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences at North Carolina State University is an opportunity to build and lead a nationally recognized program exploring how artificial intelligence and machine learning can transform the way students learn, educators teach, and agricultural communities access knowledge. Through research, Extension, and teaching, this role will shape innovative approaches that strengthen learning experiences and advance agricultural and food systems education for the future.<br><br></div><div>In this role, you will get to explore emerging technologies in ways that create meaningful, real-world impact. You will collaborate with interdisciplinary faculty across the CALS Applied AI Cluster, develop innovative research and Extension initiatives, and help shape how AI and machine learning are integrated into agricultural education, communication, and outreach. From designing personalized learning experiences to supporting producers, educators, and stakeholders through technology-driven solutions, your work will influence both the future of education and the future of agriculture. You will also have the flexibility to pursue bold ideas, build collaborative partnerships, mentor students, and create programs that extend beyond the classroom into communities across North Carolina and beyond.</div><div><strong><br>Key Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Establish and lead a forward-thinking research program exploring the use of AI and ML in agricultural and Extension education, including areas such as personalized learning, predictive analytics, adaptive assessments, virtual assistants, and emerging educational technologies.</li><li>Develop innovative approaches that enhance communication and learning outcomes for students, agricultural professionals, producers, and community stakeholders.</li><li>Design and deliver impactful Extension programming that helps agricultural and food systems organizations understand, adopt, and apply AI-driven technologies and tools.</li><li>Create hands-on workshops, trainings, and educational resources focused on topics such as data-informed decision making, AI applications in agriculture, and technology integration strategies.</li><li>Collaborate with interdisciplinary faculty, research centers, strategic initiatives, and external partners to advance applied AI efforts across agriculture and life sciences.</li><li>Develop technology-enhanced teaching and learning experiences that prepare future educators, Extension professionals, and agricultural leaders to effectively integrate emerging technologies into their work.</li><li>Teach and contribute to undergraduate and graduate programs related to agricultural and Extension education, communication, and technology applications.</li><li>Mentor and advise graduate students while supporting student research, innovation, and professional development.</li><li>Conduct scholarly and translational research that bridges theory, practice, and community impact across teaching, Extension, and applied research initiatives.</li><li>Secure external funding and build partnerships that expand the reach and impact of AI and ML initiatives within agriculture and food systems.</li><li>Contribute to departmental, college, university, and professional service activities that support the broader mission of CALS and the agricultural community.<br><br></li></ul><div>This 12-month appointment includes responsibilities across Research (50%), Extension (30%), and Teaching (20%).<br><br></div><div>If you are excited to push boundaries, translate emerging technologies into meaningful practice, and help shape the future of agricultural education and outreach, we encourage you to apply.</div> 111401 - Agricultural and Human Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232982 2026-07-21T15:23:05-04:00 2026-07-21T15:23:06-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar- Bioelectricity Lab <div>The Department of Molecular Biomedical Sciences trains doctors of veterinary medicine, graduate students, and postdoctoral fellows interested in research-based careers focused on discovering new information about animal and human health and disease and using those findings to enhance animal and human health and well-being.<br><br>The Bioelectricity Lab develops next generation technologies for the detection and treatment of diseases using electric field mediated phenomena.<br><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong><br>The candidate for the Bioelectricity Postdoc Research Scholar position will lead large/small animal studies investigating the immunological effects a new pulsed electric field cancer therapy.<br><br>The candidate will additionally lead with other experiments in the laboratory using in vitro models to investigate the effects of pulsed electric fields on biological tissues.</div> 194001 - Department of Molecular Biomedical Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233948 2026-08-18T13:58:00-04:00 2026-08-18T13:58:01-04:00 Journey Utilities Plant Operator - Nightshift <div>Behind every class, laboratory, residence hall, and campus event is a team making sure NC State has the reliable utilities it needs to operate. As a <strong>Utility Plant Operator</strong>, you’ll play a critical role in keeping the university running by operating and monitoring the central utility systems that provide electricity, steam, hot water, and chilled water across campus.</div><div><br>This is an opportunity to work with a variety of utility equipment and gain valuable experience in a central utility environment. You’ll work alongside experienced utility professionals to monitor plant operations, troubleshoot equipment, perform inspections, and help maintain safe, efficient, and reliable service. If you're dependable, safety-minded, mechanically inclined, and interested in learning how large-scale utility systems work, <strong>we want to hear from you. Please apply today!</strong></div><div><br></div><div><strong>Working Hours: 12 hr shift, 3-4 days a week, 6:00 PM - 6:00 AM.<br>Hours may adjust to meet the needs of the organization.</strong><br><br><strong>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:</strong></div><ul><li>Assist in the control and monitoring of steam boilers, chillers, cogeneration equipment, and auxiliary systems to ensure continuous utility services.</li><li>Maintain safe and efficient operations of steam, hot water, and chilled water production systems.</li><li>Conduct real-time monitoring, make necessary adjustments, and ensure compliance with emissions and regulatory standards.</li><li>Inspect plant equipment, including fuel gas compressors, air filters, pumps, and cooling towers.</li><li>Work under the guidance of a shift supervisor to optimize plant performance and address operational challenges.</li><li>Support the operation of the Cates and Centennial Cogeneration Facilities to produce electricity and steam for the campus.</li><li>Lift and transport materials weighing up to 60 pounds with or without reasonable accommodations and perform a wide range of physically demanding tasks in varied outdoor environments, ensuring safe handling practices and maintaining productivity in varying weather and terrain conditions.</li><li>Communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, and the general public, and follow verbal and written instructions related to work assignments and operational needs.</li></ul> 445050 - Utilities and Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/226390 2026-01-12T09:11:17-05:00 2026-01-12T09:11:18-05:00 Temporary Research Technician <div>This position will be responsible for working alongside program research technician and aid in duties including, but not limited to, hauling equipment (tractors, planters, combine, etc) to research stations, collecting and analyzing data, organizing extension events, and processing harvested materials.&nbsp;</div> 110901 - Crop and Soil Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234479 2026-09-01T14:23:14-04:00 2026-09-01T14:23:15-04:00 Director, Admissions <div>The Poole College of Management is seeking a strategic, data-driven <strong>Director of Undergraduate Admissions</strong> to lead recruitment, yield, and enrollment initiatives. Reporting to the Assistant Dean of Undergraduate Programs, the Director will develop and implement strategies to attract and enroll a strong undergraduate student body while advancing the College’s enrollment priorities.<br><br></div><div>As the College’s primary undergraduate admissions resource, you will collaborate with University Enrollment Management and Student Services (EMAS), College leadership, academic programs, Student Affairs, Marketing and Communications, Development, and external partners. You will also supervise professional and student staff, lead the Student Ambassador program, and manage an annual admissions budget of approximately $40,000.</div><div><br><strong>In this role, you will:<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Lead Recruitment and Yield</strong></div><ul><li>Develop and execute first-year and transfer recruitment and yield strategies aligned with College enrollment goals.</li><li>Lead prospective and admitted student events, information sessions, outreach campaigns, and recruitment programming.</li><li>Build partnerships with schools, community colleges, community organizations, and pre-college programs to strengthen prospective student pipelines.</li><li>Represent Poole College in university-wide recruitment and enrollment initiatives.</li></ul><div><strong>Drive Data-Informed Enrollment Planning</strong></div><ul><li>Analyze applicant, admission, enrollment, yield, and student profile data to identify trends and guide strategy.</li><li>Provide College leadership with enrollment insights, recommendations, and opportunities.</li><li>Establish measures to evaluate recruitment and yield effectiveness and refine strategies based on results.</li><li>Track prospective student engagement and enrollment outcomes.</li></ul><div><strong>Manage Admissions Programs and Systems</strong></div><ul><li>Serve as the College’s primary resource and subject-matter expert for the Slate admissions platform.</li><li>Lead the Dean’s Scholars selection process and recruitment activities connected to pre-college partnerships.</li><li>Participate in admissions and enrollment committees, including the Global Scholarship Committee.</li><li>Collaborate with Student Affairs and Advocacy to connect recruitment and enrollment efforts with student engagement.</li></ul><div><strong>Lead People and Programs</strong></div><ul><li>Supervise, mentor, and develop professional and student staff.</li><li>Lead the Student Ambassador program, including selection, training, development, and engagement.</li><li>Strengthen admissions processes, practices, and operations within the Office of Undergraduate Programs.</li></ul><div><strong>Build Partnerships and Manage Resources</strong></div><ul><li>Serve as a primary liaison with EMAS and represent Poole College on university enrollment initiatives and committees.</li><li>Manage the undergraduate admissions budget and allocate resources to support recruitment and yield priorities.</li><li>Collaborate across the College to advance enrollment goals and advise leadership on admissions trends, opportunities, and emerging needs.</li></ul> 200401 - Poole College of Management - Office of Undergraduate Programs https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/229794 2026-04-22T14:40:21-04:00 2026-04-22T14:40:22-04:00 Temporary Research Technician <div>Responsibilities include maintaining laboratory, greenhouse, state vehicle, and storage areas, as well as managing supplies and equipment. The technician will support students and researchers during field trials by assisting with soil sampling, plot setup, plant measurements, irrigation, fertilization, staking, tying, and harvesting.&nbsp;</div> 111501 - Horticultural Science https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232257 2026-06-30T09:50:53-04:00 2026-06-30T09:50:54-04:00 Utilities Plant Operator - Night Shift <div>Are you looking to power the future of a world-class campus? We are seeking a skilled <strong>Utility Plant Operator</strong> to join our team. In this role, you won't just be maintaining equipment—you'll be the backbone of our university’s infrastructure, ensuring the lights stay on and the campus stays comfortable.<br><br></div><div>As a Utility Plant Operator, you will be responsible for the reliable operation of our central utility systems. From managing our advanced <strong>Cates Cogeneration Facility</strong> to overseeing massive chilled water systems, your expertise will directly impact thousands of students, faculty, and researchers every day.</div><div><strong><br>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:</strong></div><ul><li>Control, monitor, and operate steam boilers, chillers, cogeneration equipment, and auxiliary systems to provide continuous utility services.</li><li>Maintain safe and efficient operation of steam, hot water, and chilled water production systems.</li><li>Perform real‑time monitoring of plant systems, make operational adjustments, and ensure compliance with emissions, safety, and regulatory requirements.</li><li>Inspect, operate, and perform routine maintenance on plant equipment, including fuel gas compressors, pumps, air filters, cooling towers, and related systems.</li><li>Support the operation of the Cates Cogeneration Facility to produce electricity and steam for campus use.</li><li>Identify and respond to equipment or system issues, escalating concerns to the shift supervisor as appropriate.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Why Join Us?</strong></div><div>This is more than a maintenance job; it’s an opportunity to work with cutting-edge energy technology in a stable, supportive environment.<strong>Ready to power our campus? Apply today!</strong></div> 445050 - Utilities and Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234367 2026-08-31T00:00:31-04:00 2026-08-31T00:00:32-04:00 Director of Philanthropy <div>The Director of Philanthropy is responsible for identifying, qualifying, soliciting, and stewarding major gift donors to NC State University. This position will serve as a member of the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences (CALS) Advancement team, reporting to a Senior Director of Philanthropy with an effort to maximize support to all areas of the College. As appropriate, the Director will also identify and coordinate the management of key volunteers. The Director will be expected to manage a portfolio of discovery and actively managed prospects while also collaborating with fundraising colleagues across campus. <br><br><strong>Duties and responsibilities include, but are not limited to:</strong><br><br><strong>Major Gift Prospect Identification, Cultivation, and Solicitation</strong></div><ul><li>Identification, qualification, cultivation and solicitation of prospects to create and grow a pipeline of major gifts ($100,000 and above) donors.</li><li>Strategic development and management of a portfolio with approximately 150 prospects and donors capable of making gifts of $100,000 or more.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Fundraising Strategy, Goals, and Portfolio Management</strong></div><ul><li>Consistently meet or exceed annual goals for outreach contacts, qualifications, visits, proposals submitted, and funds raised.</li><li>Creation and implementation of comprehensive strategies to leverage networks and maximize fundraising results.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Volunteer, Donor, and Campus Collaboration</strong></div><ul><li>Identification, coordination and management of key volunteers.</li><li>Collaboration with colleagues to help align donors’ philanthropic interests with various priorities across campus.</li><li>Coordination of stewardship efforts as needed with appropriate College Advancement and other campus colleagues.</li></ul> 110199 - Agriculture Related Foundation https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228972 2026-03-31T13:08:13-04:00 2026-03-31T13:08:13-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>This postdoctoral research position is intended to support a research project investigating the interaction between ultrasound waves platelet-like microgels designed for wound healing applications. Artificial platelets based on ultra-low cross-linked (ULC) microgels represent a promising therapeutic approach to enhance clot formation and accelerate tissue repair. However, the physical mechanisms governing how ultrasound interacts with these soft microgel structures remain poorly understood.<br><br>The purpose of this position is to conduct experimental and quantitative studies of the acoustic response of these microgels under ultrasound exposure. The postdoctoral researcher will design and operate ultrasound experimental setups to characterize how acoustic fields influence the behavior, deformation, aggregation, and functional performance of artificial platelets with different cross-linking densities. Understanding these mechanisms will enable the development of ultrasound-enabled strategies to control or enhance microgel-mediated hemostasis and wound healing. This position will contribute to advancing knowledge at the intersection of ultrasound physics, biomaterials, and biomedical engineering, with the long-term goal of improving therapeutic approaches for wound healing.<br><br>The postdoctoral researcher will conduct experimental research investigating the interaction between ultrasound waves and ultra-low cross-linked microgel particles designed to mimic platelet function for wound healing applications.<br><br><strong>Primary responsibilities include:</strong></div><ul><li>Designing, assembling, and operating ultrasound experimental setups for acoustic characterization of microgel suspensions.</li><li>Investigating the effects of microgel cross-linking density on acoustic scattering, deformation, and aggregation behavior under ultrasound exposure.</li><li>Conducting quantitative measurements of ultrasound-microgel interactions using transducers, pulsing systems, and acoustic measurement instrumentation.</li><li>Developing experimental protocols to evaluate the response of artificial platelet microgels to different acoustic parameters (frequency, pressure, pulse sequence).</li><li>Processing and analyzing experimental data using signal processing and quantitative analysis techniques.</li><li>Collaborating with interdisciplinary research teams working in biomaterials, biomedical engineering, and acoustics.</li><li>Preparing research manuscripts, conference presentations, and reports documenting experimental findings.</li><li>Contributing to the mentoring of graduate and undergraduate students involved in the research project.</li></ul> 140901 - Mechanical & Aerospace Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231758 2026-06-16T11:31:30-04:00 2026-06-16T11:31:32-04:00 Maintenance Mechanic <div>The primary purpose of this position is to perform plumbing, HVAC, carpentry, electrical, and household appliance repair and preventative maintenance services so that the residence halls are kept in good repair and meet all local, state, and federal regulations for safe and habitable housing.<br><br>The Maintenance Mechanic will complete assigned work orders and process information through the use of an online work order system. The Maintenance Mechanic will also oversee and train student maintenance staff.</div> 242290 - Housing Facilities Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233223 2026-07-28T13:37:23-04:00 2026-07-28T13:37:25-04:00 Tobacco Extension Agronomist <div>The Department of Crop and Soil Sciences at NC State University invites applications for an Assistant Professor and Crop Production Management Specialist. Supported through the Collins Endowment Distinguished Fellowship, this position will provide statewide leadership in applied tobacco agronomy through an integrated program of Extension, research, and teaching focused on improving the sustainability, productivity, and profitability of North Carolina’s flue-cured and burley tobacco industries.<br><br></div><div>In this role, you will work alongside growers, industry partners, county Extension agents, researchers, and agricultural professionals to address real-world crop production challenges and translate research into practical solutions. You will help shape tobacco production recommendations, support producers through emerging challenges, and strengthen the connection between university research and on-farm practices. This position provides an opportunity to build upon NC State’s longstanding leadership in tobacco agronomy while advancing innovation in crop management, sustainability, and production efficiency.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities fall within three key areas outlined below:&nbsp;</strong></div><div><strong>Extension:</strong></div><ul><li>Provide statewide Extension leadership and technical support to tobacco growers, county Extension agents, crop consultants, NCDA&amp;CS regional agronomists, agribusiness partners, and other industry stakeholders.</li><li>Develop and deliver educational programs through grower meetings, field days, county agent trainings, farm visits, and stakeholder workshops.</li><li>Translate research findings into practical recommendations that address production challenges and support informed decision-making.</li><li>Maintain and update the NC Flue-Cured Tobacco Production Guide to ensure producers and agricultural professionals have access to current production recommendations and best management practices.</li><li>Build and strengthen partnerships with state, regional, and national tobacco organizations to advance the tobacco production industry.</li></ul><div><strong>Research:</strong></div><ul><li>Develop an applied research program focused on improving flue-cured and burley tobacco production systems.</li><li>Evaluate new products, technologies, and production practices, including precision agriculture approaches, to improve crop productivity, sustainability, and profitability.</li><li>Investigate management strategies related to climate variability, pest management, herbicide-resistant weeds, and other emerging production challenges.</li><li>Conduct on-farm research and demonstrations to evaluate practical solutions under producer conditions.</li><li>Collaborate with the Director of the Official Variety Testing Program to evaluate tobacco cultivars across multiple environments.</li><li>Partner with NC State faculty and external collaborators on research related to pesticide residue expectations and the evaluation of new agricultural chemicals for tobacco production.</li></ul><div><strong>Teaching:</strong></div><ul><li>Co-teach the undergraduate course CS 116/216 – Agronomic Crops – Cotton, Peanuts, and Tobacco during the fall semester.</li><li>Provide approximately five weeks of instruction during the fall and spring semesters and two weeks every other summer.<br><br></li></ul><div>In this role, you will have the opportunity to make a meaningful impact through Extension, research, and teaching while contributing to the continued success of North Carolina agriculture.If you are passionate about applying agricultural research to real-world challenges, building strong relationships with industry and community partners, and preparing the next generation of agricultural professionals, we encourage you to apply.&nbsp;</div> 110901 - Crop and Soil Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223238 2025-09-25T10:53:01-04:00 2025-09-25T10:53:01-04:00 Temporary Internship Program Assistant <div>Supporting with the overall coordination, including logistics, administration, planning professional development and evaluation of the summer internships for the Center for Environmental Farming Systems.<br><br></div><div>Specific duties include:</div><ul><li>Internship recruiting</li><li>Planning and coordinating logistics or professional development</li><li>Tracking and documenting activities</li><li>Assist with paying farmers</li><li>Support interns with day-to-day activities as needed</li></ul><div><br><strong>Schedule:<br></strong>Spring work schedule is approximately 10 hours per week and summer is 20-25 hours.</div><div>*Possibility of extending after August</div> 111502 - Center for Environmental Farming Systems (CEFS) https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234104 2026-08-24T14:53:02-04:00 2026-08-24T14:53:03-04:00 Automation Engineer <div>The <strong>Automation Engineer</strong> will help build the systems that make SPEED’s laboratories smarter, more data-rich, and capable of running complex experiments remotely. You’ll connect robots, scientific instruments, sensors, reactors, and safety systems to SPEED’s orchestration software—turning sophisticated laboratory procedures into reliable, reusable automated workflows.<br><br></div><div>This is a hands-on engineering role with direct impact on the <strong>reliability, throughput, safety, and reproducibility</strong> of a national user facility.</div><div><br></div><div><strong>What You’ll Do<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Build and Integrate Laboratory Automation</strong></div><ul><li>Design and implement automated workflows for synthesis, materials processing, sample preparation, testing, and characterization.</li><li>Integrate robots, liquid handlers, pumps, valves, reactors, instruments, sensors, and other laboratory equipment.</li><li>Translate scientific protocols into robust, executable workflows with defined operating limits and error handling.</li><li>Develop reusable device drivers and equipment interfaces for SPEED’s automation platform.</li><li>Commission and validate new robotic systems, workcells, and scientific instruments.</li></ul><div><strong>Develop Control Software and Interfaces</strong></div><ul><li>Develop and maintain Python software for device control, workflow execution, monitoring, and diagnostics.</li><li>Connect laboratory equipment to SPEED’s scheduling, orchestration, data, and user-access systems.</li><li>Implement reliable communications using APIs, Python libraries, TCP/IP, OPC UA, REST, serial, digital I/O, and other protocols.</li><li>Establish consistent device states, commands, metadata, and error handling across varied equipment.</li><li>Use version control, testing, and code review to deliver dependable automation software.</li><li>Develop simulation or digital-twin capabilities to test workflows before running them on physical equipment.</li></ul><div><strong>Improve Reliability and Performance</strong></div><ul><li>Monitor automated systems and troubleshoot robotics, motion control, fluid handling, sensors, instruments, software, and network issues.</li><li>Develop preventive maintenance, calibration, readiness checks, and recovery procedures.</li><li>Analyze failures and implement improvements that increase uptime, repeatability, and ease of recovery.</li><li>Work with manufacturers and vendors to resolve technical issues and support critical systems.</li><li>Maintain clear documentation of configurations, dependencies, maintenance, and known failure modes.</li></ul><div><strong>Build Safe, Usable Systems</strong></div><ul><li>Design automation with appropriate interlocks, alarms, safe states, emergency stops, and shutdown logic.</li><li>Conduct hazard and failure-mode reviews for robotic, fluidic, thermal, pressure, gas-handling, and unattended operations.</li><li>Create system documentation, operating procedures, troubleshooting guides, and maintenance instructions.</li><li>Train researchers, operators, students, and technical staff to use automated platforms safely and effectively.</li><li>Help establish engineering standards and platform designs that enable automation to scale across SPEED and partner facilities.<br><br></li></ul><div>You’ll work at the intersection of <strong>automation, robotics, software, and scientific research</strong>, solving challenging engineering problems that enable experiments to run faster, more reliably, and at greater scale. Your work will help transform advanced laboratory capabilities into dependable platforms that scientists can use to accelerate discovery.</div> 230101 - Interdisciplinary Programs - SVP https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/227544 2026-02-17T09:41:57-05:00 2026-02-17T09:41:58-05:00 Post Doctoral Research Scholar - Bee Research <div>The SE CASC is a federal USGS - university partnership funded by a competitive cooperative agreement to NCSU. The mission of the SE CASC is to co-produce actionable science to help fish, wildlife ecosystems, and Tribal Nations adapt to a changing climate.<br><br>The Southeast Climate Adaptation Science Center (SE CASC), in partnership with the Consortium, hosted by NC State University are seeking a Postdoctoral Scholar to further climate adaptation research and synthesis on invertebrates, focusing on bees. The mission of the SE CASC is to help fish, wildlife, ecosystems, and people adapt to a changing climate. The SE CASC focuses on the co-production of actionable science for natural and cultural resource management. The number of invertebrate species far outpaces the number of vertebrates, with almost 1.3 million invertebrates described. Many perform activities critical to ecosystem functions and services, but invertebrates are often missing in climate adaptation plans for species or habitats.</div><div><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>We seek a Postdoctoral Research Scholar with expertise in the analysis of large monitoring datasets to understand climate impacts on invertebrates, in particular bees.</li><li>We are particularly interested in applicants with a variation of quantitative skills to analyze and visualize data, but will also consider applicants who have strong taxonomic and natural history expertise in bees and regional threats to specific bee groups to develop management briefs for climate adaptation.</li></ul><div><br>Applicants should submit a cover letter, CV, a research statement (2 pages maximum) describing their prior research and clearly identifying their quantitative and/or taxonomic skills, and the names and contact information for three professional references. <br><br>Application review will begin on <strong>March 15, 2026 </strong>and continue until the position is filled.<br><br></div> 112101 - Applied Ecology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234567 2026-09-02T16:25:49-04:00 2026-09-02T16:25:51-04:00 Facilities Maintenance Technician <div>Join NC State University’s <strong>Building Maintenance and Operations (BMO)</strong> team as a <strong>Facilities Maintenance Technician – Contributing</strong> and build your skills while helping keep our campus facilities safe, reliable, and operating at their best. This hands-on position works within a zone maintenance shop on NC State’s <strong>Original Campus, Centennial Campus, or Centennial Biomedical Campus (Veterinary School)</strong> and supports routine maintenance, operations, and repairs across building systems.</div><div><br>In this role, you’ll develop practical experience across <strong>HVAC, plumbing, electrical, steam, and other building systems</strong> while working alongside experienced mechanics and technicians. You’ll perform preventive maintenance, assist with repairs, operate basic tools and equipment, monitor equipment, identify potential problems, and know when to elevate more complex issues to higher-level mechanics. If you’re dependable, hands-on, eager to learn, and interested in developing your skills in the skilled trades, <strong>we invite you to apply and become part of the NC State Facilities team!</strong></div><div><strong><em><br>Key responsibilities and duties include but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li>Perform basic maintenance, preventive maintenance, and repairs across <strong>HVAC, plumbing, electrical, steam, and other building systems</strong>.</li><li>Assist higher-level mechanics with maintenance, troubleshooting, repairs, installations, and other assigned work.</li><li>Perform routine tasks such as <strong>lubricating, cleaning, inspecting, re-lamping, repairing plumbing fixtures, and checking equipment</strong>.</li><li>Take readings on boilers, chillers, and other equipment; check chemical levels and report unusual conditions or discrepancies.</li><li>Recognize signs of equipment or system problems, including leaks, unusual noises, temperature changes, electrical outages, and equipment malfunctions.</li><li><strong>Troubleshoot basic maintenance issues</strong>, determine potential causes, and refer complex problems to higher-level mechanics or supervisors.</li><li>Operate and maintain the basic tools and equipment used in assigned trades and follow appropriate procedures for each task.</li><li><strong>Follow preventive maintenance work orders and maintenance requests</strong>, completing assigned work accurately and within established timelines.</li><li>Accurately document work performed, including <strong>time, materials, equipment readings, and identified issues</strong>.</li><li>Develop working knowledge across multiple building systems and cross-train in building equipment operations and maintenance.</li><li>Properly<strong> isolate and lock out equipment and systems </strong>as required before performing maintenance or repairs.</li><li>Maintain a clean, organized, and safe work environment and follow all University safety, health, and established safe-work practices.</li><li>Communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, and the general public, and follows verbal and written instructions related to work assignments and operational needs.</li><li>Perform lifting duties up to 30 pounds frequently and 60 pounds occasionally, with or without reasonable accommodations.</li><li>Perform tasks requiring standing for extended periods, working in confined spaces, and utilizing various tools. Safely climb and work from different types of ladders to access elevated or below-grade areas (such as roofs, above-ceiling spaces, and utility pits). Be exposed to and work in areas where insects or small animals are occasionally present. All essential tasks must be performed with or without reasonable accommodations.</li></ul> 445030 - Building Maintenance & Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230717 2026-05-18T09:07:08-04:00 2026-05-18T09:07:09-04:00 Food Service Worker <div><strong>NC State Dining </strong>is seeking enthusiastic individuals to Join our Pack as a <strong>Food Service Worker</strong>. Successful candidates may work in a dining hall, retail operation, food court, or restaurant setting.<br><br>Essential job duties include:</div><ul><li>Perform general food service work including food prep, line service, and restocking food and beverage items.</li><li>Clean and restock work station and assist with the cleaning of the common kitchen and production areas.</li><li>May assemble menu items as per the recipe specifications and assist in maintaining the food temperature control and safety.</li><li>May prepare quality beverages, such as espresso-based beverages, coffee, other drinks, and food products consistently by adhering to all recipe and presentation standards.</li><li>Assist in receiving and stocking store merchandise.</li><li>Accurately process transactions through the Point of Sale (POS) by following NC State Dining procedures.</li><li>Provide exemplary customer service to every guest.</li><li>Following all health, safety and sanitation guidelines, and perform necessary cleaning tasks in accordance with maintaining a Grade “A” Sanitation rating.</li><li>Other duties as assigned to ensure NC State Dining business needs are met. Position may be asked to assist in other locations as necessary.</li></ul><div><br>This is an hourly position, that includes a State required 32-day break at the end of 11 months. Superior work performance may provide an opportunity to renew employment and/or advance into a benefits eligible permanent position.<br><br><a href=\"https://dining.ncsu.edu/about/overview/\"><strong>NC State Dining</strong></a> provides campus wide food services to students, staff, and faculty on the North Carolina State University Campus. NC State Dining operates 10 cafes, 3 food courts and 6 dining halls, 4 markets, 1 restaurant, vending, a nutritional counseling service, and a catering department. The department employs approximately 1000 temporary and student employees and approximately 190 permanent EPS and SHRA employees.</div> 471501 - Dining Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232767 2026-07-15T15:41:38-04:00 2026-07-15T15:41:39-04:00 Game Operations Assistant (Temp) <div>Game Operations Assistants provide support to Facilities &amp; Operation staff in maintaining and operating all NC State Athletics facilities and game-day events.<br><br>Responsibilities:<br>• Complete game-day operations tasks as assigned including assisting with customer service to patrons, ingress and egress of events, serving as a liaison for the athletics department to various guest personnel, etc.<br>• Assist with event set-ups, changeovers, and event support for a variety of events held in all athletic facilities or non-athletic facilities that may be hosting an athletics event.<br>• Provide administrative support to Facilities &amp; Operations, including clerical, data gathering, research, and surveys.<br>• Complete tasks as assigned by Athletic Department Administrators as needed.</div> 107005 - Athletics - Game Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233778 2026-08-12T14:20:40-04:00 2026-08-12T14:20:41-04:00 Research Technician - Horticultural Science <div>The Department of Horticultural Science is seeking a motivated and experienced professional to support the day-to-day operations of the Horticulture Field Lab (HFL), a large-scale outdoor and greenhouse research complex serving faculty, students, researchers, and industry partners across multiple departments. The HFL supports a wide range of field and greenhouse research, teaching, extension, and industry testing activities and is home to the JC Raulston Arboretum.</div><div><br>The position provides operational and research support for the HFL and assists with operations at the Fox and HFL Greenhouses. The position works closely with the HFL/Fox Greenhouse Coordinator and HFL Research Operations Coordinator to maintain safe, efficient, and functional research and teaching environments. Responsibilities include coordinating facility maintenance and repairs, managing work requests and equipment resources, supporting irrigation and water systems, maintaining operational and inventory records, assisting with field and greenhouse activities, and coordinating resources to meet the needs of approximately 22–25 active research projects and Principal Investigators.</div><div><br>The position serves as a key operational resource for faculty, staff, students, and other facility users and assists with the planning and allocation of facility space, equipment, and other resources. Additional responsibilities include supporting safety and compliance activities, guiding temporary personnel in facility operations, assisting with teaching and extension events, and monitoring facility operations during weekends or other periods when designated coordinators are unavailable. The successful candidate will be expected to independently prioritize competing requests, troubleshoot operational challenges, coordinate resources, and effectively communicate with a varied group of facility users and stakeholders.</div> 111501 - Horticultural Science https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223802 2025-10-08T10:25:50-04:00 2025-10-08T10:25:50-04:00 UTS- Electronics Specialist Journey / Fire Alarm Technician - 2nd Shift - UNC Chapel Hill <div>This position serves as part of the <strong>Life Safety/Electronics-Specialty Shop</strong> team, ensuring the smooth operation of critical safety and monitoring systems across campus. The role involves both <strong>preventive maintenance</strong> and <strong>hands-on installations or modifications</strong> to essential systems that support the safety, security, and functionality of the university.</div><div>As an Electronics Specialist, you will perform tasks such as:</div><ul><li>Conducting <strong>routine and preventive maintenance inspections</strong> on:<ul><li>Life Safety Systems</li><li>Fire Detection and Suppression Systems</li><li>Security Systems</li><li>CCTV (Closed-Circuit Television) Systems</li><li>Equipment Monitoring Systems</li><li>Other miscellaneous electronic systems and subsystems</li></ul></li><li><strong>Installing, maintaining, and modifying</strong> fire detection and suppression systems, Life Safety equipment, card access systems, CCTV, and equipment monitoring tools.</li><li><strong>Responding to repair requests and troubleshooting</strong> issues with various electronic components to ensure systems operate at peak performance.</li><li><strong>Coordinating with other teams and vendors</strong> to guarantee timely inspections and installations.</li></ul> 445030 - Building Maintenance & Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232310 2026-07-01T09:15:31-04:00 2026-07-01T09:15:32-04:00 Environmental Services Technician I <div>We are seeking a passionate and diligent Environmental Service Technician to join our illustrious team. The primary responsibility of the organizational unit is providing cleaning, sanitizing, and pest control services to over 10 million square feet of campus space. This service is provided through the utilization of over 320 staff members. The overall function of this position is to utilize a standard cleaning assignment to clean and sanitize an area of responsibility within a building.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key responsibilities and duties include, but not limited to:</strong></div><ul><li>Clean and sanitize assigned areas using standard housekeeping equipment and procedures.</li><li>Perform routine cleaning tasks such as vacuuming, mopping, dusting, disinfecting, and restocking supplies.</li><li>Remove recycling and waste from centralized collection sites.</li><li>Report maintenance issues, safety concerns, or discrepancies to supervisors.</li><li>Support emergency and inclement weather response, including snow and ice removal.</li><li>Periodically strip and refinish floors, shampoo and extract carpets, and pressure wash entrances, walls, and windows.</li><li>Participate in team cleaning and special projects during academic breaks or when areas are accessible.</li><li>Ensure safe, efficient, and high-quality cleaning practices across assigned spaces.</li><li>Able to grasp, push, and pull cleaning equipment; reach overhead; and perform physical cleaning tasks like bending, squatting, lifting up to 60 lbs., and climbing ladders, safely and efficiently, with or without reasonable accommodation.<br><br></li></ul><div>We encourage candidates who take pride in maintaining a clean and safe environment to apply. This role provides the opportunity to contribute to a well-functioning campus community while working in a collaborative and supportive team setting.</div> 443501 - Housekeeping Services - Region 3 https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233517 2026-08-04T15:37:30-04:00 2026-08-04T15:37:31-04:00 Emergency Reflief Veterinarian <div>&nbsp;Emergency duties include being a primary emergency receiving clinician managing primary/referral emegency cases while mentoring and instructing house officers and senior veterinary students.&nbsp;</div> 190101 - College Of Veterinary Medicine https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223311 2025-09-25T11:46:59-04:00 2025-09-25T11:47:00-04:00 Lecturer - Biomedical Program <div>Delivery of lectures that cover the course objectives as stated on the course syllabus following the dates of the NCSU university calendar for the given semester.<br>Assessment of student performance including exams, projects, laboratory assignments and/or homework assignments as outlined on the course syllabus.<br>If the course contains a lab section, overseeing the laboratory component to include:<br>• Successful completion of student objectives<br>• Appropriate measures for student safety<br>• Meaningful content of lab exercises<br>• Coordination of lab with lecture<br>All other responsibilities associated with teaching the course including grading exams, papers and projects in a timely manner and providing help to students during schedule office hours.<br>If a teaching assistant is provided, provide supervision to the teaching assistant and coordinate assignments not exceeding the workload of the teaching assistant.</div> 147501 - Lampe Joint Department of BME https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234272 2026-08-27T14:49:55-04:00 2026-08-27T14:49:56-04:00 Client Support Specialist <div>The <strong>Client Support Specialist</strong> serves as the primary point of contact for all client-related inquiries within the Veterinary Hospital Business Office. This role ensures high-quality customer service by managing multi-channel communications, addressing client concerns, and processing financial transactions with accuracy and professionalism.&nbsp;Additionally, this role serves as a key liaison between clients and hospital staff to resolve complex issues, while providing operational support to maintain seamless business office coverage.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities</strong></div><ul><li>Manage incoming phone calls and the business office email inbox, serving as the first line of response for client inquiries, questions, and concerns.</li><li>Accurately process client payments, handle refunds, and manage daily transaction updates in accordance with hospital financial policies.</li><li>Communicate and collaborate effectively with other hospital staff to investigate and resolve client billing or service issues.</li><li>Administer and manage the client satisfaction survey program to gather feedback and support hospital service improvements.</li><li>Perform routine Accounts Receivable and Accounts Payable tasks as needed to assist with business office coverage.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Operational Support (as needed)</strong></div><ul><li>Accounts Receivable: Ensure hospital invoices are paid according to University guidelines; process employee reimbursements and supplemental pay; manage staff and student pagers and cell phones; and reconcile financial transactions (processing JVs and IDTs).</li><li>Accounts Payable: Manage, audit, resolve, and deliver the daily hospital deposit and revenue recap; manage bad debt, past-due accounts, and aged credits; handle communications and reconciliations with Collections and the Attorney General; and execute other duties including but not limited to posting payments, issuing refunds, credit card and Care Credit troubleshooting.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Key Qualifications</strong></div><ul><li>Ability to maintain a high volume of professional, empathetic, and clear verbal and written communication with clients and staff for extended periods.</li><li>Capacity for frequent, repetitive motions associated with digital payment processing, data entry, email management, and utilizing hospital information systems.</li><li>Ability to efficiently switch between customer service, conflict resolution, and detailed financial tasks in a fast-paced office environment.</li></ul><div><strong><br></strong>If you are<strong> </strong>a customer-focused professional who enjoys helping others, thrives in a fast-paced environment, and takes pride in delivering accurate, responsive service, we encourage you to apply. If you excel at building positive relationships, communicating with empathy and professionalism, and balancing customer service with detailed financial and administrative responsibilities, we'd love to have you join our Veterinary Hospital Business Office team.</div> 192001 - CVM Teaching Hospital https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228476 2026-03-16T13:04:55-04:00 2026-03-16T13:04:56-04:00 Temporary STEM Instructor 2026 <div>The Science House (TSH) is the College of Sciences statewide K-12 STEM outreach unit. Established in 1991, TSH is a national model of university collaboration between university departments, businesses, and the K-12 community. TSH’s mission is to collaboratively maintain and develop K-12 teacher and student programs that inspire a passion for STEM learning, research, and discovery. The Science House achieves this through four strategic priorities: 1) Teacher Professional Development centered on equipping teachers to use engaging instructional practices, emerging technologies, guided inquiry, and project-based learning, 2) Student Programs focused on problem-solving, applied learning, collaboration, communication, critical thinking, and competition, 3) Rural Satellite Offices that provide teacher professional development, student programs, and an Equipment and Technology Loan Program in rural, high-need areas, and 4) Broader Impact Support that helps NC State faculty in developing Impact Identities and carrying out broader impacts activities integrated with their research that advance open and participatory science. While working in this unit, you will be expected to foster a supportive and welcoming environment.<br><br>The Science House's Imhotep and Kyran Anderson programs introduce elementary and middle school students to the world of STEM. Programs are designed to enhance students’ enthusiasm and preparation for science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) with a particular emphasis on reaching under-served and students historically underrepresented in STEM disciplines. Students actively discover the wonder of STEM through inquiry-based activities, field trips, presentations, and career exploration activities with STEM professionals.<br><br>Kyran Anderson Academy is a one week daily summer program for rising third through sixth graders. Imhotep Academy offers one week daily summer sessions, as well as Fall and Spring sessions for middle school students on Saturdays from 8:45 a.m. to 1:00 p.m.<strong> Instructors may be hired for either or both camps.</strong><br><br>The STEM Instructor will plan, prepare, and deliver curricula and instruction for Imhotep and Kyran Anderson camps, academies, and track-out programs.<br><br>NC State promotes an integrated approach to problem solving that transforms lives and provides leadership for social, economic, and technological development across North Carolina and around the world. NC State’s land grant mission of teaching, research and service is dedicated to the service of North Carolina and its people. Applicants are encouraged to review the institution’s mission, vision and strategic plan, and consider how their background, interest and experience would enable them to support the university.</div> 171788 - The Science House Grads & Temps https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234701 2026-09-04T09:19:08-04:00 2026-09-04T09:19:09-04:00 Intermediate Care Veterinary Assistant <div>The Randall B. Terry Companion Animal Medical Center is seeking a highly skilled Patient Care Technician to join our Intermediate Care (IMC) department. This role is designed for a technician who thrives in a high-acuity environment and enjoys the balance of advanced clinical practice and clinical mentorship.</div><div><br>As the primary technical point of contact for the IMC, you will manage complex hospitalizations, execute advanced treatment plans, and facilitate communication between specialty services, students, and clients.</div><div><strong><br>Core Responsibilities</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Advanced Clinical Intervention:</strong> Execute high-level technical procedures including long-term central venous access, TPN and chemotherapy administration, blood transfusions, and complex wound management.</li><li><strong>Case Coordination:</strong> Serve as the central liaison for the unit; admitting patients, synthesizing diagnostic updates, and ensuring the projected plan of care is communicated effectively to clinicians and students.</li><li><strong>Clinical Mentorship:</strong> Provide direct supervision and coaching to 4th-year veterinary students, interns, and residents, evaluating their technical proficiency and patient care standards.</li><li><strong>Patient Advocacy:</strong> Manage comprehensive post-operative monitoring and specialized drug administration, applying a deep understanding of anatomy, physiology, and pharmacology.</li><li><strong>Departmental Operations:</strong> Maintain rigorous infectious disease protocols, manage internal billing for procedures, and ensure the unit is stocked and transition-ready.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Technical Qualifications</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Clinical Experience:</strong> Previous experience in a feline/canine veterinary setting is required.</li><li><strong>Communication:</strong> Proven ability to maintain professional composure and clear communication during emotionally charged or high-pressure situations.</li><li><strong>Technical Proficiency:</strong> Strong command of medical software, diagnostic equipment, and specialized pharmacology.</li><li><strong>Physical Standards:</strong> Ability to lift up to 50 lbs. with or without reasonable accommodations.</li></ul> 192001 - CVM Teaching Hospital https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231234 2026-06-04T10:32:30-04:00 2026-06-04T10:32:32-04:00 General Hospital Veterinary Assistant <div>The General Hospital Veterinary Assistant provides advanced technical support and clinical coordination for the General Hospital Department (GH) within the Randall B. Terry Companion Animal Medical Center (TC). Operating as a central liaison between clinicians, students, and clients, this role ensures seamless communication and high-standard patient care. The incumbent serves as the primary point of contact for patient admissions and is responsible for executing complex clinical procedures, managing varied caseloads, and providing hands-on instruction to veterinary students and house officers in a fast-paced academic environment.</div><div><strong><br>Key Responsibilities:</strong></div><div><br><strong>Clinical &amp; Technical Patient Care<br></strong><br></div><ul><li><strong>Advanced Nursing:</strong> Deliver comprehensive care using a deep understanding of anatomy, physiology, and pharmacology to proactively manage patient comfort and hygiene.</li><li><strong>Technical Procedures:</strong> Execute high-level clinical tasks including venipuncture, IV catheter placement (indwelling and short-term), and drug administration (IV, Oral, SQ).</li><li><strong>Specialized Treatments:</strong> Manage advanced wound care, bandage applications, and chemotherapy administration with precision.</li><li><strong>Post-Operative Management:</strong> Provide intensive monitoring for surgical recovery cases, focusing on vital sign stability and pain management protocols.</li><li><strong>Physical Demands:</strong> Must be able to lift up to 50lbs with our without reasonable accomidations.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Coordination &amp; Communication<br></strong><br></div><ul><li><strong>Case Management Liaison:</strong> Direct the admissions process and synthesize diagnostic information to provide real-time updates to students, staff, and clinicians.</li><li><strong>Client Relations:</strong> Act as a professional and empathetic point of contact for pet owners during patient visitations and updates.</li><li><strong>Internal Service Coordination:</strong> Facilitate \"internal customer service\" by collaborating across hospital departments to streamline patient transitions and consultations.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Instruction &amp; Mentorship<br></strong><br></div><ul><li><strong>Clinical Teaching:</strong> Supervise and evaluate senior veterinary students, interns, and residents in a clinical setting.</li><li><strong>Skill Development:</strong> Provide technical orientation and hands-on instruction regarding hospital protocols and procedural techniques.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Administration &amp; Operations<br></strong><br></div><ul><li><strong>Medical Records &amp; Finance:</strong> Maintain precise electronic medical records (EMR) and oversee internal billing to ensure all charges and treatments are captured accurately.</li><li><strong>Facility Oversight:</strong> Ensure the clinical environment adheres to infectious disease standards and strictly follow OSHA, radiation safety, and EHS protocols.</li><li><strong>Inventory &amp; Equipment:</strong> Manage equipment maintenance and ensure procedural stations are fully stocked for daily operations.</li></ul> 192001 - CVM Teaching Hospital https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232995 2026-07-21T15:48:29-04:00 2026-07-21T15:48:30-04:00 ICU Veterinary Assistant <div>The<strong> </strong>Intensive Care Unit (ICU) Veterinary Assistant provides advanced nursing care within the 24/7 high-acuity unit of the Randall B. Terry Jr. Companion Animal Medical Center. Operating in a fast-paced teaching environment, this role manages a varied caseload of small animal and exotic patients. The position requires a high level of clinical expertise, professional communication, and the ability to coordinate care seamlessly between specialty services, clinicians, and students.</div><div><strong><br>Clinical Excellence &amp; Patient Care<br></strong><br></div><ul><li><strong>Advanced Nursing Interventions:</strong> Perform complex medical treatments, including catheter placement (venous, arterial, and central), oxygen therapy (nasal/chest tubes), urinary management, and blood/plasma transfusions.</li><li><strong>Critical Monitoring &amp; Diagnostics:</strong> Monitor vital signs (TPR, BP, ECG), manage ventilator support, and utilize specialized equipment such as pulse oximeters, wound vacuums, and ultrasound machines.</li><li><strong>Emergency Response:</strong> Perform CPR and provide immediate life-saving support for patients in crisis.</li><li><strong>Pharmacological Management:</strong> Calculate and administer high-risk medications, including constant rate infusions (CRI), intravenous fluids, and complex pain management protocols.</li><li><strong>Specialized Recovery:</strong> Manage post-anesthetic and post-surgical recovery for high-risk neurological, soft tissue, trauma, and extracorporeal patients.</li><li><strong>Infection Control:</strong> Maintain rigorous standards for isolation protocols, antibiotic-resistant cases, and infectious disease management.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Coordination &amp; Educational Support<br></strong><br></div><ul><li><strong>Clinical Communication:</strong> Coordinate patient admissions and care plans by synthesizing information from clinicians and students regarding diagnostics and treatment goals.</li><li><strong>Instruction &amp; Mentorship:</strong> Actively teach, coach, and orient 4th-year veterinary students, interns, residents, and new staff on hospital policies and advanced patient care techniques.</li><li><strong>Data Management:</strong> Accurately record and communicate patient data, diagnostic results, and treatment histories using hospital information systems.</li><li><strong>Client Relations:</strong> Support and facilitate client visitations, maintaining professional empathy in emotionally charged or challenging situations.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Professional Standards &amp; Operations<br></strong><br></div><ul><li><strong>Safety &amp; Compliance:</strong> Maintain a clean, functional workspace in accordance with OSHA, EHS, radiation safety, and hospital infectious disease policies.</li><li><strong>Team Collaboration:</strong> Participate actively in problem-solving and team advancement, working independently with sound judgment while requesting assistance when necessary.</li><li><strong>Operational Agility:</strong> Demonstrate the physical ability to lift up to 50 pounds, work around potential hazards (zoonotic diseases, chemicals, gases), and extend work hours as required by patient needs.</li></ul> 192001 - CVM Teaching Hospital https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233957 2026-08-18T15:56:16-04:00 2026-08-18T15:56:17-04:00 COSS Support Specialist, Gaston County <div>The County Support Specialist is a member of the staff of the North Carolina Cooperative Extension Service (NCCES). The full-time County Support Specialist’s primary responsibility is to provide specialized office support for staff and volunteers, program marketing assistance, and some administrative support duties for Extension program areas.<br><br></div><div>The position duties reflect a relatively high degree of independence and accountability. Requires fluent computer and marketing skills, excellent customer service, and basic accounting skills.</div> 117624 - South Central District - Gaston https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/226652 2026-01-20T09:36:11-05:00 2026-01-20T09:36:13-05:00 Lecturer, MIE <div>The Department of Management, Innovation, and Entrepreneurship invites applications for part-time Non-Tenure Track Faculty Lecturer positions. These appointments are made for one semester or one academic year, with the possibility of renewal. Applications will be kept on file for up to one year and may be considered for future openings as they arise.<br><br></div><div>Selected candidates may teach one to three courses at the undergraduate (BSBA) and/or graduate (MBA) level. Instructional areas may include business law, entrepreneurship, human resources management, organizational behavior, and strategic management. Courses are taught on the main campus in Raleigh.<br><br></div><div>Specific instructional needs for the upcoming academic year have not yet been finalized.</div> 204001 - Management, Innovation & Entrepreneurship https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234488 2026-09-01T15:32:25-04:00 2026-09-01T15:32:25-04:00 Temporary Transformational Scholars Supervisor for Eastern/Central NC <div><strong>Overview of the College of Education: </strong>As one of the nation’s top-ranked colleges of education, NC State’s College of Education leads the way in North Carolina in preparing extraordinary educators, conducting transformational research, and engaging communities where they are to improve the educational outcomes of all learners and advance the greater good.The college’s faculty are widely-recognized thought leaders who use their research to transform the practice of teaching, learning, and leading. They have particular strengths in improving technology-enhanced learning and teaching; fostering community-based learning and PK-16 partnerships; and innovating leadership and workforce development, educational policy, and evaluation. About two-thirds of the faculty are grant active. They are engaged in over 225 active projects totaling $151 million, making them the most productive in terms of research activity compared with other colleges of education in North Carolina and among the Top 5% in the nation. In FY24, the college’s research expenditures exceeded $20 million.The college has over 1,600 students studying across five graduate certificates, seven undergraduate majors, two undergraduate minors, 13 master’s degrees, three Ph.D., and two Ed.D. programs housed in three academic departments: Educational Leadership, Policy, and Human Development; Science, Technology, Engineering, and Mathematics Education; and Teacher Education and Learning Sciences, with approximately 130 tenure-track and professional-track faculty, 110 support and professional staff, and about 160 graduate and teaching assistants. The College of Education is also home to the Belk Center for Community College Leadership and Research, the Friday Institute for Educational Innovation, and Elevate, NC State’s inclusive postsecondary education program for students with intellectual and developmental disabilities.The college has over 18,000 living alumni, with over 6,000 working in public schools in every county in North Carolina. Those who are teachers rate among the highest in effectiveness on surveys of school employers in North Carolina.</div><div><strong>This role will support student teachers placed in Harnett, Duplin, Edgecombe, and Wilson counties. It is ideal for someone within commuting distance to those areas, as the work will primarily be performed on-site within these counties. <br></strong><br>The Transformational Scholars Student Teaching Supervisor plays a vital role in supporting and mentoring student teachers participating in the Transformational Scholarship Program. This is a temporary, part-time position running from November to May. This program focuses on developing teacher leaders committed to serving rural eastern North Carolina communities. Supervisors will provide guidance, observation, feedback, and support to ensure student teachers' success while adhering to program requirements and donor stipulations. This position requires strong communication, collaboration, and organizational skills.</div><div><br></div><div><strong>Essential Duties and Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Relationship Building: Supervisors will foster strong working relationships with their assigned student teachers This involves initial meetings, planning sessions, and establishing effective communication channels.</li><li>Supervision and Evaluation Supervise and provide regular feedback to assigned Transformational Scholars student teachers. Conduct formal observations, utilizing established rubrics, and provide constructive feedback to promote growth and development. Complete all required documentation and evaluations in a timely manner. This includes:<ul><li>Weekly check-in meetings (virtual or in-person) with each student teacher.</li><li>Bi-weekly in-person visits to each student teacher's classroom.</li></ul></li><li>Mentoring and Support: Offer ongoing mentorship and support to student teachers, addressing challenges and celebrating successes. Serve as a liaison between student teachers, cooperating teachers, and university faculty. Assist in conflict resolution and provide guidance on best practices.</li><li>Community Liaison: Actively connect student teachers with relevant community resources, organizations, and individuals. This may involve identifying and facilitating partnerships with local businesses, non-profits, community leaders, and other stakeholders to enrich the student teachers' learning experience and expand their professional networks. Supervisors will help student teachers leverage these community connections to benefit their students and teaching practice.</li><li>Program Coordination: Collaborate with program coordinators and fellow supervisors to ensure the effective implementation of the program. Attend relevant meetings and contribute to the overall success of the Transformational Scholars Program.</li><li>Curriculum Alignment: Ensure that student teaching placements align with the program curriculum and learning objectives. Work closely with cooperating teachers to provide relevant support and materials.</li><li>Communication: Maintain consistent and clear communication with student teachers, cooperating teachers, program administrators, and other relevant stakeholders. Provide timely updates and address concerns effectively.</li><li>Travel: Travel to various school districts in eastern North Carolina to supervise student teachers</li></ul><div><br>Temporary position<br>Dates of employment: August 16th-May 15th 2026</div><div><br><strong>This role will support student teachers placed in Harnett, Duplin, Samson, Edgecombe, and Wilson counties. It is ideal for someone within commuting distance to those areas, as the work will primarily be performed on-site within these counties.</strong></div><div>.<br><br></div><div><strong>Schedule: 5-12 hours a week, subject to change based on needs<br></strong><br><br></div><div><br></div> 130801 - Student Success and Engagement https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230090 2026-04-30T14:36:08-04:00 2026-04-30T14:36:09-04:00 Lecturer of Sociology <div>The Department of Sociology and Anthropology at North Carolina State University invites applications for Lecturer positions to support instruction across Summer 2026, Fall 2026, and Spring 2027. Multiple hires may be made from this posting, with most roles structured as part-time, in-person teaching. This is an opportunity to contribute to undergraduate education in sociology and criminology while shaping engaging, high-quality learning experiences.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>&nbsp;Teach undergraduate courses in sociology and/or criminology&nbsp;</li><li>&nbsp;Coordinate and plan course delivery aligned with departmental expectations&nbsp;</li><li>&nbsp;Develop and prepare course syllabi and instructional materials&nbsp;</li><li>&nbsp;Deliver engaging classroom instruction in an in-person setting&nbsp;</li><li>&nbsp;Assess student learning and provide timely, constructive feedback&nbsp;</li><li>&nbsp;Manage course-related activities, including grading and student communication&nbsp;</li></ul><div><br></div><div>If you are passionate about teaching, committed to student success, and eager to contribute your expertise in sociology or criminology, we encourage you to apply and join our academic community.</div> 168301 - Sociology & Anthropology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233785 2026-08-13T08:07:49-04:00 2026-08-13T08:07:50-04:00 Associate Vice Chancellor, Research Commercialization <div>The Associate Vice Chancellor for Research Commercialization (AVC‑RC) provides executive leadership for NC State’s commercialization enterprise, spanning intellectual property management, licensing, startup formation, venture development, and investment programs. The AVC sets university‑wide commercialization strategy, ensures compliance with federal and institutional requirements, and leads programs that accelerate the translation of research into societal and economic impact.</div><div><br></div><div>As the AVC-RC, you will have the opportunity to expand NC State’s innovation ecosystem by connecting researchers, entrepreneurs, investors, and industry partners to accelerate the journey from discovery to impact. You will lead initiatives such as the Wolfpack Investor Network (WIN), commercialization funding programs, and the NSF I-Corps Mid-Atlantic Hub, while representing NC State within regional and national innovation communities. Through your vision, partnership-building, and strategic leadership, you will help position NC State’s research enterprise for greater economic and societal impact while advancing a culture of innovation across the university.</div><div><br></div><div>Key Responsibilities fall within the following functional areas.</div><div><strong><br>Strategic Leadership for Research Commercialization:</strong></div><ul><li>Establishes and advances NC State’s research commercialization strategy, aligning innovation priorities with institutional research goals, economic development initiatives, and national R1 expectations.</li><li>Sets enterprise-level priorities, performance measures, and service standards that strengthen commercialization outcomes across the university.</li><li>Advises the Vice Chancellor for Research and Innovation on commercialization policy, innovation strategy, and external engagement.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Intellectual Property, Licensing &amp; Commercialization Governance:</strong></div><ul><li>Provides executive oversight for intellectual property strategy, ensuring effective management, protection, and commercialization of university-developed innovations.</li><li>Establishes policies, frameworks, and practices that support licensing, agreements, compliance, and responsible stewardship of intellectual assets.</li><li>Provides university-wide leadership and guidance on intellectual property and commercialization matters.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Venture Development &amp; Innovation Funding:</strong></div><ul><li>Leads NC State’s venture development ecosystem, expanding pathways for research discoveries to advance through startup formation, market validation, and investment opportunities.</li><li>Provides strategic oversight for the Wolfpack Investor Network (WIN), commercialization funding programs, and NSF I-Corps Mid-Atlantic Hub activities.</li><li>Cultivates relationships with investors, entrepreneurs, accelerators, and industry partners to strengthen commercialization opportunities.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Innovation Ecosystem &amp; Executive Leadership:</strong></div><ul><li>Builds strategic partnerships with industry, investors, and innovation organizations to expand research impact and strengthen NC State’s role within regional and national innovation ecosystems.</li><li>Provides leadership for a multidisciplinary commercialization team, fostering a culture of innovation, collaboration, and continuous improvement.</li><li>Ensures effective governance, operational excellence, and high-quality services that advance the university’s commercialization mission.<br><br></li></ul><div>This is an opportunity for an accomplished commercialization leader to shape strategy, influence innovation at scale, and build connections that move groundbreaking research from discovery to impact. If you are ready to lead with vision and purpose, we invite you to apply.</div> 067401 - ORC - Technology Licensing Office https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/224376 2025-10-23T10:24:22-04:00 2025-10-23T10:24:23-04:00 Research Operations Manager II <div>The Prestage Department of Poultry Science is looking for a Research Operations Manager II to oversee our poultry research facilities, supporting faculty research, teaching, and extension programs. In this role, you’ll ensure our facilities run smoothly, safely, and efficiently—from broiler breeder houses and layer operations to our hatchery and processing units—while leading a dedicated team and helping our research programs succeed.</div><div><br></div><div>You will also provide logistical and operational support for research trials, including coordinating overlapping projects, managing grant-related compliance, monitoring flock health and welfare, and documenting outcomes. You’ll build strong relationships with faculty, students, and external partners, hosting site visits, supporting collaborative initiatives, and communicating updates on research and farm operations. Your work will ensure research projects run smoothly, ethically, and efficiently, while maintaining confidentiality and high standards of biosecurity and safety. You will accomplish this by performing tasks in key areas of responsibility outlined below.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div><strong>Key Areas of Responsibility:<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Manage Facilities, Operations, and People</strong></div><ul><li>Lead and supervise full- and part-time staff, including scheduling, training, and performance management.</li><li>Foster a collaborative, accountable, and safety-focused team culture.</li><li>Maintain poultry houses, hatchery, and processing units, overseeing environmental systems, preventive maintenance, and repairs.</li><li>Coordinate inventories, vendor services, and operational logistics to keep facilities running efficiently.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Oversee Finances and Administration</strong></div><ul><li>Manage budgets, reconcile accounts, and approve purchases.</li><li>Ensure compliance and cost-effective operations.</li><li>Prepare reports and coordinate with finance staff and vendors.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Ensure Safety and Compliance</strong></div><ul><li>Enforce biosecurity, animal welfare, and safety standards.</li><li>Conduct inspections, maintain records, and investigate incidents.</li><li>Promote a culture of safety and compliance throughout the team.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Support Research and Build Relationships</strong></div><ul><li>Coordinate facility use for research trials and projects.</li><li>Assist with animal handling, sample collection, and accurate recordkeeping.</li><li>Serve as primary contact for faculty, students, staff, and external partners.</li><li>Host site visits and represent the program professionally.</li><li>Maintain detailed records, support collaborative initiatives, and ensure research compliance.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Oversee Product Handling and Sales</strong></div><ul><li>Manage egg, chick, and poultry product collection, processing, and sales.</li><li>Maintain inventory, labeling, and financial documentation.</li><li>Ensure humane handling and biosecurity in all operations.<br><br></li></ul><div>If you enjoy leading teams, solving operational challenges, and contributing to impactful research, this role offers the chance to make a real difference in our department and the poultry industry.</div> 111901 - Prestage Poultry Science https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234377 2026-08-31T09:20:11-04:00 2026-08-31T09:20:11-04:00 Temporary Research Specialist - Kannapolis, NC <div><strong>1. Oversee trial evaluation for disease in carrot. This task will require some travel to the research station in Clinton on a weekly base.&nbsp;</strong></div><div><strong>2.&nbsp; Manage in vitro culture material&nbsp;</strong></div><div><strong>3. Perform routine laboratory duties including molecular biology assays (DNA extraction, PCR, real time)<br></strong><br></div> 110308 - Kannapolis Research https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/229002 2026-03-31T15:28:34-04:00 2026-03-31T15:28:35-04:00 EFNEP Educator <div>Are you looking for a rewarding career that will let you give back to your community? NC State Extension extends research-based knowledge to all North Carolinians, helping them transform science into everyday solutions that improve their lives and grow our state. We are the model of excellence in non-formal, research-based education that advances agriculture, the environment, human health and well-being, youth and communities, while creating prosperity for all North Carolinians. Check out this link (<a href=\"https://youtu.be/vgmaS33u230\"><strong>https://youtu.be/vgmaS33u230</strong></a>) to learn what other EFNEP Educators say about their experience working for EFNEP.<br><br>While our employees are located in NC County offices, they reap the benefits of employment at NC State University. Some of our benefits include:<br><br></div><div>· Paid time off<br><br></div><div>· Paid tuition<br><br></div><div>· Health Insurance<br><br></div><div>· Flexible Scheduling<br><br></div><div>· Retirement Savings<br><br></div><div>· Supplemental Benefits Programs<br><br></div><div>· WolfPerks!<br><br></div><div>You Belong Here! At NC State, our goal is for all employees to reach their fullest potential at work. As you consider this opportunity, we encourage you to review our <a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>Employee Value Proposition</strong></a> (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/</strong></a>) and learn more about what makes NC State the best place to learn and work for everyone.</div><div><br>The Extension EFNEP Educator is an employee of NC Cooperative Extension, NC State University. Under the supervision of the County Extension Director with guided assistance from the District EFNEP Extension Associate, the EFNEP Educator delivers nutrition and related subject matter to enrolled participants.<br><br></div><div><br><strong>Primary Function of Organizational Unit<br></strong>North Carolina Cooperative Extension (NCCE) gives our residents easy access to the resources and expertise of NC State University and North Carolina A &amp; T State University. Through educational programs, publications and events, Cooperative Extension delivers unbiased, research-based information to North Carolina citizens.<br><br>For over 50 years, EFNEP has been helping limited-resource youth and families with children learn to eat healthier meals and snacks, make better use of food dollars and reduce the risk of foodborne illnesses. In collaboration with many organizations through the “Eat Smart, Move More” initiative, NC EFNEP aims to help reduce the rising tide of obesity by teaching youth and families practical skills resulting in changed behaviors to eat smart, move more, and achieve a healthy weight.<br><br>Northampton County covers 537 square miles of prime agricultural land in the Upper Coastal Plains in northeastern North Carolina. The Roanoke River is located along the southern border of Northampton County. Two lakes (Gaston and Roanoke Rapids Lake) are located at the western end of Northampton. Northampton County is located approximately 103 miles from the nearest beach, Virginia Beach, VA and is located approximately 100 miles from our State Capital, Raleigh, NC. The county population is 16,580 (2024). The county seat and home of the Northampton County Extension Center is Jackson, North Carolina. Our Northern border is the North Carolina/Virginia State Line.</div><div><br></div><div>For more information about Northampton County see <a href=\"http://www.northamptonnc.com/\"><strong>http://www.northamptonnc.com/</strong></a></div><div><br></div><div>To learn more about the NCCE Northampton County Center go to</div><div><a href=\"https://northampton.ces.ncsu.edu/\"><strong>https://northampton.ces.ncsu.edu/</strong></a></div><div><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities</strong><br>The individual in this position will recruit and enroll adult and school-aged youth participants into the program, make regular teaching visits to enrollees either in the home or in group settings, teach basic principles of nutrition and food preparation skills, submit required program records as requested, market the program within the assigned area, recruit volunteers from within the community involving them in leadership roles, collaborate with local Cooperative Extension staff and agencies, and assist with securing program resources.<br><br><br></div> 117717 - Northeast District - Northampt https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231787 2026-06-16T15:32:19-04:00 2026-06-16T15:32:20-04:00 Postdoc Research Scholar- Nascone-Yoder Lab <div>The Postdoctoral Researcher will conduct independent research focusing on the development of anatomical left-right asymmetry. Specifically, the incumbent will investigate the cellular and molecular mechanisms of left-right asymmetric organ morphogenesis within the developing digestive tract. Research methodologies will utilize advanced gene misexpression strategies (CRISPR/morpholino oligonucleotides), transcriptomics (bulk and/or spatial), 3D imaging modalities (nano-CT), and/or computational modeling (CompuCell3D).</div><div><strong><br>Core Competencies &amp; Responsibilities</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Project Leadership:</strong> Demonstrated ability to independently lead, manage, and execute complex research projects while working effectively within an interdisciplinary team environment.</li><li><strong>Communication &amp; Collaboration:</strong> Excellent oral and written communication skills, with a proven track record of successful scientific collaboration.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Technical Expertise &amp; Preferred Qualifications</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Advanced Research Methodologies:</strong> Demonstrated expertise in microscopy, image processing/analysis, gene editing, immunostaining, and/or molecular biology.</li><li><strong>Scholarly Output:</strong> A strong record of publication in peer-reviewed scientific journals, reflecting the ability to synthesize and disseminate research findings.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Research Design and Execution</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Independent Experimentation:</strong> Independently design and conduct research experiments to investigate developmental processes.</li><li><strong>Advanced Methodologies:</strong> Utilize advanced technical methodologies, including gene expression analysis, cell lineage tracing, and manipulation of genetic pathways.</li><li><strong>Model Organism Care:</strong> Manage, work with, and care for living model organisms (e.g., mouse, <em>Xenopus</em>) as required by research protocols.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Data Analysis and Interpretation</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Technical Analytics:</strong> Analyze complex experimental data using advanced techniques such as microscopy imaging and bioinformatics.</li><li><strong>Data Visualization:</strong> Generate high-quality figures, charts, and diagrams to clearly present research data.</li><li><strong>Results Interpretation:</strong> Interpret experimental results to draw meaningful, data-driven conclusions about the mechanisms of development.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Publication and Dissemination</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Manuscript Production:</strong> Prepare and draft scientific manuscripts for publication in peer-reviewed journals, managing data presentation, discussion text, and subsequent revisions.</li><li><strong>Research Presentation:</strong> Disseminate research findings at national and international conferences, as well as within internal collaborative networks.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Collaboration</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Intra-Lab Support:</strong> Collaborate with laboratory colleagues and contribute to ongoing projects involving alternative organ systems.</li><li><strong>Cross-Disciplinary Partnership:</strong> Partner with researchers across scientific disciplines to exchange expertise and advance shared research aims.</li></ul> 194001 - Department of Molecular Biomedical Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233236 2026-07-28T16:25:53-04:00 2026-07-28T16:25:54-04:00 Professor of the Practice in Media Arts and Production <div>The Department of Communication is seeking a collaborative scholar-practitioner who is passionate about advancing creative media production through innovative storytelling, emerging technologies, and critical inquiry. In this role, you will help prepare the next generation of media professionals by combining creative practice with meaningful engagement across the social, cultural, political, and technological dimensions of contemporary communication.<br><br></div><div>You will contribute to a forward-thinking academic community by teaching media arts and production courses, helping shape the future of the department's media production curriculum, and fostering connections that extend beyond the classroom. Through interdisciplinary collaboration, partnerships with industry and community organizations, and leadership of shared creative spaces, you will play an important role in expanding the department's strengths in digital media production, creative practice, and critical making.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in media arts and production, including areas such as film and video production, documentary and nonfiction storytelling, transmedia storytelling, and other emerging forms of creative media.</li><li>Incorporate innovative approaches to storytelling and media technologies, including audiovisual, interactive, immersive, generative, AI-assisted, mobile, locative, social media, installation, and game-based media, as appropriate to your expertise.</li><li>Contribute to the continued development and evolution of the department's media production curriculum.</li><li>Coordinate instructional staffing for media production courses.</li><li>Provide oversight of the department's media lab and support an engaging learning environment for students and faculty.</li><li>Develop interdisciplinary collaborations across NC State to expand opportunities for creative scholarship and teaching.</li><li>Build and maintain partnerships with media industries, arts organizations, and community partners to strengthen experiential learning and public engagement.</li><li>Advance creative media production that encourages critical reflection, public engagement, and innovative forms of communication.</li><li>Participate actively in departmental initiatives and contribute to a collaborative and collegial academic community.<br><br></li></ul><div>Join a department where creativity, critical inquiry, and emerging media converge. If you're ready to help students explore new forms of storytelling while contributing to an innovative and collaborative academic community, we encourage you to apply.</div> 160701 - Communication https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223243 2025-09-25T10:56:02-04:00 2025-09-25T10:56:02-04:00 UTS - Temporary Electronics Specialist at UNC Chapel Hill (2nd Shift) <div>This position serves as part of the <strong>Life Safety/Electronics-Specialty Shop</strong> team, ensuring the smooth operation of critical safety and monitoring systems across campus. The role involves both <strong>preventive maintenance</strong> and <strong>hands-on installations or modifications</strong> to essential systems that support the safety, security, and functionality of the university.</div><div>As an Electronics Specialist, you will perform tasks such as:</div><ul><li>Conducting <strong>routine and preventive maintenance inspections</strong> on:<ul><li>Life Safety Systems</li><li>Fire Detection and Suppression Systems</li><li>Security Systems</li><li>CCTV (Closed-Circuit Television) Systems</li><li>Equipment Monitoring Systems</li><li>Other miscellaneous electronic systems and subsystems</li></ul></li><li><strong>Installing, maintaining, and modifying</strong> fire detection and suppression systems, Life Safety equipment, card access systems, CCTV, and equipment monitoring tools.</li><li><strong>Responding to repair requests and troubleshooting</strong> issues with various electronic components to ensure systems operate at peak performance.</li><li><strong>Coordinating with other teams and vendors</strong> to guarantee timely inspections and installations.</li></ul><div><strong>Schedule of M-F 4:00 pm to 12:30 am (2nd Shift)</strong></div> 480502 - UHR UTS/UNC Partnership https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234111 2026-08-24T16:53:01-04:00 2026-08-24T16:53:02-04:00 Electronics Specialist <div>The primary purpose of the position is to perform the day-to-day operation of the NCSU Phytotron through proper function of the electrical, electronic, and mechanical systems of the facility.&nbsp;<br><br>The position will also conduct daily surveillance and needed repairs of environmental chamber operations, diagnose any source of malfunction, and manufacture, assemble, install, calibrate, repair, and maintain/modify environmental control and measurement systems within the Phytotron with little supervision. &nbsp;<br><br>In this role you must also be able to lift and carry up to 50 lbs., with or without reasonable accommodations.&nbsp;</div> 111701 - Phytotron https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/227642 2026-02-19T14:24:06-05:00 2026-02-19T14:24:07-05:00 Architectural Campus Planner <div>The <strong>Architectural Campus Planne</strong>r will play a key role in guiding long-term development, strategic initiatives, and space planning across one of the most dynamic and innovative campuses in the country. This is an exciting opportunity for a forward-thinking professional who enjoys solving complex planning challenges, collaborating with varied stakeholders, and making a meaningful impact on the physical environment of the university.<br><br>In this role, the position provides Campus Planning and NCSU Colleges with expertise in planning for long-range campus development projects, campus strategic initiatives, and space analysis and management. The position works closely with other Facilities Division units and stakeholders across campus. This position may manage outside designers, may produce in-house studies, and provide space data analysis for decision-making.</div><div><strong><br>Essential Duties and Responsibilities:<br></strong>The work required for this role involves a high degree of complexity and requires functioning as&nbsp; the technical or subject matter expert. Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not&nbsp; limited to:<strong><br><br>Planning, Analysis, and Design</strong></div><ul><li>Translate the university’s strategic plan into the required campus facilities to meet the plan’s goals and objectives.</li><li>Provide design solutions for a broad range of architectural, interior, and engineering planning issues essential to meet the university’s physical development needs.</li><li>Analyze, document, and develop planning projects, policies and procedures, and informal project designs to address the university’s physical campus needs.</li><li>Typical duties include:<ul><li>Developing facilities planning and design strategies for building architecture and interior spaces.</li><li>Developing space programs and space analysis.</li><li>Leading architectural and space studies, including coordination with building (MEP) and lab systems analysis.</li><li>Identifying and developing project alternative design options.</li><li>Recommending planning and design solutions guided by the Physical Master Plan.</li></ul></li></ul><div><strong>Support for the Physical Master Plan</strong></div><ul><li>Assist in providing recommendations on implementing the Physical Master Plan focused on architecture, interior, and land use design. This is accomplished through the development of schematic designs, feasibility studies, and development plans considering the various facility components of the campus.</li><li>Projects may also include assisting with the planning and design of: Infrastructure, Mobility support systems, ADA accessibility, and other general facilities planning and development support functions.</li></ul><div><strong>Stakeholder Coordination and Representation</strong></div><ul><li>Work requires considerable contact with campus leadership, as well as with architects, engineers, and other related industry professionals.</li><li>This requires the ability to lead and assemble the appropriate stakeholders to make the highest and best decisions for the university.</li><li>This position represents the university as an expert.</li></ul><div><strong>Complexity of Work</strong></div><ul><li>Work assignments may involve unique factors and be lacking in precedence on which to base decisions.</li><li>Assignments may be technically complex as evidenced by a high number of variables.</li><li>Work assignments are often technically complex, involving unique challenges with limited precedent, requiring a high degree of professional expertise in detailed technical procedures.</li><li>Work is often performed independently, requiring professional knowledge of complex and/or detailed technical procedures.</li><li>Work performed includes exercising judgment and decision- making that directly impacts life, health, safety, and/or the environment.</li></ul><div><strong>Potential Oversight and Authority</strong></div><ul><li>This position may be responsible for consultation, evaluation, planning, design, design review, and approval of campus development plans, buildings, utility systems, site plans, mapping, and infrastructure.</li><li>The position may provide project management oversight.</li><li>Responsibilities may include overseeing major campus planning efforts and managing the implementation of projects/plans according to building codes and zoning regulations, which may include approval authority.</li><li>Work may require considerable public contact to explain planning or design projects, analysis of data, and standards. This may require appearances before campus administration, college administrations, regulatory bodies, and building committees.</li></ul> 442010 - Campus Planning https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234591 2026-09-03T08:49:46-04:00 2026-09-03T08:49:46-04:00 Assistant Director, Facilities HR Programs <div>The <strong>Assistant Director of Facilities Human Resources (FHR)</strong> serves as a key member of the Facilities Human Resources leadership team and provides strategic direction, oversight, and continuous improvement for a comprehensive portfolio of HR programs that support the employee lifecycle across the Facilities Division. Reporting directly to the Director of Facilities Human Resources, this position leads a team of 3-4 HR professionals and is responsible for advancing division-wide initiatives related to <strong>talent acquisition, onboarding, training and development, engagement and recognition</strong>.<br><br></div><div>As a trusted HR leader and consultant, the Assistant Director works collaboratively with Facilities executives, managers, supervisors, employees, and University Human Resources to ensure the delivery of responsive, consistent, and high-quality HR services that support the division's operational goals and NC State University's strategic priorities. The position plays a critical role in fostering a positive workplace culture by developing programs and practices that enhance employee engagement, strengthen workforce capability, promote employee success, and support long-term talent retention.<br><br></div><div>This role combines <strong>strategic program leadership, staff supervision, workforce analytics, change management, and process improvement</strong>. The Assistant Director evaluates organizational needs, identifies emerging workforce trends, and implements innovative solutions designed to improve the employee experience and operational effectiveness. The position also serves as a senior HR advisor on complex employee and organizational issues, providing guidance that balances business needs, policy compliance, legal requirements, and employee well-being.</div><div><br></div><div>This position offers the opportunity to make a significant impact on the Facilities Division by shaping HR programs, developing talent, improving organizational processes, and ensuring employees have the resources and support necessary to thrive and contribute to the University's mission. Individuals who are passionate about <strong>building innovative HR programs, developing people, leading teams, solving complex organizational challenges, and creating meaningful workplace improvements</strong> are encouraged to apply and join the Facilities Human Resources team at NC State University.</div><div><strong><br></strong><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include but are not limited to: </em></strong><strong><br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Provide strategic leadership and oversight for division-wide programs related to <strong>recruitment, onboarding, employee development, engagement and recognition</strong>.</li><li><strong>Lead the design, implementation, assessment, and continuous improvement of HR initiatives that enhance the employee experience and support Facilities Division goals.</strong></li><li><strong>Oversee recruitment and talent acquisition strategies</strong>, partnering with leaders and hiring managers to strengthen hiring practices and improve the attraction, selection, and retention of high-quality talent.</li><li>Oversee onboarding programs that ensure a welcoming, informative, and consistent experience for new employees while supporting successful integration into the organization.</li><li>Assess organizational and workforce development needs and implement training, leadership development, mentoring, e-learning, and professional growth opportunities for employees and supervisors.</li><li><strong>Develop and execute employee engagement initiatives </strong>by analyzing survey results, feedback, workforce trends, and organizational data to identify opportunities for improvement.</li><li>Manage employee recognition programs that celebrate achievements, reinforce organizational values, and promote a culture of appreciation and belonging.</li><li><strong>Supervise, coach, mentor, and evaluate a team of HR professionals</strong>, establishing performance expectations and ensuring high-quality, customer-focused service delivery.</li><li>Research and analyze complex HR issues and develop practical, compliant, and business-focused solutions aligned with University policies, employment regulations, and organizational priorities.</li><li>Build and maintain strong collaborative relationships with Facilities leadership, University Human Resources, and campus partners to ensure consistent application of HR practices and successful implementation of initiatives.</li><li><strong>Utilize workforce data, operational metrics, and employee feedback to evaluate program effectiveness, identify trends, and support strategic decision-making.</strong></li><li>Develop and deliver presentations, training programs, communications, reports, and recommendations to a variety of stakeholders, including senior leadership.</li><li>Oversee HR records, reporting processes, and data management activities while ensuring confidentiality, data integrity, and compliance with applicable requirements.</li><li>Monitor changes in HR best practices, employment law, higher education trends, and workforce strategies and recommend program enhancements as appropriate.</li><li><strong>Lead HR technology initiatives and process improvement efforts, including collaboration on the continued enhancement and utilization of AiM and other HR systems to improve data management, reporting capabilities, and operational efficiency.</strong></li><li>Support organizational change initiatives and special projects that advance Facilities Division and University objectives.</li></ul> 447020 - Facilities Human Resources https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230723 2026-05-18T09:24:07-04:00 2026-05-18T09:24:09-04:00 Lead Cook <div><strong>NC State Dining</strong> is seeking individuals to Join our Pack as a <strong>Lead Cook</strong>. Successful candidates will receive instructions from the chef and other culinary professionals in a dining hall, food court or restaurant setting.<br><br>Essential job duties include:</div><ul><li>Provide professional guidance and instructions to temporary Line Station Cooks and student Cooks.</li><li>Prepare food as per the standardized recipes and specifications.</li><li>Perform small batch cooking and made to order items, display station set-up and service, and transport cooked foods to hot boxes for line service.</li><li>Provide support to the front of house operations with back-up food to ensure that there is no lag time between changing out food items.</li><li>Portion food products prior to cooking according to standard portion sizes and recipe specifications.</li><li>Perform specialty tasks such as display station set up and service, enhanced garnishing, recipe development and event meal assistance.</li><li>Check temperatures and proper storage of food items, and follow proper guidelines in the storage of food to prevent contamination and spoilage.</li><li>Operate all kitchen equipment safety and as recommended by manufacturer.</li><li>Perform necessary cleaning of utensils, work space, tables, shelves, and equipment in accordance with maintaining a Grade “A” Sanitation rating.</li><li>Participate in recipe development, and event meal assistance.</li><li>Other duties as assigned to ensure NC State Dining business needs are met. Position may be asked to assist in other locations as necessary.</li></ul><div><br>This is an hourly position, that includes a State required 32-day break at the end of 11 months. Superior work performance may provide an opportunity to renew employment and/or advance into a benefits eligible permanent position.<br><br><a href=\"https://dining.ncsu.edu/about/overview/\"><strong>NC State Dining</strong></a> provides campus wide food services to students, staff, and faculty on the North Carolina State University Campus. NC State Dining operates 10 cafes, 3 food courts and 6 dining halls, 4 markets, 1 restaurant, vending, a nutritional counseling service, and a catering department. The department employs approximately 1000 temporary and student employees and approximately 190 permanent EHRA and SHRA employees.</div> 471501 - Dining Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232788 2026-07-16T10:00:16-04:00 2026-07-16T10:00:17-04:00 Assistant Extension Agent, Ag (Assistant, Associate, Full Agent) <div>Are you looking for a rewarding career that will let you give back to your community? NC State Extension extends research-based knowledge to all North Carolinians, helping them transform science into everyday solutions that improve their lives and grow our state. We are the model of excellence in non-formal, research-based education that advances agriculture, the environment, human health and well-being, youth and communities, while creating prosperity for all North Carolinians. Check out this <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3MptxITpJ54&amp;t=3s\"><strong>video</strong></a> (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3MptxITpJ54&amp;t=3s) on what it's like to work for NC State Extension.<br><br><strong>The position may be hired at the Assistant, Associate, or Full Agent level if the selected candidate meets the required qualifications.</strong><br><br></div><div>While our employees are located in NC County offices, they reap the benefits of employment at NC State University. Some of our benefits include:<br><br></div><div>· Paid time off<br><br></div><div>· Paid tuition<br><br></div><div>· Health Insurance<br><br></div><div>· Flexible Scheduling<br><br></div><div>· Retirement Savings<br><br></div><div>· Supplemental Benefits Programs<br><br></div><div>· WolfPerks!<br><br></div><div>You Belong Here! At NC State, our goal is for all employees to reach their fullest potential at work. As you consider this opportunity, we encourage you to review our <a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>Employee Value Proposition</strong></a> (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/</strong></a>) and learn more about what makes NC State the best place to learn and work for everyone.<br><br><strong>Primary Function of Organizational Unit<br></strong>North Carolina Cooperative Extension gives our residents easy access to the resources and expertise of NC State University and NC A&amp;T State University. Through educational programs, publications, and events, Cooperative Extension field faculty deliver unbiased, research-based information to North Carolina citizens. Our mission is to partner with communities to deliver education and technology that enrich the lives, land and economy of North Carolinians.<br><br>At 954 sq. miles, Columbus County is the third largest county in North Carolina and is home to over 55,000 residents. Columbus County is famous for its small towns and friendly atmosphere. To find out more about Columbus County, see https://columbusco.org/. To learn more about the NCCE Columbus County Center, go to https://columbus.ces.ncsu.edu/<br><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities</strong><br>N.C. Cooperative Extension in Columbus County is seeking to hire a Livestock Extension Agent. The agent in this position will provide leadership for the development, implementation, and evaluation of an effective educational program and informational assistance to meet the needs of the county youth and adult populations in the following area of responsibility – Livestock, to include but not limited to swine, cattle, goats, sheep, as well as forages and pasture management. A successful candidate must demonstrate the ability and enthusiasm for serving audiences.<br><br>The person selected for this position will be responsible for educational programming and informational assistance related to all phases of livestock production and management. The agent will be responsible for providing leadership in planning, implementing, evaluating and marketing educational programs and activities with measurable impacts in livestock production/management and forage/pasture management. Additionally, the person selected for this position will be expected to work closely with the 4-H Agent to provide leadership in planning, implementing, evaluating and marketing a youth livestock program.<br><strong><br></strong>This position demands a responsible and self-motivated individual with outstanding relationship skills and a strong background in livestock production and 4-H livestock programming.<br><br>Frequent travel within the county and occasional travel outside the county is required.</div> 117822 - Southeast District - Columbus https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230276 2026-05-06T09:13:29-04:00 2026-05-06T09:13:31-04:00 Floor Technician <div>Are you someone who takes pride in seeing a floor gleam after a job well done? Do you enjoy working behind the scenes to create clean, safe, and welcoming spaces for others? If so, <strong>NC State’s Facilities Division</strong> wants <em>you</em> to be part of our <strong>Environmental Services team</strong>!<br><br></div><div>We’re on the lookout for a <strong>motivated and detail-oriented Floor Technician</strong> who’s ready to roll up their sleeves and help keep over <strong>10 million square feet</strong> of our beautiful campus looking its best. This is more than just a cleaning job; it’s a chance to make a visible impact every single day in a vibrant, collaborative, and purpose-driven environment.<br><br>The ideal candidate is a <strong>confident and clear communicator</strong>, capable of easily connecting with supervisors, teammates, and the community. If you’re someone who loves a challenge, pays attention to the little things, and takes satisfaction in creating clean, safe spaces, we’d love to meet you.&nbsp; The candidate must also be able to lift a minimum of 60 pounds continuously and 90 pounds occasionally, with or without reasonable accommodations.</div><div><br></div><div><strong>Apply today and take the first step toward becoming a key player in keeping NC State at its best!</strong></div><div><br><strong>Working Hours: 8:00 AM - 4::30 PM - Monday - Friday<br><br></strong><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li><strong>Strip and refinish hard surface floors, extract and clean carpets, and conduct routine maintenance using equipment such as burnishers, auto scrubbers, and carpet extractors.</strong></li><li>Complete assigned cleaning tasks, including vacuuming, mopping, shower scrubbing, and floor finish application, to maintain designated areas.</li><li><strong>Adhere to established safety procedures</strong> for handling cleaning products and operating equipment. Wear appropriate <strong>Personal Protective Equipment (PPE)</strong> and post caution signage during cleaning activities.</li><li><strong>Identify and report building discrepancies</strong>, safety hazards, and equipment malfunctions to the manager promptly.</li><li>Support cleaning and maintenance needs during emergencies and inclement weather, ensuring the safety and functionality of campus spaces.</li><li>Accurately complete incident reports, <strong>interpret Safety Data Sheets (SDS), </strong>and apply knowledge of safety protocols in daily operations.</li></ul> 445080 - Environmental Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232375 2026-07-02T12:51:13-04:00 2026-07-02T12:51:13-04:00 Temporary Lab Technician <div><strong>Duties Include:</strong></div><ul><li>Plant tissue culture, regeneration, and transformation of varying crops potentially including Hosta, Miscanthus, Liriope</li><li>General plant culture.<strong><br></strong><br></li></ul> 111501 - Horticultural Science https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233524 2026-08-04T17:01:03-04:00 2026-08-04T17:01:04-04:00 Clinical Research Technician – Small Animal Surgery <div>Join a collaborative clinical and surgical research team dedicated to advancing veterinary medicine through high-quality research. Working with Dr. Bae and Dr. McPhetridge, the Research Technician provides critical technical, administrative, and operational support that helps ensure the successful execution of multiple active research studies. This position plays an integral role in coordinating research activities, maintaining accurate data and regulatory records, and supporting studies from planning through completion while contributing to the overall success of the research program.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Research Coordination &amp; Study Management</strong></div><ul><li>Coordinate prospective and retrospective clinical research studies, ensuring research activities are organized, completed on schedule, and conducted in accordance with study protocols.</li><li>Perform medical record reviews, collect research data, maintain study databases, and coordinate owner questionnaires and follow-up communications to support accurate and complete data collection.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Research Operations &amp; Regulatory Support</strong></div><ul><li>Coordinate and support cadaveric research activities while assisting with regulatory documentation and study compliance requirements.</li><li>Manage research purchasing, inventory, and supply coordination to support the day-to-day operational needs of multiple active research projects.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Collaboration &amp; Administrative Support</strong></div><ul><li>Partner with clinicians, residents, students, staff, and pet owners to facilitate research activities and ensure effective communication throughout the research process.</li><li>Maintain organized research records, support confidential data management, and provide administrative and operational assistance that contributes to the overall efficiency of the research program.<br><br></li></ul><div><br>If you are<strong> </strong>a highly organized professional who enjoys supporting collaborative research, managing multiple priorities, and working closely with clinicians, researchers, students, and clients, we encourage you to apply. If you have strong attention to detail, excellent communication skills, and are committed to advancing veterinary clinical research through accurate, organized, and compliant study coordination, we'd love to have you join our team.</div> 195001 - Department of Clinical Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223316 2025-09-25T11:55:15-04:00 2025-09-25T11:55:16-04:00 Open Rank Non Tenure Track in Media Arts, Design and Technology <div>The Department of Media Arts, Design and Technology is accepting applications for part-time non-tenure-track faculty appointments for a one-semester or one-year appointment with the possibility of renewal.<br><br>Rank to be determined based upon education and professional experience. Specific teaching opportunities include art studios at all levels, including advanced comprehensive studios and/or special topic seminar courses. Applications will be reviewed as these part-time non-tenure-track teaching opportunities arise.</div> 120501 - Art and Design https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234329 2026-08-28T14:22:19-04:00 2026-08-28T14:22:20-04:00 Painter <div>The Painter will be responsible for painting duties, which may include, but are not be limited to painting of buildings, fixtures, and equipment. The Painter will determine whether surfaces need to be sanded or patched before applying paint, and will then clean, sand, patch, prime and prepare areas to be painted. After the work of sanding, puttying or patching has been completed by self or others, the Painter will check surfaces before beginning the next phase of work.<br><br>The Painter maintains a safe, clean and non-disruptive work area; sets up scaffolding or ladders; determines exact amount of paint that will be needed and how many coats of paint to apply, and works independently and/or assists other Painters as assigned. The Painter will also establish and maintain effective working relationships with a very wide range of contacts to include co-workers, contractors, other trades team members, vendors, customers/residents, and other University personnel. This position must also&nbsp; have the ability to set up and break down scaffolding, work from extreme heights and lift up to 30 pounds frequently and up to 60-90 pounds occasionally.</div> 242290 - Housing Facilities Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234301 2026-08-27T23:56:15-04:00 2026-08-27T23:56:16-04:00 Temporary Financial Operations Specialist <div>The Temporary Financial Operations Specialist provides accounting and financial operations support within the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences (CALS) Business Services unit. Business Services is transitioning to a cross-functional service model in which employees develop proficiency across multiple financial operations functions. Employees will support Accounts Payable, Accounts Receivable, Travel, and Procurement Card (PCard) operations and may be assigned primary responsibilities based on operational needs. Work assignments may shift across functional areas to support workload balancing, team coverage, and continuity of business operations.<strong><br><br>Primary Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Process and support Accounts Payable, Accounts Receivable, Travel, and Procurement Card (PCard) transactions, as assigned.</li><li>Process, review, reconcile, and analyze financial transactions for accuracy, completeness, and compliance with university policies and procedures.</li><li>Apply accounting principles and established business processes to transactions across multiple funding sources.</li><li>Review financial activity for appropriate documentation, classification, and compliance with applicable university, state, federal, and sponsor requirements.</li><li>Research and resolve transaction discrepancies while maintaining accurate financial records and supporting documentation.</li><li>Assist with financial monitoring, reconciliation, reporting activities, and audit support.</li><li>Utilize university financial systems, including PeopleSoft, ServiceNow, and reporting tools, to process, review, and monitor financial activity.</li><li>Provide customer service and guidance to departments regarding financial processes, documentation requirements, and transaction status.</li><li>Collaborate with campus partners to resolve financial issues and support efficient business operations.</li><li>Identify opportunities to improve processes, strengthen internal controls, and enhance operational effectiveness.</li><li>Participate in cross-training and provide backup coverage across Business Services functions as operational needs require.</li><li>Perform other duties as assigned</li></ul> 110305 - CALS Business Office https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228507 2026-03-17T09:16:22-04:00 2026-03-17T09:16:22-04:00 Temporary Research Technician <div>Research tech will be part of the cotton breeding and genetics research team. He/she will help with processing of cotton samples including ginning and acid delinting, seed preparation for storage and planting and help grow the plants in the greenhouse and the fields, and selfing, crossing and harvesting of cotton in the breeding nurseries and field trials. Other essential duties of this position are to perform day-to-day activities of greenhouse care and maintenance and assist the post docs, grad students and research specialist while collecting phenotypic data and tissue samples from the greenhouses, growth chambers and cotton fields and perform lab-based experiments including DNA isolation, PACE marker assays, gene expression analyses, bioinformatic analyses of genotypic data, and analyze and summarize the molecular biology and phenotypic data.</div> 110901 - Crop and Soil Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234704 2026-09-04T09:50:51-04:00 2026-09-04T09:50:52-04:00 Medical Lab Technologist <div>Lab technologist to perform routine testing in Cytology, Coagulation, Hematology, Chemistry, Special Chemistry, and Urinalysis. Perform accessioning and communicate with hospital clinicians and staff. Assist in training DVM students and new employees.</div> 192001 - CVM Teaching Hospital https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231412 2026-06-08T13:14:52-04:00 2026-06-08T13:14:54-04:00 Environmental Services Technician I - 40311 <div>Are you looking for a fulfilling career where you can make a tangible difference in creating a clean and welcoming environment? We are seeking a passionate and detail-oriented<strong> Environmental Service Technician </strong>to join our exceptional team at <strong>NC State’s Facilities Division</strong>. In this vital role, you will help maintain over 10 million square feet of campus space, ensuring a safe and inviting atmosphere for students, staff, and visitors.<br><br>With the support of a dynamic team of over 320 professionals, you’ll have the opportunity to grow your skills and take pride in your work while contributing to our <strong>vibrant campus community</strong>. Join us and be a part of a team that takes pride in delivering exceptional service to one of the most prestigious campuses in the region. Apply today and help us keep NC State shining bright!<br><br></div><div><strong>Working Hours: 8:00 a.m.- 4:30 p.m., Saturday – Wednesday</strong></div><div><br><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li><strong>Clean and sanitize</strong> designated areas using standard cleaning assignments to maintain a safe and hygienic environment</li><li><strong>Report any discrepancies, damages, or maintenance needs</strong> within buildings and promptly respond to emergencies and inclement weather</li><li><strong>Operate housekeeping equipment</strong> such as vacuums, mops, and scrubbers to complete tasks like dusting, disinfecting, and replenishing supplies</li><li><strong>Remove and manage recycling</strong> from centralized indoor recycling sites</li><li>Occasionally shampoo and extract carpets, and assist with pressure washing of entrances, exterior walls, and windows<strong>.</strong></li><li>Assist with window washing and other seasonal cleaning tasks as needed</li><li>Communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, and the general public, and follow verbal and written instructions related to work assignments and operational needs.</li><li>Perform lifting duties up to 30 pounds frequently and 60 pounds occasionally, with or without reasonable accommodations, and perform physical tasks such as grasping, pushing, and pulling cleaning equipment; reaching overhead; bending, squatting, climbing, and maintaining balance.</li></ul> 445080 - Environmental Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233067 2026-07-23T10:22:45-04:00 2026-07-23T10:22:46-04:00 Radiation Oncology Anesthesia Technician <div>The Radiation Oncology Department provides advanced oncological support to a varied patient population across the hospital campus, including small, large, and exotic species. This position requires advanced veterinary medical knowledge, strong time-management skills, and the ability to thrive in a fast-paced, teaching-focused clinical environment.<br><br>The position collaborates closely with specialty services, clinicians, staff, and students to coordinate and deliver high-quality anesthetic and service specific patient care needs. Responsibilities include comprehensive patient assessment, administration of complex anesthetic and medical treatments, continuous monitoring during diagnostic procedures, post-procedural recovery, and strict adherence to infectious disease and safety protocols. This role also supports hospital operations through documentation, workflow coordination, and the training and mentoring of learners and new staff.</div><div><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities:<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Advanced Patient Care &amp; Anesthesia Support</strong></div><div>Provide comprehensive anesthetic and peri-anesthetic care, including pre-anesthetic assessments, high-risk drug calculations, advanced monitoring, life-saving interventions, pain management, and recovery for small, large, and exotic species.</div><div><br></div><div><strong>Monitoring, Equipment &amp; Technical Proficiency</strong></div><div>Operate, interpret, and maintain advanced anesthetic and physiologic monitoring equipment and specialized patient care devices to ensure patient safety during diagnostic, and imaging procedures.</div><div><br></div><div><strong>Interdisciplinary Collaboration</strong></div><div>Coordinate care and communicate effectively with specialty services, clinicians, staff, and students across multiple hospital service areas to support complex cases..</div><div><br></div><div><strong>Education, Training &amp; Mentorship</strong></div><div>Orient, teach, and coach new staff, students, interns, and residents on anesthetic procedures, hospital policies, and patient care standards in a teaching-hospital environment.</div><div><br></div><div><strong>Documentation, Safety &amp; Compliance</strong></div><div>Accurately document patient care and treatments within hospital information systems while maintaining a clean, safe work environment and adhering to infectious disease protocols, OSHA, EHS, and radiation safety standards. Requires ability to lift up to 50 pounds, with or without reasonable accommodations.</div> 192001 - CVM Teaching Hospital https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/217458 2025-04-17T15:30:10-04:00 2025-04-17T15:30:10-04:00 Area Specialized Extension Agent-(MIMB) More in My Basket Regional Outreach Facilitator-East <div>Are you looking for a rewarding career that will let you give back to your community? NC State Extension extends research-based knowledge to all North Carolinians, helping them transform science into everyday solutions that improve their lives and grow our state. We are the model of excellence in non-formal, research-based education that advances agriculture, the environment, human health and well-being, youth and communities, while creating prosperity for all North Carolinians. Working for NC State Extension allows you to teach without being tied to a classroom and school bell. Check out this <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3MptxITpJ54&amp;t=3s\"><strong>video</strong></a> on what it’s like to work for NC State Extension.<br><br></div><div>No two days in Extension are ever the same, there is always something new! Extension Agents are active in their communities and often need to meet the clients when and where they are available. This is not your typical desk job!<br><br></div><div>While our employees are located in NC County offices, they reap the benefits of employment at NC State University. Some of our benefits include:<br><br></div><div>· Paid time off<br><br></div><div>· Paid tuition<br><br></div><div>· Health Insurance<br><br></div><div>· Flexible Scheduling<br><br></div><div>· Retirement Savings<br><br></div><div>· Supplemental Benefits Programs<br><br></div><div>· WolfPerks!<br><br></div><div>You Belong Here! At NC State, our goal is for all employees to reach their fullest potential at work. As you consider this opportunity, we encourage you to review our <a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>Employee Value Proposition</strong></a> (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/</strong></a>) and learn more about what makes NC State the best place to learn and work for everyone.<br><br></div><div>The Regional Outreach Facilitator (ROF) is an integral part of the NC Cooperative Extension and the More in My Basked program. The ROF is housed in Carteret County Extension Center and works under the guidance and direct supervision of Shawn Banks, Carteret County Extension Director and Dr. Carolyn Bird, Professor and Principle Investigator/Program Director.<br><br>The applicant will serve as the Regional Outreach Facilitator with the following requirements:<br><br>· Collaborate with and support Extension Agent outreach in eight primary eastern counties: Carteret, Columbus, Duplin, Jones, New Hanover, Onslow, Wayne, and Wilson.</div><div>· Liaison with and provide the MIMB campus-based team with information on specialized needs for the region.</div><div>· Organize and/or conduct regional agent update meetings with MIMB agents.</div><div>· Collaborate with eastern Extension agents, including those neighboring the primary counties, to engage with the More In My Basket Program, for example, to;</div><ul><li>identify organizations serving low-resource audiences to expand partnerships</li><li>build and extend community relationships with organizations</li><li>participate in the delivery of workshop and booth outreach events</li><li>facilitate SNAP eligibility screening and application completion assistance through connection with campus program team</li><li>organize and/or conduct regional information meetings and outreach to neighboring counties not currently participating in MIMB</li></ul><div>· Serve as liaison between county program staff (Cooperative Extension Agents and CEDs) and NCSU program staff for any programmatic issues.</div><div>· Provide day-to-day management of the ROF planned participation in outreach events.</div><div>· Provide on-site support to county staff for outreach activities individually or in collaboration with other Program Staff.</div><div>· Facilitate program evaluation by assisting with curricula evaluation during MIMB workshops and sending completed forms to the campus team.<br>· Support primary Eastern and surrounding counties’ outreach through the development of community partnerships and participate in community coalition and related meetings to advance program outreach venues.<br>· Collaborate with the MIMB campus team to include the Outreach Strategy and Planning Manager, Program Coordinator, Communications and Social Media staff, Bilingual Outreach Facilitator, and Resource Navigator.<br>· Contribute to the campus partnership database through regular reports to support relationships and ongoing communication with collaborators to facilitate outreach events.<br>· Refer local clients to the campus team for FNS/SNAP benefit screenings and application completion assistance.<br>· Other duties as assigned.</div><div><br>The program, More In My Basket (MIMB), promotes and facilitates the delivery of outreach education, information, and programming to the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP) eligible population. A key role of the Regional Outreach Facilitator-East is to promote and facilitate the delivery of outreach information and programming to the SNAP-eligible population. You will work with Extension agents in the Family and Consumer Sciences and 4-H Youth Development programs as well as partnering agencies to coordinate and support a multi-county education program targeted to these populations. The position requires that you be self-initiating, as well as a collaborator, able to multi-task effectively in a complex educational/academic environment, and be skilled in managing its schedule of participation in events/meetings.</div> 116101 - County Program Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233987 2026-08-19T12:58:58-04:00 2026-08-19T12:58:59-04:00 Research Administrator <div><strong>Essential Job Duties<br></strong><br>As part of the College’s research administration team, The Research Administrator plays an important role in supporting faculty and ensuring the effective management of sponsored projects. The position provides financial and administrative support throughout the full award lifecycle, from proposal development and submission through award management, reporting, and closeout. By partnering with faculty and university stakeholders, this role helps ensure sponsored projects are managed effectively and in compliance with university, state, federal, and sponsor requirements.</div><div><br><br></div><div><strong>Proposal Development and Submission<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Prepare, compile, and submit grant and contract proposals for assigned faculty through NC State University systems, including RED, and sponsor electronic submission portals such as Grants.gov, Research.gov, and EERE Exchange.</li><li>Collaborate with Principal Investigators (PIs) to develop proposal budgets, budget justifications, and other required documents in accordance with university and sponsor requirements.</li><li>Create and maintain RED records for faculty and coordinate with campus offices to facilitate timely and accurate proposal submissions.</li><li>Provide guidance and training to faculty within the assigned portfolio regarding NC State business processes and requirements for proposal development and submission.</li><li>Review proposal documentation for completeness, accuracy, and compliance with applicable university and sponsor guidelines.</li><li>Serve as a resource to faculty throughout the proposal process, identifying potential issues and coordinating resolutions with appropriate university offices.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Award Management<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Manage an assigned portfolio of sponsored projects by monitoring financial activity, expenditures, budgets, and projections and providing Principal Investigators (PIs) with timely information to support effective project and financial decision-making.</li><li>Provide financial guidance and oversight by applying accounting principles, sponsor requirements, and university policies to budgeting, forecasting, personnel expenses, cost allocations, and other fiscal activities.</li><li>Partner with PIs throughout the award lifecycle by conducting regular financial reviews, discussing project spending and future plans, identifying potential issues, and recommending appropriate actions to keep projects on track.</li><li>Coordinate award modifications and approvals by preparing and managing project modification requests, including rebudgeting, no-cost extensions, changes in PI, equipment purchases, travel, pre-award costs, and other actions requiring university or sponsor approval.</li><li>Manage subcontracts and subawards throughout their lifecycle, including setup, monitoring, reporting, compliance, and closeout, while coordinating with PIs, university offices, and sponsoring agencies as needed.</li><li>Ensure financial and regulatory compliance by monitoring sponsored project activity, reviewing personnel and financial expenditures, supporting effort certification, and addressing issues in accordance with federal, state, university, and sponsor requirements.</li><li>Coordinate sponsored project closeout by resolving outstanding financial and administrative matters, completing final reconciliations, obtaining required approvals, maintaining project records, and coordinating timely submission of final reports and documentation.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Grant Reporting<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Provide financial reports, budget analysis, and consultation to PIs to support effective fiscal management of sponsored projects.</li><li>Assist PIs and project teams with sponsor-required financial and progress reporting, ensuring reports are accurate, complete, and submitted by established deadlines.</li><li>Monitor and manage cost-share commitments and reporting, including maintaining appropriate supporting documentation and ensuring expenditures are accurately tracked and reported.</li><li>Reconcile project financial activity and provide supporting documentation for sponsor and university reporting requirements.</li><li>Respond to questions from faculty and project stakeholders regarding project financial status, reporting requirements, and allowable expenditures.</li><li>Maintain accurate and complete financial records and documentation to support reporting, audits, and compliance requirements.<br><br></li></ul><div><br>If you are a detail-oriented professional who enjoys working with faculty, managing complex projects, and using financial expertise to support research, we’d love to hear from you.</div> 150101 - College of Natural Resources https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/226707 2026-01-22T09:42:13-05:00 2026-01-22T09:42:14-05:00 Temporary Summer Camp Staff - Reidsville, NC <div>Are you ready to make a positive impact and create lasting memories in a fun-filled setting? Betsy-Jeff Penn 4-H Center is seeking responsible and enthusiastic individuals for the Summer 2026 season and we want YOU to join our team!<br><br>Situated on 220 beautiful acres in Reidsville, NC, we offer traditional, overnight, summer camp experiences to campers ages 8-17 and serve over 800 youth from a wide range of communties each summer. Camp activities offered include: archery, arts &amp; crafts, canoeing, challenge course, climbing wall, environmental education, goat husbandry, swimming, and more!</div><div>As part of our team, you’ll help foster a safe and welcoming environment for campers to explore new activities, form friendships, and especially have fun! Qualified candidates will possess patience, compassion, responsibility, active listening skills and adaptability. If you’ve always wanted to serve as a positive role-model while fostering your personal and professional development, apply to join our Summer 2026 team today!<br><br>Benefits:</div><div>● Starting pay of $300-$425 (varies depending on position), paid out bi-monthly</div><div>● Housing provided for duration of employment in gendered cabin spaces</div><div>● Meals/snacks provided during camp sessions (Sunday - Friday)</div><div><strong>Additional Information – <br><br></strong>We are seeking the following positions for Summer 2026:<br><br></div><div>● Cabin Counselors</div><div>● Adventure Program Counselors</div><div>● Activity Counselors (Areas may include: Arts &amp; Crafts, Archery, Climbing Wall/Challenge Course, STEM)</div><div>● Lifeguards</div><div>● Aquatics Coordinator</div><div>● Healthcare Staff</div><div>● Lead Staff (Prior camp experience required)<br><br></div><div>Brief position details listed below:<br><br></div><div><strong>Cabin Counselors (Starting Pay $300/week):<br></strong>Responsible for the daily care and supervision of campers aged 8-12. Cabin groups are paired together during daily programming to make a co-ed activity group. Primary job duties of cabin counselors include, but are not limited to: group supervision 24/6 (day and night), group management, encouraging positive relationship building among campers, behavior management, and facilitation of basic programming. <br><br><strong>Adventure Program Counselors (Starting Pay $325/week):<br></strong>Adventure Program Counselors facilitate our outdoor living and outdoor adventure programming tailored for campers aged 12-14. Primary duties of this position include those of the Cabin Counselor description, as well as participating in overnight tent-camping experiences and off-site river tubing/rock climbing trips led by the Adventure Coordinator. Adventure counselors will participate in American Red Cross Lifeguard training, site-specific High Ropes training, and outdoor climbing training at Pilot Mountain (all training provided by camp).<br><br></div><div><strong>Activity Counselors (Starting Pay $300/week):<br></strong>Activity Counselors instruct our specialized programing and manage the various activity areas around camp. Specialized activities may include: Arts &amp; Crafts, Archery, Climbing Wall/Challenge Course, and STEM classes. Primary responsibilities include providing activity instruction, ensuring participant safety, management of equipment and supplies, program area set-up/break-down, basic curriculum development (with assistance from Director), various support tasks around camp, and camper supervision during activities and overnight in cabins.<br><br></div><div><strong>Lifeguards (Starting Pay $300/week):<br></strong>Lifeguards run all aquatic programming at camp. Lifeguards work in groups of 2-3 to ensure participant safety, prepare activity areas, lead program instruction, maintain equipment/areas, perform support tasks around camp, and participate in camper supervision during activities and overnight in cabins. All lifeguards must hold a current certification provided by a nationally recognized certifying body (American Red Cross Lifeguard class will be provided during training). To ensure continued competence in water rescue and resuscitation techniques, lifeguard certified staff participate in regular in-service trainings.<br><br></div><div><strong>Aquatics Coordinator (Starting Pay $425/week):<br></strong>The Aquatics coordinator responsibilities include those of the Lifeguard position, as well as: management of the lifeguard team, ensuring smooth flow of the aquatics activity schedule, completing daily equipment checks, communication with upper leadership, participating in and/or leading in-service trainings, performing support tasks, and overnight camper supervision. Current lifeguard certification (or willingness to become certified) and at least 1 year prior relevant experience required.<br><br></div><div><strong>Healthcare Staff (Starting Pay $425/week):<br></strong>Our Healthcare Staff manage the day to day general health and wellbeing of our campers and staff. Primary responsibilities include working with the Camp Nurse, organizing medical forms, sorting/administering medications, providing minor first aid, responding to emergency medical situations, and being “on-call” at all times campers are on site. Must be currently certified in First Aid/CPR/AED (American Red Cross First Aid/CPR/AED training provided). <em>Wilderness First Aid certification, EMT, CNA, or other comparable certifications are a plus.&nbsp;<br></em><br></div><div><strong>Lead Staff - Various positions (Starting Pay $425/week):<br></strong>Lead staff perform administrative, camper/staff management, and support tasks around camp. All lead staff are expected to facilitate positive team dynamics, assist with set-up and breakdown of various activity areas, communicate professionally and effectively with staff and directors, and serve as positive role models for the Summer Staff Team.<br><br></div><div><strong>Work Schedule – </strong>Approximate employment from <strong><em>May 29th, 2026 – August 7th, 2026</em></strong>. Actual start dates may vary based on position. Most work weeks begin around noon on Sunday and end around 2pm on Friday. You will be with campers at all times from Sunday through Friday (except during scheduled breaks), and are <strong>required to live on site Sunday-Friday</strong>. Applicants will live in gendered cabin spaces while supervising groups of campers. <br><br><strong>Minimum Experience/Education – </strong>Applicants must be 18 years of age or older by their work start date, possess a positive attitude, professionalism, compassion, love for sharing knowledge with children, and the ability to follow directions and work independently.</div> 112412 - Betsy-Jeff Penn 4-H Camp https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234504 2026-09-02T08:08:42-04:00 2026-09-02T08:08:42-04:00 UTS - Temporary Part-time Business Manager at UNC Chapel Hill <div>The department is seeking an experienced, detail-oriented Part-Time Business Manager to support the financial and administrative operations of our quasi-state agency. This position is central to the fiscal integrity of our contract/grant-funded programs and the long-term financial health of the organization.<br><br>Core responsibilities include contract/grant financial reporting, accounts payable and receivable, and developing budgets for contract/grant applications. We are particularly interested in candidates with hands-on experience navigating North Carolina state agency contract processes and reporting and non-profit financial management. Familiarity with NC state government reporting requirements and contract administration is a significant asset in this role. This is a part-time position of approximately 20 hours per week. A flexible schedule is available, and<br>hours can be structured to accommodate the candidate’s availability.</div> 480502 - UHR UTS/UNC Partnership https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233804 2026-08-13T11:59:59-04:00 2026-08-13T12:00:00-04:00 Sponsored Research Administrator <div>The College of Engineering is looking to hire multiple Sponsored Research Administrators (SRA) within the newly established college-managed operating model, responsible for orchestrating both pre-award and post-award lifecycles for an assigned departmental faculty portfolio. While the incumbent is physically co-located within a department to provide high-touch, localized customer service, the position is an integral part of the centralized College ORA team.</div><div><br>The primary purpose of this role is to apply advanced accounting principles, federal/state regulations, and university guidelines to ensure the fiscal and administrative integrity of all sponsored projects. Operating under this modernized model, the incumbent must balance localized portfolio execution with central compliance directives, actively participating in college-wide resource-sharing and standardized service-level agreements (SLAs).<br><br></div><div><strong>Detailed Description of Core Responsibilities<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Pre-Award Portfolio Management &amp; Proposal Development</strong></div><ul><li>Localized Faculty Consultation: Partner directly with PIs in the embedded unit to develop accurate, compliant proposal budgets and detailed justifications from initial concept through final submission.</li><li>Standardized Regulatory Screening: Analyze complex agency Requests for Proposals (RFPs) and Funding Opportunity Announcements (FOAs) to ensure strict alignment with F&amp;A rules, cost-sharing restrictions, budget caps, and direct cost allowability, reducing front-end submission errors.</li><li>Institutional System Management: Fully execute and route records within institutional software suites, including RED Proposal Development, ensuring data integrity is validated at the departmental level before final central routing.</li><li>Electronic Research Administration (eRA): Build, format, and package proposal submissions within varied sponsor portals (Research.gov, GRANTS.gov, eRA Commons, ASSIST), acting as the localized technical expert for the department's faculty.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Post-Award Financial Management, Forecasting &amp; Closeout</strong></div><ul><li>Award Onboarding &amp; Budget Translation: Review select incoming award notices alongside the PI to establish accurate internal ledger setups, ensuring F&amp;A is calculated correctly based on MTDC or TDC parameters.</li><li>Compliance Monitoring &amp; Risk Mitigation: Perform continuous financial analysis of active grant ledgers. Monitor expenditures for accuracy, allowability, and strict compliance with Cost Accounting Standards (CAS), Uniform Guidance, state rules, and university policies.</li><li>Projections &amp; Planning Models: Build multi-year spending projections and variance analysis models for faculty to optimize fund utilization, prevent over-expenditures, and manage labor distributions/personnel charges.</li><li>Reconciliations &amp; Cost-Share Tracking: Audit human resource expenditures, travel vouchers, and procurement charges against grant terms. Track, document, and report mandatory or voluntary committed cost-sharing obligations.</li><li>Lifecycle Closeouts: Execute comprehensive closeouts for expiring grant segments. Prepare and process Project Modification Requests (PMRs) for budget modifications, no-cost extensions, or residual balance distributions, ensuring audit-ready documentation is permanently archived.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Model Alignment, Cross-Coverage &amp; Leadership</strong></div><ul><li>Matrixed Team Collaboration: Actively participate in the broader College ORA network to ensure alignment with university-wide research administration standards. Navigate the dual priorities of central College compliance policy and local departmental workflow.</li><li>Cross-College Workload Balancing: Participate in the new model's agile resource framework, willingly stepping in to provide temporary pre- or post-award coverage for other COE departments during peak submission cycles or staff absences to maintain college-wide continuity.</li><li>Faculty Onboarding &amp; Training: Develop and deliver targeted resources, onboarding guides, or info-sessions for new faculty and departmental business officers regarding centralized ORA standard operating procedures and financial management tools.</li></ul> 143301 - Engineering Research Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/225113 2025-11-20T16:48:46-05:00 2025-11-20T16:48:47-05:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>The Department of Forestry and Environmental Resources has a wide variety of strengths in forestry and natural resources management, policy and economics, environmental science and technology, wildlife and conservation biology, tree improvement, silviculture, forest biotechnology, and international forestry. The Department has over 90 full time faculty and staff, and more than 750 undergraduate and 150 graduate students. Faculty, staff, and students work together with business, industry, government, community groups, and non-government organizations to address a wide range of challenges facing our state and the world. Partnerships include Camcore, the Forest Productivity Cooperative (FPC), the Southern Forest Resource Assessment Consortium (SOFAC), and the Tree Improvement Program (TIP).These partnerships facilitate research and discovery, teaching and learning, and outreach and service. Additional information about the department can be found at<strong> </strong><a href=\"http://cnr.ncsu.edu/fer\"><strong>http://cnr.ncsu.edu/fer</strong></a><strong>.</strong></div><div><br></div><div>The College of Natural Resources (CNR) at North Carolina State University is a world leader in the sustainable use of natural resources. The college’s comprehensive approach to teaching, research, and service encompass the many and varied ways in which people’s physical, economic, and social needs are met through the wise use of natural resources.</div><div><br><strong>Primary purpose of the position:<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Designing and conducting experiments in the areas of<ol><li>Innovations in delivery of CRISPR components to tree cells,</li><li>Innovations in regeneration technologies in tree tissue culture,</li><li>Molecular analysis of edited samples,</li><li>Phenotypic characterization of trees with improved traits, including productivity, adaptation, wood quality, and resilience.</li></ol></li><li>Conducting independent and collaborative studies, analyze and interpret data, and communicate results through reports, presentations, and publications in peer-reviewed scientific journals.</li><li>Exploring fundamental questions in genome editing while contributing to practical outcomes—developing technologies that accelerate the improvement of traits of interest in forest trees.</li></ul><div><br></div><div>This role is ideally suited for curious and motivated scientists with experience in plant research, who thrive on problem-solving, experimentation, and contributing to both basic and applied advances in plant biotechnology.</div> 150401 - Forestry & Environmental Resources Academic Research https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234403 2026-08-31T12:57:28-04:00 2026-08-31T12:57:28-04:00 Ticket Office Operations Assistant <div>The NC State Athletics Ticket Office is a division of the Athletic Department at NC State University and is responsible for all ticket operations at sporting events hosted by NC State Athletics. Ticket operations include the following sports: football, men’s and women’s basketball, volleyball, baseball, softball, wrestling, and gymnastics. Ticket Office employees will be responsible for providing assistance to the ticket office and its customers on game days. They will be handling customer inquiries, the distribution of event tickets, and other tasks as needed.<br><br>Part-time Ticket Office employees will learn how to use the Paciolan Ticketing System for walk-up sales on the day of the game. Part-time employees will work home NC State sports events. Work schedules are flexible and can be coordinated with the part-time employee supervisor.</div> 105002 - Athletics - Internal/External Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/229042 2026-04-01T12:54:44-04:00 2026-04-01T12:54:45-04:00 Fire/Life Safety Special Events - EMT <div>Respond to calls for assistance that involve medical and/or injury at special events to include sporting events. Respond to fire/ haz-mat related incidents as requested, mitigate bio-hazards as required, assist advanced life support personnel as needed, complete all paperwork required / requested by the University Fire Marshal.</div><div><strong>Please note this position is as needed and primarily utilized for home football games.</strong></div> 427201 -  Fire and Life Safety https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231844 2026-06-18T11:40:49-04:00 2026-06-18T11:40:50-04:00 Advanced Manufacturing & Service Center Laboratory Specialist <div>50% of the position will involve working with Senior Design laboratory and Research Fabrication Shop in the department. Primary focus will involve manufacturing machining equipment. Position will be expected to fabricate specialized components or devices for use in research or course instruction.<br><br></div><div>The range of duties includes, but is not limited to: primary operation of water jet; assist Laboratory Manager with ME 416 and ME 200 class as needed; assist Research Fabrication Facility Supervisor with fabrication of research projects that require machine work; assist Research Fabrication Facility Supervisor with all CNC programming, set-up and operation of all CNC machines in MAE Department’s Research Fabrication Facility and the ME Senior Design Laboratory; assist in the consultation with researchers and faculty concerning materials, design etc. ; assist with the laser cutter and 3D printing: laboratory audits; MAE annual inventory certification and assist building liaison.<br><br></div><div>Laboratory safety is an important element of this position. Work requires knowledge of advanced manufacturing concepts and design, precision machining, 3D modelling and CNC machine operation, manufacturing practices and procedures, laboratory operations, regulatory and safety requirements, and planning. Ability to read and interpret engineering drawings and required knowledge of CNC (laser cutter, waterjet, lathes, milling machines) and additive manufacturing equipment, as well as knowledge of reverse engineering using laser scanners. Ability to read and interpret engineering drawings and required knowledge of calipers, micrometers and laser scanning arm. Responsible for lab safety training for all new graduate students and training undergraduate and graduate students on machines. Performing work in a manner so as to provide proper safety conditions to all personnel and property involved.<br><br></div><div>50% of the job will be operation of the hot press facility that is part of Service Center. The job involves:<br><br></div><ul><li>Set up and operate machine</li><li>Maintain logs on machine use</li><li>Collaborate with Environmental Health and Safety to develop and maintain a lab safety plan</li><li>Develop machine protocols to ensure the safety of operators and equipment</li><li>Train new users on machine operation</li><li>Supervise new users as necessary on machine operation</li><li>Assist machine users and customers with recipe and process development</li><li>Assist machine users with accessing the machine</li><li>Work with users and customers to troubleshoot issues</li><li>Perform routine maintenance on machine</li><li>Maintain logs on machine maintenance</li><li>Maintain a safe and clean environment</li><li>Coordinate with OEM to complete repairs as necessary</li><li>Use assistive devices to load and unload heavy samples</li><li>Maintain appropriate levels of machine consumables on hand to ensure machine readiness at all times</li><li>Deliver customer needs while testing using the hot press</li></ul> 140901 - Mechanical & Aerospace Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233380 2026-07-31T09:00:17-04:00 2026-07-31T09:00:18-04:00 Clinical Pathology Residency <div>The College of Veterinary Medicine at North Carolina State University invites outstanding candidates to apply for a 3-year residency position in veterinary clinical pathology beginning July 2027. The training program focuses on in-depth learning of clinical pathology and ACVP board preparation under the supervision of 4 ACVP board-certified clinical pathologists.&nbsp;<br><br>The resident will join a busy clinical pathology service and play a role in student education and hospital interactions. In addition, there are local veterinary diagnostic labs and the graduate programs at the CVM draw on abundant resources available in the Research Triangle region (universities, industry, and government) to provide unique research and career opportunities. Applicants must possess a DVM or equivalent degree(s), have an excellent academic record, strong communication skills, and be committed to a career in veterinary clinical pathology. Clinical or a post-DVM experience is desirable.<br><br>NC State University is near two other major universities (Duke University and the University of North Carolina - Chapel Hill) and the Research Triangle Park, which includes multiple government agencies, non-profit institutes, biotechnology, and large pharmaceutical companies.<br>To view full vacancy announcement, additional requirements, and to apply online, please visit https://jobs.ncsu.edu/<br><br>See https://cvm.ncsu.edu/education/residencies/residencies-clinical-pathology/ for program description and additional information. Applications should include (1) a curriculum vitae, (2) statement of career goals, (3) official college and professional school transcripts, and (4) three references (see link to form). We will begin reviewing applications on Oct. 15, 2026, and continue until a suitable candidate is found. The position is available July 15, 2027.&nbsp;<br><br>The following link is for the reference form which will be in place of traditional letters. Please provide this link to your references https://forms.gle/Mm7Sgb2vBiwhNWZ79<br>Questions related to the application process can be directed to Caitlin Falls in CVM Human Resources. Reference forms and transcripts can be sent to her via email or regular mail.</div> 196001 - Department of Population Health and Pathobiology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223245 2025-09-25T10:57:04-04:00 2025-09-25T10:57:05-04:00 Temporary-Professional NonFaculty <div>This posting is for the hiring of a variety of Professional non-faculty positions for short-term hire's, that are needed in various areas of specific topics on an as-needed basis in the department. . Areas include Laboratory research and support for academic course development. This position stays open year-round to assist in various as-needed temporary hires.&nbsp;</div> 140901 - Mechanical & Aerospace Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234120 2026-08-25T09:40:01-04:00 2026-08-25T09:40:02-04:00 Equine and Farm Animal Attendant <div>The <strong>Equine and Farm Animal Attendant</strong> plays a critical role in providing exceptional daily care and husbandry support for all patients at the Equine and Farm Animal Veterinary Center. From handling varied large animal species to maintaining strict sanitary standards, this position ensures a clean, safe, and supportive healing environment for our patients and clinical team.</div><div><strong><br>Key Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Patient Care &amp; Husbandry:</strong><ul><li>Provide daily feeding, watering, grooming, and handling for patient species including equine, bovine, caprine, ovine, and camelids.</li><li>Safely restrain animals to assist clinical staff during treatments and examinations.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Hospital Sanitation &amp; Biosecurity:</strong><ul><li>Maintain a clean and hygienic hospital environment by strictly following established protocols.</li><li>Perform stall cleaning, deep cleaning, disinfection of clinical areas, and regular trash removal.</li><li>Strictly adhere to all Infectious Disease and biosecurity protocols.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Facility &amp; Equipment Maintenance:</strong><ul><li>Maintain indoor and outdoor hospital grounds as needed.</li><li>Monitor supplies and equipment, assisting with routine maintenance and repair schedules.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Operations &amp; Operations Support:</strong><ul><li>Assist with inventory control, supply ordering, and purchasing.</li><li>Collaborate and maintain effective communication with hospital departments and ancillary services.</li><li>Participate in an on-call schedule, including weekend and holiday rotations as required.</li></ul></li></ul><div><strong><br>Qualifications &amp; Expectations</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Experience:</strong> Prior experience handling and caring for large animals (equine, livestock, etc.) in a veterinary, farm, or clinical setting preferred.</li><li><strong>Physical Stamina:</strong> Ability to perform physically demanding tasks (lifting, walking, bending, stall cleaning) in varying weather conditions.</li><li><strong>Skills:</strong> Strong communication skills, high attention to detail, and a team-first mindset.</li><li><strong>Availability:</strong> Flexibility to participate in evening, weekend, holiday, and on-call rotations.</li></ul> 192001 - CVM Teaching Hospital https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/227803 2026-02-25T15:40:02-05:00 2026-02-25T15:40:03-05:00 Instructor/Tutor - 21st Century Community Learning Center <div><strong>The Division of Academic &amp; Student Affairs</strong> (DASA) is where students discover connections and community. It is where students live, where they gather, where they improve themselves, each other, and their university. DASA is where students seek guidance and support, both in and outside of the classroom. The division serves all students at NC State through three integrated components: academic affairs, student affairs, and University College.</div><div><br></div><div>NC State wants employees to achieve their highest potential at work, learn more about our employee value proposition here (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>You Belong Here</strong></a>). The Division of Academic and Student Affairs is the place to work for values-driven faculty and staff who are passionate about promoting the success of the whole student.</div><div><br>The TRIO Programs, including <strong>21st Century Community Learning Center</strong>, provide academic enrichment support to low-income, first generation students. Collectively all programs serve students from middle school through college. The mission of the NCSU programs is to provide equal access to quality education for participants who have academic potential to succeed in college and to encourage and motivate participants to enroll and graduate from college.</div><div><br>The focus of the <strong>Instructor/Tutor</strong> is to provide instruction and one-to-one or group tutorial services to 21st Century Community Learning Center students.</div><div><br></div><div>The Instructor/Tutor will be responsible for the following:</div><ul><li>Assisting participants in academic skill building for core curricula courses, especially rigorous curriculum courses</li><li>Conduct or secure facilitators for Special Labs, which may include instruction in ACT test prep, STEM exposure and instrument, workforce development skills, family enrichment activities, etc.</li></ul> 240405 - TRIO Pre-College https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234605 2026-09-03T10:23:12-04:00 2026-09-03T10:23:13-04:00 Student Success Navigators  for Elevate (IPSE) <div><strong>Note: This position is open only to currently enrolled NC State students who are in good academic standing.</strong><br><br><strong><br>Primary purpose of this position:<br></strong>NC State’s inclusive postsecondary education program for students with intellectual and developmental disabilities, Elevate is seeking a motivated and reliable Student Success Navigator to support in study halls, courses to promote self-determined learning among Elevate students. This role provides the opportunity to support students individually and in small groups in developing skills such as time management, navigation, and assignment completion.<br><br></div><div><strong><br>Job Responsibilities:<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Provide 5 to 15 hours per week of support, including academic and social mentoring responsibilities</li><li>Provide individualized academic support in study sessions and Elevate program or inclusive Courses</li><li>Provide individualized support during Elevate’s Living Learning class or workshops</li><li>Assist students with navigation, assignment planning, and time management, including use of Google Suite tools and other mobile applications</li><li>Support Elevate students with independent living, social goal, and academic goal attainment including attending events/ activities and facilitating event/ activity registration</li><li>Document activities completed with Elevate students, as instructed by Elevate staff</li><li>Participate in trainings led by Social or Academic Peer Coaches or Elevate program staff</li><li>Clearly communicate availability and provide adequate notice if scheduling conflicts arise</li><li>Opportunities to serve as a job coach for on campus student employment internship(s)<br><br></li></ul><div><strong><br>Eligibility Requirements:<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Must be an undergraduate or graduate student currently enrolled at NC State University</li><li>Strong academic, organizational, and interpersonal skills</li><li>Must have a passion for inclusion</li><li>Proficiency with Google Suite tools and digital scheduling and communication mechanisms used by the Elevate program<br><br></li></ul><div><br></div> 131103 - IPSE https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230772 2026-05-19T10:25:25-04:00 2026-05-19T10:25:25-04:00 UTS - Temporary Environmental Services at NC State <div>Thank you for applying with University Temporary Services! Please note, this application is the first step in the process of being considered for Environmental Services roles at NC State. This is not a specific position but rather, an application showing your skillset and interest in becoming a candidate for consideration with UTS.<br><br>Main responsibilities:<br>• Performing routine manual housekeeping work while cleaning various areas across campus.<br>• Cleaning, mopping, scrubbing, sweeping, and vacuuming floors, as well as stairwells and hallways.<br>• Cleaning of lavatories, washing windows, moving furniture to thoroughly clean areas, and collecting and removing trash.<br><br>This is an ongoing recruitment.</div> 480501 - UHR Temporary Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232812 2026-07-16T13:25:23-04:00 2026-07-16T13:25:24-04:00 Surgery Staff Supervisor <div>The Surgical &amp; Sterile Services Manager is a mission-critical leadership role responsible for the operational success of our surgical theaters across the Randall B. Terry Jr. Companion Animal Medical Center and the Equine and Farm Animal Veterinary Center.</div><div>This role requires a sophisticated balance of administrative oversight, clinical expertise, and educational leadership. You will lead a multidisciplinary team of Technicians and Sterile Processing specialists, ensuring the highest standards of care for both elective and complex, non-elective procedures across a vast range of species.</div><div><br><strong>Core Responsibilities:<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Operations &amp; Personnel Leadership</strong></div><ul><li>Strategic Oversight: Manage daily workflow, case assignments, and room readiness. You serve as the primary liaison between specialty services and anesthesia providers to ensure seamless patient transitions.</li><li>Talent Management: Oversee the full lifecycle of staff employment, including recruitment, performance evaluations, and professional development.</li><li>Logistics: Coordinate complex 24/7 staffing models, managing on-call rotations, PTO, and student schedules to ensure uninterrupted operational readiness.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Clinical Education &amp; Technical Support</strong></div><ul><li>Instructional Leadership: Direct the training of new employees and DVM students in sterile technique, equipment operation, and SOPs.</li><li>Surgical Support: Provide technical guidance during complex interventions in specialties such as Neurology, Soft Tissue, Orthopedics, Ophthalmology, and Cardiology.</li><li>Advanced Knowledge: Maintain a high level of proficiency in drug calculations, intra-op medication administration, and multi-species surgical protocols.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Compliance, Safety &amp; Asset Management</strong></div><ul><li>Safety Authority: Serve as the Primary Laser Safety Officer for three medical hospitals, maintaining all safety certifications and leading biannual training.</li><li>Sterile Integrity: Partner with the Infectious Disease Committee to manage nosocomial culture programs and hospital-wide sterilization policies.</li><li>Capital Assets: Oversee the maintenance and vendor relationships for high-value surgical instrumentation, including portable C-ARMs, autoclaves, and cautery systems.</li></ul><div><br><strong>The Ideal Candidate</strong></div><div>We are seeking a high-level professional who excels in fast-paced, academically rigorous environments.</div><ul><li>Clinical Judgment: Ability to remain composed and exercise sound judgment in emotionally charged or high-stakes clinical situations.</li><li>Academic Commitment: A genuine interest in the mentorship and development of 4th-year veterinary students.</li><li>Autonomy: A self-starter capable of managing large-scale hospital systems with minimal supervision.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Environmental &amp; Physical Requirements</strong></div><ul><li>Ability to work in an environment involving sick animals, hazardous materials, and gas anesthetics.</li><li>Capability to lift up to 50 pounds and remain on-site beyond scheduled hours when surgical cases require extended support.</li></ul> 192001 - CVM Teaching Hospital https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234524 2026-09-02T10:32:35-04:00 2026-09-02T10:32:36-04:00 Associate Vice Chancellor, Integrated Strategic Communications <div><strong><em>This is a repost: Previous applicants need not reapply</em></strong><br><br>We are seeking a collaborative leader with significant experience leading strategic communications, media relations, and reputation management within complex, high-visibility organizations. This executive role serves as a university’s spokesperson and primary reputation promoter and guardian, shaping how NC State engages with news media, navigates complex issues, and translates research and institutional impact into clear public understanding.<br><br></div><div>This is a role where steady judgment, sound decision-making, and clear communication in fast-moving, high-pressure environments are essential. As a leader in this position, you will build trust through transparency and consistency when circumstances are evolving and visibility is high. You will foster alignment across internal partners, media, and executive stakeholders, ensuring clarity in moments of complexity and providing confident direction that strengthens institutional credibility and response.<br><br></div><div>Through a responsive, relationship-driven approach, you will strengthen the university’s readiness and performance in high-stakes environments. You will build and sustain a robust network of media relationships at the local, national, and global level—securing credible earned coverage that positions NC State as a trusted source of expertise and innovation. Overseeing Public Relations, Strategic Communications and Media, and Research Communications, you ensure the university’s “Think and Do” impact is communicated with clarity, coherence, and purpose.<br><br></div><div>Key responsibilities fall within the five core functional areas outlined below.<br><br></div><div><strong>Strategic Media Relations &amp; Brand Visibility</strong></div><ul><li>Build and sustain relationships with local, national, and international media to elevate NC State expertise and research.</li><li>Lead proactive storytelling that highlights student success, faculty achievement, and institutional and research impact.</li><li>Oversee news operations, ensuring timely, accurate, and high-quality news content.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Issues Management &amp; Crisis Communications</strong></div><ul><li>Lead crisis and issues management with clarity and composure in high-pressure situations.</li><li>Advise university leadership on emerging issues, reputational risk, and sensitive communications.</li><li>Coordinate cross-campus response to ensure aligned and consistent messaging during critical incidents.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Research Communications &amp; Impact Storytelling</strong></div><ul><li>Translate complex research into clear, compelling narratives that demonstrate public value.</li><li>Partner with research leadership to increase visibility in prestige, trade, and industry media.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Executive Communications &amp; Thought Leadership</strong></div><ul><li>Shape leadership messaging, including speeches, op-eds, and institutional narratives.</li><li>Align executive communications with broader reputation and media strategy to ensure consistency and trust.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Collaboration &amp; Culture</strong></div><ul><li>Collaborate and partner closely with the AVC for Brand and Integrated Strategic Operations and the AVC for Integrated Content Strategy</li><li>Develops and mentors team, with a strong focus on division-wide culture<br><br></li></ul><div>If you are a communications leader who thrives in complex, high-visibility environments and is motivated by shaping how a major institution is understood, trusted, and represented in the public sphere, we encourage you to apply.</div> 010101 - Chancellor's Office And Staff https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230300 2026-05-06T13:23:17-04:00 2026-05-07T08:59:54-04:00 Temporary Undergraduate Research Technician <div>The Temporary Undergraduate Research Technician will be engaged in various activities such as wet lab tasks, DNA extraction from fungal herbarium specimens. Proficiency in aseptic techniques is essential, as the new hire will be responsible for maintaining mycological cultures with precision. This involves tasks such as isolating RNA/DNA from mycological tissues, performing techniques like PCR and quantitative PCR, executing agarose gel electrophoresis, and conducting plant pathological assessments.</div> 111801 - Entomology and Plant Pathology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232519 2026-07-07T15:37:28-04:00 2026-07-07T15:37:29-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar - PHHI <div>The Postdoctoral Scholar will assist the Principal Investigator (Dr. Andrew Neilson) in the Plants for Human Health Institute by leading and conducting independent research (80%), along with some general laboratory management (10%), and some mentoring of graduate students as appropriate (10%).<br><br><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>The primary duties of this position will include conducting advanced independent and/or directed research with a focus on the interaction between plant-derived phytochemicals, the human gut microbiome and nutrition/health outcomes. Major research thrusts in the Neilson lab currently involve 1) utilizing phytochemicals to modulate production of gut microbiome-derived uremic toxins such as trimethylamine N-oxide (TMAO), phenylacetic acid, phenylacetylglutamine, and imidazole propionate, and 2) understanding individual genetic differences predict phytochemical bioavailability, metabolism, and efficacy for disease prevention, among others.</li><li>The majority of the work will be performed using 4 key research tools: 1) anaerobic fecal fermentations, 2) rodent models of nutrition/disease (including models of genetic variety), 3) mammalian and/or bacterial cell culture, and 4) instrumental analysis (particularly LC-MS/MS). This will include study design, collection and analysis of data, ensuring quality of data, basic statistics analysis, generating tables and figures for presentation (in manuscripts and conference presentations) as it relates to impacts on human health, participation in preparation and submission manuscripts, reports and grant proposals.</li><li>Other general duties will include general lab and instrument maintenance and management as well as other duties as assigned. In addition to performing assigned experiments, the successful candidate will contribute to the intellectual direction of the lab by identifying important research questions, formulating hypotheses and designing experiments (including adoption of new techniques) to test those hypotheses.</li><li>The candidate must have a demonstrated ability to work independently and creatively. She/He will provide support to peers, collaborative partners, and students, have good communication skills, and be able to articulate clearly the scientific and technical needs in the field, set clear goals, and work within an interdisciplinary setting.</li><li>The successful candidate will be expected to develop and submit (with guidance from the Principal Investigator) postdoctoral fellowship applications and other grant applications to sponsors such as USDA, NIH, etc. The successful candidate is expected to routinely publish their research in relevant peer-reviewed academic journals.</li></ul><div><br><br></div><div><strong>This position is located in Kannapolis, NC.</strong></div> 110308 - Kannapolis Research https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233552 2026-08-05T10:18:59-04:00 2026-08-05T10:19:00-04:00 Small Animal Internal Medicine (SAIM) Fellowship <div>The Small Animal Internal Medicine Service at the NC State Veterinary Hospital is seeking a highly motivated veterinarian for a <strong>one to two-year Small Animal Internal Medicine Fellowship</strong>. This program is specifically designed for individuals pursuing an <strong>academic career path</strong>, providing a bridge between residency training and a faculty role. Candidates will engage in advanced clinical service and specialized research training.<br><br><strong>Specialized Tracks:<br></strong>Applicants must select one of three core focus areas for their fellowship. Each track is designed to provide mastery in a research field while maintaining a strong foundation in general internal medicine:</div><ol><li><strong>Aerodigestive Disease</strong></li><li><strong>Vector-Borne Diseases</strong></li><li><strong>Education and Artificial Intelligence</strong></li></ol><div><strong>Program Structure &amp; Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Clinical Commitment:</strong> 25 weeks per year on the Small Animal Internal Medicine service.</li><li><strong>Specialized Training:</strong> Hands-on clinical and research experience directly tailored to your chosen track.</li><li><strong>Professional Development:</strong> Earn professional certificates in your specialty where available (such as Teaching Certificate for the Education track).</li><li><strong>Research:</strong> Completion of a research project suitable for publication in a peer-reviewed journal, with a focus on your chosen sub-specialty.</li></ul> 195001 - Department of Clinical Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223319 2025-09-25T11:56:18-04:00 2025-09-25T11:56:19-04:00 Open Rank Non-Tenure Track in Landscape Architecture & Environmental Planning <div>The Department of LAEP is accepting applications for part-time non-tenure-track faculty appointments for a one-semester or one-year appointment with the possibility of renewal. Rank to be determined based upon education and professional experience.</div><div><br></div><div>Specific teaching opportunities include landscape architecture and environmental planning studios at all levels, including advanced comprehensive studios and/or seminar courses.</div><div><br></div><div>Applications will be reviewed as these part-time non-tenure track teaching opportunities arise.</div> 120401 - Landscape Architecture https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234332 2026-08-28T15:21:34-04:00 2026-08-28T15:21:35-04:00 TRIO Chaperone <div><strong>The Division of Academic &amp; Student Affairs</strong> (DASA) is where students discover connections and community. It is where students live, where they gather, where they improve themselves, each other, and their university. DASA is where students seek guidance and support, both in and outside of the classroom. The division serves all students at NC State.</div><div><strong><br></strong>NC State wants employees to achieve their highest potential at work, learn more about our employee value proposition here (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>You Belong Here</strong></a>). The Division of Academic and Student Affairs is the place to work for values-driven faculty and staff who are passionate about promoting the success of the whole student.</div><div><br><strong>The Upward Bound Program</strong> is an early intervention pre-college program that helps students prepare for higher education. Upward Bound brings high school students to college campuses after school, on the weekends, and during the summer to receive instruction in mathematics, laboratory sciences, composition, literature, and foreign languages. Tutoring, counseling, mentoring, cultural enrichment, and work-study programs are additional components to the Upward Bound Program. The NC State University Program serves 186 students annually from 5 high schools (Vance, Louisburg, Bunn, Franklinton, and East Wake). The Program has been a part of the University since 1978 and has assisted hundreds of students to enroll in college and gain a college degree.<br><br>The Upward Bound Program is looking for Chaperones that will be responsible for monitoring the transportation of students to and from program activities, provide head counts for TRIO staff, and help program staff with students during activities.</div> 240405 - TRIO Pre-College https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234320 2026-08-28T11:14:01-04:00 2026-08-28T11:14:02-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>The Abolhasani Research Group in the Department of Chemical &amp; Biomolecular Engineering at North Carolina State University is seeking a Postdoctoral Research Scholar in microreaction engineering and automated flow chemistry as part of the NSF-funded SPEED Programmable Cloud Laboratory.<br><br>The successful candidate will develop automated continuous-flow and microfluidic experimental platforms for accelerated chemical and materials discovery. Research will combine microreaction engineering, multiphase transport, automated reaction screening, in-line/online analytical characterization, and closed-loop optimization.<br><br>Applications may include homogeneous catalysis, photocatalysis, nanoparticle and semiconductor materials synthesis, and other solution-phase chemical and materials systems relevant to SPEED.<br>Highly motivated candidates with experience in flow chemistry, microfluidics, reaction engineering, transport phenomena, catalysis, or automated chemical experimentation are encouraged to apply.<br><strong><br>Duties and Responsibilities include:</strong></div><ul><li>Design and develop automated continuous-flow and microfluidic reactor platforms.</li><li>Develop flow systems for homogeneous, gas-liquid, liquid-liquid, photochemical, and/or materials synthesis applications.</li><li>Integrate pumps, valves, mass-flow controllers, temperature/pressure control, and other process equipment.</li><li>Integrate flow reactors with in-line and online analytical characterization tools such as HPLC, GC, MS, UV-Vis, fluorescence, Raman, or IR spectroscopy.</li><li>Develop automated reaction-screening and optimization workflows.</li><li>Investigate mixing, mass transfer, heat transfer, residence-time distributions, kinetics, and other fundamental transport and reaction phenomena.</li><li>Interface flow platforms with SPEED automation, data-management, and AI/ML infrastructure.</li><li>Conduct high-throughput and closed-loop experimental campaigns.</li><li>Analyze experimental results and develop mechanistic and data-driven understanding of chemical systems.</li><li>Collaborate with chemists, materials scientists, automation engineers, and AI/ML researchers.</li><li>Prepare scientific manuscripts, presentations, technical documentation, and project reports.</li></ul><div><br><br></div> 140201 - Chemical & Biomolecular Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228622 2026-03-19T15:32:03-04:00 2026-03-19T15:32:05-04:00 CUP Maintenance Technician - Electrical <div>We are seeking a highly skilled <strong>Electrical Maintenance Technician</strong> to join our <strong>Central Utility Plants</strong> operations team. This position will perform a variety of specialized tasks to support the operations of our Central Utility Plants. These tasks involve testing, renovating, overhauling, replacing, and performing <strong>preventive maintenance</strong> on standard electrical systems, including electrical distribution panels, motors, soft starters, variable frequency drives, UPS, and emergency lighting systems across our five utility plants located on the Main campus, Centennial Campus, and Centennial Biomedical Campus. If you are a highly skilled facilities professional who takes pride in maintaining complex building systems and enjoys working in a collaborative, team-focused environment, we encourage you to apply. <strong>Apply today to be part of a team that keeps our campus running.</strong><br><br><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li><strong>Troubleshoot electrical issues</strong> on plant equipment such as chillers, boilers, combustion turbines, fuel gas compressors, and auxiliary equipment</li><li>Collaborate with inspectors, contractors, and department liaisons as required</li><li><strong>Repair and maintain electrical equipment</strong>, distribution systems, minor controls, and signal equipment</li><li>Inspect work sites, assessing material and tool requirements, and determining repair methods</li><li>Plan and execute conduit runs, wire pulls, and equipment assembly</li><li>Perform tasks such as replacing light bulbs, fixtures, ballasts, switches, motors, soft starts, etc.; removing and replacing defective wiring and circuit breakers; and<strong> balancing loads in electrical panels</strong></li><li>Install new <strong>electrical systems</strong> to support minor modifications</li><li>Maintain motors, electrical distribution systems, and auxiliary equipment</li><li><strong>Provide comprehensive and accurate information</strong> on planned/preventive maintenance items via the Facility Operations maintenance tracking system</li><li>Communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, and the general public, and follow verbal and written instructions related to work assignments and operational needs.</li><li>Perform lifting duties up to 60 pounds, with or without reasonable accommodations</li></ul> 445050 - Utilities and Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234706 2026-09-04T10:26:28-04:00 2026-09-04T10:26:29-04:00 UTS - Temporary Radiology Technician at NC State <div>The primary duties of this position include the following:</div><ul><li>Taking X-ray images of the human body per order by provider.</li><li>Interact with internal staff, patients, and referring providers through email, phone, and in-person communications.</li><li>Keeping equipment clean and in working order.</li><li>Responsible for maintaining equipment and quality control.</li><li>Attend scheduled meetings, drills, and training.</li><li>Perform technical procedures, including patient positioning for radiographs and digital image capturing.</li><li>Provide excellent patient care by applying full knowledge of anatomy, physiology, and disease processes.</li><li>Provide quick and accurate information regarding each patient, acting as a liaison between clinicians and radiologists.</li><li>Proficiently enter data into the electronic health record.</li><li>Maintain a clean working environment to ensure standards are followed and infection is not spread.</li><li>Handle medical, biological, and infectious waste appropriately.</li><li>Follow all radiation safety, OSHA, and Environmental Health and Safety standards.</li></ul> 480501 - UHR Temporary Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231485 2026-06-09T15:14:30-04:00 2026-06-09T15:14:31-04:00 Academic Advisor <div>The purposed of this position is to support the mechanical and aerospace engineering degree programs (including site-based programs) and educational mission with teaching, undergraduate advising, scheduling and registration, recruitment and communications. This position reports directly to the Director of Undergraduate Advising. <br><br>Duties include:<br><br><strong>Academic Advising &amp; Student Support — 45%</strong></div><ul><li>Serve as assigned advisor for MAE undergraduate students</li><li>Pre-registration advising (EFY and post-CODA students)</li><li>Course selection, academic requirements, and success strategies</li><li>Hold scheduled advising office hours</li><li>Assist with student registration and related paperwork</li></ul><div><strong>Advising Operations &amp; Process Improvement — 15%</strong></div><ul><li>Contribute to improvement of advising portals, materials, and processes</li><li>Provide support to advising office operations</li><li>Serve as a resource on academic policies and procedures</li></ul><div><strong>Student Engagement, Communication &amp; Events — 15%</strong></div><ul><li>Support departmental events (orientation, commencement, symposiums, info sessions)</li><li>Communicate opportunities, resources, and policies to students</li></ul><div><strong>Collaboration &amp; Team Contribution — 10%</strong></div><ul><li>Work collaboratively with MAE advising team</li><li>Cross-functional departmental support</li></ul><div><strong>Data, Reporting &amp; Accreditation Support — 10%</strong></div><ul><li>Assist with ABET data collection and reporting</li><li>Generate student/course reports for faculty and leadership</li></ul><div><strong>Scheduling &amp; Administrative Support — 5%</strong></div><ul><li>Provide backup support for course scheduling</li><li>Other administrative duties as assigned</li></ul> 140901 - Mechanical & Aerospace Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233074 2026-07-23T10:55:23-04:00 2026-07-23T10:55:24-04:00 CLAWS Fabrication Technician <div>The CLAWS Fabrication Technician will play a key role in supporting research and development activities related to III-V Power, RF, and Photonics device fabrication. The successful candidate will be responsible for hands-on execution of process flows, process module development, and data collection, contributing to the optimization of fabrication processes. This role requires a combination of practical skills in a cleanroom environment, analytical abilities, and a commitment to continuous improvement.<br><br></div><div><strong>Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Device Fabrication: Be responsible for hands-on execution of III-V Power, RF, and Photonics device fabrication, including full process flows, short flows, and unit processes.</li><li>Process Module Development and Optimization:<ul><li>Work with engineering staff to develop and optimize robust process modules including:<ul><li>Execute Design of Experiments (DOEs) for process optimization.</li><li>Identify opportunities for continuous improvement in fabrication processes.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Fabrication Methods: Establish hands-on proficiency in relevant fabrication methods, including but not limited to:<ul><li>Photolithography</li><li>Metal and dielectric deposition</li><li>Etching (wet and dry)</li><li>Wafer dicing</li></ul></li><li>Metrology and Data Analysis: Generate in-line metrology data for Statistical Process Control (SPC).</li><li>Documentation: Maintain accurate and detailed records of experiments, procedures, and results.</li><li>Equipment Operation and Maintenance: Operate and maintain process equipment, ensuring proper functionality and adherence to safety protocols.</li><li>Collaboration: Work closely with engineers, scientists, and other technicians to achieve research objectives.</li></ul> 140110 - CLAWS https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/220323 2025-06-30T15:16:20-04:00 2025-06-30T15:16:22-04:00 Lecturer <div>This position will teach one or more undergraduate or graduate course(s) in department.<br><br>Department of Computer Science is accepting applications for the position of Lecturer (part-time). Specific hiring needs for the academic year and summer sessions are yet to be determined. Applications will be kept on file should openings arise in subsequent semesters. The position is one-semester or one-year, non-tenure track appointments with possibility of renewal. This is for Summer 2025, Fall 2025, and Spring 2026 sessions.<br><br>Successful candidate(s) will teach one or two courses per semester.</div> 141901 - Computer Science Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233996 2026-08-19T14:15:29-04:00 2026-08-19T14:15:30-04:00 CLAWS Lithography Engineer <div>We are seeking a highly-skilled lithography engineer to join our team and contribute to the ownership for NNF’s suite of lithography capabilities. The ideal candidate will have deep, hands-on experience in all lithographic processes; including contact lithography tools, stepper/projection lithography tools, automated wafer coat/develop stations, and e-beam/laser direct-write systems.<br><br><strong>Process Research &amp; Development</strong></div><ul><li>Advanced Development: Lead the design and optimization of cutting-edge photolithography processes tailored for next-generation applications, specifically targeting wide-bandgap devices for RF, power electronics, and photonics.</li><li>Technical Consultation: Provide expert-level process integration support to internal and external users, translating complex device requirements into repeatable lithographic workflows.</li><li>Service Excellence: Execute high-precision service work for external customers, ensuring all deliverables meet rigorous industry standards for semiconductor fabrication.</li></ul><div><strong>Equipment Engineering &amp; Maintenance</strong></div><ul><li>Serve as the primary technical lead for lithography toolsets, ensuring maximum uptime and process stability through regular baseline monitoring.</li><li>Maintenance &amp; Repair: Independently perform scheduled preventative maintenance and diagnose/repair unscheduled hardware failures to minimize facility downtime.</li><li>Vendor Management: Manage relationships with equipment manufacturers; coordinate complex service visits, manage service contracts, and oversee major tool overhauls.</li></ul><div><strong>User Training &amp; Technical Communication</strong></div><ul><li>Develop and deliver comprehensive training programs for students and researchers, certifying users on tool operation, chemical safety, and lithographic theory.</li><li>Act as the central communication hub for the lithography area, providing timely updates to management and users regarding tool status, process drifts, or facility interruptions.</li></ul><div><strong>Facility Operations &amp; Compliance</strong></div><ul><li>Actively participate in facility safety audits and uphold strict cleanroom protocols to ensure a safe working environment for all personnel.</li><li>Maintain meticulous records including Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs), maintenance logs, user databases, and training certifications.</li><li>Oversee the lithography supply chain, managing the inventory of specialized chemicals, resists, and critical spare parts to ensure uninterrupted lab operations.</li></ul> 140110 - CLAWS https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/226788 2026-01-27T10:14:43-05:00 2026-01-27T10:14:44-05:00 Assistant Professor <div>We are seeking to hire an Assistant Professor whose work centers on developing AI-driven approaches that strengthen decision-making in complex, real-world settings. Situated within the Department of Civil, Construction, and Environmental Engineering (CCEE) at North Carolina State University (NCSU), this work advances innovation across civil, construction, and environmental engineering, with impacts spanning infrastructure design and operations, community resilience and safety, and the sustainable management of resources. The experience you bring will contribute to any of the eight core research areas in CCEE:<br><br></div><div>· Computing, AI and Systems</div><div>· Construction Engineering</div><div>· Environmental, Water Resources, and Coastal Engineering</div><div>· Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering</div><div>· Mechanics and Materials</div><div>· Structural Engineering and Mechanics</div><div>· Transportation Materials</div><div>· Transportation Systems</div><div><br></div><div>This opportunity may be a strong fit for individuals whose work develops and applies artificial intelligence to real-world engineering challenges and who are driven by complex systems, societal impact, and the advancement of resilient, sustainable infrastructure.</div> 140301 - Civil Construction & Environmental Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232856 2026-07-17T08:31:38-04:00 2026-07-17T08:31:39-04:00 Catering Event Coordinator <div><a href=\"https://rave.ncsu.edu/\"><strong>Rave! Catering</strong></a> is looking for an organized, high-energy <strong>Event Coordinator</strong>&nbsp;to join our premier catering team. Rave! Catering proudly employs experienced, certified chefs and catering professionals dedicated to delivering the highest level of service.<br><br>We don't expect you to know everything on day one. Our dedicated, expert team is ready to provide hands-on, on-the-job training to help you thrive, grow, and lead.<br><br><strong>Key Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Mastermind the Logistics</strong>: Translate event visions into reality. You will organize staff, equipment, and logistics, utilizing <em>Catertrax</em> to proactively build equipment lists and event floor plans.</li><li><strong>Ensure Flawless Execution:</strong> Stay one step ahead by making sure every linen, fork, and menu item is ordered, on-hand, and ready to go before the guests arrive.</li><li><strong>Lead and Inspire: </strong>Supervise events on-site. On some days, you’ll run lean, independent events; on others, you will lead a large service team to execute a flawless dining experience.</li><li><strong>Deliver \"Rave\" Service:</strong> Act as the friendly, professional face of Rave! Catering, building relationships with university clients and following up to ensure their expectations were exceeded.</li><li><strong>Be a Collaborative Pack Member:</strong> Support the broader NC State Dining mission by stepping in to assist at other campus dining locations when unique business needs arise.</li></ul><div><br>We offer flexible schedules and opportunities for learning, growth, and promotion. Superior work performance may provide an opportunity to renew employment and/or advance into a benefits-eligible permanent position. <br><br><a href=\"https://dining.ncsu.edu/about/overview/\"><strong>NC State Dining</strong></a><strong> </strong>provides campus-wide food services to students, staff, and faculty on the North Carolina State University Campus. NC State Dining operates 10 cafes, 3 food courts, 6 dining halls, 4 markets, 1 restaurant, vending, a nutritional counseling service, and a catering department. The department employs approximately 1000 temporary and student employees and approximately 180 permanent EHRA and SHRA employees.</div> 471501 - Dining Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233827 2026-08-13T15:21:22-04:00 2026-08-13T15:21:23-04:00 Research Assistant <div>The Research Assistant plays a key role in advancing the Turfgrass Entomology Program’s laboratory, greenhouse, and field research. Working in collaboration with the Principal Investigator (PI), this position independently coordinates multiple concurrent research projects, exercises scientific judgment, and ensures research activities are implemented with quality, accuracy, and efficiency. The position supports externally funded research and Extension programming by coordinating field trials, evaluating research data, maintaining research operations, and contributing to the communication and dissemination of findings.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Applied Research &amp; Field Trial Coordination</strong></div><ul><li>Independently coordinate laboratory, greenhouse, and field research, including scheduling seasonal field operations, implementing experimental protocols, and monitoring trial quality and integrity.</li><li>Collect, validate, organize, and interpret research data; perform preliminary statistical analyses and recommend modifications to improve research quality and methodology.</li><li>Serve as the primary field coordinator for multiple concurrent research projects, ensuring timely implementation of experiments and resolving novel research challenges.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Laboratory &amp; Research Operations</strong></div><ul><li>Maintain laboratory and field equipment, inventories, insect colonies, and research materials while ensuring adherence to laboratory safety, quality assurance, and EHS requirements.</li><li>Coordinate pesticide applications and equipment calibration and support field operations using appropriate pest management practices.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Research Communication &amp; Extension Support</strong></div><ul><li>Prepare research summaries, presentations, online educational materials, manuscripts, grant reports, and Extension publications in support of program activities.</li><li>Support field days and stakeholder demonstrations and contribute to the dissemination of research findings.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Training &amp; Program Support</strong></div><ul><li>Train undergraduate employees and supervise temporary workers in established research procedures and practices.</li><li>Develop and maintain standard operating procedures (SOPs), research documentation, and other materials that support consistent and compliant program operations.</li><li>Contribute intellectually to publications and grant development and assist the PI with research direction, methodology, and interpretation of complex scientific data.<br><br></li></ul><div>If you are a research professional with hands-on experience in turfgrass entomology, a strong understanding of insect pests affecting turfgrass ecosystems, and the ability to apply scientific judgment to laboratory, greenhouse, and field research, we invite you to apply. If you enjoy solving research problems, coordinating complex field activities, and contributing to research that supports both scientific discovery and Extension programming, this position offers an opportunity to make a meaningful impact.</div> 111801 - Entomology and Plant Pathology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/225714 2025-12-11T14:06:20-05:00 2025-12-11T14:06:21-05:00 Temporary Howling Cow Team Member <div>A team member is responsible for daily operations of the Creamery.<br><br>Essential Job Functions:</div><ul><li>This position is much more than just scooping ice cream! We operate in a fast paced, high volume and demanding environment (during the weekends and in prime weekday hours). Speed and accuracy are a must! Must communicate with customers, fill orders and clean following set list, throughout the shift.</li></ul><div><br>Work Schedule:</div><ul><li>Work schedule includes the weekday, weekend, and holiday shifts, with the hours ranging from 10:30am – 9:00pm. Hours may be varied due to operational needs.</li></ul> 111102 - Dairy And Process Applications https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234410 2026-08-31T14:18:12-04:00 2026-08-31T14:18:13-04:00 Research Assistant (GIS) <div>A temporary Research Assistant is needed and the primary responsiblities involve utilizing GIS software for digitizing tasks, and efficiently managing and analyzing data in varied formats. A strong background and expertise in working with GIS software (e.g., QGIS), or other industry-standard tools is needed.<br><br><br></div> 196001 - Department of Population Health and Pathobiology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/229449 2026-04-13T11:59:06-04:00 2026-04-13T11:59:07-04:00 Equine and Farm Animal Attendant (weekends, afterhours) <div>This temporary position will be responsible for maintaining a clean and sanitary hospital through routine stall/cage maintenance, hospital cleaning and disinfection, and waste removal. Restrain and provide basic husbandry for equine and farm animal patients. Small animal hospital backup, same duties.</div> 192001 - CVM Teaching Hospital https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232024 2026-06-23T13:03:39-04:00 2026-06-23T13:03:40-04:00 Solid Waste & Recycling Collections CDL Driver <div>Join NC State Facilities as a <strong>Solid Waste &amp; Recycling Collections CDL Driver </strong>and help keep our campus clean, safe, and sustainable. In this role, you will operate front-end loading collection trucks to service landfill, recycling, and compost containers across campus while ensuring safe and efficient operations.<br><br></div><div>As part of a skilled team of professional drivers, you will <strong>transport materials to disposal and recycling facilities, perform basic equipment maintenance, and support the University's goal of diverting 70% of waste from landfills. </strong>This is a great opportunity for a safety-focused CDL professional who enjoys working independently, operating heavy equipment, and making a meaningful impact on the NC State community. Apply today and join a team that helps keep NC State moving forward.</div><div><br></div><div><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include but are not limited to:<br></em></strong><br></div><ul><li><strong>Safely operate front-end loading collection trucks and container carrier vehicles to service campus dumpsters.</strong></li><li>Complete assigned collection routes, servicing an average of 50 locations per day.</li><li><strong>Transport collected materials to designated disposal, recycling, or compost facilities.</strong></li><li>Adapt to unplanned service needs, route changes, and emergency operations.</li><li>Conduct daily <strong>pre- and post-trip vehicle inspections</strong> and complete required documentation.</li><li>Utilize onboard technologies such as electronic routing systems, vehicle scales, and cameras.</li><li>Identify and report safety concerns, service issues, equipment damage, or incidents.</li><li>Operate vehicles safely in high-traffic and confined campus environments.</li><li><strong>Perform basic vehicle and equipment maintenance</strong>, including greasing components, checking fluids, and replacing lights or filters.</li><li>Communicate effectively with supervisors and team members and maintain positive customer relationships.</li><li>Possess technical knowledge of collection equipment such as front-end loaders, container carriers, rear loaders, dumpsters, and compactors. May operate other equipment such as forklifts, backhoes and snow removal equipment as needed.</li><li>Lift and transport materials weighing up to 60 pounds with or without reasonable accommodations and perform a wide range of physically demanding tasks in varied outdoor environments, ensuring safe handling practices and maintaining productivity in varying weather and terrain conditions.</li></ul> 445060 - Landscape Maintenance & Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233399 2026-07-31T14:26:36-04:00 2026-07-31T14:26:38-04:00 Radiology Technician <div>Help deliver high-quality diagnostic care to the NC State community by providing professional radiology services in a patient-centered clinical environment. As a Radiology Technician at Campus Health, you will produce high-quality diagnostic images using modern radiology equipment and cloud-based software while supporting providers with timely, accurate imaging that contributes to exceptional patient care. This role combines technical expertise, patient interaction, and a commitment to safety to ensure efficient clinical operations and positive patient experiences.</div><div><strong><br>Key Responsibilities<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Diagnostic Imaging &amp; Patient Care<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Perform radiographic procedures, including patient positioning and digital image capture, to produce high-quality diagnostic images for Campus Health providers.</li><li>Apply knowledge of anatomy, physiology, and disease processes to deliver safe, accurate, and compassionate patient care throughout the imaging process.</li><li>Communicate relevant patient information to clinicians and radiologists, serving as a liaison to support timely diagnosis and treatment.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Clinical Operations &amp; Documentation<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Accurately enter patient information into the electronic health record and communicate effectively with patients, providers, and staff through phone, email, and in-person interactions.</li><li>Maintain radiology equipment through routine quality control, cleaning, and monitoring to ensure reliable operation and high-quality imaging.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Safety &amp; Regulatory Compliance<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Maintain a clean clinical environment and follow infectious disease protocols to support patient and staff safety.</li><li>Properly handle medical, biological, and infectious waste while adhering to radiation safety, OSHA, and Environmental Health and Safety standards.</li><li>Participate in scheduled meetings, drills, training, and other departmental activities to support ongoing operational readiness.</li></ul><div><br>If you are<strong> </strong>a detail-oriented radiology professional who enjoys combining technical expertise with compassionate patient care and thrives in a collaborative healthcare environment, we encourage you to apply. Join Campus Health and play an important role in supporting the health and well-being of the NC State community.</div> 244030 - Campus Health https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223256 2025-09-25T11:04:34-04:00 2025-09-25T11:04:35-04:00 Linux Desktop Support <div>We are seeking a highly skilled Linux Desktop Support professional to join our team and take charge of the hardware and software administration for our growing (200+) fleet of desktops, workstations, and laptops running RedHat Enterprise Linux and Ubuntu Linux. As a Linux Desktop Support Professional, you will be responsible for ensuring the smooth operation and optimal performance of our Linux-based desktop infrastructure, providing technical support to end-users, and resolving hardware and software-related issues efficiently.</div><div><br></div><div>We offer a <a href=\"https://onboarding.ncsu.edu/candidateguide/\"><strong>benefits package and opportunities</strong></a> for growth and development. If you are a Linux enthusiast with a passion for providing exceptional desktop support, we would love to hear from you. Apply now to join our dynamic team!</div><div><strong><br>Responsibilities include but are not limited to:</strong></div><ul><li>Perform hardware and software administration for two hundred desktops, workstations, and laptops running RedHat Enterprise Linux and Ubuntu Linux.</li><li>Deploy, configure, and maintain Linux-based operating systems and applications.</li><li>Provide technical support to end-users regarding Linux desktop-related issues, troubleshooting hardware and software problems, and resolving technical incidents in a timely manner.</li><li>Troubleshoot and assist end user issues with networking, printing, drivers, and network storage within the Gnome desktop environment.</li><li>Conduct regular system maintenance, including OS upgrades, patches, and security enhancements.</li><li>Manage user accounts, permissions, and access rights on Linux desktop systems.</li><li>Monitor system backups and perform archival of data as users leave or systems are replaced.</li><li>Monitor system performance and proactively identify potential issues or areas for improvement.</li><li>Document system configurations, processes, and troubleshooting procedures.</li><li>Stay updated with the latest industry trends and technologies related to Linux desktop support.</li></ul> 140401 - Electrical & Computer Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234122 2026-08-25T10:20:38-04:00 2026-08-25T10:20:39-04:00 Research Specialist – Virology Laboratory Sharma <div>The successful candidate will support the day-to-day operations of a multidisciplinary virology and structural biology research laboratory conducting NIH-funded research on viral pathogenesis, host-pathogen interactions, and translational infectious disease research. This role balances administrative, compliance, and operational duties with hands-on technical support across multiple collaborative research projects in a dynamic academic research environment.</div><div><br></div><div><strong>Laboratory Operations, Inventory &amp; Logistics</strong></div><div><br></div><ul><li>Maintain general laboratory organization, workflows, and efficient daily operations.</li><li>Manage purchasing, vendor interactions, supply ordering, shipment receiving, and inventory control.</li><li>Prepare, maintain, and catalog biological inventories (viral stocks, plasmid DNA, bacterial glycerol stocks, cell lines, and specimen archives).</li><li>Oversee shared equipment scheduling, routine maintenance, calibration, and basic troubleshooting.</li><li>Serve as the primary liaison with institutional core facilities.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Technical &amp; Research Support</strong></div><div><br></div><ul><li>Provide technical assistance to graduate students, postdoctoral researchers, andlab staff across virology, molecular biology, protein biochemistry, and immunology projects.</li><li>Maintain mammalian cell cultures.</li><li>Execute standard laboratory techniques including viral propagation/titration,molecular cloning, nucleic acid/protein analyses, protein purification,immunoblotting, flow cytometry, and microscopy.</li><li>Prepare common laboratory reagents, stock solutions, and buffers.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Compliance, Safety &amp; Training</strong></div><div><br></div><ul><li>Assist with laboratory biosafety, Environmental Health and Safety (EHS), and institutional regulatory compliance activities.</li><li>Coordinate onboarding, laboratory orientation, and safety training for incoming personnel.</li><li>Maintain standard operating procedures (SOPs), safety documentation, and regulatory compliance records.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Documentation, Data Integrity &amp; Administrative Support</strong></div><div><br></div><ul><li>Maintain rigorous, accurate laboratory records and electronic database inventories.</li></ul><div><br></div><div>If you are a collaborative and detail-oriented research professional who enjoys hands-on laboratory work and contributing to innovative virology research, we invite you to bring your skills to the Sharma Laboratory and join NC State’s research community.&nbsp;</div> 196001 - Department of Population Health and Pathobiology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/227841 2026-02-26T14:16:35-05:00 2026-08-26T09:45:07-04:00 Lecturer <div>The Department of English is accepting applications for the position of Lecturer on a full- time and/or part-time basis in areas such as Composition, Literature, Linguistics, Film Studies and Screenwriting, Creative Writing and Professional Writing for Summer 2026, Fall 2026 and Spring 2027.&nbsp;</div> 160201 - English https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234615 2026-09-03T11:14:47-04:00 2026-09-03T11:14:48-04:00 Assistant Carpenter/Architectural Renovation Technician <div>We are seeking an<strong> Assistant Carpenter/Architectural Renovation Technician</strong> to join our team. This role involves <strong>assisting with various carpentry tasks, including drywall installation, ceiling systems, flooring, cabinetwork, and countertop installations.</strong> A basic understanding of renovation trades, tools, and safety standards is required, along with general knowledge of other trades. The ideal candidate will work within our trade shops and<strong> be an integral part of assisting with maintaining and managing over 2,240 acres within our campus</strong>. This role will grow into assisting within multiple facets of our trades. If you’re looking for a hands-on opportunity to <strong>develop your skills</strong> in carpentry and renovation while contributing to a dynamic team, we encourage you to apply and grow with us!</div><div><br></div><div><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li>Assist with <strong>drywall installation, ceiling systems, flooring, cabinetwork, and countertop installations</strong></li><li>Work on metal or wood stud wall systems</li><li><strong>Perform trim work</strong>, hang doors, drill and set door hardware, and set windows</li><li>Remove walls, install partitions, and alter rooms or add to existing structures using hand and power tools</li><li>Use machines, tools, instruments, equipment, and materials associated with masonry, roof maintenance, downspout and gutter repair, and waterproofing below-grade</li><li><strong>Perform accurate measurements and calibrations for carpentry work</strong></li><li>Assess safety conditions regularly, identify potential hazards, and communicate them to supervisors</li><li><strong>Implement accident prevention and corrective measures</strong> in work-related areas</li><li>Communicate with customers to improve service delivery based on their feedback, relaying information to the Journey Level Carpenter or Supervisor</li><li>Perform lifting duties up to 30 pounds frequently and 60 pounds occasionally, with or without reasonable accommodations</li><li>Perform tasks requiring standing for extended periods, working in confined spaces, and utilizing various tools. Safely climb and work from different types of ladders to access elevated or below-grade areas (such as roofs, above-ceiling spaces, and utility pits). Be exposed to and work in areas where insects or small animals are occasionally present. All essential tasks must be performed with or without reasonable accommodations.</li></ul> 445030 - Building Maintenance & Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230784 2026-05-19T16:16:58-04:00 2026-05-19T16:17:00-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar - DEPP <div>The overall aim of this project is to develop gene drive strains for suppression of the New World screwworm. In the initial phase of the project we will work secondary screwworm, a very closely related species. We will work with primary screwworm in ACL3 containment at a later date, if an import permit is granted.<br><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>The scholar will work closely with Dr Amarish Yadav in developing docking lines for phiC31 recombinase.</li><li>They will use these lines to create fsRIDL gene drive strains, building on our earlier work with fsRIDL lines.</li><li>The postdoctoral scholar may work on developing suppression gene drive strains targeting sex determination genes such as doublesex. This involves making gene constructs, developing transgenic lines and evaluating the lines.<br><br></li></ul> 111801 - Entomology and Plant Pathology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234027 2026-08-20T14:12:12-04:00 2026-08-20T14:12:13-04:00 Research Technician-Burclaff Lab <div>The Burclaff Lab is interested in better understanding how metabolism and cellular energetics are regulated within intestinal stem cells and how dynamic regulation underlies the intestine’s ability to maintain itself at homeostasis and following injuries or disease. Our major focus is on the stem cell compartment, studying how the intestinal stem cells maintain their identity, undergo fate decisions, regulate cell cycle, and the dynamic plasticity that occurs within crypt populations following injury. We use a mix of animal and human models, including cutting-edge genetically modified primary human intestinal stem cell cultures, to advance our understanding of the mammalian intestine.</div><div><strong><br>Position Overview<br></strong><br></div><div>We are seeking a motivated, detail-oriented team member to support research operations, assisting with experiments, and assist with lab management. This role combines hands-on scientific research with core lab oversight in a collaborative environment.</div><div><strong><br>Key Responsibilities &amp; Qualifications<br></strong><br></div><ul><li><strong>Research &amp; Lab Work:</strong> Execute experiments using animal models (mouse line maintenance/tissue collection), cell lines, and primary cultures. Perform molecular biology techniques (RNA/protein work, staining, tissue sectioning, live-image analysis).</li><li><strong>Lab Management:</strong> Oversee day-to-day operations, manage equipment and inventory, and coordinate multiple concurrent projects.</li><li><strong>Data &amp; Documentation:</strong> Maintain accurate records, manage scientific data, and contribute to publications, grant applications, and presentations.</li><li><strong>Technical &amp; Problem-Solving Skills:</strong> Prior research experience required. Proficient in standard lab equipment, troubleshooting, computers, and data management tools.</li><li><strong>Work Ethic &amp; Soft Skills:</strong> Self-motivated, highly organized, and able to work independently while fostering a collaborative, supportive team environment.</li></ul> 194001 - Department of Molecular Biomedical Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/227057 2026-02-03T11:06:35-05:00 2026-02-03T11:06:36-05:00 Turf Health Technician  I <div>The <strong>Turf Health Technician I</strong> plays a hands-on role in maintaining and enhancing the landscapes that support NC State’s vibrant campus community. <strong>This position provides essential irrigation system maintenance and turf horticultural services across the Main, Centennial, and Centennial Biomedical campuses.</strong> Working as part of a collaborative grounds team, the technician helps ensure the health, appearance, and performance of more than 60 irrigation systems and a wide variety of turf and landscape areas.</div><div>This role is ideal for someone who enjoys outdoor work, takes pride in well-maintained landscapes, and wants to contribute to a campus environment that supports learning, research, and community life. If you enjoy working outdoors, value teamwork, and want to help maintain the landscapes of a dynamic university campus, we encourage you to apply.<br><br><strong>Join NC State’s Grounds team and be part of the work that keeps our campus healthy, functional, and beautiful year-round.</strong></div><div><strong><br></strong><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li><strong>Perform routine maintenance and repairs on campus-wide irrigation systems to ensure proper operation and efficiency.</strong></li><li><strong>Diagnose and troubleshoot</strong> issues related to 2-wire irrigation systems, pumps, leaking pipes, and irrigation heads.</li><li><strong>Set up, program, and operate various irrigation controllers</strong>.</li><li>Assist with new irrigation installations, including manual excavation and operating small equipment.</li><li><strong>Maintain and troubleshoot low-voltage landscape lighting systems.</strong></li><li>Install, maintain, and support turf areas such as lawns, prairies, meadows, playfields, and ground cover associated with new construction projects.</li><li><strong>Carry out seasonal horticultural practices, including aeration, top dressing, and composting.</strong></li><li>Apply sound horticultural standards to promote healthy, sustainable, and visually appealing campus landscapes.</li><li>Support campus operations during inclement weather events and leaf collection activities.</li><li><strong>Collaborate with turf and horticulture teams on special projects and assigned initiatives</strong>.</li><li>Safely perform physically demanding work, including lifting and transporting materials up to 30 pounds (with or without reasonable accommodations) and working outdoors in varying weather and terrain conditions.</li><li>Communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, and the campus community, and follow verbal and written instructions related to work assignments.</li></ul> 445060 - Landscape Maintenance & Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234535 2026-09-02T12:28:35-04:00 2026-09-02T12:28:36-04:00 IT Manager, Student Information System <div>This position performs technical and functional duties associated with application development and provides managerial leadership within the EAS Student Information System (SIS) Team. It is responsible for ensuring exceptional customer service and support. Maintaining and enhancing the partnership between the IT systems and the central office business owners and campus community impacting over 50,000 customers of the Student Information System is a core expectation. This position requires an expert in business process analysis with highly advanced technical solution development skills needed to support highly complex systems and associated integrations across campus. Excellent verbal, written, organization and project management skills are essential.<br><br>This team directly supports the SIS Financial Aid module in collaboration with the Office of Scholarships and Financial Aid and the SIS Student Financials module in collaboration with the University Cashier's Office. This position leads initiatives of high complexity and is expected to interact with leadership positions in customer and IT units to ensure that developed solutions align with business needs.<br><br>This position manages a team of application analysts performing business process analysis, change request evaluation, functional and technical specification development, coding, unit and system test development and execution, code migration, production support &amp; system maintenance.<br><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities</strong><br><br><strong>Functional/Technical Knowledge &amp; Leadership </strong><br><br></div><ul><li>Working manager that participates and supervises a team of application analysts to design, build and/or implement practical, cost effective business solutions. These solutions must seamlessly integrate within a technically complex enterprise system that has broad and institution-wide impacts</li><li>Research and/or develop new technologies and keep abreast of new technologies to evaluate feasibility for organizational needs</li><li>Translate high level organizational goals into actionable technical plans and objectives</li><li>Interpret complex financial and operational reports and data, explain complex business processes and technological functions, advise and inform/persuade data stewards and other policymaking bodies of the appropriate resource and technology solutions</li><li>Provide technical leadership on various committees and task forces to address audits, disaster recovery, federal and state regulations, and security</li><li>Ensure that application development efforts align closely with business requirements, from design to delivery, and that applications are user friendly, scalable, secure, and can be efficiently maintained once deployed</li><li>Maintain a thorough knowledge of the assigned applications’ technical and functional capabilities and weaknesses</li><li>Use persuasion and negotiation to build cooperation and consensus towards decision</li><li>Work effectively across customers, technical teams, and the enterprise to understand and communicate requirements, status updates, issues, problems, and solutions with a focus on more detailed, “closer to the ground” issues</li><li>Manage vendor relationships and escalate technical support within vendor organizations as needed</li></ul><div><br><strong>Planning and Organizing </strong><br><br></div><ul><li>Prepare and present detailed development and/or implementation plans for projects and monitor project status</li><li>Coordinate long-term project planning for large development projects</li><li>Make significant changes in the organizational structure, methods and procedures to respond to new goals and programs</li><li>Plan for utilization of staff, equipment and other resources</li><li>Ensure the development of internal policies and procedures</li><li>Develop/ensure the development of, and give final approval to, unit rules, standards and guidelines governing quality and quantity of work</li><li>Determine applicability in controversial or precedent-setting situations</li><li>Determine budget needs and recommend, with significant influence, priorities based on program mission, costs and projected revenues</li><li>Prepare comprehensive project plans that encompass the entire software development life cycle (SDLC); ensure these plans dovetail into larger plans as needed</li><li>Maintain a strong knowledge of the SDLC at NC State University combined with outstanding organization and communication skills</li><li>Maintain a deep understanding of software development project management methodologies and tools (Microsoft Project, QuickBase, Stat, etc.) and a thorough understanding of project expectations and procedures</li></ul><div><br><strong>Strategic Development &amp; Program Management </strong><br><br></div><ul><li>Define business strategies and contribute to the enterprise’s vision (typically through participation in customer strategic planning)</li><li>Determine needs and establish goals and objectives that incorporate a broad range of internal and external factors when solving problems and making decisions</li><li>Clearly translate technical issues into understandable terms for audiences with varying degrees of technical expertise so that decisions can be reached; these audiences can include members from all levels of the university</li><li>Recognize and act on strategic opportunities for success</li><li>Weigh alternatives and consequences to make informed decisions on long-term operational issues</li><li>Evaluate organizational readiness and capacity for change and adjust accordingly</li><li>Continuously evaluate new technology and refine supported applications and solutions to align with customer needs and expectations</li><li>Clearly communicate plans with current and potential project stakeholders, campus wide.</li><li>Plan strategically for the adoption of application technologies and independently prioritizes the team’s work to best meet the immediate and long term goals of the university</li><li>Maintain a clear understanding of the organization, the strengths and weaknesses of current processes and a clear understanding of larger organizational priorities</li><li>Think creatively and communicate effectively in order to contribute in practical ways towards the growth and success of the organization and future strategies</li></ul><div><br><strong>Human Resource Function<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Recruit, hire and supervise staff and direct the development, enhancement, and maintenance of workflow processes and application support for a varied group of entities</li><li>Involve the director, other managers and staff in the development and establishment of long and short range goals and priorities for the department, including the development and maintenance of corresponding policies and procedures</li><li>Facilitate the growth and development of team members</li><li>Pursue disciplinary actions as needed</li><li>Review accomplishments to assure that program missions and goals are being met</li><li>Conduct final review for most difficult, controversial or sensitive work. Ensure team members understand concepts related to the organization’s policies</li><li>Provide coaching, conduct annual performance reviews, and manage the total skill set of the unit. Actively seek resources and opportunities for employee training and growth</li><li>Determine staffing needs and motivational techniques to build and maintain a productive and cohesive team</li><li>Provide feedback and mentoring of staff to help them meet future goals and expectations</li></ul> 515001 - Enterprise Application Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230358 2026-05-07T14:31:52-04:00 2026-05-07T14:31:54-04:00 Floor Technician #62678 <div>We are seeking a dedicated and detail-oriented <strong>Floor Technician</strong> to join our team at NC State’s Facilities Division. In this role, you will play a key part in maintaining the cleanliness and appearance of over 10 million square feet of campus space. <strong>You will utilize specialized cleaning equipment and techniques to ensure a safe and welcoming environment for students, staff, and visitors.</strong> If you take pride in delivering high-quality floor care and enjoy working in a dynamic and collaborative team setting, this is the perfect opportunity for you!<br><strong><br>Working Hours: 4:00 a.m.-12:30 p.m. M-F</strong></div><div><strong><br></strong><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include but not limited to:</em></strong><strong><br></strong><br></div><ul><li><strong>Strip and refinish hard surface floors, extract and clean carpets, and conduct routine maintenance using equipment such as burnishers, auto-scrubbers, and carpet extractors.</strong></li><li>Complete assigned cleaning tasks, including vacuuming, mopping, shower scrubbing, and floor finish application, to maintain designated areas.</li><li><strong>Adhere to established safety procedures</strong> for handling cleaning products and operating equipment. Wear appropriate <strong>Personal Protective Equipment (PPE)</strong> and post caution signage during cleaning activities.</li><li><strong>Identify and report building discrepancies</strong>, safety hazards, and equipment malfunctions to the manager promptly.</li><li>Support cleaning and maintenance needs during emergencies and inclement weather, ensuring the safety and functionality of campus spaces.</li><li>Accurately complete incident reports, <strong>interpret Safety Data Sheets (SDS)</strong>, and apply knowledge of safety protocols in daily operations</li><li>Perform lifting duties up to 30 pounds frequently and 60 pounds occasionally, with or without reasonable accommodations</li><li>Perform physical tasks such as grasping, pushing, and pulling cleaning equipment; reaching overhead; bending, squatting, climbing, and maintaining balance; and communicate effectively with supervisors and the public.</li><li>Communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, and the general public, and follows verbal and written instructions related to work assignments and operational needs.</li></ul> 445080 - Environmental Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232612 2026-07-09T16:07:53-04:00 2026-07-09T16:07:54-04:00 Accounting Technician <div>This position provides fiscal support for the department, largely focused on assisting the faculty and staff with research and Extension grant funding. You will work with the department’s Business Service Coordinator and Student Services Coordinator, focusing on day-to-day financial and procurement operations, ensuring compliance with federal, state, and university guidelines. Our department grant funding derives from a varied portfolio of federal, state, and industry/commodity partners with an annual average of $16M and growing.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities include but are not limited to:</strong></div><ul><li>Collaborate with faculty and staff to monitor grant funding to ensure accounts comply with sponsor guidelines and reporting schedules, and are closed out timely</li><li>Provide financial administrative support to primary investigators (PIs), including voucher payments, journal vouchers, review of Pcard transactions, travel authorizations and reimbursements</li><li>Analyze financial data for accuracy, resolve issues, and correct or reverse entries as needed</li><li>Attend departmental, college, and university meetings to stay updated on financial policies and procedures</li><li>Maintain compliance, internal controls, and proper documentation of transactions, providing them to the college Contracts and Grants post-award team as requested</li><li>Collaborate and partner with CALS CBO</li></ul><div><br></div><div>If you are a collaborative team player with strong communication skills, a client-focused mindset, and the ability to manage multiple tasks while maintaining accuracy, we encourage you to apply.</div><div><strong><br></strong><em>You Belong Here! At NC State, our goal is for all employees to reach their fullest potential at work. As you consider this opportunity, we encourage you to review our </em><a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong><em>Employee Value Proposition</em></strong></a><em> (</em><a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong><em>https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/</em></strong></a><em>) and learn more about what makes NC State the best place to learn and work for everyone.</em></div> 118101 - Biological And Agricultural Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233584 2026-08-05T16:40:07-04:00 2026-08-05T16:40:08-04:00 IT System Administrator <div>As a member of the AWS team, you will bridge the gap between business needs and technology. You will work closely with application development teams to identify areas for process improvement and implement efficient solutions to meet business requirements. Using your technical and business knowledge to integrate and analyze data from multiple sources to draw conclusions and make recommendations, identify and investigate incidents, and collaborate with cross-functional teams to resolve or escalate issues and track them to resolution. You will be expected to work independently and handle multiple concurrent time-sensitive tasks that require attention to detail using various software technologies necessary to support the University’s enterprise applications and business operations requirements. Responsibilities include:</div><div><br><strong>Process Automation</strong></div><ul><li>Develop, implement, and execute production and test processes and processing schedules using IBM Workload Scheduler, or other scheduling automation applications</li><li>Develop, implement, and execute secure data transmissions using EDI or similar tools</li><li>Configure and implement reports and bursting rules within the report distribution and archiving solution</li><li>Assist with automation efforts and onboard new resources to ensure the completeness and accuracy of the ServiceNow Configuration Management Database</li><li>Use scripting languages to connect, enhance functionality, and optimize back-end applications</li></ul><div><strong><br>System software administration</strong></div><ul><li>Configure and administer automated workload scheduling software</li><li>Configure and administer secure data transmission software</li><li>Assist in planning and implementation of software upgrades required to support functional enhancements or maintenance agreement</li><li>Perform routine daily and ad hoc administrative tasks, monitor performance, address errors, and modify configurations to meet business requirements</li><li>Monitor and maintain Configuration Items in the ServiceNow Configuration Management Database</li></ul> 517101 - Shared Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223323 2025-09-25T11:57:26-04:00 2025-09-25T11:57:27-04:00 Banquet Bartender <div>JOIN THE PACK!<br><br>We are seeking enthusiastic individuals to Join our Pack as a Banquet Bartender.<br><br>Essential job duties include:</div><ul><li>Able to work all types of events anywhere on campus</li><li>Able to work by themselves</li><li>Proper set up and break down of bars. Setup events, work the event, and clean up after the event, including removing all catering items from the event space(s), washing any dirty catering items and returning all cleaned and restocked items to the appropriate places within the catering stock rooms.</li><li>Communicating with clients and guests as needed</li><li>Catering support during down time</li><li>Making sure the kitchen is clean before they leave when in Talley (taking out trash, sinks and counters are clean, floors are swept and mopped, dishwasher is drained and turned off).</li><li>Able to check for identification ensuring no minors are being served.</li><li>Other duties as assigned to ensure NC State Dining business needs are met. Position may be asked to assist in other locations as necessary.</li></ul><div><br><a href=\"https://dining.ncsu.edu/about/overview/\"><strong>NC State Dining</strong></a> provides campus wide food services to students, staff, and faculty on the North Carolina State University Campus. NC State Dining operates 11 cafes, 3 food courts and 6 dining halls, 3 markets, 2 restaurants, vending, a nutritional counseling service, and a catering department. The department employs approximately 1000 temporary and student employees and approximately 190 permanent EHRA and SHRA employees.<br><br>This is an hourly position, that includes a State required 32-day break at the end of 11 months. Excellent performance during this time may provide an opportunity to renew employment and/or advance.</div> 471501 - Dining Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234334 2026-08-28T15:24:16-04:00 2026-08-28T15:24:17-04:00 UTS - Temporary Graphic Designer at UNC Chapel Hill 480502 - UHR UTS/UNC Partnership https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234325 2026-08-28T13:23:48-04:00 2026-08-28T13:23:49-04:00 Housekeeper <div>The primary purpose of this position is to clean and perform routine housekeeping duties at the Vernon James Center.<br><br><strong>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:</strong></div><ul><li>Clean and sanitize designated areas according to established schedules and safety protocols to ensure a hygienic environment</li><li>Operate and maintain housekeeping equipment, including vacuums, mops, and scrubbers, to complete tasks</li><li>Perform window washing and other seasonal cleaning tasks as needed</li><li>Maintain cleaning supplies inventory and request replenishment when needed.</li><li>Assist with setting up designated spaces for meetings or events</li><li>Perform minor maintenance, such as replacing light bulbs and performing paint touch-ups; promptly report damages or major maintenance needs.</li><li>Provide outstanding customer service, remaining professional and respectful.</li><li>Ability to lift a minimum of 60 lbs., with or without reasonable accommodations.</li><li>Ability to stand for long periods of time, walking, bending, twisting, pulling and pushing.</li></ul><div><br></div><div>This position requires a high degree of flexibility to respond to and coordinate with the needs of the faculty, staff, and public. Communication with the team and supervisor is critical for success.<br><br></div><div><strong>This position is located in Plymouth, NC.<br>This position is for 20 hours per week.</strong></div> 113804 - Vernon G James Research And Extension Center https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228691 2026-03-23T14:34:06-04:00 2026-03-23T14:34:07-04:00 Assistant Director / Case Manager <div>Join a dynamic and growing Violence Prevention &amp; Threat Management team as an Assistant Director/Case Manager, where your work directly contributes to campus safety and well-being. In this role, you will support the Director of Risk Assessment and play a key part in advancing the university’s behavioral threat assessment, case management, and prevention efforts. As referrals to the Behavior Assessment Team (BAT) and outreach initiatives continue to expand, this position offers an exciting opportunity to make a meaningful impact in a high-visibility, mission-driven program.</div><div><br>This full-time, 12-month position collaborates closely with campus partners, including Academic and Student Affairs and Human Resources, to support a coordinated, multidisciplinary approach to threat assessment and intervention. As the Case Manager, you will lead and support behavioral threat assessments using structured professional judgment—conducting intake, gathering and analyzing information, assessing risk, and documenting cases. You will coordinate case reviews, develop and implement individualized management strategies, and work with stakeholders to monitor and reduce risk. Your attention to detail will ensure accurate records, timely follow-up, and compliance with institutional policies and applicable laws.</div><div><br>This role may include on-call responsibilities to respond to emerging concerns and provide operational support to the Behavior Assessment Team. You will also contribute to prevention, consultation, and training initiatives that promote early identification, reporting, and intervention. Working with students, employees, and non-affiliated individuals, you will help foster a safer campus environment grounded in best practices.<br><br><strong><em>Additional duties include:</em></strong><br><br></div><div><strong>Consulting and Advising (Behavioral Threat Assessment and Management) – 30%<br></strong>Play a central role in identifying and addressing potential risks to campus safety. You will conduct behavioral threat assessments using a structured, evidence-informed approach to evaluate concerning behaviors, risk factors, and potential for harm. In partnership with campus stakeholders, you’ll develop and implement individualized management strategies that focus on risk reduction, stabilization, and prevention. This includes safety planning with potential victims and providing expert consultation to faculty, staff, and students on warning signs, concerning behaviors, and risk indicators. You will also coordinate multidisciplinary interventions and connect individuals to appropriate campus and community resources.</div><div><strong>Professional Knowledge and Case Management Practice – 30%<br></strong>Bring your analytical skills and professional expertise to dynamic case management work. You will gather and synthesize behavioral and contextual information to assess and monitor risk over time, recognizing that situations evolve. Using best practices in threat assessment, you’ll maintain clear, objective documentation and ensure cases are actively managed, with strategies adjusted as needed to address changes in behavior or circumstances.</div><div><strong>Communication and Multidisciplinary Collaboration – 20%<br></strong>Be part of a highly collaborative team committed to prevention and coordinated response. As an active member of the Behavioral Assessment Team (BAT) and the NC State Cares Team, you’ll contribute to case reviews and planning efforts. You will work closely with partners across campus—including General Counsel, Counseling Services, Employee Relations, Student Conduct, and University Police—to ensure responses are thoughtful, legally sound, and focused on safety. You’ll also engage with external agencies and law enforcement when situations require elevated response.</div><div><strong>Outreach and Prevention – 20%<br></strong>Help shape a proactive culture of safety and awareness. You will support the development and delivery of training and outreach initiatives that empower the campus community to recognize and report concerning behaviors early. Your work will contribute to broader institutional efforts to prevent targeted violence, workplace violence, and other forms of harm through education, engagement, and early intervention.</div> 423501 - Risk Assessment https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234710 2026-09-04T11:02:13-04:00 2026-09-04T11:02:14-04:00 Associate Vice Provost and Director of Scholarships and Financial Aid <div>Reporting to the Senior Vice Provost for Enrollment Management, the Associate Vice Provost and Director of Scholarships and Financial Aid provides strategic and operational leadership for the university’s comprehensive financial aid and scholarship enterprise. Leading a large and complex operation, the position shapes institutional approaches to financial aid, affordability, and student support while ensuring effective stewardship of university resources and compliance with federal and state requirements.</div><div><br>In this role, you will serve as a trusted advisor to university and division leadership, translating policy, data, and emerging trends into strategies that advance enrollment, affordability, student success, and institutional priorities. You will shape financial aid policies and awarding methodologies, guide continuous improvement across operations and technology, and lead a high-performing team committed to service, collaboration, professional growth, and operational excellence.</div><div><strong><br>Key Areas of Responsibility:<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Strategic Leadership &amp; Institutional Advising:</strong></div><ul><li>Advise university and Enrollment Management leadership on financial aid policies and strategies affecting enrollment, affordability, student success, and institutional resources.</li><li>Contribute to the development and implementation of strategic priorities for Enrollment Management and Services.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Financial Aid Strategy &amp; Compliance:</strong></div><ul><li>Establish financial aid packaging methodologies and oversee federal, state, private, and institutional financial aid programs.</li><li>Ensure financial aid operations and policies comply with applicable federal and state regulations.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Operational Excellence &amp; Continuous Improvement:</strong></div><ul><li>Lead continuous improvement of financial aid operations, technology, data use, compliance, and customer service practices.</li><li>Exercise independent judgment in policy interpretation, awarding methodology, resource allocation, and operational priorities.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Organizational &amp; Collaborative Leadership:</strong></div><ul><li>Lead a complex financial aid and scholarship operation while fostering service, collaboration, professional development, and operational excellence.</li><li>Build strong partnerships across the university to advance shared enrollment and student success priorities.</li></ul><div><br>If you are a strategic financial aid leader who can translate complex policy, data, and operational challenges into thoughtful strategies that advance affordability, enrollment, and student success, we encourage you to apply.</div> 320301 – Sch & Fin Aid Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231537 2026-06-10T09:44:15-04:00 2026-06-10T09:44:16-04:00 Part Time Lecturer - Dance or Somatic Lecturer <div>The Department of Performing Arts and Technology at North Carolina State University seeks applications for Lecturers to teach undergraduate dance, Pilates, or Yoga courses. Responsibilities include teaching and grading one (1) credit-hour courses (primarily).<br><br>The position(s) are part-time; one-semester or one-year, non-tenure-track appointments with possibility of renewal. Compensation is determined by the number of assigned credit hours.<br><br><strong>Please note: This position is based on need and availability. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis. Candidates should specify the techniques, practices, or areas for which they are qualified to provide instruction.</strong></div> 248401 - Performing Arts and Technology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233144 2026-07-24T12:08:03-04:00 2026-07-24T12:08:04-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar - DEPP <div>We seek a highly motivated Postdoctoral Research Scholar to conduct research in nematode genomics and molecular plant-nematode interactions. The position will focus on both basic and translational research aimed at understanding root-knot nematode (RKN) virulence, host adaptation, and mechanisms associated with resistance breaking in crops.<br><br>The major focus of the project will be characterizing virulence-associated genes in M. enterolobii and investigating their interactions with crop hosts, including sweet potato. Additional projects will involve the integration of multi-omics datasets to better understand RKN-hosts interactions. <br><br>The postdoc scholar will lead both laboratory experiments and computational analyses. The work will combine molecular biology, nematology, genomics, bioinformatics, and comparative transcriptomics. Depending on research interests and project needs, the successful candidate may also contribute to functional characterization of candidate nematode genes and host- resistant associated genes. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to work closely with collaborators at NC State University and other institutions<br><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Conduct laboratory and greenhouse experiments related to plant-nematode interactions, nematode virulence, and host resistance</li><li>Perform molecular biology experiments, including DNA and RNA extractions, PCR, qPCR, preparation of samples for genomics and transcriptomics analyses</li><li>Conduct bioinformatics analyses using high-performance computing environments</li><li>Maintain clear research records and contribute to reproducible bioinformatics workflows</li></ul> 111801 - Entomology and Plant Pathology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/220842 2025-07-11T08:52:46-04:00 2025-07-11T08:52:47-04:00 Lecturer <div>We are seeking a Lecturer to support the TELS program’s mission of preparing highly effective educators who lead innovation in teaching and learning. This role focuses on delivering engaging instruction and hands-on experiences that integrate digital technologies, design thinking, and teaching principles into K–12 education. The Lecturer will collaborate with colleagues to advance research-informed practices, support student development, and contribute to the discovery and dissemination of new knowledge in the field. Through teaching and service, the Lecturer will help shape the future of education and inspire the next generation of instructional leaders.<br><strong><br>Core Responsibilities &amp; Duties<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>1. Instruction &amp; Curriculum Delivery</strong></div><ul><li>Teach 1–3 courses per semester in areas such as elementary education, middle grades education, learning sciences, , subject-matter methods (e.g., math, science), or other TELS-defined specialties</li><li>Foster best practices, integrate research-based instructional strategies and digital technologies .</li></ul><div><strong>2. Course &amp; Program Development</strong></div><ul><li>Design, revise, and implement course content; coordinate syllabi with program objectives.</li><li>Ensure alignment with NC standards (e.g., NC Standard Course of Study, NGSS), and collaborate with program coordinators to maintain curricular coherence</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>3. Student Mentoring &amp; Advising</strong></div><ul><li>Support and advise students, including preservice teachers, in both academic and field-based experiences (e.g., clinical placements).</li><li>Provide mentorship for undergraduate, graduate, or doctoral students, as applicable .</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>4. Assessment &amp; Evaluation</strong></div><ul><li>Develop fair and rigorous assessments, grade assignments and exams.</li><li>Gather and reflect on student feedback, peer reviews, and teaching evaluations to continuously improve instruction</li></ul> 131101 - Teacher Education and Learning Sciences (TELS) https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230936 2026-05-27T09:48:31-04:00 2026-05-27T09:48:31-04:00 Temporary Graduate Student Intern <div>The purpose of the project/s on which the candidate will work is to evaluate current food safety programs and resources to identify barriers and limitations; plan, implement and evaluate a food safety campaign on the information center; aid in strategizing and coordinating food safety communication and outreach plans; design resources, record videos and more to supplement programs and information center; assist team members with extension program efforts such as translating materials in different languages; and aid in updating information sheets to improve accessibility.</div> 111401 - Agricultural and Human Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232912 2026-07-17T17:18:39-04:00 2026-07-17T17:18:39-04:00 Extension Agent, FCS (Assistant, Associate, or Full Agent) <div>Are you looking for a rewarding career that will let you give back to your community? NC State Extension extends research-based knowledge to all North Carolinians, helping them transform science into everyday solutions that improve their lives and grow our state. We are the model of excellence in non-formal, research-based education that advances agriculture, the environment, human health and well-being, youth and communities, while creating prosperity for all North Carolinians. Working for NC State Extension allows you to teach without being tied to a classroom and school bell.<br><br><strong>The position may be hired at the Assistant, Associate, or Full Agent level if the selected candidate meets the required qualifications.</strong><br><br>While our employees are located in NC County offices, they reap the benefits of employment at NC State University. Some of our benefits include:<br><br>· Paid time off<br><br>· Paid tuition<br><br>· Health Insurance<br><br>· Flexible Scheduling<br><br>· Retirement Savings<br><br>· Supplemental Benefits Programs<br><br>· WolfPerks!<br><br>You Belong Here! At NC State, our goal is for all employees to reach their fullest potential at work. As you consider this opportunity, we encourage you to review our <a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>Employee Value Proposition</strong></a> (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/</strong></a>) and learn more about what makes NC State the best place to learn and work for everyone.<br> <br>Essential responsibilities for the position include planning, marketing, conducting and evaluating educational programs in Family and Consumer Sciences (FCS) for Cumberland County.<br> <br> Emphasis of the Family and Consumer Sciences program is on nutrition, food safety, health and wellness, local foods and cooking, chronic disease reduction, and volunteer management. The position will conduct viable, proactive research-based educational programs in these areas for both adult and youth audiences. The position will collaborate with Extension agents in the region on common educational program initiatives. All agents are expected to have a youth component in their programming.<br> <br> <strong>Primary Function of Organizational Unit</strong><br> North Carolina Cooperative Extension gives our residents easy access to the resources and expertise of NC State University and NC A&amp;T State University. Through educational programs, publications and events, Cooperative Extension agents deliver unbiased, research-based information to North Carolina citizens.<br> <br>To learn more NC Cooperative Extension, Cumberland County Center go to: <a href=\"https://cumberland.ces.ncsu.edu/\"><strong>https://cumberland.ces.ncsu.edu/</strong></a></div> 117606 - South Central District - Cumbe https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233881 2026-08-17T09:52:16-04:00 2026-08-17T09:52:17-04:00 Temporary Food Service Server - Columbia, NC <div><strong>Job Responsibilities include:</strong></div><ul><li>Assisting cooks with preparing meals</li><li>Help with cleaning areas such as kitchen, dining room and dining hall</li><li>Serve meals to guests</li><li>Refill food/beverage stations</li><li>Bus tables</li></ul> 112402 - Eastern 4H Environmental Education Conference https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/225719 2025-12-11T14:16:55-05:00 2025-12-11T14:16:56-05:00 Swine Extension Program Associate <div>The North Carolina State University Department of Animal Science is seeking a Swine Extension Program Associate. This position will be primarily responsible for working with NCSU scientists to develop and deliver swine educational opportunities throughout North Carolina and beyond Specific extension programming efforts include organizing, managing, and assisting with NCSU Swine Innovation Forum, NC Pork Conference, NC State Fair Swine Show, regional swine meetings and the development of electronic educational materials for stakeholders.<br><br>This position will lead outreach &amp; producer &amp; public communications efforts, including newsletters, a swine extension website, and social media platforms. The position will generate and collate extension impact data and reports. The position will also assist with the management of applied research projects on both university and industry-owned pig farms relative to project data collection, validation, summary, and dissemination of reports.<br><br><strong>Duties include, but are not limited to:</strong><br><br></div><ul><li>Process daily requests for educational assistance from stakeholders and county staff</li><li>Use technology and web-based data systems to complete work assignments</li><li>Strong interpersonal communication skills</li><li>Complete daily work to meet established deadlines for swine research projects on university and industry-owned swine farms.</li><li>Disseminate program information at Swine Research Forum, NC Pork Conference, Regional Swine Conferences, and NC State Fair.</li><li>Collect research data using data management system to maintain, manage, and monitor research project data and compile summary reports based on specific requests</li><li>Collect research data, organize and disseminate reports to faculty</li><li>Respond to requests from faculty to report factual information and provide verbal and written reports</li></ul><div><br></div><div>You Belong Here! At NC State, our goal is for all employees to reach their fullest potential at work. As you consider this opportunity, we encourage you to review our <a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>Employee Value Proposition</strong></a> (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/</strong></a>) and learn more about what makes NC State the best place to learn and work for everyone.</div> 110701 - Animal Science https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234433 2026-08-31T16:59:06-04:00 2026-08-31T16:59:07-04:00 Lead Project Manager <div>In this role, you will provide project and change management expertise to lead complex, cross-team initiatives across DELTA. This role brings structure, collaboration, and organizational excellence to projects that span technology, instructional development, media production, marketing, communications, support, governance, and other strategic priorities. The Lead Project Manager independently manages high-impact projects, coordinates teams and resources, advises colleagues, and helps advance organizational excellence practices across DELTA and the university. Reporting to the Director, Project Management, this position works in a collaborative, team-based environment and serves as a trusted resource for project management, change management, coaching, and organizational improvement.</div><div><br><strong>Key Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Lead the prioritization, definition, coordination, and management of complex DELTA projects, processes, and initiatives involving multiple teams, deliverables, resources, and organizational impacts.</li><li>Partner with development teams, production managers, faculty, clients, vendors, and university colleagues to define project scope, goals, deliverables, dependencies, resources, and timelines.</li><li>Analyze proposed projects and resource requirements to support effective prioritization and organizational planning.</li><li>Develop project schedules, timelines, reports, and workflows that promote visibility, accountability, and effective resource management.</li><li>Facilitate feasibility discussions, resolve competing priorities, negotiate deadlines, and bring teams together to address challenges related to scope, resources, timelines, and deliverables.</li><li>Lead teams and groups without formal supervisory authority, providing direction, coaching, and informal leadership to colleagues.</li><li>Manages and mediates complex conversations involving competing priorities, differing perspectives, sensitive topics, or organizational tensions with sound judgment and professionalism.</li><li>Serve as a subject-matter expert and thought leader in project and change management, applying methodologies and practices such as Agile, Scrum, Prosci, and ADKAR.</li><li>Collaborate with senior leadership to align projects and organizational practices with DELTA’s strategic goals.</li><li>Research, evaluate, and implement emerging project and change management tools, techniques, and practices to improve efficiency and effectiveness.</li><li>Administer DELTA’s project management system and lead related training, updates, and process improvements.</li><li>Review and recommend improvements to procedures, workflows, and project management practices.</li><li>Provide consultation, training, presentations, and guidance on project and change management across DELTA and the university.</li><li>Represent DELTA and NC State University on campus committees, interest groups, conferences, and presentations, sharing project and organizational excellence expertise.</li><li>Build and maintain strong professional relationships across DELTA and the university while fostering effective collaboration and communication.</li><li>Mentor new project managers and part-time employees and, as needed, provide oversight of part-time staff.<br><br></li></ul><div>The successful candidate will bring excellent written and verbal communication skills, strong interpersonal and organizational abilities, and the confidence to communicate effectively with individuals and groups at all levels. You will be comfortable coordinating across technical, design, instructional, production, and administrative teams and working with internal and external clients and vendors. Experience with web-based collaboration tools and project management platforms such as ClickUp or Monday is highly valued. Most importantly, you will bring the ability to lead through influence, navigate competing priorities, build productive relationships, and use project and change management expertise to move complex work forward.</div> 339001 - DELTA https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/229507 2026-04-14T12:10:55-04:00 2026-04-14T12:10:56-04:00 Temporary Lab and Field Technician <div>The position will be responsible for completing the objectives for the grant entitled “What Practices Provide the Best Economic Return for Area Wide Management of <em>Helicoverpa zea</em>”. This will include rearing <em>H. zea</em>, releasing beneficial insects with a UAV, and pheromone disruption of <em>H. zea</em> in corn.</div> 111801 - Entomology and Plant Pathology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232058 2026-06-24T08:07:43-04:00 2026-06-24T08:07:44-04:00 Lecturer <div>The Department of Psychology is seeking a lecturer to teach psychology courses. The Professional Faculty is expected to work successfully with students, other faculty and industry partners.</div> 164501 - Psychology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233419 2026-08-03T08:59:21-04:00 2026-08-03T08:59:21-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>The Department of Forestry and Environmental Resources has a wide variety of strengths in forestry and natural resources management, policy and economics, environmental science and technology, wildlife and conservation biology, tree improvement, silviculture, forest biotechnology, and international forestry. The Department has over 90 full time faculty and staff, and more than 750 undergraduate and 150 graduate students. We are committed to fostering a collaborative environment where innovation, respect, and excellence&nbsp; in education, extension, and research. Faculty, staff, and students work together with business, industry, government, community groups, and non-government organizations to address a wide range of challenges facing our state and the world. These partnerships facilitate research and discovery, teaching and learning, and outreach and service. Additional information about the department can be found at http://cnr.ncsu.edu/fer.<br><br>The College of Natural Resources (CNR) at North Carolina State University is a world leader in the sustainable use of natural resources. The college’s comprehensive approach to teaching, research, and service encompass the many and varied ways in which people’s physical, economic, and social needs are met through the wise use of natural resources.<br><br><strong>Duties and responsibilities include:</strong></div><ul><li>Being a productive member of the Tree Physiology and Ecosystem Science Lab;</li><li>Collaborating with USDA Forest Service EFETAC;</li><li>Disseminating research results through publications and presentations at scientific meetings;</li><li>Contributing to grant proposal writing;</li><li>Providing teaching and other services as required and opportunities arise.</li></ul> 150401 - Forestry & Environmental Resources Academic Research https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223261 2025-09-25T11:11:12-04:00 2025-09-25T11:11:13-04:00 Fire/Life Safety Special Events - Fire Watch <div>Perform fire watch and assist with life safety programs at special events, to include sporting events. Respond to fire related incidents as requested.</div> 427201 -  Fire and Life Safety https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234148 2026-08-25T16:12:46-04:00 2026-08-25T16:12:47-04:00 Extension Program Assistant-Agriculture <div>Are you looking for a rewarding career that will let you give back to your community? NC State Extension extends research-based knowledge to all North Carolinians, helping them transform science into everyday solutions that improve their lives and grow our state. We are the model of excellence in non-formal, research-based education that advances agriculture, the environment, human health and well-being, youth and communities, while creating prosperity for all North Carolinians. Working for NC State Extension allows you to teach without being tied to a classroom and school bell.</div><div><br></div><div>While our employees are located in NC County offices, they reap the benefits of employment at NC State University. Some of our benefits include:</div><ul><li>Paid time off</li><li>Paid tuition</li><li>Health Insurance</li><li>Flexible Scheduling</li><li>Retirement Savings</li><li>Supplemental Benefits Programs</li><li>WolfPerks!</li></ul><div><br></div><div>You Belong Here! At NC State, our goal is for all employees to reach their fullest potential at work. As you consider this opportunity, we encourage you to review our <a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>Employee</strong></a><strong> </strong><a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>Value Proposition</strong></a><strong> </strong>(<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/</strong></a>) and learn more about what makes NC State the best place to learn and work for everyone.</div><div><br>This position is a full-time (.94 FTE), 37.5-hour per week position. The position is 100% county funded.<br><br></div><div>The Extension Ag Program Assistant is a member of the staff of North Carolina Cooperative Extension (NCCE). The Ag Program Assistant’s primary responsibility is to provide support to the Extension staff where assigned. The Ag Program Assistant cooperates with colleagues in the NCCE and county leaders in performing assigned responsibilities. The performance of assigned responsibilities is governed by the philosophy, objectives, policies, and procedures of the NCCE.</div> 117108 - West District - Henderson https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228011 2026-03-03T15:38:19-05:00 2026-03-03T15:38:19-05:00 Temporary Data and AI at Work Instructor <div>The Data Science and AI Academy is the first of several NC State Academies, which are true university-wide efforts involving all 10 colleges. The Data Science and AI Academy’s goal is to network and catalyze data science across all three pillars of the university’s land-grant mission: education, research and service to the state of North Carolina. At NC State, data science is for everyone.</div><div><br></div><div>The Data Science and AI Academy offers 1-credit project-based courses and customized non-credit courses on a variety of topics grounded in our <a href=\"https://datascienceacademy.ncsu.edu/courses/course-model/\"><strong>ADAPT course model</strong></a> (All Campus Data Science Accessible Project-based Teaching and Learning). We seek instructors from industry, government and academia who are excited about broadening participation in data science and welcoming learners from many disciplines.</div><div><br></div><div>Our courses attract faculty and staff from across the university as well as learners outside of the university. Instructors will join a professional teaching community that meets twice monthly to discuss data science teaching and learning research and practice.<br><br>Data at Work and AI at Work courses are developed and include consultation with the client. They have minimal lecturing and address problems, tasks and challenges from the clients work environment, using the same, simulated or adjacent data as that found in the workplace. Learners engage in practice and/or projects relevant to their daily work. Data at Work and AI at Work (not-for-credit) courses have a customized schedule.</div><div><br>The successful candidate(s) will develop and teach customized, active, hands-on, project-based non-credit courses in Data Science and AI.</div><div><br></div><div>Examples of Past Data at Work and AI at Work Courses:</div><ul><li>A Hands on Introduction to AI and Machine Learning,</li><li>Exploration of AI Uses in Human Resources,</li><li>Data at Work for Extension Agents,</li><li>Data Science Bootcamp for Incoming Graduate Students.</li><li>Advanced Excel and Power BI for government employees</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Appointments will be for on a contract basis with the possibility of renewal.</strong></div><div><br></div><div><strong>Note that this position is only for teaching on a temporary and part-time basis.</strong></div> 231010 - Data Science Academy https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234641 2026-09-03T12:09:42-04:00 2026-09-03T12:09:43-04:00 Carpenter <div>Are you a skilled carpenter who takes pride in quality craftsmanship and creating functional, well-built spaces? We are seeking a talented <strong>Carpenter</strong> to join our <strong>Building Maintenance and Operations team</strong>. <strong>In this role, you will perform a variety of construction, renovation, repair, and installation projects that support the upkeep and improvement of campus facilities.</strong> Your work will directly impact the appearance, safety, and functionality of buildings and spaces used every day.<br><br></div><div>This position is ideal for an experienced trades professional who enjoys hands-on work, problem-solving, and delivering high-quality results. From <strong>renovations and space reconfigurations to finish carpentry and fixture installation</strong>, you'll have the opportunity to apply your skills across a wide range of projects while collaborating with other skilled trades professionals.<br><br></div><div>​If you're ready to put your carpentry skills to work in a dynamic environment where your craftsmanship makes a lasting impact, <strong>we encourage you to apply today. Join a team that values quality, professionalism, safety, and continuous improvement while helping build and maintain exceptional facilities for our community.</strong></div><div><strong><br></strong><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li>Perform carpentry work related to renovations, repairs, alterations, and construction projects, including the installation and finishing of drywall, ceilings, flooring, cabinetry, countertops, and related building components.</li><li><strong>Install and repair doors, windows, trim, millwork, hardware, partitions, and other interior and exterior building features.</strong></li><li>Modify and reconfigure existing spaces by removing or constructing walls, installing partitions, and making structural and non-structural alterations to support facility needs.</li><li><strong>Read and interpret blueprints, drawings, specifications, and work orders to ensure accurate project execution and compliance with design requirements.</strong></li><li>Utilize a variety of hand tools, power tools, and construction equipment to complete projects safely, efficiently, and with a high level of craftsmanship.</li><li><strong>Identify facility-related issues associated with renovation and construction activities and recommend practical solutions to support project objectives.</strong></li><li>Collaborate with project managers, supervisors, and other trades personnel to coordinate work, resolve construction challenges, and ensure projects are completed on time and within scope.</li><li>Maintain compliance with applicable building codes, safety regulations, quality standards, and material specifications.</li><li><strong>Apply knowledge of carpentry and related building trades to support facility maintenance and construction efforts while contributing to a collaborative team environment.</strong></li><li>​Safely climb and work from different types of ladders to access elevated or below-grade areas (such as roofs, above-ceiling spaces, and utility pits). Be exposed to and work in areas where insects or small animals are occasionally present. All essential tasks must be performed with or without reasonable accommodations.</li><li>Perform lifting duties up to 30 pounds frequently and 60 pounds occasionally, with or without reasonable accommodations</li><li>Communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, and the general public, and follows verbal and written instructions related to work assignments and operational needs.</li></ul> 445030 - Building Maintenance & Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233183 2026-07-27T14:26:41-04:00 2026-07-27T14:26:41-04:00 Grounds Crew <div>The <a href=\"https://lonniepoolegolfcourse.com/\"><strong>Lonnie Poole Golf Course</strong></a> is seeking reliable and energetic individuals to Join our Pack as a <strong>Groundskeeper</strong>.<br><br>Essential Job Duties:</div><ul><li>Landscaping and sodding</li><li>Applying fertilizer and pesticides on and around the course under the direct supervision of an employee who is licensed in Ornamental &amp; Turf Pest Control</li><li>Cutting grass on the tee boxes, fairways, roughs, fringes and greens</li><li>Golf course upkeep</li><li>Sprinkler system maintenance, fencing and tree trimming</li></ul><div><br>Equipment used includes, but not limited to, walking and riding mowers, tee mowers, fairway mowers, rough mowers, rollers, bunker rakes, carts, push mowers and a variety of powered and non-powered hand tools.<br><br>As a temporary employee, you are eligible for the following <a href=\"https://onboarding.ncsu.edu/other-employee-perks/#temp\"><strong>temporary employee perks</strong></a> designed to enhance your personal and professional well-being.<br><br>This is an hourly position, that includes a State required 32-day break at the end of 11 months. Superior work performance may provide an opportunity to renew employment and/or advance into a benefits eligible permanent position.</div> 470101 - Campus Enterprises https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/222753 2025-09-10T09:52:51-04:00 2025-09-10T09:52:52-04:00 Temporary Part-time IEP Instructor <div>The Intensive English Program (IEP) serves as a gateway to undergraduate programs by offering intensive English training for conditionally admitted international students who are academically competitive but have not yet met the English proficiency requirements.<br><br>Temporary Part-time IEP instructors can teach any number of hours of skills-based ESL classes with additional program-related responsibilities in a given semester. IEP instructors responsibilities may include but are not limited to, curriculum development, text selection, test administration, and end-of-term ceremonies.<br><br>•Teach non-credit skill-based ESL courses for academic purposes in short-term programs and/or regular 15-week semester<br><br>•Participate in prep week activities and other IEP instructor meetings, as scheduled throughout the semester<br><br>•Create lesson plans designed to meet curricular goals and objectives<br><br>•Begin and dismiss assigned classes at the appointed hours<br><br>•Follow curriculum guidelines for each class<br><br>•Adhere to lead-instructor created assignments, quizzes, tests, and exams<br><br>•Hold regularly scheduled office hours and/or be available to meet with students when requested<br><br>•Maintain Moodle page for each assigned class; Enter grades into Moodle in a timely fashion to keep students aware of where they stand academically<br><br>•Other duties as assigned.</div> 390601 - Intensive English Program https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234038 2026-08-21T08:49:04-04:00 2026-08-21T08:49:05-04:00 Landscape Construction Project Manager <div>Do you have a passion for landscape construction and enjoy turning plans into well-executed projects? NC State University is seeking a <strong>Landscape Construction Project Manager</strong> to help shape and improve the campus landscape through thoughtful planning, strong project management, and a commitment to quality.<br><br>In this role, you will manage landscape construction projects from planning and design through construction and final closeout. You’ll collaborate with University project managers, designers, consultants, contractors, and Facilities leadership to keep projects on schedule, within budget, and aligned with university standards. Your expertise in landscape construction, horticulture, hardscaping, irrigation, and stormwater systems will help ensure projects are built to last and successfully transition into ongoing campus maintenance. This is an opportunity to see your work become part of the NC State campus while working with a collaborative team dedicated to creating safe, functional, beautiful, and sustainable outdoor spaces.<br><br><strong>If you’re ready to bring your landscape construction expertise to projects that have a lasting impact on the NC State campus, we encourage you to apply!</strong></div><div><br></div><div><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li>Manage landscape construction projects from planning and design through construction and final closeout, monitoring <strong>scope, schedules, budgets, milestones, and overall project progress</strong>.</li><li>Use AiM, shared documentation, and team collaboration tools to track project information and maintain accurate project records.</li><li>Identify and resolve project challenges and operational roadblocks while adapting project scope to meet university priorities.</li><li>Develop, review, and update <strong>Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs)</strong> to promote consistent and efficient project delivery.</li><li>Manage project documentation, including contracts, competitive bids, product warranties, project records, and final closeout materials.</li><li>Serve as the primary point of contact for project-related communication and provide timely, clear updates to stakeholders and leadership.</li><li>Collaborate with <strong>University Project and Design Managers, designers, consultants, contractors, and internal Facilities teams</strong> throughout planning, design, and construction.</li><li>Provide concise project status, milestone, and issue updates during weekly leadership meetings.</li><li>Build strong working relationships with Grounds Leadership, Landscape Construction Services, Waste Reduction and Recycling, Grounds Planning, and other campus partners.</li><li>Participate in design reviews and evaluate project plans to ensure alignment with university standards and industry best practices.</li><li>Apply knowledge of <strong>landscape construction, plant and tree identification, hardscape construction, commercial irrigation systems, and stormwater control measures (SCMs)</strong>.</li><li>Conduct project site inspections to verify construction meets approved designs, specifications, quality standards, and campus expectations.</li><li>Identify construction issues and coordinate solutions with project partners to maintain quality and project objectives.</li><li>Lead project closeout activities, including final inspections and walk-throughs, verification and filing of as-built drawings, and transition of completed projects to the Grounds Maintenance team.</li></ul> 445060 - Landscape Maintenance & Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/227251 2026-02-09T14:00:09-05:00 2026-02-09T14:00:10-05:00 Lecturer, Accounting <div>The Department of Accounting is accepting applications for the position of Non-Tenure Track Faculty Lecturer (part-time). Non-Tenure Track Faculty positions are one-semester or one-year, with the possibility of renewal. Applications will remain on file for one year should openings arise in subsequent semesters.<br><br>Successful candidates will teach 1 to 3 courses for the BSA (Bachelor of Science in Accounting), MAC (Masters of Accounting) and/or MBA (Masters of Business Administration). Teaching is in one of the following areas: accounting information systems, auditing, business/data analytics, business law, financial accounting, intermediate accounting, managerial accounting, taxation, and not for profit/government accounting.<br><br>Specific hiring needs for the upcoming academic year have yet to be determined.</div> 201001 - Accounting - Poole College of Management https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234537 2026-09-02T12:30:18-04:00 2026-09-02T12:30:19-04:00 Director of Information Technology <div>Are you a visionary technology leader who is passionate about driving innovation, building high-performing teams, and advancing academic excellence through technology? We are seeking a <strong>Director of Information Technology</strong> to lead and oversee IT operations for the College of Sciences and play a pivotal role in shaping the future of technology across teaching, research, and administration.<br><br></div><div>In this influential leadership position, The Director of Information Technology will direct, manage, and provide strategic technology support for faculty, staff, postdoctoral researchers, and students across six academic departments. The Director serves as a trusted advisor, technology advocate, and change leader, ensuring IT resources effectively support the College's mission and goals.</div><div><br></div><div>Reporting the Dean of the College of Sciences, the Director of Information Technology will be at the forefront of technological advancement, helping create innovative solutions that enhance instruction, research, and operational excellence. The Director will lead a talented team of IT professionals while collaborating with university leadership to develop forward-thinking technology strategies that support the evolving needs of higher education.</div><div><strong><br>What You'll Do</strong></div><div><strong>Drive Strategic Technology Leadership</strong></div><ul><li>Develop and execute IT strategies aligned with the College's mission and long-term objectives.</li><li>Lead strategic planning, innovation initiatives, and resource allocation.</li><li>Identify emerging technologies that enhance teaching, research, and administrative effectiveness.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Oversee IT Operations &amp; Infrastructure</strong></div><ul><li>Manage the deployment, maintenance, upgrade, and support of hardware, software, servers, operating systems, and networked resources.</li><li>Ensure IT systems remain reliable, secure, scalable, and responsive to user needs.</li><li>Support instructional and administrative computing services throughout the College.</li><li>Maintain clear and consistent communication with leadership, faculty, staff, and end users regarding technology initiatives.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Lead and Develop High-Performing Teams</strong></div><ul><li>Provide leadership, mentorship, and professional development opportunities for IT staff.</li><li>Foster a collaborative, service-oriented team culture.</li><li>Oversee recruitment, retention, training, and supervision of technical support personnel, including student employees.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Manage Budgets and Technology Investments</strong></div><ul><li>Develop and administer annual IT budgets.</li><li>Ensure effective stewardship of resources and maximize return on technology investments.</li><li>Lead procurement efforts for IT hardware, software, and services.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Champion Security, Compliance &amp; Continuity</strong></div><ul><li>Establish and enforce policies that safeguard the integrity, privacy, and security of institutional data and technology assets.</li><li>Ensure compliance with applicable regulations and industry standards.</li><li>Lead disaster recovery and business continuity planning efforts.</li><li>Provide strategic guidance on technology needs for classrooms, laboratories, buildings, and future facilities.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Lead Technology Projects</strong></div><ul><li>Oversee complex IT projects from planning through implementation.</li><li>Deliver projects on time, within scope, and within budget.</li><li>Collaborate with departments to identify technology needs and implement effective solutions.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Build Strong Partnerships</strong></div><ul><li>Serve as the primary IT resource for faculty, staff, and departmental leadership.</li><li>Collaborate with vendors, service providers, and campus partners to ensure exceptional technology support.</li><li>Provide proactive guidance for instructional, research, and administrative technology needs.</li></ul><div><br></div><div>This role extends beyond departmental leadership. The Director will represent and advocate for the College's technology interests on key university-wide committees and initiatives, including:</div><ul><li>IT Strategic Advisory Committee</li><li>Academic IT Directors Committee</li><li>Campus IT Directors Committee</li><li>Data Science and AI Initiatives</li><li>Other University Technology Governance Groups</li></ul><div><strong><br></strong>This is an exceptional opportunity for a forward-thinking technology leader to shape and advance the College's technology strategy. The ideal candidate is a strategic thinker, collaborative leader, and skilled IT professional who can balance operational excellence with innovation. They excel in dynamic environments, build strong relationships across varied stakeholder groups, and are committed to delivering exceptional technology services that support teaching, learning, research, and administrative success. Through leadership, vision, and partnership, this individual will help drive transformative technology initiatives that advance the College's mission and position it for the future.</div><div><br></div><div>If you're ready to lead transformative technology initiatives, inspire a talented team, and make a lasting impact on the future of education and research, we encourage you to apply today and help drive innovation, collaboration, and excellence through technology.</div> 170109 - COS - IT https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230485 2026-05-11T15:02:10-04:00 2026-05-11T15:02:10-04:00 Temporary Agroecology Lab Coordinator <div>The Agroecology Lab Coordinator will work under the supervision of the Agroecology Professor to support the establishment and growth of the Agroecology Lab, the undergraduate Agroecology Major, and the Agroecology Education Farm. The coordinator will assist in a variety of tasks aimed at enhancing the functionality and efficacy of the program while contributing to ongoing agroecological research and education projects. The coordinator will play a key role in developing and fostering relationships with farmers, private and public sector actors in the region to support collaborative research and community-based outreach efforts. The coordinator will collaborate with the Agroecology Education Farm manager and Sustainable Agriculture Systems Unit technician at the Lake Wheeler Field Lab to ensure smooth project execution and coordination across various activities. The coordinator will also assist in reviewing and summarizing relevant scientific literature, drafting reports and educational materials to enhance agroecology curriculum. <br><br><strong>Responsibilities will likely include:</strong></div><ul><li>Network with organizations in the public sector (e.g., non-profits, community-based entities) striving to improve access to food and education</li><li>Create outreach and education materials, such as videos, social media posts, brochures for the Agroecology Major, the Agroecology Education Farm, and the Sustainable Agriculture Systems Unit at the Lake Wheeler Field Lab</li><li>Assist with the implementation of long-term demonstration cropping systems at the Sustainable Agriculture Systems Unit</li><li>Assist with grant-writing</li><li>Set up long-term data collection and automation at the Sustainable Agriculture Systems Unit, such as an on-site weather station, soil moisture probes, harvest record-keeping, irrigation systems.&nbsp;</li><li>Maintain Agroecology Lab and Agroecology Education Farm websites</li><li>Lead administrative tasks for Agroecology Education Farm tours</li></ul><div><br>This position will be best served by a good communicator with strong computer and organizational skills. It is expected that the person serving in this role will develop skills and knowledge on the job so a good attitude, strong interest in sustainable agriculture, and enthusiasm to learn will be essential.&nbsp;</div> 110901 - Crop and Soil Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232618 2026-07-10T09:14:49-04:00 2026-07-10T09:14:49-04:00 Research Scholar <div>The School Planning and Transportation group supports Local Education Authorities (LEAs) in comprehensive planning for schools and communities (SchoolCAMP), enrollment, facilities and boundary planning (OREd) and school transportation (Bike/Ped, Transit, School Bus). This position will support multiple initiatives at ITRE that support local, state and national Public Pre-Kindergarten to 12 th grade school district operational needs through technical expertise and project leadership. This position combines demographics, transportation, land use, and community needs utilizing current best practices in K12 Operations and quality Applied Research. This position will support SchoolCAMP and OREd, research and public sector planning consulting programs designed to assist LEAs with enrollment forecasting, comprehensive planning, alternatives development, impact analysis, and implementation of school facility development and boundary planning that is sensitive to local community needs and school transportation concerns. The successful individual will facilitate quality, trusting relationships with LEA leadership and staff, school board members, community partners, local elected officials, industry advocacy groups, and the research community. The person in this position is expected to support relationships with these key stakeholders and leverage these relationships to acquire future funding to sustain and grow the program, all while providing excellent service to existing partners.</div> 062001 - Institute For Transportation Research & Education https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233683 2026-08-10T12:05:37-04:00 2026-08-10T12:05:38-04:00 Research Technician <div>Join the Cotton Genetics and Breeding Lab and contribute to research that advances cotton genetics, genomics, and plant breeding. As a Research Technician, you will provide essential technical and operational support across field, greenhouse, growth chamber, laboratory, and seed-processing environments. Working alongside faculty, graduate students, postdoctoral researchers, and collaborators, you will help ensure research activities are conducted safely, accurately, and efficiently while supporting the development and evaluation of improved cotton breeding materials.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Research Operations &amp; Technical Support</strong></div><ul><li>Support cotton genetics and breeding research by preparing field plots, greenhouse materials, and controlled-environment facilities for planting, phenotyping, harvesting, and research trials.</li><li>Perform mechanical and manual planting, harvesting, ginning, acid-delinting, seed processing, and sample preparation while advancing and maintaining breeding populations through controlled crosses and self-pollination activities.</li><li>Collect, measure, and record phenotypic and experimental data from field, greenhouse, growth chamber, and laboratory studies to support ongoing research projects.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Data Management &amp; Research Compliance</strong></div><ul><li>Maintain accurate electronic records for phenotypic data, seed inventories, experimental documentation, and research activities using tools such as Excel and Google Sheets.</li><li>Prepare and maintain documentation related to chemical storage, sample tracking, laboratory safety, and equipment use while ensuring compliance with institutional research and Environmental Health and Safety (EHS) requirements.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Equipment, Facilities &amp; Resource Management</strong></div><ul><li>Operate, maintain, and perform basic troubleshooting of field equipment, laboratory instruments, ginning and acid-delinting machinery, irrigation tools, and state vehicles.</li><li>Manage research resources by organizing seed stocks, supplies, greenhouse and field materials, and laboratory inventories to support efficient research operations.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Collaboration &amp; Field Operations</strong></div><ul><li>Travel to research stations and off-campus facilities to assist with experimental setup, plot layout, planting, crop maintenance, data collection, and harvest activities.</li><li>Coordinate daily research activities with cooperating scientists, graduate students, postdoctoral researchers, research station staff, collaborators, and visiting scientists to support timely project completion and effective program operations.</li></ul> 110901 - Crop and Soil Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223343 2025-09-25T12:07:49-04:00 2025-09-25T12:07:49-04:00 NCJC Language Instructor <div>The Japanese Language Extension Instructor shall teach varying levels of non-credit Japanese language courses. Instructors shall teach a minimum of one course per semester for a total minimum of three courses per year. Each course lasts fifteen weeks, with one class per week and each class lasts 2 hours. The only exception is a special weekday course offering that lasts ten weeks, with two classes per week and each class lasts 90 minutes. Based on experience and interest, instructors will be assigned to courses ranging from beginner level up to preparation for the JLPT N1 exam. Textbooks vary for each course and will be chosen by NC Japan Center management. Based on student demand Instructor availability, Instructors may teach more than one class per week, but the total number of weekly hours shall be fewer than twenty hours.</div> 390401 - Japan Center https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234336 2026-08-28T15:26:45-04:00 2026-08-28T15:26:46-04:00 Assistant Professor - Plant Pathology <div>The Department of Entomology and Plant Pathology seeks a tenure-track Assistant Professor to advance its undergraduate educational mission in plant pathology while developing a complementary research program. <strong>This is a 9-month faculty position that has split responsibilities of 60% teaching and 40% research.</strong>The role provides leadership in delivering and evolving the undergraduate plant pathology curriculum, integrating active learning, case-based instruction, emerging technologies, and innovative approaches to prepare students for careers and continued study in the discipline. The role includes approximately two to three courses annually, spanning foundational plant pathology instruction, team-taught and specialized courses, and the development of undergraduate and distance education offerings.&nbsp;<br><br></div><div>In this role, you will help shape how plant pathology is taught, experienced, and applied across the curriculum while building a research program that complements the department’s strengths. Your scholarship may engage areas such as plant disease diagnostics and management, fungal biology, soilborne pathogens, plant disease ecology, plant–soil–microbe systems, or epidemiology. You will have opportunities to develop innovative teaching and research programs, create meaningful experiential learning experiences, and foster collaboration across disciplines while engaging students in discovery and inquiry.<br><br></div><div>Key Responsibilities fall within the following functional areas:</div><div><strong>Teaching and Mentoring:</strong></div><ul><li>Deliver and modernize core undergraduate plant pathology curriculum, including PP 315 (Principles of Plant Pathology) and its laboratory component, through innovative approaches such as active learning, case studies, emerging technologies, and distance education.</li><li>Contribute to graduate, team-taught, and specialized courses while helping shape curriculum innovation and assessment across the program.</li><li>Create meaningful opportunities for students to connect classroom learning with research, discovery, and other experiential learning.</li><li>Mentor undergraduate researchers, graduate students, and postdoctoral scholars in their academic and professional development.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Research and Discovery:</strong></div><ul><li>Build and sustain an active, externally visible research program that complements departmental strengths and advances knowledge in plant pathology.</li><li>Develop collaborative research that engages students and connects teaching, discovery, and emerging areas such as soilborne pathogens, fungal biology, and plant–soil–microbe interactions.</li><li>Pursue scholarly work, external support, and partnerships that expand the reach and impact of your research.</li><li>Contribute to a culture of inquiry and discovery across the department and broader scientific community.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong><br>Department Service and Professional Engagement:</strong></div><ul><li>Contribute to department, college, and university priorities through service, faculty governance, and academic leadership.</li><li>Foster interdisciplinary collaborations and professional partnerships that strengthen teaching, research, and engagement.</li><li>Engage with the broader scientific community through professional leadership, manuscript and grant review, and scholarly activities.</li><li>Support outreach and initiatives that enhance the visibility and impact of the department and university while fostering a collegial and productive academic environment.</li></ul><div><br></div><div>If you are an innovative educator, collaborative researcher, and engaged academic who is passionate about advancing plant pathology education and discovery, we encourage you to apply.</div><div><br></div> 111801 - Entomology and Plant Pathology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228711 2026-03-24T10:47:42-04:00 2026-03-24T10:47:43-04:00 CLAWS Electronics Test Engineer <div>We are seeking a highly skilled Test Engineer to join our team and contribute to the development of next-generation GaN semiconductor devices. The ideal candidate will have expertise in Power or RF semiconductor device test, preferably with deep hands-on experience testing RF devices.<br><br></div><div>Key Responsibilities<br><br></div><ul><li><strong>Execute on-wafer and packaged device tests in state-of-the-art CLAWS test facility (50%)</strong><ul><li>RF devices, including amplifiers, passives, and MMICs. Small signal (S-parameter), load-pull, pulsed I-V, DC characteristics, parametric test, thermal effects.</li><li>Power devices, including transistors and diodes. DC and switching characteristics of high voltage and high current electronics.</li><li>Go/No-Go electrical testing of process control monitors.</li><li>Reconfigure test equipment to support varied test specifications.</li></ul></li></ul><div><br></div><ul><li><strong>Implement new device/wafer characterization plans, methods for data reduction, and parameter extraction (30%)</strong><ul><li>Support design for test (DFT) initiatives that facilitate high throughput testing.</li><li>Develop and qualify new test capabilities.</li><li>Collaborate with MES engineers to integrate test capabilities with centralized data infrastructure.</li></ul></li></ul><div><br></div><ul><li><strong>Ensure the availability of test equipment and capabilities (20%)</strong><ul><li>Leverage statistical methods to define test capabilities such as GR&amp;R or MSA.</li><li>Oversee equipment calibrations and apply known-good standards regularly to demonstrate conformance with specifications.</li><li>Develop and document SOPs, training other users where necessary.</li><li>Maintain or repair test equipment with support from OEMs. Replace consumables.</li></ul></li></ul> 140110 - CLAWS https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234716 2026-09-04T13:12:39-04:00 2026-09-04T13:12:40-04:00 UTS - Temporary Accounting Technician at NC State <div>Duties to include (not limited to): Purchasing &amp; Accounts Payable, travel and expense reimbursements, p-card management, requisitions (small and large), Journal vouchers</div> 480501 - UHR Temporary Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231584 2026-06-10T16:42:37-04:00 2026-06-10T16:42:39-04:00 Carnivore Medicine Clinical Technician <div>Are you an advanced veterinary technician passionate about wildlife conservation and specialized nursing? The NC State College of Veterinary Medicine is seeking a Carnivore Medicine Clinical Technician to provide high-level, primary technical support for our Red Wolf program, alongside secondary support to our world-class Exotic Animal Medicine Clinical Service.<br><br></div><div>In this dynamic role, you will blend advanced clinical nursing, intricate anesthesia monitoring, and laboratory diagnostics with program administration and the mentorship of the next generation of veterinary professionals.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities<br>Advanced Clinical Nursing &amp; Anesthesia</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Anesthesia &amp; Sedation:</strong> Confidently administer and monitor anesthesia, precisely evaluating patient depth and vital signs using established parameters such as outward involuntary physical response (i.e. jaw tone, palpebral reflex, eye position), blood pressure, ECG, pulse oximetry, heart rate, respiratory rate, and ventilation status.</li><li><strong>Pharmacology Mastery:</strong> Demonstrate high proficiency in metric conversions, drug dose calculations, solution preparation, and IV fluid rates.</li><li><strong>Diagnostics:</strong> Run essential in-house lab testing, including PCV, Total Protein, Urinalysis, and comprehensive fecal analyses (direct smears, floats, gram stains, and acid-fast stains).</li></ul><div><br><strong>Program Administration &amp; Operations</strong></div><ul><li>Manage day-to-day operations for the Red Wolf program (scheduling, inventory control, and purchasing).</li><li>Ensure accurate clinical billing, treatment sheet compliance, and meticulous record-keeping.</li><li>Maintain medical equipment and ensure strict adherence to hospital Infectious Disease and safety protocols.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Communication &amp; Student Education</strong></div><ul><li>Assist in the clinical instruction, training, and orientation of veterinary students, vet tech students, and interns in carnivore restraint and nursing care.</li><li>Serve as a core communicator, fostering seamless collaboration between the Red Wolf program, exotic services, and all ancillary hospital departments.</li></ul><div><strong><br>What You Bring to the Team</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Experience:</strong> Strong background in advanced veterinary nursing; experience with wildlife, exotics, or carnivore medicine is highly preferred.</li><li><strong>Technical Skills:</strong> High proficiency in anesthetic monitoring, veterinary math/calculations, and laboratory diagnostics.</li><li><strong>Leadership &amp; Communication:</strong> Excellent interpersonal skills with a passion for teaching and mentoring students.</li><li><strong>Physical Capabilities:</strong> Ability to stand, bend, and assist with animal restraint for extended periods, and safely lift/carry up to 50 pounds.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Why NC State CVM?<br></strong>NC State is globally recognized for veterinary excellence. This position offers a rare opportunity to work directly with an endangered species recovery program while enjoying the robust professional resources, benefits, and collaborative culture of a premier academic medical center.</div> 192001 - CVM Teaching Hospital https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228122 2026-03-05T15:01:42-05:00 2026-03-05T15:01:43-05:00 Lecturer or Assistant Teaching Professor in Religious Studies <div>The Department of Philosophy and Religious Studies at NC State University invites applications for potential short-term teaching positions in Religious Studies during the 2026-27 academic year.&nbsp;<br><br>These positions may involve teaching one or more courses for a single semester or the full academic year, depending on departmental needs.&nbsp;<br><br>Courses that may require instructors include Religious Traditions of the World, Asian Religions, Introduction to the Old Testament, Introduction to the New Testament, The Hindu Tradition, The Buddhist Traditions, Islam, and other Religious Studies (REL) courses listed in the NC State course catalog.&nbsp;<br><br>We welcome applications from qualified candidates and will review submissions as teaching needs arise.</div> 160501 - Philosophy & Religious Studies https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234674 2026-09-03T13:26:29-04:00 2026-09-03T13:26:29-04:00 Social Media Manager, Enterprise Content <div>The Social Media Manager for Enterprise Content helps shape how NC State connects with its audiences across the university’s official social media channels. As a member of the Social Media Hub within University Communications and Marketing, this role manages the day-to-day creation, publishing and community engagement that bring the university’s stories to life. From faculty research and student experiences to major university events, the Social Media Manager turns complex ideas and moments into timely, engaging digital content.</div><div><br></div><div>In this hands-on role you will be a part of shaping the @NCState social presence, combining digital storytelling, short-form video production and community management to connect people with the university and with one another. You’ll have the opportunity to be where the action is through conceptualizing and capturing content, covering events in real time, collaborating with students, faculty, staff and alumni, and responding to the conversations happening across NC State’s social channels. The Wolfpack community and the university’s communication priorities will be strengthened through your efforts. <br><br>These efforts will include these key impact areas and responsibilities: <br><br><strong>Manage daily social content and community engagement</strong> across official enterprise channels, including Facebook, Instagram, LinkedIn, X, Threads, Bluesky, YouTube and TikTok.</div><div><strong>Create social-first stories and short-form video</strong> from concept through filming and editing, translating university priorities, research, events and experiences into compelling content.</div><div><strong>Capture the university in the moment</strong> through live social coverage of events and timely content that keeps audiences connected to what’s happening across NC State.</div><div><strong>Build meaningful community connections</strong> by monitoring conversations, trends and emerging issues; responding thoughtfully to comments and interactions; and appropriately managing sensitive content.</div><div><strong>Collaborate across the university</strong> with students, faculty, staff, alumni and student content creators to develop authentic, audience-focused content aligned with enterprise social strategy.</div><div><strong>Use social insights and performance data</strong> to understand what resonates with audiences and inform future content and engagement strategies.</div><div><br></div><div>If you’re a creative storyteller who thrives in a fast-paced environment, brings strong communication and collaboration skills, and enjoys finding new ways to connect people through social media, we encourage you to apply.</div> 281001 - Payroll https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230960 2026-05-27T13:45:17-04:00 2026-05-27T13:45:18-04:00 Wayne Williamson and Bette Rose Distinguished Professorship <div>The Department of Parks, Recreation and Tourism Management (PRTM) in the College of Natural Resources (CNR) at NC State invites applications for Professor for youth development through recreation and sports. We are seeking an interdisciplinary scholar and visionary leader who has a demonstrated record of success and impact, and is nationally and/or internationally recognized for their distinguished scholarly contributions. The position is a 9-month appointment with approximately 45%-Research, 45%-Teaching, and 10%-Service.<br><br>Applicants may be considered for the Wayne Williamson and Bette Rose Distinguished Professor. The Williamson and Rose Professor will be a leader in research, outreach and teaching related to youth development through recreation and sports. Appointment at the level of distinguished professor is at the discretion of the Chancellor.<br><br>The Department seeks candidates who have a highly productive research, teaching, and engagement record that focuses on youth development through sport and/or recreation. The ideal candidate should bring a strong theoretical, methodological, and community engagement background as well as strong evidence of external support and student mentoring. Attractive research programs could focus on youth sport, physical activity, environmental education, out-of-school time recreation, nature-based recreation, and other topics of national significance. In addition, ideal candidates should be active participants and leaders in conferences and symposia within their areas of specialization. Ideal candidates should also be able to contribute to teaching and curriculum innovation at the undergraduate and graduate level, as well as recruit and support graduate students and postdoctoral fellows.<br><br>Candidates must show development of an established record of nationally and/or internationally recognized, externally funded research programs. Additionally, candidates must show a willingness and ability to publish in leading academic outlets and present at topically relevant conferences, both domestically and internationally, that will advance the significance of a critical, sustainable, and community focused lens on youth development, youth leadership, and youth engagement. Robust and sustained engagement with relevant youth-serving organizations is expected.<br><br>The ideal candidate will demonstrate ability to build on interdisciplinary strengths within the College of Natural Resources and partnerships with the College of Agriculture and Life Science’s Department of Agricultural and Human Sciences, College of Humanities and Social Sciences’ Department of Sociology/Anthropology and Department of Psychology, Poole College of Management’s Department of Management, Innovation and Entrepreneurship, and College of Design’s Department of Landscape Architecture and Environmental Planning.<br><br>Responsibilities include:</div><ul><li>Expanding on an existing distinguished record of research, teaching and engagement;</li><li>Leading collaborative external research funding initiatives;</li><li>Instructing undergraduate and graduate courses, with a teaching load of two courses per semester in the fall and spring semesters;</li><li>Contributing to curricular development within PRTM and alongside other relevant campus collaborators;</li><li>Serving on and leading PRTM, CNR, and University-wide committees;</li><li>Engaging in relevant professional and public service activities; and,</li><li>Sustaining a robust network of community or industry stakeholders to which research and teaching outputs are disseminated.\\</li></ul> 150201 - Parks, Recreation & Tourism Management https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232915 2026-07-17T17:48:50-04:00 2026-07-17T17:48:51-04:00 Extension Agent, 4-H Youth Development (Assistant, Associate or Full Agent) <div>As a Pack member, <a href=\"https://hr.ncsu.edu/work-at-nc-state/employee-value-proposition/\"><strong>you belong here,</strong></a> and can enjoy <a href=\"https://benefits.hr.ncsu.edu/wellness-recognition-perks/wolfperks/\"><strong>exclusive perks</strong></a><strong> </strong>designed to enhance your personal and professional well-being. What we offer: Health Insurance Tuition waiver for university coursework <a href=\"https://onboarding.ncsu.edu/other-employee-perks/\"><strong>Attend</strong></a> non-revenue generating sporting events for free.<br> <br> Attain Work-life balance with our <a href=\"https://hr.ncsu.edu/benefits-and-wellness/child-care/\"><strong>Childcare discounts</strong></a>, <a href=\"https://wellrec.dasa.ncsu.edu/non-student-membership/\"><strong>Wellness &amp; Recreation Membership</strong></a>, and <a href=\"https://hr.ncsu.edu/benefits-and-wellness/wellness/\"><strong>Wellness Programs</strong></a> that aim to build a thriving wolfpack community.<br> <br> Disclaimer: Perks and Benefit eligibility is based on Part-Time or Full-Time Employment status. Eligibility and Employer Sponsored Plans can be found within each of the links offered.<br> <br> <strong>The position may be hired at the Assistant, Associate, or Full Agent level if the selected candidate meets the required qualifications.</strong><br> <br> <strong>Primary Function of Organizational Unit</strong><br> North Carolina Cooperative Extension gives our residents easy access to the resources and expertise of NC State University and NC A&amp;T State University. Through educational programs, publications, and events, Cooperative Extension field faculty deliver unbiased, research-based information to North Carolina citizens.<br> <br> The 4-H Agent provides leadership for the overall 4-H Youth program, planning, implementing, and evaluating initiatives for youth ages 5 to 19. In this role, the agent establishes and maintains traditional 4-H clubs, school enrichment, summer camps, and after-school programs that engage youth in positive life-skill development. The agent is also responsible for overseeing the mandated 4-H Military Program at Fort Bragg.&nbsp; The position will be responsible for working with adult volunteers to design, develop, implement and evaluate programs that meet the needs and interests of youth ages 5-19 to engage them in positive, informal life-skill development activities using a variety of educational methods such as organized 4-H clubs, school enrichment, 4-H summer programs, and after-school programs.<br> <br> This position also recruits, evaluates, trains and coordinates adult and teen volunteers.<br> <br> In addition, this position will be responsible for supervising the overall 4-H program which includes establishing and maintaining 4-H clubs within Cumberland County, recruiting and training present and potential volunteers, manage clubs and special programs, such as Empowering Youth and Families (EYFP), and identifying resources to assist in conducting the 4-H program.<br> <br> Responsibilities will include supervision of program staff and management of programs funded through grants and fees.<br> <br>Located in southeastern NC, Cumberland County has a strong military presence and a viable agricultural economy. The county includes its seat, the City of Fayetteville, and is home to Fort Bragg—the nation's largest military installation. The local agricultural industry is made up of small family farms producing livestock and crops, including soybeans, corn, tobacco, etc.&nbsp; Additionally, the county is home to the fifth-largest school district in the state, serving approximately 49,000 students across 86 schools. For additional information, visit <a href=\"https://www.cumberlandcountync.gov/\"><strong>cumberlandcountync.gov</strong></a>. <br> <br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong><br> Manage and grow the 4-H Youth Development program through clubs, afterschool programs, school enrichment programs, summer fun day camps and traditional 4-H camps. Assist the Livestock Agent with the annual Cumberland County Livestock show. Seek out sponsorships, grants, and other funding sources to support activities and programs. Ensure the 4-H program is accessible to youth across the county. Duties include developing and maintaining 4-H clubs, special interest youth programs, and school enrichment programs. Responsibilities will include recruiting and managing volunteers as well as evaluating and reporting impacts of program efforts. The successful candidate will have a background in digital skills and STEM to focus on developing those skills in county youth. The applicant must be self-directed and possess a strong work ethic with skills in leadership, communication, grantsmanship, public relations, planning, implementation, and evaluation. As a member of the Cumberland County Extension staff the individual in this position will recognize the importance of teamwork and fully support the total Extension program.<br>&nbsp;<br>&nbsp;</div> 117606 - South Central District - Cumbe https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233907 2026-08-17T17:17:56-04:00 2026-08-17T17:17:56-04:00 CaSA Temporary Administrative Support Associate <div>The Administrative Associate provides office support, under supervision, to the Climate and Sustainability Academy faculty and staff, focused on essential operational, logistical, and clerical support to the Academy team. The candidate will ensure compliance with university policies and procedures while providing exceptional customer service.</div> 230101 - Interdisciplinary Programs - SVP https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/225839 2025-12-15T16:38:52-05:00 2025-12-15T16:38:53-05:00 Temporary Outdoor Education Instructor - Reidsville, NC <div>Primary Purpose of Position (Includes the day to day duties of the position) – NC State University’s Betsy-Jeff Penn 4-H Center is seeking Outdoor Education Instructors for our Spring program season beginning March 2, 2026 and ending May 22, 2026. Staff with an interest may continue employment for summer camp (June-August) and Fall programming (August- November). The Penn 4-H Center provides day and overnight programs to elementary, middle, and high school students on our 220 acres in Reidsville, NC. Program staff Provide instruction for nearly 20 programs in environmental science and cultural history to elementary school students in the Environmental Education Program. Topics include aquatic ecology, forest measurements, predator/prey relationships, wildlife habitats, pioneer living history, canoeing and much more. Staff also facilitate team challenges and Low and High Ropes course elements with students in the Soaring Thru Spaces program. A 2 week paid staff training is provided at the beginning of the season (March 2-6 / 9 - 13) and as part of that there will also be a 3 day high ropes course training provided that staff are required to attend. Instructors do NOT need to be comfortable working at heights to safely facilitate our high ropes course. Please check us out at bjpenn4h.org to learn more about us.<br><br>Internships may also be available.Questions regarding the position should be directed to Kyle Shillinglaw at 336-349-9445 ext.305 or by email to <a href=\"mailto:kyle_shillinglaw@ncsu.edu\"><strong>kyle_shillinglaw@ncsu.edu</strong></a> <br><br><strong>Work Schedule:<br></strong>Varies, but generally 5 day work weeks, some weekend work required. Long work days (8am-9pm) are sometimes required but no overnight supervisory duties in the cabins is required during our fall/spring school programming. This is a seasonal position that ends in late May with opportunities for continued employment during our summer and fall programs. A Private room in our staff house and meals when working are included during the duration of spring employment.<br><br>Internships may also be available. Questions regarding the position should be directed to Kyle Shillinglaw at 336-349-9445 ext.303 or by email to <a href=\"mailto:kyle_shillinglaw@ncsu.edu\"><strong>kyle_shillinglaw@ncsu.edu</strong></a><strong><br><br>Please Email Kyle at </strong><a href=\"mailto:kyle_shillinglaw@ncsu.edu\"><strong>kyle_shillinglaw@ncsu.edu</strong></a><strong> after applying to confirm your application has been received in the NCSU jobs system</strong></div> 112412 - Betsy-Jeff Penn 4-H Camp https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234442 2026-08-31T17:15:41-04:00 2026-08-31T17:15:41-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>The Abolhasani Research Group in the Department of Chemical &amp; Biomolecular Engineering at North Carolina State University is seeking a Postdoctoral Research Scholar to develop artificial intelligence and machine learning methods for autonomous scientific experimentation as part of the NSF-funded SPEED Programmable Cloud Laboratory.<br><br></div><div>The successful candidate will develop AI/ML methodologies that enable autonomous laboratories to learn from experimental data, select informative experiments, optimize complex chemical and materials systems, and generate transferable scientific knowledge. Research topics may include Bayesian optimization, active learning, uncertainty quantification, representation learning, multimodal scientific data, surrogate modeling, sequential decision-making, and AI-driven experimental design.<br><br></div><div>A major emphasis will be translating AI/ML methodology into algorithms that operate directly with automated experimental platforms and real-world experimental data.<br><strong><br>Duties and Responsibilities include:</strong></div><ul><li>Develop AI/ML algorithms for autonomous experimental design and optimization.</li><li>Develop surrogate models and uncertainty-aware approaches for learning from sparse and heterogeneous experimental datasets.</li><li>Develop chemical and materials representations suitable for closed-loop experimental optimization.</li><li>Implement Bayesian optimization, active learning, and related sequential experimental-design methods.</li><li>Develop methods for incorporating prior knowledge and historical experimental datasets into experimental decision-making.</li><li>Integrate AI/ML algorithms with SPEED automated experimental platforms.</li><li>Analyze multimodal experimental datasets and identify scientifically meaningful structure-property-processing relationships.</li><li>Evaluate algorithm performance using both benchmark datasets and physical experimental campaigns.</li><li>Develop reusable software modules and documented computational workflows.</li><li>Collaborate closely with experimental scientists, automation engineers, and SPEED users.</li><li>Prepare peer-reviewed publications, presentations, technical reports, and research proposals.</li></ul> 140201 - Chemical & Biomolecular Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/229570 2026-04-15T12:02:16-04:00 2026-04-15T12:02:17-04:00 Environmental Services Technician I <div>Are you looking for a fulfilling career where you can make a tangible difference in creating a clean and welcoming environment? We are seeking a <strong>passionate and detail-oriented Environmental Service Technician </strong>to join our exceptional team at <strong>NC State’s Facilities Division</strong>. In this vital role, you will help maintain over 10 million square feet of campus space, ensuring a safe and inviting atmosphere for students, staff, and visitors.<br><br>With the support of a dynamic team of over 320 professionals, you’ll have the opportunity to grow your skills and take pride in your work while contributing to our <strong>vibrant campus community</strong>. Join us and be a part of a team that takes pride in delivering exceptional service to one of the most prestigious campuses in the region. Apply today and help us keep NC State shining bright!<br><br></div><div><strong>Working Hours: 4:00 a.m.-12:30 p.m., Monday – Friday</strong></div><div><br><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li><strong>Clean and sanitize</strong> designated areas using standard cleaning assignments to maintain a safe and hygienic environment.</li><li><strong>Report any discrepancies, damages, or maintenance needs</strong> within buildings and promptly respond to emergencies and inclement weather.</li><li><strong>Operate housekeeping equipment</strong> such as vacuums, mops, and scrubbers to complete tasks like dusting, disinfecting, and replenishing supplies.</li><li><strong>Remove and manage recycling</strong> from centralized indoor recycling sites.</li><li>Occasionally shampoo and extract carpets, and assist with pressure washing of entrances, exterior walls, and windows<strong>.</strong></li><li><strong>Assist with window washing and other seasonal cleaning tasks as needed</strong>.</li><li>Communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, and the general public, and follow verbal and written instructions related to work assignments and operational needs.</li><li>Perform lifting duties up to 30 pounds frequently and 60 pounds occasionally, with or without reasonable accommodations, and perform physical tasks such as grasping, pushing, and pulling cleaning equipment; reaching overhead; bending, squatting, climbing, and maintaining balance.</li></ul> 445080 - Environmental Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232172 2026-06-26T15:10:25-04:00 2026-06-26T15:10:26-04:00 Research Technician - PHHI <div>The Komarnytsky Lab, part of the Plants for Human Health Institute and Department of Food, Bioprocessing, and Nutrition Sciences, has developed an integrated translational pipeline linking molecular insights from cell culture and animal models to physiological outcomes in clinical settings, with special focus on metabolic resilience, pharmacogenomics, optimized physical performance, and innovative botanical supplementation strategies.<br><br>The clinical research program includes the Human Performance Laboratory (HPL) unit that supports mechanistic and intervention clinical studies evaluating exercise- and obesity-induced immune dysfunction, inflammation, and oxidative stress. The HPL unit also facilitates collaborative studies in metabolic, gastrointestinal, and cardiovascular health, advancing nutrition and botanical intervention research through integrated physiological assessments and translational clinical approaches.<br><br>The Research Technician will provide vital, hands-on support to the clinical research program, contributing to both clinical and laboratory investigations. This position is integral to the precise execution of the HPL clinical trials, including participant screening and recruitment, clinical assessments, sample processing, biomarker analysis, cell culture, and data management to ensure high-quality, reproducible research outcomes.<br><br><strong>Duties include but are not limited to:</strong><br><br></div><ul><li>Preparing, calibrating, and operating clinical research equipment (e.g., metabolic carts, treadmills, cycle ergometers, body composition analyzers)</li><li>Performing standardized procedures and protocols to support clinical studies</li><li>Collecting and processing biological samples (e.g., blood, plasma, urine, stool, saliva) using standardized research protocols</li><li>Processing, collecting, culturing, and cryopreserving cells or tissue samples under sterile conditions</li><li>Collecting, recording, and tracking clinical study data</li><li>Performing biochemical and ELISA assays for chemical and biomarker quantification</li><li>Screening, recruiting, and scheduling participants; obtaining informed consent; and conducting study visits</li><li>Maintaining operational readiness, including stocking clinical supplies, monitoring inventory, and coordinating equipment service logs</li><li>Ensuring adherence to University, local, state, and federal regulations, policies, and protocols</li></ul><div><br><strong>This position is based in Kannapolis, NC.</strong></div> 110308 - Kannapolis Research https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233421 2026-08-03T09:02:33-04:00 2026-08-03T09:02:34-04:00 UTS- Temporary Clinical Research Coordinator at UNC Chapel Hill <div><br>The UNC Institute for Trauma Recovery within the Department of Psychiatry seeks to advance understanding of trauma recovery and develop interventions that improve recovery. The foundation of our past and current success is our fantastic team of faculty and staff. Our team believes that achieving new discoveries for trauma survivors around the globe, via the conduct of observational studies and randomized controlled trials, is an extremely meaningful and satisfying way to spend one’s professional life. We work to place the trauma survivor at the center of all of our decisions and to serve them through our work. We continuously work to create a community where our team members serve trauma survivors and actualize their career growth and individual leadership potential.<br><br></div><div><br>The Clinical Research Coordinator will support the Institute’s Better Tomorrow Network (BTN), the first research network in the world dedicated to improving treatments and care for survivors of sexual assault. The mission of the Better Tomorrow Network is to remove barriers to the conduct of high-quality, large-scale research studies that improve the lives of sexual assault survivors. The Network brings together leading sexual assault treatment centers, researchers, community stakeholders, and survivors into a survivor-centered collaborative focused on advancing prevention, early intervention, treatment, and systems-level improvements in care and outcomes for survivors.<br><br></div><div><br>This position will play an important role in supporting multi-site clinical research studies conducted through the Better Tomorrow Network. The Clinical Research Coordinator will assist with study operations, participant recruitment and retention efforts, regulatory and study documentation, data collection and quality assurance activities, and coordination with collaborating clinical sites and research partners. The position requires strong organizational skills, attention to detail, professionalism, and the ability to work effectively with interdisciplinary teams in a fast-paced research environment focused on trauma-informed care and survivor-centered research practices.<br><br></div><div><br>The Clinical Research Coordinator is responsible for the planning, organization, and conduct of multiple clinical research study protocols, including independently collecting and recording data, project administration, and communication with various stakeholders.<br><br></div><div><br><br></div><div><br></div> 480502 - UHR UTS/UNC Partnership https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223270 2025-09-25T11:16:13-04:00 2025-09-25T11:16:14-04:00 Driver <div>The person in this role will help with airport pick-up/drop-off as well as scheduled events for NC State international visitors participating in a GTI and other international program. The selected candidate will transport program participants to/from their temporary accommodations, training locations, and other scheduled activities. The selected candidate must have flexibility in availability as evening and weekend hours will be required. Hours will vary based on program needs.</div> 390701 – Global Training Initiative https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234203 2026-08-26T12:54:24-04:00 2026-08-26T12:54:25-04:00 Research Technician - Olby Lab <div>The Translational Research in Pain team is seeking a Research Technician who is passionate about advancing pain management through innovative clinical and basic research. This position offers the opportunity to contribute to meaningful studies that improve pain assessment and treatment approaches in veterinary medicine while working alongside an interdisciplinary research team in a collaborative laboratory environment.<br><br></div><div>In this role, you will support scientifically rigorous research activities across the Gait Lab, Lascelles RB Lab, and the Lascelles/Mishra Rodent Behavior Suite while gaining hands-on experience in clinical research, laboratory techniques, patient care activities, and data analysis. Additionally, you get to work closely with an interdisciplinary research team to help advance translational pain research and contribute to studies focused on improving pain assessment and treatment approaches in veterinary medicine.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Support clinical and translational research studies focused on pain management and pain assessment.</li><li>Assist with the organization and execution of clinical trials, including participant recruitment, screening, scheduling, and study coordination.</li><li>Perform pain assessments and provide patient care support for clinical and laboratory research subjects.</li><li>Collect, process, and analyze kinetic, behavioral, and laboratory research data in accordance with established protocols.</li><li>Assist with tissue collection and processing activities to support ongoing research initiatives.</li><li>Support data entry, data management, and data analysis activities to ensure accurate and reliable research outcomes.</li><li>Assist with constructing, monitoring, and maintaining compliance with IACUC protocols and research procedures.</li><li>Learn to operate and maintain specialized research equipment, including gait laboratory systems, pressure mats, force plates, QST equipment, rodent behavior systems, and laboratory benchtop equipment.</li><li>Contribute to manuscript review and other research-related documentation activities.<br><br></li></ul><div>If you are excited by the opportunity to contribute to innovative pain research, enjoy working in a collaborative clinical and laboratory environment, and are eager to build hands-on experience in translational veterinary research, we encourage you to apply.</div> 195001 - Department of Clinical Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233192 2026-07-27T15:04:10-04:00 2026-07-27T15:04:10-04:00 Housing Facilities Work Crew <div>The <strong>Division of Academic &amp; Student Affairs</strong> (DASA) is where students discover connections and community. It is where students live, where they gather, where they improve themselves, each other, and their university. DASA is where students seek guidance and support, both in and outside of the classroom. The division serves all students at NC State through three integrated components:&nbsp; academic affairs, student affairs, and University College.</div><div><br></div><div>NC State wants employees to achieve their highest potential at work, learn more about our employee value proposition here (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\">You Belong Here</a>).&nbsp; The Division of Academic and Student Affairs is the place to work for values-driven faculty and staff who are passionate about promoting the success of the whole student.</div><div><br>University Housing Facilities is seeking to fill several positions to serve as Housing Facilities Work Crew members to begin working mid-May through mid-August.<br><br>They will be required to assist the General Shop and/or other trades teams by moving furniture, removing carpet, painting, cleaning HVAC units, changing filters, and completing other duties as assigned.<br><br>Team members will also assist the Conference Services staff with lowering and raising beds in residence halls and delivering/retrieving items from the warehouse.</div> 242290 - Housing Facilities Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/222975 2025-09-16T19:59:23-04:00 2025-09-16T19:59:23-04:00 Temporary Research Technician - Cotton Breeding <div>Temporary employee will be part of the cotton breeding and genetics research team. <br><br><strong>Duties include:</strong></div><ul><li>Making crosses in the field</li><li>Selfing cotton flowers in the field</li><li>Harvesting, ginning and acid delinting cotton samples</li><li>Seed preparation for storage</li><li>Planting and helping to grow plants in the greenhouse and fields</li><li>Perform day-to-day activities of greenhouse care and maintenance</li><li>Assist post docs, grad students and research specialist while collecting phenotypic data and tissue samples from the greenhouses and cotton fields</li><li>Perform lab based analyses including DNA isolation</li></ul> 110901 - Crop and Soil Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234060 2026-08-21T14:22:25-04:00 2026-08-21T14:22:26-04:00 Shipping & Receiving Assistant <div>Join our team as a <strong>Shipping and Receiving Assistant</strong>, a vital role supporting faculty, staff, and students across the Centennial Biomedical Campus. In this hands-on position, you will support daily receiving, asset tracking, mail runs, event setup, fleet care, and heavy equipment operation. You will also safely handle specialized items, including refrigerated supplies, controlled substances, anatomy specimens, and hazardous diagnostic samples.</div><div><br><strong>Key Responsibilities:</strong><br>This position plays a crucial role in supporting the sponsored activities of faculty, staff, and students within the College by performing a variety of tasks and responsibilities across different areas:</div><div><br></div><ul><li>Sign for and inventory all packages received daily from delivery trucks, confirming their accuracy against delivery manifests, including perishable, infectious, controlled substances, and valuable items.</li><li>Conduct detailed inventories of all packages delivered to the Centennial Biomedical Campus, including various buildings and units within the college.</li><li>Deliver packages to designated locations within the college campus, obtaining recipient signatures and recording delivery dates, especially for perishable items requiring refrigerated storage.</li><li>Assist with the annual inventory of assets spread across multiple campuses and locations, including locating items, recording their details, and updating the university asset management system.</li><li>Assist the Mail Room Manager with mail operations, including operation of the mail machine, deliveries and pickups from the local post office, courier runs, and retrieving pathology samples from the NC Department of Agriculture Veterinary Diagnostic Laboratory.</li><li>Transport veterinary diagnostic samples to and from diagnostic laboratories, ensuring proper handling of infectious materials.</li><li>Handle formaldehyde-preserved large and small animal cadavers for the Anatomy Lab, including transportation and use of protective equipment.</li><li>Receive, document, and transport infectious disease samples for the Vector Born Laboratory to avoid public health risks.</li><li>Locate and tag new purchases made by the college, documenting their details and entering them into the inventory database.</li><li>Assist in documenting, tagging, and removing surplus items for disposal, adhering to safety procedures, and maintaining accurate inventory records.</li><li>Assist with setting up and logistics for special events at the college, including deliveries, setup, and furniture rearrangement.</li><li>Operate heavy equipment such as forklifts, pallet jacks, backhoes, dump trucks, and box trucks, ensuring proper safety training and certification.</li><li>Assist with light maintenance on departmental vehicles, including fueling, cleaning, and scheduling maintenance and repairs.</li><li>Assist with various moves within the college, including office and laboratory moves, and transporting heavy or delicate equipment.</li><li>Provide customer service throughout the college and the College of Engineering Biomedical Engineering department, assisting customers with their needs.</li><li>Demonstrate knowledge of various programs administered by Shipping and Receiving and explain them to others.</li><li>Address various requests from customers across campus, ensuring policies and procedures are communicated and followed.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Abilities Necessary to Perform Job Duties:</strong></div><ul><li>Must be able to lift up to 50 pounds, with or without reasonable accommodations, on a frequent basis.</li></ul> 190701 - Property Management https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/227271 2026-02-09T21:02:17-05:00 2026-02-09T21:02:18-05:00 Temporary Summer Camp Nurse - Ellerbe, NC <div>Millstone 4H Educational Center is seeking licensed RNs to join our team for the 2026 Summer Camp season! The Camp Nurse serves as a vital member of the Camp Healthcare Team, and oversees all health care operations for approximately 120 residential campers each week. The ideal candidate will enjoy working with youth of all ages, have the ability to work in unpredictable outdoor conditions, and thrive in a fast-paced dynamic environment outside of the clinical setting. Due to the on-call nature of this position, applicants must be able to reside on-site (housing provided) or within a half-hour commute of camp for the duration of the summer season. If you want to apply your nursing skills to a residential camp setting and work with a team like no other, apply today!</div><div><br><strong>Primary Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Ability to follow all health care policies/procedures as described in facility standing orders, treatment procedures, and Staff Manual</li><li>On call to respond to medical emergencies at all times campers are on-site, including overnight</li><li>Perform basic assessment and first aid for conditions including but not limited to: minor soft tissue and musculoskeletal injuries, insect bites/stings, dehydration, sunburns, general illness; providing follow-up checks as needed</li><li>Facilitate proper medication administration, for both routine prescription and over the counter medications, during all administration periods throughout the day</li><li>Maintain written medical records for all medications and treatments administered according to state and American Camp Association Standards</li><li>Prepare and record insurance and accident report forms</li><li>Oversee Sunday check-in and Friday check-out of all camper medications each week, discussing any medical concerns with parent/guardian as needed</li><li>Review all camper health forms and communicate medical needs to counselors as necessary</li><li>Manage a team of 1-2 Health Care Coordinators who may assist with primary and secondary responsibilities</li><li>Prepare and distribute camper’s medication schedule list to all counselors before dinner on Sundays<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Secondary Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Attend all relevant staff trainings</li><li>Keep camp Infirmary and medical supply storage space clean and organized</li><li>Prepare and distribute all campus first aid kits, conducting routine checks and restocks as necessary</li><li>Perform medical supply/equipment inventory and place restock orders<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Work Schedule:<br>The summer season runs from May 29th - August 7th, 2026. </strong>Workdays will begin at noon on Sunday of each week and end at noon on Friday. These positions demand a high level of responsibility and attention to detail. You will be on call 24/7 including overnight, meal times and during activities to ensure overall health of campers and staff. Lodging (7 days a week, if needed) and Meals (5 days a week) provided. <strong>Potential to modify to a part-time role based on applicant’s needs and experience, please contact us directly to discuss options.<br><br>Child discount for camp is negotiable</strong></div> 112413 - Millstone 4-h Camp https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234546 2026-09-02T14:00:42-04:00 2026-09-02T14:00:42-04:00 Director of Research Communications <div><strong><em>This is an internal posting for current NC State permanent and temporary employees, and candidates with RIF priority.&nbsp;</em></strong></div><div><br>The University Communications team is seeking a skilled research communicator and collaborative leader to shape how the university’s research and innovation enterprise is understood, valued, and shared. This position leads enterprise-wide research communications that translate complex discoveries, expertise, and innovation into clear, compelling narratives for the audiences who influence NC State’s reputation, resources, and impact—including news media, policymakers, peer institutions, donors, partners, prospective students, faculty, and the public.<br><br></div><div>In this role, you will serve as the university’s strategic communications partner to the research enterprise, working across University Communications and Marketing and closely with the Vice Chancellor for Communications and Marketing and the Vice Chancellor for Research and Innovation. You will shape research storytelling and national and trade media visibility, connect research communicators across the university, and help position NC State’s discoveries and expertise in conversations that matter beyond campus. Your ability to move comfortably between the laboratory and the newsroom will be essential—you will build trust with researchers, identify the stories behind their work, and make complex research meaningful to audiences who may never read the original paper.<br><br></div><div>Key responsibilities fall within the following functional areas: <br><br><strong>Research Storytelling and Content Strategy:</strong></div><ul><li>Shape the university’s research narrative and content strategy, identifying and elevating compelling stories across fundamental discovery, translational science, interdisciplinary research, and community-engaged scholarship for audiences and platforms that extend NC State’s reach and influence.</li><li>Translate complex scientific and technical work into compelling, accessible narratives that maintain research rigor while connecting discovery to the people, questions, and real-world impact behind the work.</li><li>Develop enterprise research communications strategy in alignment with the division’s integrated communications strategy and NC State’s “Think and Do” brand, message pillars, and visual standards.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Earned Media and National Visibility for Research:</strong></div><ul><li>Expand NC State’s national and industry visibility as a research and innovation leader through proactive media strategy and outreach that increases share of voice and positions faculty as trusted experts on issues of consequence.</li><li>Orchestrate communications around high-impact research moments, including major discoveries, awards, rankings, funding, and other milestones, in partnership with the division’s media relations function.</li><li>Cultivate relationships with science, research, trade, and industry journalists while strengthening faculty readiness to serve as credible, compelling expert sources.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Campus Research Communications Network:</strong></div><ul><li>Lead and strengthen a university-wide research communications network spanning colleges, centers, institutes, and the Office of Research and Innovation, creating greater alignment, visibility, and strategic value while respecting decentralized ownership.</li><li>Connect communicators, research leadership, faculty, and University Communications and Marketing around shared priorities, creating coordinated pathways for identifying, developing, and amplifying research stories across university channels.</li><li>Establish shared standards, story pipelines, and collaborative practices that increase the consistency, efficiency, and reach of research communications across the enterprise.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Issues and Sensitive Research Communications</strong></div><ul><li>Provide strategic counsel on emerging and sensitive research-related issues, helping ensure accurate, timely, and coordinated communications in partnership with university leadership and the division’s issues and crisis communications function.</li><li>Navigate and translate an evolving federal research funding and policy environment, helping internal and external audiences understand implications for NC State’s research enterprise in coordination with the Office of Research and Innovation, legal counsel, and divisional partners.</li><li>Guide coordinated research communications during periods of disruption or heightened scrutiny, including funding changes, policy transitions, leadership changes, and other issues affecting the university’s research mission and reputation.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Collaboration, Culture, and Operations</strong></div><ul><li>Leverage the division’s existing digital, editorial, marketing, and communications infrastructure to strengthen the reach and effectiveness of research storytelling across web, marketing, and other strategic channels.</li><li>Lead and develop assigned communications staff, fostering a high-performing, collaborative culture grounded in professional growth, strong execution, and shared accountability.</li><li>Use audience insight, analytics, and performance measures to strengthen communications strategy, continuously refining storytelling, channels, and engagement based on what advances organizational goals.</li><li>Oversee external partners, vendors, and project delivery for research communications initiatives, ensuring resources and timelines support strategic priorities.</li></ul><div><br>If you are a strategic communicator who can move seamlessly between scientific discovery, institutional strategy, and compelling storytelling, we encourage you to apply and help shape how NC State’s research and innovation are understood, valued, and shared with the world.</div> 281001 - Payroll https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230547 2026-05-12T15:44:58-04:00 2026-05-12T15:44:59-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>The Department of Crop and Soil Sciences encompasses varied disciplines in research, extension, and teaching. The department’s mission is to develop future leaders in crop, soil, and environmental sciences, improve crop plants and products, devise effective and sustainable crop production systems, devise effective and sustainable soil management techniques in both agriculture and urban settings, and disseminate crop, soil, and environmental science knowledge for the benefit of users and producers of food, feed, turf, biofuels, and fiber in North Carolina, the nation, and the world.<br><br><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>A postdoctoral researcher will lead the development of a genome-wide haplotype stacking framework to better understand and improve complex polygenic traits in small grains.</li><li>The project integrates high-resolution genomic, transcriptomic, and phenotypic data with predictive modeling, simulation, and optimization approaches to identify and assemble favorable haplotype combinations.</li><li>The postdoctoral associate will also lead the preparation of scientific manuscripts, results, and interpretations.</li></ul> 110901 - Crop and Soil Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232706 2026-07-14T07:54:42-04:00 2026-07-14T07:54:43-04:00 ICU Technician/Assistant <div>ICU Assistant procedures include IV catheter placement, urinary catheterization, blood transfusions, blood pressure monitoring, fluid input/output, hourly treatments, patient rounds with coworkers and doctors.</div> 192001 - CVM Teaching Hospital https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233687 2026-08-10T13:07:37-04:00 2026-08-10T13:07:38-04:00 Research Security Specialist <div><strong>Professional Knowledge&nbsp;</strong></div><ul><li>The IT Security Professional provides technical implementations and daily monitoring of the university’s complex IT environment in accordance with best practices and standards such as NIST 800-171, CMMC, CUI, NIST 800-53, PCI DSS (Payment Card Industry Data Security Standards), DMCA, FERPA, GLBA, HIPAA, , etc. Responsibilities include cybersecurity reviews, risk assessments, risk management, data management, policies/standards and guidelines, cybersecurity awareness and training, audit coordination and project management.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Security Operations, Risk Management and Compliance&nbsp;</strong></div><ul><li>Specifically, this position reviews, coordinates and monitors information technology security controls that protect confidentiality, integrity and availability of the organization’s controlled secure research data in accordance with contractual, legal, regulatory and institutional requirements. The position is responsible for ensuring that users with access to secure research data receive appropriate training.</li></ul><div><br></div><div>The position consults with faculty/researchers, college/unit IT staff, applicable OIT staff, applicable Office of Research and Innovation (ORI) staff, and other subject matter experts to ensure technology solutions and compliance standards are in line with contract requirements. Moreover, the position will ensure appropriate auditing and documentation, providing guidance and recommendations to the research community in areas of data security, from award negotiation through project close-out.</div><div><br></div><div>This position will work closely with ORI Sponsored Programs &amp; Regulatory Compliance to assist with monitoring the secure research environment setups, conducting follow-up reviews, and ensuring contract terms and conditions are in line with NC State standards for data security. This position serves as the formal Information Systems Security Manager (ISSM) for the university’s Secure University Research Environment (SURE), which is the university’s C3PAO CMMC Level 2 certified environment.</div><div><br></div><div>The overall duties are as follows:</div><ul><li>Serve as the bridge between IT, information security and research requirements</li><li>Lead the maintenance and growth of the existing NIST 800-171 security and compliance program, especially in the research context.</li><li>Assist OIT, ORI and campus stakeholders on maintaining compliance with CMMC 2.0 level 1 and 2</li><li>Serve as the SURE Information Systems Security Manager (ISSM)</li><li>Assist the ISRA staff with processing cybersecurity requests, such as ITPC items that require a security review / risk assessment</li><li>Explore opportunities for the use of AI and their impacts on cybersecurity, data usage and compliance</li><li>Participate in programs to improve the university's cybersecurity awareness and outreach</li><li>Assist the ISRA staff with GRC project strategies, project tasks and testing of the service</li><li>Assist in the enhancement of existing PRRs and and the construction of needed procedures across OIT and campus IT</li></ul><div><br>This position involves access to information, items, or technology controlled under the International Traffic in Arms Regulations (ITAR) or Export Administration Regulations (EAR). To comply with federal export control laws, candidates must be a \"U.S. Person\" as defined by 22 C.F.R. § 120.62 (e.g., U.S. Citizen, U.S. Lawful Permanent Resident / Green Card Holder, Refuged or Asylee status under 8 U.S.C. 1324b(a)(3)).</div> 511001 - Security & Compliance https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223349 2025-09-25T12:12:33-04:00 2025-09-25T12:12:35-04:00 Lecturer, Industrial and Systems Engineering <div>The <strong>Fitts Department of Industrial and Systems Engineering (ISE)</strong> invites applicants for a <strong>Lecturer</strong> position. The primary role of the Lecturer will be to deliver high-quality undergraduate and/or graduate instruction for the department, supporting teaching needs during the spring, fall, and/or summer sessions of 2024-2025. Teaching emphasis will be on courses in <em>manufacturing, supply chain management, and computing, as well as specialized topics in sustainability, artificial intelligence, cybersecurity, or engineering management.</em></div><div><br></div><div>Applicants are encouraged to specify courses within the department’s curriculum they are qualified to teach or propose special topics relevant to their expertise.</div><div><br></div><div><strong>Key Duties and Responsibilities</strong></div><ul><li>Deliver engaging course content for both undergraduate and graduate students, ensuring alignment with departmental and institutional goals.</li><li>Prepare course materials, assignments, and assessments, fostering a challenging yet supportive learning environment.</li><li>Provide academic support and guidance to students.</li><li>Collaborate with department faculty to review and update curriculum materials as needed to reflect current industry trends.</li><li>Utilize various instructional methods and technologies to support a range of learning styles and backgrounds.</li><li>Participate in department meetings and contribute to curriculum planning and development initiatives.</li></ul> 140601 - Fitts Department of Industrial & Systems Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234343 2026-08-28T16:15:42-04:00 2026-08-28T16:15:43-04:00 Facilities Maintenance Technician - HVAC Mechanic <div>The <strong>Facilities Maintenance Technician – HVAC Mechanic</strong> plays a critical role in maintaining the comfort, reliability, and efficiency of campus facilities. In this position, you will support the operation, maintenance, and repair of a wide range of building systems, including HVAC equipment, boilers, piping systems, and supporting electrical and mechanical components within an assigned maintenance zone.<br><br></div><div>This role is ideal for a skilled technician who enjoys <strong>hands-on troubleshooting, solving complex mechanical issues, and working across multiple building systems</strong>. You will help ensure that heating, cooling, steam, and hot water systems operate safely and efficiently while supporting the daily operations of campus facilities.<br><br></div><div>Working as part of a collaborative maintenance team, you will interact with coworkers, supervisors, and campus partners to diagnose issues, complete repairs, and perform preventative maintenance that helps prevent system failures and disruptions. Your expertise will directly contribute to maintaining safe, reliable environments that support teaching, research, and campus life.<br><br>If you are a <strong>skilled HVAC technician who enjoys problem-solving, working with varied building systems, and making a meaningful impact on facility operations</strong>, we encourage you to apply.</div><div><strong>Apply today to join our team and help maintain the systems that keep our campus running smoothly.</strong></div><div><strong><br>Key responsibilities and duties included, but not limited to:</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Perform maintenance, repair, and installation of HVAC systems, including air conditioning, refrigeration equipment, and ventilation systems.</strong></li><li>Operate, maintain, and troubleshoot steam and hot water boilers and associated auxiliary equipment.</li><li><strong>Diagnose and repair mechanical equipment,</strong> including pumps, fan turbines, condensate receivers, steam lines, pressure-reducing stations, and expansion joints.</li><li>Inspect, maintain, and repair piping systems, insulation, strainers, valves, and other components used in steam and hot water distribution systems.</li><li><strong>Conduct preventive maintenance on refrigeration and air-conditioning equipment in accordance with manufacturer recommendations.</strong></li><li>Troubleshoot and repair pneumatic and electronic control systems, including temperature controls, regulators, humidity controllers, sensors, and control valves.</li><li>Analyze faults and malfunctions in secondary electrical systems up to 600 VAC and implement corrective actions.</li><li>Maintain and repair electrical components, including conduit wiring, electrical panels, breakers, switchgear, magnetic starters, motors, disconnects, switches, and receptacles.</li><li>Diagnose and resolve control system faults affecting HVAC and related building systems.</li><li>Perform minor plumbing and piping installations to support HVAC and building mechanical systems.</li><li><strong>Maintain and repair equipment used in steam generation and distribution, including air compressors and water softeners.</strong></li><li>Develop solutions for both routine and occasionally complex maintenance issues across HVAC, electrical, and plumbing systems.</li><li>Coordinate with coworkers, supervisors, and other departments to ensure repairs and maintenance tasks are completed efficiently and safely.</li><li>Communicate with campus customers and stakeholders to minimize disruptions and maintain building operations.</li><li>Identify when specialized expertise or additional assistance is required and coordinate with appropriate personnel.</li><li>Document maintenance activities and service requests to ensure accurate records and effective work order management.</li><li>Perform tasks requiring standing for extended periods, working in confined spaces, and utilizing various tools. Safely climb and work from different types of ladders to access elevated or below-grade areas (such as roofs, above-ceiling spaces, and utility pits). Be exposed to and work in areas where insects or small animals are occasionally present. All essential tasks must be performed with or without reasonable accommodations.</li></ul> 445030 - Building Maintenance & Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228786 2026-03-26T11:48:21-04:00 2026-03-26T11:48:21-04:00 Temporary Research Technician - Kannapolis, NC <div>The position will support the operations at the Human Performance Laboratory located at the North Carolina Research Campus. This role is vital for executing clinical research and community fitness testing by conducting specialized procedures. The research technician is responsible for ensuring quality control during data collection, conducting subject recruitment and scheduling, and maintaining lab equipment while strictly complying with all environmental health and safety policies.</div> 110308 - Kannapolis Research https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234719 2026-09-04T14:54:43-04:00 2026-09-04T14:54:44-04:00 Cybersecurity Program Operations Analyst <div>The Friday Institute for Educational Innovation is a large, multi-faceted Institute housed in a 33,000 square foot state-of-the-art facility on NC State University’s Centennial Campus in Raleigh. Our work encompasses educational research, professional development, policy and program evaluation, and direct services to schools, districts, state departments of education and public- and private sector organizations. We are deeply involved in work throughout North Carolina, leading some of the major statewide K-12 education initiatives and working closely with the Department of Public Instruction, the State Board of Education, and the majority of school districts in the State on the transition to digital teaching and learning. Since opening in 2005, the Friday Institute has developed a strong national presence, working with organizations throughout the country, and is increasingly developing international connections. More information is available at <a href=\"http://www.fi.ncsu.edu\"><strong>http://www.fi.ncsu.edu</strong></a>.<br><br>The Friday Institute supports the North Carolina Department of Public Instruction (NCDPI) <a href=\"https://sci.fi.ncsu.edu/cybersecurity\"><strong>K-12 Cybersecurity Program</strong></a>, which organizes and aligns business and technical functions across the state so that the Public School Unit (PSU) will have greater visibility into the people, processes, and technologies deployed for cybersecurity. The goal of the program is to help PSUs achieve Strong Cyber Hygiene and this position will be entirely focused on supporting this program.<br><br><br>The K-12 Cybersecurity Program has expanded significantly, and the core team requires additional project and operational support. Friday Institute seeks a K-12 Cybersecurity Program Operations Analyst to assist with program communications and reporting, outreach, knowledge management, data utilization and metrics, dashboard development, project coordination, and other routine but critical activities that keep the program organized and efficient.<br><br>This is a time-limited contract-based position that will provide services in support of the K-12 Cybersecurity Program under the terms, scope, and period of performance established in the applicable contract. Continuation of the position is contingent upon contract requirements, funding availability, satisfactory performance, and any applicable renewal or extension of the contract.<br><br>Work in a North Carolina PSU is ideal, but not required. Familiarity with general K-12 IT practices and cybersecurity are essential.<br><br><br>Due to access to security-sensitive information, only U.S. citizens living in the state of North Carolina should apply. All work must be completed on a university provided and managed Macbook.</div> 139001 - Friday Institute https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231686 2026-06-12T15:13:30-04:00 2026-06-12T15:13:31-04:00 Anatomic Pathology Resident <div>The College of Veterinary Medicine at North Carolina State University (NC State) invites outstanding candidates to apply for three open 3-year residency positions in veterinary anatomic pathology beginning July 2027. The training program focuses on hands-on learning of anatomic pathology and ACVP board preparation under the supervision of eight ACVP board-certified anatomic pathologists, with special interests in aquatics/fish, swine, cancer, oral, and ocular pathology as well as pedagogy and viral oncogenesis. The resident will experience a busy, varied caseload on both necropsy and biopsy services with curricular teaching and research opportunities. The caseload is predominantly cats, dogs, small ruminants, horses, exotics, zoo animals, and aquatics, with smaller numbers of lab animals, cows, poultry, and pigs. One position, as relevant, may have flexibility to do a specialized track with increased service in aquatic pathology. Applicants must possess a DVM or equivalent degree from a North American AVMA-accredited or international AVMA-listed veterinary college, have an excellent academic record, strong communication skills, and be committed to a career in veterinary anatomic pathology. For additional questions about the program, please contact Dr. Abby Armwood at <a href=\"mailto:arwisnet@ncsu.edu\"><strong>arwisnet@ncsu.edu</strong></a> or Dr. Liz Rose at <a href=\"mailto:ecrose@ncsu.edu\"><strong>ecrose2@ncsu.edu</strong></a>.<br><br></div><div>NC State University is near two other major universities (Duke University and the University of North Carolina - Chapel Hill) and the Research Triangle Park, which includes multiple government agencies, non-profit institutes, biotechnology, and large pharmaceutical companies. In addition, the state diagnostic laboratory lies within close proximity to the CVM campus. Opportunities for collaboration and externships are possible for capable, interested residents.<br><br></div><div><br>See <a href=\"https://cvm.ncsu.edu/academics/residencies/anatomic-pathology/\"><strong>https://cvm.ncsu.edu/academics/residencies/anatomic-pathology/</strong></a> for program description and additional information. Stipends will begin at $54,000 per year. Applications should include (1) a curriculum vitae, (2) statement of career goals, (3) official transcripts from their DVM or equivalent degree from a North American AVMA-accredited or international AVMA-listed veterinary college, (4) three references submitted via the reference form available in the link below, and (5) a GPA and/or class rank (can be listed on transcripts, stated in CV, or in alternative format such as a Dean’s letter). <strong>Applications submitted by September 22, 2026</strong> will be considered first; applications submitted past this date may be reviewed if needed to identify a suitable candidate. Estimated start date is July 19, 2027.<br><br></div><div><br>The following link is for the reference form which will be in place of traditional recommendation letters. Please provide this link to your references: https://forms.gle/EDk5yYwu4sm1BStq6<br><br></div><div><br>Questions related to the application process can be directed to Caitlin Falls in CVM Human Resources. Transcripts can be sent to her via email or regular mail.<br><br></div><div>Caitlin Falls</div><div>NC State University<br>CVM- Human Resources<br>1060 William Moore Drive</div><div>Raleigh, NC 27607<br>Phone: 919-513-6526<br>Email: cvm-humanresources@ncsu.edu</div><div><br><br></div><div><br><br></div> 196001 - Department of Population Health and Pathobiology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234247 2026-08-27T09:33:45-04:00 2026-08-27T09:33:46-04:00 Communications & IT Support Specialist <div>The Communications &amp; IT Support Specialist provides unit‑level professional support to CMAST through integrated communications, digital media, and desktop IT services. The role delivers specialized expertise in social media, accessibility‑compliant communications, website maintenance, event support, and frontline IT troubleshooting for faculty, staff, and students. This position ensures consistent, accurate, and accessible communication aligned with CMAST and college priorities while maintaining reliable desktop technology operations in coordination with OIT.<br><strong><br>Functional Responsibilities:<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Communications &amp; Staff Management:</strong></div><div>Develops and manages CMAST digital communications, including social media, branding, videography, writing, and digital content. Ensures all communications meet federal accessibility guidelines. Supports annual activity reports, communications plans, and calendars aligned with CMAST and college priorities. Tracks communication metrics and adjusts strategies based on effectiveness.<br><br></div><div><strong>Desktop IT Support:</strong></div><div>Provides frontline desktop support for CMAST faculty, staff, and students, including Eduroam connectivity, software installations, printer driver setup, malware troubleshooting, and coordination with OIT for building access and technical issues. Ensures reliable and responsive IT support for daily operations.<br><br></div><div><strong>Project Management &amp; Resource Prioritization:</strong></div><div>Supports CMAST website maintenance, high‑touch content creation, event communications, logistical coordination, and public tours of the building/program.</div><div><strong><br>Strategic Communications &amp; Philanthropy Support:</strong></div><div>Collaborates with communications and development staff from CALS, CVM, COS, ORI, and University partners to promote CMAST research, extension, and academic programs to the public and prospective donors.</div> 061201 - Center For Marine Science & Technology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228218 2026-03-09T10:12:09-04:00 2026-03-09T10:12:10-04:00 Temporary Field/Lab Technician <div>Most activities for this position are field related. Duties, include cutting plants, planting seed, planting transplants, grafting plants, applying fertilizer, evaluating commodity quality, and harvesting. Candidate should be able to work within a group setting as well as individually. Candidate must be able to have some flexibility in schedule and willing to perform manual labor daily. Working conditions are very often hot and humid.<br><br>Prefer candidate to have interest in vegetable production or horticulture.<br><br>Work Schedule: Monday-Friday, 6am-6pm is the general range of hours, start and end times will vary depending on daily travel and tasks to be completed (aim is for 40-45 hours per week, with some overtime expected during the peak season, 50-55 hours). This full time work schedule would begin in earnest in late April or May. We would also like to have some employees start as early as March, part time.</div> 111501 - Horticultural Science https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234679 2026-09-03T14:49:59-04:00 2026-09-03T14:50:00-04:00 Microbiology Laboratory Research Technician <div>The Kleiner Lab at North Carolina State University is seeking a Research Technician to support research focused on complex microbial communities, microbial symbioses, and metaproteomics. Reporting to the Kleiner Lab research team, this position contributes to the design, execution, and analysis of experiments that advance the lab’s research program and generate findings for publications, conference presentations, and grant development.<br><br></div><div>You will work in a dynamic, collaborative research environment where your technical expertise and attention to detail directly contribute to ongoing scientific projects. Additionally, you will assist with laboratory management, including maintaining safety documentation and compliance, coordinating undergraduate research assistant schedules, and overseeing equipment purchasing, maintenance, and repair.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Manage multiple concurrent research projects and coordinate day-to-day research activities.</li><li>Cultivate and maintain bacterial cultures and perform molecular and biochemical assays.</li><li>Compile, analyze, and interpret experimental data to support research objectives.</li><li>Contribute to research outcomes through publications, conference presentations, and grant development.<br><br></li></ul><div>If you are a detail-oriented and collaborative researcher who enjoys hands-on laboratory work, managing multiple priorities, and contributing to meaningful scientific discovery, this is an opportunity to make a direct impact on the Kleiner Lab’s research and day-to-day operations. Your technical skills, organization, and commitment to high-quality research will help advance projects investigating the complex interactions within microbial communities.</div> 110801 - Plant & Microbial Biology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231019 2026-05-29T09:45:58-04:00 2026-05-29T09:45:59-04:00 Career Counselor, CALS Career Services <div>The Career Counselor will enhance the career and professional development of CALS students through individual career counseling and training programs. The Career Counselor’s work will contribute to 4,500+ CALS students improving their career readiness, participating in experiential learning, and securing post-graduation opportunities aligning with their interests. The person in this position will report to the Director of CALS Career Services.</div><div><strong><br>Primary Responsibilities include, but are not limited to:<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Student Career and Professional Training</strong></div><ul><li>Provide individual career counseling and coaching to current CALS students and alumni within 12 months of graduation.</li><li>Present career and professional development presentations and workshops to groups of students through classes, clubs, and other group sessions.</li><li>Present career information that is tailored to specific CALS majors.</li><li>Coordinate with faculty members to provide career and professional development training to their students, including through tailored activities such as resume and mock interview events.</li><li>Coordinate networking and professional development events and programs to train students and link them with industry personnel.</li><li>Manage and grow the CALS Jumpstart career and professional development conference into a premier opportunity for CALS students.</li><li>Develop career-related resources and communicate up-to-date information on career opportunities specific to CALS majors.</li><li>Provide students with regular communication regarding career and professional development information, upcoming events, and career-related opportunities.</li><li>Consult with and support departmental efforts to enhance career and professional development in students.</li><li>Engage with alumni and industry professionals to support current CALS student career and professional development.</li><li>Train faculty in providing up-to-date career and professional development information to students.</li></ul><div><strong>Program Management</strong></div><ul><li>Manage the CALS Peer Career Coach (PCC) program, including recruiting, training, and supervising up to 12 PCCs per year.</li><li>Manage CALS student recruiting and participation in the Agriculture Future of America (AFA) program.</li><li>Oversee publication of a weekly email newsletter marketing career-related events and opportunities.</li><li>Provide support for the CALS Rural Works! program, including conducting internship site visits.</li><li>Serve as a representative of CALS Career Services as needed.</li></ul> 110101 - CALS - Academic Programs https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232935 2026-07-20T13:51:31-04:00 2026-07-20T13:51:32-04:00 SEVIS Data and Compliance Analyst <div>The SEVIS Data and Compliance Analyst will work to ensure NC State compliance with all the maintenance of F-1 and J-1 records in SEVIS in accordance with federal regulations under the supervision of the Interim SEVIS Systems and Compliance Manager. The SEVIS Data and Compliance Analyst will serve as an authorized school official for student (DSO for F-1) and exchange visitor (ARO for J-1) programs, working individually and with other OIS staff to ensure compliance with immigration regulations and reporting requirements. They will run database queries and reports to support NC State's federally mandated Student and Exchange Visitor Information System (SEVIS) reporting to the U.S. Department of Homeland Security (DHS), and submit event based and periodic reporting to SEVIS as required.</div><div><br></div><div><strong>Data Management and Regulatory Compliance 60%</strong></div><ul><li>Act as a Designated School Official (DSO) and Alternate Responsible Officer (ARO) as required to perform event based and periodic reporting.<ul><li>Under the direction of the Interim SEVIS Systems &amp; Compliance Manager, synthesize data from multiple sources to manage the new student registration process, identify potential compliance issues for new students, and generate routine reports on expected arrivals for stakeholders.</li><li>Perform as delegated the registration process for all returning F-1 and J-1 students.</li><li>In coordination with the Interim SEVIS Systems and Compliance Manager, review and resolve student &amp; scholar alerts in SEVIS and Sunapsis such as changes of curriculum and level, address changes, or assign to advisor for review</li></ul></li><li>Perform delegated SEVIS-related database maintenance and data clean-up services</li><li>Run queries in SIS and Sunapsis to assist with data compliance and institutional reporting</li><li>Remain current with SEVIS-related and regulatory updates pertaining to international students and scholars to ensure institutional compliance.</li><li>Advise international students and scholars to the extent necessary to move them through OIS processes and procedures.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Electronic Business Services Maintenance &amp; Development 30%</strong></div><ul><li>Assist in monitoring the effectiveness of new processes, assisting with technical documentation for end users.</li><li>Coordinate continual updates and maintenance of standard Sunapsis email texts with advisors.</li><li>Manage departmental and administrative user access</li><li>Assist clients and departments with questions related to access to Sunapsis and eForm submission, Sunapsis pin resets, merging records and eForm resets</li><li>Participate in training OIS staff in use of advisor-related SEVIS and Sunapsis functions. Assist in preparing campus-wide Sunapsis training and on-going production support including documentation to be used for training and on-going reference.</li><li>Assist with maintenance of campus-wide electronic business process documentation and update as needed due to updates in MyPack, Sunapsis, OnBase, SEVIS and other database systems, as well as changes in internal OIS processes and procedures.</li><li>Act as University back-up on all topics related to Sunapsis/Global Home.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Other duties 10%</strong></div><ul><li>Back up the Intake Specialist as needed during illness/vacation in assigning cases to advisors</li><li>Attend and provide technical assistance to policies and procedures discussions with advisors</li><li>Provide back-up support to the front desk as needed.</li><li>Assist with communicating process updates and providing training to departments and other campus stakeholders.</li><li>Attend conferences and give conference presentations when needed.</li><li>Perform all other duties as assigned.</li></ul><div><br>This position is scheduled for 29 hours per week though work outside of standard hours may be required due to business needed.</div> 390201 - Office of International Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233934 2026-08-18T11:11:23-04:00 2026-08-18T11:11:24-04:00 Landscape Maintenance Technician <div>Do you enjoy working outdoors and taking pride in creating beautiful, healthy landscapes? Join <strong>NC State University’s Landscape Maintenance and Operations team</strong> as a <strong>Landscape Maintenance Technician</strong> and help shape the campus environment that students, faculty, staff, and visitors experience every day.</div><div><br>In this hands-on role, you’ll provide professional landscape care across NC State’s campus, using your knowledge of horticulture, turf management, and landscape maintenance to keep our grounds safe, attractive, and sustainable. You’ll work as part of a dedicated team that takes pride in maintaining one of the university’s most visible and important assets, its campus landscape. If you’re dependable, enjoy hands-on work, and want to see the results of your work make a difference every day, <strong>we invite you to apply and join the NC State Facilities team!</strong></div><div><strong><br></strong><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li><strong>Perform routine landscape maintenance, including mowing, edging, trimming, blowing, mulching, leaf collection, and general debris removal.</strong></li><li>Install and maintain plant materials including annuals, perennials, trees, shrubs, bulbs, and sod.</li><li>Apply herbicides, pesticides, and insecticides safely and in accordance with all regulatory and product guidelines.</li><li><strong>Prune shrubs, trees, and ornamental plants to promote plant health, structure, and seasonal bloom.</strong></li><li>Monitor turf and plant health, identify pests, diseases, and soil deficiencies, and report concerns promptly.</li><li>Operate and perform basic maintenance on landscape equipment such as trimmers, mowers, chainsaws, blowers, and utility vehicles.</li><li><strong>Assist with turf care through soil testing, seeding, fertilization, irrigation support, and sod installation.</strong></li><li>Collaborate with the irrigation team by monitoring system performance and assisting with repairs when needed.</li><li>Maintain clean, organized, and safe worksites, vehicles, and storage areas.</li><li>Respond to customer and field concerns professionally, escalating issues to Field Leads or Zone Managers when appropriate.</li><li>Complete daily work logs, timesheets, and records with accuracy and attention to detail.</li><li>Lift and transport materials weighing up to 60 pounds with or without reasonable accommodations and perform physically demanding outdoor tasks safely in various weather and terrain conditions.</li><li>Ability to verbally communicate effectively with supervisors and the general public and understand verbal and written instructions and other communications regarding work assignments and other matters.</li></ul> 445060 - Landscape Maintenance & Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/225888 2025-12-17T11:18:37-05:00 2025-12-17T11:18:38-05:00 Theriogenology Intern <div>The Theriogenology internship at North Carolina State University is a 1-year program that is clinically-based with an emphasis primarily on equine and canine species, with additional experience in bovine and small ruminant species.<br><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>There are 2 full-time faculty members, and a 3rd emeritus faculty member in the College with expertise in small animal and equine species.</li><li>There are additional faculty members with board certification in the American College of Veterinary Internal Medicine and American College of Veterinary Surgeons with interests in neonatology, urology and other related fields.</li><li>The intern in Theriogenology will participate in reproductive cases as well as reproductive emergencies in the equine and small animal hospitals.</li><li>Clinical duties will also involve participation in the breeding management, embryo transfer and assisted reproductive techniques (ARTs) of mares and stallions housed at the Theriogenology clinic at the Reedy Creek Farm in Raleigh, NC.</li><li>The intern will be involved in clinical teaching of veterinary students.</li><li>The intern will participate in current research projects of the Theriogenology section.</li><li>Applicants must have a DVM or equivalent degree.</li></ul> 195001 - Department of Clinical Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234445 2026-08-31T17:24:56-04:00 2026-08-31T17:24:57-04:00 Temporary Laboratory Assistant <div>Testing of pectinolytic, cellulytic, and hemi‑cellulolytic activities inherent to endophytes that influence the quality of fermented cucumbers and may enhance the bioactive properties of fermented vegetable blends. The work will involve classic microbiology, aseptic techniques, and microscopy.</div> 111101 - Food, Bioprocessing & Nutrition Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/229641 2026-04-17T09:04:51-04:00 2026-04-17T09:04:52-04:00 Catering Driver <div><a href=\"https://rave.ncsu.edu/\"><strong>Rave! Catering</strong></a> is seeking individuals to Join our Pack as a <strong>Catering Driver</strong>. Rave! Catering proudly employs experienced, certified chefs and catering professionals to provide the highest degree of service possible to ensure successful events.<br><br>Essential job duties include:</div><ul><li>Transport food, equipment and staff for Rave! Catering.</li><li>Pick up prepared foods and deliver to campus retail locations.</li><li>Transport campus supplies within Campus Enterprises departments.</li><li>Load and off-load cargo for delivery or pick up.</li><li>Assist in pulling inventory to be delivered.</li><li>Other duties as assigned to ensure NC State Dining business needs are met. Position may be asked to assist in other locations as necessary.</li></ul><div><br></div><div>Dedicated and experienced professional staff are available to provide on-the-job training and assistance so you become a successful member of the catering team. We offer flexible schedules and the opportunity for learning, growth, and promotion. Superior work performance may provide an opportunity to renew employment and/or advance into a benefits eligible permanent position. This is an hourly position, that includes a State required 32-day break at the end of 11 months. <br><br><a href=\"https://dining.ncsu.edu/about/overview/\"><strong>NC State Dining</strong></a> provides campus wide food services to students, staff, and faculty on the North Carolina State University Campus. NC State Dining operates 11 cafes, 3 food courts and 6 dining halls, 3 markets, 2 restaurants, vending, a nutritional counseling service, and a catering department. The department employs approximately 1000 temporary and student employees and approximately 190 permanent EPS and SHRA employees.<br><br></div> 471501 - Dining Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232208 2026-06-29T08:57:28-04:00 2026-06-29T08:57:28-04:00 Temporary Cotton Breeding Research Associate <div>The research associate will be an integral member of the cotton breeding and genetics research team. The incumbent will play a major role in map-based cloning of agronomically important genes in cotton. Key responsibilities include conducting advanced genetic, genomic, and molecular biology experiments to determine gene structure and function to validate the candidate genes. Essential activities associated with this role include plant phenotyping; harvesting and processing seed; growing and maintaining genetic stocks in greenhouses; collecting tissue samples; performing DNA and RNA isolations; conducting multiple sequence alignments and phylogenetic analyses; qRT-PCR; virus-induced gene silencing; phytochemical analyses; and basic bioinformatics analyses. The research assistant is also expected to collaborate with team members to develop gene knockouts using CRISPR–Cas9 genome editing technologies and methodically documenting and summarizing results and assist in writing draft manuscripts.</div> 110901 - Crop and Soil Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233427 2026-08-03T09:17:13-04:00 2026-08-03T09:17:14-04:00 Temporary-Shop Technician (University Theatre, Costumes, Scenery) <div>The <strong>Division of Academic &amp; Student Affairs</strong> (DASA) is where students discover connections and community. It is where students live, where they gather, where they improve themselves, each other, and their university. DASA is where students seek guidance and support, both in and outside of the classroom. The division serves all students at NC State through three integrated components: academic affairs, student affairs, and University College.</div><div><br></div><div>NC State wants employees to achieve their highest potential at work, learn more about our employee value proposition here (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>You Belong Here</strong></a>). The Division of Academic and Student Affairs is the place to work for values-driven faculty and staff who are passionate about promoting the success of the whole student.</div><div><br><strong>University Theatre</strong> is the campus’ open access community theatre and operates multiple facilities: Stewart Theatre, in the Talley Student Center, and the Titmus Theatre and Kennedy McIlwee Studio Theatre, along with the associated shops and classroom spaces for producing theatre.</div><div>Our mission is to create a welcoming student-centered community that inspires meaningful connections grounded in the experience of making and sharing theatre. We present a wide variety of productions, experiences and provide spaces for campus organizations to present events.</div><div><br><strong>Please note: </strong>These positions are based on need and availability. Applicants will be reviewed on a rolling basis.<br><br>NC State's University Theatre is looking for a Shop Technician that will assist the Costume Shop, Scene Shop, or Electrics Shop Manager with the execution of designs in the respective shops on an as-needed basis.<br><br>Costume Shop Duties include: sewing (by machine and by hand), alterations, repairs, ironing, laundry, fittings, costume crafts, and other assignments based upon skill set and shop needs.<br><br>Scene Shop Duties include: safe use of power and hand tools, basic carpentry and construction, painting, re-furbishing and refinishing of furniture and set pieces, upholstery, and other assignments based upon skill set and shop needs.<br><br>Electrics Shop duties include: hanging, focusing, or striking of lighting fixtures, installation of temporary show related lighting, sound or special effects, repair or maintenance of lighting, sound and/or projection equipment, and other assignments based upon skill set and shop needs.</div> 248901 - University Theatre https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223289 2025-09-25T11:29:35-04:00 2025-09-25T11:29:36-04:00 Temporary Project YES Staff Intern Project YES interns provide youth development programs for children and youth of military families impacted by military deployments. Programs may be delivered during Yellow Ribbon Reintegration Program (YRRP) events (any phase) throughout the United States and all US territories.  Staff interns are not required to be North Carolina State University students. Interns remain at their home university during the internship and will travel (when required) to support program training, YRRP youth development programs, or other Project YES sanctioned events. Youth events may be facilitated through 1) face-to-face in-person) formats whereby interns travel from their home station to the event site or through 2) on-line, virtual platforms, such as Zoom, MS Team, etc. Project YES provides staff interns high level education in leadership development, facilitation skills, youth development and age-appropriate curriculum. Project YES interns also complete a mandatory career-enhancing project (CEP) during the course of the internship. This will be accomplished with the assistance of an individually matched mentor. Together, the mentor and intern will complete a variety of activities designed to better inform and prepare the intern for a smooth, successful school-to-work transition.\r\n\r\n*Interns must attend a mandatory Project YES Intern Orientation and mid-year training (date TBD) and commit to supporting a minimum of one YRRP weekend per month (Fri-Sun) for the duration of the internship.*\r\n\r\n*Additional orientations will be scheduled, as required. Following orientation, interns may work 1-2 weekends a month, however interns must commit to a minimum of one weekend per month (Fri-Sun) for the duration of the internship. Events and facilitation may be conducted in-person or on-line. Additional opportunities will be presented throughout the year for interns interested in supporting additional weekend events.* 111401 - Agricultural and Human Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231724 2026-06-15T14:52:37-04:00 2026-06-15T14:52:39-04:00 Electrician II <div>Are you an experienced electrician who enjoys solving complex technical challenges and making a meaningful impact on campus operations? Join our <strong>Building Maintenance and Operations team</strong> as an <strong>Electrician II</strong>, where you’ll play a vital role in ensuring the reliability, safety, and efficiency of our campus electrical and mechanical systems.<br><br></div><div>This position offers the opportunity to work on a wide range of secondary electrical systems, steam/HVAC equipment, and mechanical components while contributing to a collaborative environment that supports learning, innovation, and campus excellence. If you’re looking to grow your career while doing work that truly matters, we encourage you to apply and become part of our team.</div><div><strong><br>Key Responsibilities and duties (include but are not limited to):</strong></div><ul><li>Install, maintain, and repair secondary electrical systems (550V and below) and related equipment.</li><li><strong>Maintain and troubleshoot electrical components such as conduit, wiring, electrical panels, breakers, switchgear, magnetic starters, disconnects, and motors.</strong></li><li><strong>Repair and maintain</strong> lighting systems, including fixtures, ballasts, switches, and receptacles.</li><li><strong>Diagnose, service, and repair</strong> equipment used in steam generation and distribution, requiring strong mechanical and pipefitting skills.</li><li>Maintain critical systems such as boilers, turbines, pumps, condensate receivers, steam lines, expansion joints, strainers, pressure-reducing stations, piping, insulation, air compressors, and water softeners.</li><li><strong>Perform preventive maintenance on refrigeration and air conditioning equipment per manufacturer guidelines.</strong></li><li>Identify and correct faults in control systems and perform necessary adjustments or repairs.</li><li><strong>Repair, adjust, or replace temperature controls, humidity controllers, sensors, pneumatic control valves, and regulators.</strong></li><li>Lift, carry, and maneuver materials weighing <strong>60–90 pounds, with or without reasonable accommodations.</strong></li><li><strong>Perform</strong> tasks requiring <strong>standing for extended periods</strong>, <strong>working</strong> in confined spaces, and <strong>utilizing</strong> various tools. <strong>Safely climb and work</strong> from different types of ladders to access elevated or below-grade areas (such as roofs, above-ceiling spaces, and utility pits). <strong>Be exposed to</strong> and <strong>work</strong> in areas where insects or small animals are occasionally present. All essential tasks must be performed with or without reasonable accommodations.</li><li>Communicate effectively verbally with supervisors and the general public and understand verbal and written instructions and other communications regarding work assignments and other matters.</li></ul><div><br>If you’re a skilled electrician who takes pride in quality work, teamwork, and continuous learning, <strong>we invite you to apply today</strong>. Your expertise will help us maintain a safe, reliable, and high-performing campus for our students, faculty, and staff.</div> 445030 - Building Maintenance & Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233201 2026-07-27T16:00:00-04:00 2026-07-27T16:00:01-04:00 Senior Lab Manager <div>The Senior Lab Manager provides strategic and operational leadership across Zeis Textile Extension’s pilot-scale textile manufacturing, testing and prototyping laboratories. This role oversees laboratory managers and supports the continued growth, modernization, and utilization of ZTE’s facilities to serve industry, research, workforce development and innovation needs.<br><br></div><div>Working closely with the Zeis Director, Wilson faculty and staff as well as industry partners, the Senior Lab Manager helps position ZTE as a nationally recognized resource for applied textile innovation, technical problem solving, pilot-scale manufacturing, and workforce training. The role is responsible for advancing operational excellence, equipment strategy, laboratory safety, client engagement and business development across the organization’s technical service portfolio.<br><br>Key responsibilities include:<br><br><strong>Leadership and Personnel Management&nbsp;</strong></div><ul><li>Supervise all laboratory managers within ZTE (knitting lab, weaving lab, physical testing lab, dying and finishing lab, and testing and fabrication services, prototyping lab).</li><li>Lead, coach, and develop the site leadership team, building a culture of accountability, responsiveness, collaboration and continuous improvement.</li><li>Foster a collaborative and service-oriented culture that supports technical excellence, workforce development, and continuous learning.</li><li>Manage, develop, and provide training for employees.</li><li>Determine and manage staff assignments, and provide leadership and daily direction of other laboratory personnel.</li><li>Represent the facility to employees, customers, partners and executives.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Operations and Process Excellence&nbsp;</strong></div><ul><li>Lead ZTE technical operations, including production, maintenance, safety, production quality, delivery and financial performance.</li><li>Champion a Safety-First culture and ensure all labs maintain a clean and safe work environment. Update the laboratory safety plan with Environmental Health &amp; Safety (EH&amp;S). Serve on the Wilson College Safety Committee.</li><li>Monitor and improve operational performance across laboratory utilization, project execution timelines, quality, customer responsiveness, and resource allocation.</li><li>Drive operational excellence by optimizing staffing, scheduling, capacity and workflow to support production goals.</li><li>Collaborate with teams to implement scalable processes and improve production flow, applying Lean, Kaizen, 5S and visual management to eliminate bottlenecks and raise operational efficiency.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Business Development and Client Engagement&nbsp;</strong></div><ul><li>Identify opportunities to expand technical services, pilot manufacturing capabilities and industry engagement activities.</li><li>Collaborate with the Zeis Director to grow external funding, technical service revenue and strategic partnerships.</li><li>Serve as the primary industry liaison for priority projects.</li><li>Manage multiple laboratory resources and conduct multiple Fabrication Service Agreement (FSA) projects simultaneously, including establishing priorities in the use of laboratory resources.</li><li>Prepare FSA estimates, write FSAs, coordinate and oversee work associated with the FSAs, and invoice for work performed through the business office in partnership with lab managers.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Technical Expertise and Asset Management&nbsp;</strong></div><ul><li>Oversee the proper and safe use of laboratory equipment and manage preventive equipment maintenance and repairs.</li><li>Lead acquisitions of new equipment to modernize the laboratory and expand capabilities, soliciting quotes for new and used machinery.</li><li>Keep up to date on the latest technology in the field through a strong network of machinery manufacturing contacts and by attending trade shows.</li><li>Manage inventory control.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Strategy and Partnership&nbsp;</strong></div><ul><li>Partner with the Zeis Director and the Assistant Dean of Finance and Operations to develop and drive business planning, ensuring continuous evolution to meet customer needs.</li><li>Translate the business plan into measurable KPIs and manage actions to achieve overall budget and margin targets with each associated entity ZTE.</li><li>Ensure lab readiness for new equipment, technologies and capabilities through strong cross-functional alignment and disciplined change management.</li><li>Collaborate with the Zeis Director, academic departments heads and college faculty to provide appropriate support for academic classes</li></ul> 180401 - Textile Extension https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223231 2025-09-25T10:49:10-04:00 2025-09-25T10:49:10-04:00 LTAP Coordinator <div>This position, up to 30 hours per week, will support LTAP activities at ITRE:<br><br></div><ul><li>Complete data entry for Excel and Access database which LTAP uses to track class registrations, attendance, payments and other essential information that is required for a successful training program</li><li>Prepare communications to promote classes to listserv and on social media</li><li>Assist in preparing the quarterly newsletter</li><li>Travel as needed with classes across North Carolina to act as course administrator</li><li>Other tasks as needed</li></ul> 062001 - Institute For Transportation Research & Education https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234070 2026-08-21T16:05:53-04:00 2026-08-21T16:05:53-04:00 Assistant Professor of Linguistics <div>The Department of English at North Carolina State University invites applications for one full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Linguistics. As a faculty member, you will contribute to our undergraduate major and M.A. concentration through teaching, research, and service.</div><div>The area of specialization is open; however, we are particularly interested in candidates specializing in either:</div><ol><li>corpus linguistics, with a focus on syntax, morphology, or the lexicon; or</li><li>experimental sociolinguistics and/or experimental psycholinguistics.</li></ol><div>If you are passionate about advancing linguistic research, committed to excellence in teaching and mentorship, and eager to contribute to a collaborative academic community, we encourage you to apply.</div> 160201 - English https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/227492 2026-02-16T10:19:03-05:00 2026-02-16T10:19:04-05:00 Lecturer-IT and Analytics <div>The Department of Information Technology, Analytics, and Operations (ITAO) is accepting applications for the position of Non-Tenure Track Faculty Lecturer (part-time) in the Information Technology and Analytics area. Non-Tenure Track Faculty positions are one-semester or one-year, with the possibility of renewal.<br><br>Successful candidates will teach one or more Information Technology and/or Analytics courses for the BSBA (Bachelor of Science in Business Administration) and/or MBA (Masters of Business Administration).<br><br>Specific hiring needs for the academic year have yet to be determined but IT courses could include information systems management, database management, systems development, networking, or systems analysis and design. Analytics courses could include business analytics or data engineering.</div> 208001 - ITAO - Poole College of Management https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234552 2026-09-02T14:55:35-04:00 2026-09-02T14:55:36-04:00 Facilities Maintenance Technician <div>​Join NC State University’s <strong>Building Maintenance and Operations (BMO)</strong> team as a <strong>Facilities Maintenance Technician – Contributing</strong> and build your skills while helping keep our campus facilities safe, reliable, and operating at their best. This hands-on position works within a zone maintenance shop on NC State’s <strong>Original Campus, Centennial Campus, or Centennial Biomedical Campus (Veterinary School)</strong> and supports routine maintenance, operations, and repairs across building systems.</div><div><br>In this role, you’ll develop practical experience across <strong>HVAC, plumbing, electrical, steam, and other building systems</strong> while working alongside experienced mechanics and technicians. You’ll perform preventive maintenance, assist with repairs, operate basic tools and equipment, monitor equipment, identify potential problems, and know when to elevate more complex issues to higher-level mechanics. If you’re dependable, hands-on, eager to learn, and interested in developing your skills in the skilled trades, <strong>we invite you to apply and become part of the NC State Facilities team!</strong></div><div><strong><br>Key responsibilities and duties include but are not limited to:</strong></div><ul><li>Perform basic maintenance, preventive maintenance, and repairs across <strong>HVAC, plumbing, electrical, steam, and other building systems</strong>.</li><li>Assist higher-level mechanics with maintenance, troubleshooting, repairs, installations, and other assigned work.</li><li>Perform routine tasks such as <strong>lubricating, cleaning, inspecting, re-lamping, repairing plumbing fixtures, and checking equipment</strong>.</li><li><strong>Take readings on boilers, chillers, and other equipment; check chemical levels and report unusual conditions or discrepancies.</strong></li><li>Recognize signs of equipment or system problems, including leaks, unusual noises, temperature changes, electrical outages, and equipment malfunctions.</li><li><strong>Troubleshoot basic maintenance issues and determine potential causes.</strong></li><li>Operate and maintain the basic tools and equipment used in assigned trades and follow appropriate procedures for each task.</li><li><strong>Follow preventive maintenance work orders and maintenance requests, completing assigned work accurately and within established timelines.</strong></li><li>Accurately document work performed, including <strong>time, materials, equipment readings, and identified issues</strong>.</li><li>Develop working knowledge across multiple building systems and cross-train in building equipment operations and maintenance.</li><li>Properly isolate and lock out equipment and systems as required before performing maintenance or repairs.</li><li>Maintain a clean, organized, and safe work environment and follow all University safety, health, and established safe-work practices.</li><li>Perform tasks requiring standing for extended periods, working in confined spaces, and utilizing various tools. Safely climb and work from different types of ladders to access elevated or below-grade areas (such as roofs, above-ceiling spaces, and utility pits). Be exposed to and work in areas where insects or small animals are occasionally present. All essential tasks must be performed with or without reasonable accommodations.</li><li>Lift, carry, and maneuver materials weighing 60–90 pounds, with or without reasonable accommodations.</li><li>Communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, and the general public, and follows verbal and written instructions related to work assignments and operational needs.</li></ul> 445030 - Building Maintenance & Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230605 2026-05-13T16:41:48-04:00 2026-05-13T16:41:48-04:00 Neurology Fellowship <div>The Neurology Service at the NC State Veterinary Hospital is seeking a board-certified or residency-trained veterinary neurologist for a 12-month Neurology Fellowship. The focus of the fellowship will be dictated by candidate’s interests. Possible areas of interest include (but are not limited to) neurosurgery, neuroimaging, electro diagnostics, epilepsy, neurodegenerative diseases and dementia, and spinal cord injury.<br><br><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities:<br></strong><br>Provide additional clinical and teaching support to the Neurology Service to ensure there are 2 neurologists on clinical duty throughout the year. Time will be split between being on the clinic floor and advanced training.<br><br></div><ul><li>34 weeks rotating on the clinic floor</li><li>16 weeks dedicated to training in the fellow’s area of interest and research</li><li>2 weeks of vacation</li></ul><div><br></div><div>During the clinical blocks, the fellow will serve as one of two primary clinicians on the Neurology Service, working alongside a second senior neurologist. The majority of the clinic time will be dedicated to supporting the general Neurology Service; however, the fellow will have the opportunity to initiate focus clinics in their area of interest, if appropriate.<br><br></div><ul><li>The off-clinic blocks will be dedicated to advanced training, research efforts, and teaching activities</li></ul><div><br></div><div>If desired, the fellow will identify a research project that they can reasonably get done in the time available and for which appropriate support is available. This project could overlap with any of their training opportunities.</div> 195001 - Department of Clinical Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232744 2026-07-14T18:28:59-04:00 2026-07-15T09:28:59-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div><strong>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong><br><br></div><ul><li>The Postdoctoral Research Scholar will be working in the area of compounding and cascading extreme weather events.</li><li>Research tasks include statistical analysis of historical observations and model simulations of compounding events and evaluation of data-driven earth system model simulations for compounding events.</li><li>The postdoc researcher will support multiple NOAA and NSF funded projects by collaborating with researchers from NC State University, NOAA, and international collaborators.</li></ul><div><strong><br>This position is located in Asheville, NC.</strong></div> 063001 - NC Institute of Climate Studies https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233697 2026-08-10T13:38:56-04:00 2026-08-10T13:38:57-04:00 Budget Analyst <div>The Budget Analyst provides professional budget and financial support to the Poole College Office of Business Services (OBS). Reporting to the Finance Officer, the position assists academic and administrative units with budget development, financial monitoring, and reporting to support effective fiscal management. As, the Budget Analyst you will analyze financial data, prepares budget reports, monitors expenditures, and advises departments on budgetary options and the financial implications of operational decisions.<br><br></div><div>Additionally, you will work collaboratively with college leadership, department administrators, and central university offices to ensure compliance with university policies and funding requirements while supporting responsible stewardship of financial resources.</div><div><strong><br></strong><em>Primary Responsibilities include but aren't limited to:<br></em><br></div><div><strong>Budget Development and Financial Monitoring</strong></div><ul><li>Assist in the preparation and maintenance of annual operating, personnel, and departmental budgets.</li><li>Monitor departmental budgets throughout the fiscal year and identify variances between budgeted and actual expenditures.</li><li>Review expenditures to ensure compliance with university policies, funding restrictions, and budgetary provisions.</li><li>Assist departments in evaluating available funding and understanding the budgetary impact of proposed expenditures.</li><li>Support year-end budget reconciliation and fiscal close activities.</li><li>Monitor salary funding, labor redistributions, and position funding to ensure accurate financial reporting.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Financial Analysis and Reporting</strong></div><ul><li>Compile financial data and prepare recurring and ad hoc budget and financial reports for college leadership and departmental administrators.</li><li>Analyze budget activity and financial trends to support operational decision-making.</li><li>Maintain financial records and reconcile budget information using university financial systems.</li><li>Assist with financial reporting for operating, trust, grant, and other funding sources as assigned.</li><li>Provide supporting documentation and analysis for budget requests and financial reviews.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Budget Consultation and Customer Service</strong></div><ul><li>Advise department administrators and staff regarding budget availability, funding options, and budgetary implications of financial transactions.</li><li>Respond to budget and financial inquiries from faculty, staff, and administrators.</li><li>Interpret university financial policies and procedures related to budget administration.</li><li>Build collaborative relationships with academic and administrative units to support effective financial management.</li><li>Provide guidance on budget processes and financial reporting requirements.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Systems, Processes, and Continuous Improvement</strong></div><ul><li>Assist in developing and maintaining budget tracking tools, spreadsheets, and standard financial reports.</li><li>Identify opportunities to improve budget processes and reporting efficiency.</li><li>Support implementation of process improvements and updates to financial systems.</li><li>Maintain accurate financial documentation and records.</li></ul><div><br>If you are a detail-oriented financial professional who enjoys turning data into meaningful insights, solving problems, and partnering with others to support sound financial decisions, we invite you to apply.&nbsp;</div> 200101 - Dean's Office - Poole College Of Management https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223459 2025-09-29T17:00:31-04:00 2025-09-29T17:00:31-04:00 Temporary Veteran Agricultural Services Support Specialist <div>The Center for Environmental Farming Systems (CEFS) seeks a Veteran Agricultural Services Support Specialist to strengthen and expand apprenticeship opportunities for military veterans pursuing educational opportunities in sustainable agriculture and food systems. This position provides direct support to veterans enrolled in registered apprenticeship programs, assists with veteran recruitment, program administration, and helps coordinate services between veterans, farmers, and CEFS staff. In addition, this position will evaluate the feasibility of creating a pre-apprenticeship program that is also a Skillbridge program. The Specialist will serve as a consistent point of contact for veterans, providing guidance, troubleshooting, and resource navigation to ensure program success and compliance with GI Bill and DoD SkillBridge requirements.</div><div><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities:</strong></div><div><strong>Veteran Support &amp; Advising</strong></div><ul><li>Serve as a primary point of contact for veterans in CEFS apprenticeship and pre-apprenticeship programs.</li><li>Conduct regular check-ins with veterans to track progress, troubleshoot issues, and connect participants with resources.</li><li>Assist veterans with navigating GI Bill and VA education benefits, including Post-9/11 and VR&amp;E paperwork.</li><li>Support veterans in identifying career goals, professional development opportunities, and post-apprenticeship pathways.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Farmer &amp; Host Site Support</strong></div><ul><li>Conduct check-ins with farmer mentors to assess site needs, ensure compliance, and identify opportunities for additional support.</li><li>Provide technical and administrative assistance to farmer mentors to strengthen program quality.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Program Administration &amp; Compliance</strong></div><ul><li>Manage registration and administrative requirements for Department of Defense SkillBridge pre-apprenticeships.</li><li>Maintain records for GI Bill, VA, and apprenticeship compliance reporting.</li><li>Assist with stipend calculations, tool orders, and other program logistics as needed</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Recruitment &amp; Outreach</strong></div><ul><li>Recruit veterans into CEFS apprenticeship and pre-apprenticeship programs through outreach, partnerships, and events.</li><li>Represent CEFS at veteran-focused events, agricultural conferences, and career fairs.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Program Development &amp; Team Support</strong></div><ul><li>Participate in apprenticeship strategy, evaluation, agricultural education, and Career Pathways Initiative meetings.</li><li>Assist with planning and coordinating program events and workshops for veterans.</li><li>Contribute to grant applications, reporting, and program evaluation as needed.</li></ul> 111502 - Center for Environmental Farming Systems (CEFS) https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234356 2026-08-28T17:39:21-04:00 2026-08-31T08:29:45-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar- Intestinal Microenvironment-Stem Cell Interactions <div>The McKinney-Aguirre Lab uses in translational large animal models to study epithelial recovery following clinically-relevant epithelial injury – with a special interest in the role of the microenvironment driving intestinal regeneration. The Lab is housed in the College of Veterinary Medicine and is part of Center for Gastrointestinal Biology and Disease.<br><strong><br>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>The postdoctoral research fellow will participate in the management, processing, and analysis of studies employing a novel ex vivo intestinal perfusion models of intestinal injury.&nbsp;</li><li>The fellow will also optimize new assays for evaluating intestinal response to injury and recovery including the application of ELISAs and advanced sequencing.</li><li>This postdoctoral research fellow will interact regularly with scientists from various research backgrounds including experimental biologists. The successful candidate will be initiative-taking and enjoy working in a collaborative research environment.</li><li>The postdoctoral research fellow will complete experiments, author primary research articles, and attend scientific meetings to present research progress.</li></ul> 194001 - Department of Molecular Biomedical Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228921 2026-03-30T12:48:47-04:00 2026-03-30T13:51:46-04:00 Temporary Research Technician - Clayton, NC <div>This position will assist with all tasks related to the establishment, maintenance, and disposition of the field crops, vegetables, fruit, tobacco and animal specimens.<br><br><strong>This includes, but is not limited to:</strong></div><ul><li>The application of treatments to research subjects according to protocol including pesticides, fertilizers and irrigation</li><li>Operation of a wide variety of agriculture equipment to prepare fields, apply treatments and harvest crops.&nbsp;</li><li>Assisting research personnel with plot layout, implementation of treatments, and collection and recording of data.&nbsp;</li><li>Hand planting and harvest</li><li>Assisting research personnel in the seed lab and swine facility.&nbsp;</li><li>Working with various types of irrigation equipment including laying solid set pipe and drip irrigation.&nbsp;</li></ul><div><br>This position will also assist with maintenance of equipment, grounds, buildings, field plots and other tasks. The validity of research test depends upon the quality of work in the research technician position.&nbsp;</div> 115003 - Central Crops Research Station https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233476 2026-08-03T16:20:21-04:00 2026-08-03T16:34:17-04:00 Temporary Research Assistant <div>The successful candidate will conduct research to evaluate gene drive systems for the suppression of the New World screwworm. Responsibilities include designing, planning, conducting, and interpreting genetic experiments in consultation with Dr. Scott. The individual will collaborate closely with other members of the screwworm gene drive research team, including Dr. Yadav and Ms. Belikoff, to achieve established monthly project milestones.<br><br></div><div>Additional responsibilities include maintaining accurate and detailed laboratory notebooks, ensuring compliance with all laboratory protocols, and adhering to all biosafety and containment requirements for gene drive insects. The successful candidate will be expected to contribute effectively in a collaborative research environment while maintaining the highest standards of scientific integrity, data management, and laboratory safety.</div> 111801 - Entomology and Plant Pathology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223298 2025-09-25T11:32:21-04:00 2025-09-25T11:32:22-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>The Kinga Unocic Research Group in the Department of Materials Science and<br>Engineering at North Carolina State University is seeking a Postdoctoral Research<br>Scholar to start December 2024. The Postdoc Research Scholars’ primary duties will<br>include performing high-temperature oxidation testing, characterization of oxidation<br>coupons, and assisting with the lab setup.<br><br><br><strong>Other Duties and Responsibilities<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Assist with the lab setup</li><li>Perform high-temperature oxidation testing and monitor sample testing</li><li>Perform post-characterization</li><li>Maintain the lab operations</li><li>Develop and implement MATLAB/Python scripts for data analysis and automation</li></ul> 140801 - Materials Science &Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234261 2026-08-27T11:19:14-04:00 2026-08-27T11:19:14-04:00 Alumni Coordinator <div>The Alumni Coordinator serves as a key relationship-builder and ambassador for the College of Education by developing, strengthening, and sustaining meaningful connections with alumni, students, faculty, staff, donors, and community partners. This position is responsible for advancing alumni engagement initiatives that foster lifelong relationships with the college and promote a strong sense of community, pride, and philanthropic support. Through strategic outreach, impactful programming, stewardship activities, communications, and events, the Alumni Coordinator plays an essential role in enhancing alumni participation, volunteerism, mentorship opportunities, and advancement efforts in support of the college’s mission and long-term strategic priorities.<br><br></div><div>This position works collaboratively across the college to cultivate a vibrant alumni network that supports student success, professional development, career exploration, community engagement, and institutional advancement. This role requires a highly relational, organized, and proactive professional who is passionate about higher education, engagement, and building authentic relationships across populations and generations of alumni.<br><br><strong>Key Responsibilities<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Alumni Communications &amp; Engagement</strong></div><ul><li>Develop and support alumni engagement communication initiatives designed to strengthen alumni connection, participation, and affinity with the College of Education.</li><li>Assist in the creation and coordination of alumni-focused content including digital newsletters, email campaigns, donor stewardship messaging, alumni spotlights, event promotions, social media collaboration, and engagement materials.</li><li>Serve as a liaison and collaborative partner with university advancement and annual giving initiatives related to alumni outreach and engagement communications.</li><li>Support strategic communication efforts that promote alumni networking, mentoring, volunteer engagement, continuing education opportunities, and professional development initiatives.</li><li>Maintain and support accurate alumni records, contact information, engagement tracking, and participation data to strengthen outreach efforts and long-term relationship management.</li><li>Analyze engagement trends and assist in identifying opportunities to increase alumni participation and connectivity across programs and initiatives.<br><br></li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Relationship Development &amp; Volunteer Engagement</strong></div><ul><li>Cultivate, develop, and steward meaningful relationships with alumni across a broad range of backgrounds, professions, career stages, and geographic regions.</li><li>Recruit and support alumni volunteers for mentorship programs, speaking engagements, networking initiatives, advisory opportunities, student engagement activities, and college events.</li><li>Develop strategies to increase alumni involvement and strengthen connections between alumni, current students, faculty, and the broader college community.</li><li>Conduct proactive outreach to alumni to preserve institutional relationships, strengthen long-term engagement, and enhance continuity of institutional knowledge and community connection.</li><li>Identify and support opportunities for alumni leadership, advocacy, and volunteer participation in support of college initiatives and strategic priorities.</li><li>Performs other duties as assigned in support of alumni programming, engagement events, continuing education initiatives, and the overall mission and strategic goals of the College of Education and Advancement Office.<br><br></li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Alumni Programming, Events &amp; Continuing Education</strong></div><ul><li>Partner with alumni leadership groups and advisory boards to support the development and achievement of strategic alumni engagement goals and initiatives.</li><li>Coordinate, plan, and facilitate alumni events and engagement activities including networking events, mentoring opportunities, reunions, continuing education initiatives, recognition programs, annual celebrations, tailgates, and special college events.</li><li>Support the development of innovative programming that fosters professional growth, career connection, lifelong learning, and community engagement among alumni.</li><li>Collaborate with internal and external partners to ensure successful execution of alumni engagement initiatives and programs.</li><li>Assist with event logistics, communications, registration management, volunteer coordination, budgeting support, and post-event assessment activities.</li><li>Other duties as assigned that support the mission, goals, and strategic priorities of the Alumni Coordinator role and the College of Education Advancement Office.<br><br></li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Advancement &amp; Philanthropic Engagement Support</strong></div><ul><li>Partner with advancement and development staff to strengthen alumni engagement as it relates to philanthropy, donor cultivation, volunteerism, and institutional support.</li><li>Support donor stewardship initiatives by assisting with gratitude communications, alumni recognition efforts, engagement tracking, and relationship-building activities.</li><li>Assist with Day of Giving and other fundraising initiatives by coordinating student philanthropy activities, increasing alumni participation, and supporting donor engagement efforts.</li><li>Help cultivate a culture of philanthropy and alumni investment within the College of Education community.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>This position is open for current NC State Employees only, permanent or temporary.</strong></div> 130101 - College Of Education https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228340 2026-03-12T12:07:44-04:00 2026-03-12T12:07:45-04:00 Open Rank Lecturer or Teaching Assistant Professor <div>The successful candidate (s) will teach undergraduate courses in a specific discipline in World Languages &amp; Cultures according to departmental need and employee knowledge, skills and ability.</div><div><br>Courses may be taught in any of the languages for the classes that we offer. Appointments may be available part time for Summer 2026 and/or 2026-2027 Academic Year, all with the possibility of renewal.</div> 160401 - World Languages and Cultures https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234693 2026-09-04T08:24:11-04:00 2026-09-04T08:24:12-04:00 Equine & Farm Animal Clinical Technician <div>The <strong>Equine &amp; Farm Animal Field Services Clinical Technician</strong> provides high-level technical support across all farm animal species (including bovine, caprine, ovine, and camelid) as well as equine patients when assisting hospital floor technicians. This role collaborates closely with clinicians to perform clinical treatments, animal restraint, diagnostic testing, and operational management, while actively supporting the hands-on clinical instruction of veterinary students, interns, and house officers.</div><div><strong><br>Key Responsibilities:<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Clinical Support &amp; Patient Care</strong></div><ul><li>Assist veterinarians with physical examinations, diagnostic procedures, animal handling, and humane restraint.</li><li>Administer prescribed medications and treatments, perform diagnostic laboratory tests, and continuously monitor patient health status.</li><li>Maintain complete, accurate, and timely medical records and patient flow sheets.</li><li>Strictly adhere to and enforce all hospital Infectious Disease Control protocols to ensure biosafety.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Teaching &amp; Instruction</strong></div><ul><li>Assist in training, guiding, and supervising veterinary students, interns, and house officers in clinical skills, animal restraint, and patient management procedures.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Service &amp; Administrative Management</strong></div><ul><li>Oversee field service vehicle management, including scheduling, routine maintenance, and inspection logs.</li><li>Manage service inventory: monitor stock levels, order/purchase supplies, and ensure cost-effective resource utilization.</li><li>Ensure accurate diagnostic and treatment billing, audit flow sheets for accuracy, and reconcile service records.</li><li>Facilitate clear communication and seamless collaboration between field services, hospital floor teams, and ancillary hospital units.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Facility &amp; Equipment Maintenance</strong></div><ul><li>Maintain all clinical equipment and specialized field gear, ensuring proper sanitation, calibration, and adherence to routine repair schedules.</li><li>Keep clinical preparation areas and field response units stocked, organized, and ready for immediate deployment.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Physical &amp; Professional Demands:</strong></div><ul><li>Requires extensive mixed-animal handling experience, the ability to lift up to 75 lbs, with or without reasonable accommodation.</li><li>Requires extended physical stamina, strong technical/computer skills, and professional communication under high-stress conditions.</li></ul> 192001 - CVM Teaching Hospital https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231133 2026-06-01T15:54:52-04:00 2026-06-01T15:54:52-04:00 Research Technician (Gimeno) <div>A research technician is needed to assist with animal experiments, laboratory mainteinance, and run laboratory techniques (cell culture, extraction of nucleic acids, qPCR, RT-qPCR, flow cytometry)</div> 196001 - Department of Population Health and Pathobiology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232973 2026-07-21T14:15:36-04:00 2026-07-21T14:15:37-04:00 Landscape Architect <div><strong>Campus Planning and Strategic Investment</strong> at NC State plays a vital role in optimizing the design and use of campus land, buildings, and infrastructure to support the university’s mission. Through collaboration and data-driven analysis, the team ensures financial and environmental stewardship while planning for both current and future needs.</div><div><br>As our next <strong>Landscape Architect</strong>, you’ll help translate NC State’s strategic vision into the physical spaces that bring it to life. This position offers the opportunity to influence how our campus grows—<strong>developing innovative and sustainable solutions that enhance the student experience, foster community, and advance the university’s long-term goals.<br></strong><br></div><div>If you’re passionate about purposeful design, strategic planning, and creating spaces that inspire connection and innovation, please apply.</div><div><br><strong>General Scope of Duties</strong></div><ul><li>Translate the strategic plans of the University into the physical facilities required to support them. Provide solutions for a broad range of landscape architectural and engineering planning issues essential to meeting the campus’s development needs.</li><li>Provide general project support for physical facilities planning efforts at NC State. Analyze, document, and develop physical planning projects, policies, procedures, and informal project designs aligned with the University’s strategic goals.</li><li>Consider and evaluate a wide range of multi-disciplinary factors, including social, economic, and environmental impacts, related to the development of the campus’s physical facilities.</li><li>Coordinate planning efforts with campus design professionals to ensure alignment with long-range physical planning goals.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Typical duties include:</strong></div><ul><li>Developing physical facilities planning and design strategies for exterior campus spaces</li><li>Conducting comparative studies and facilities program analyses</li><li>Identifying and developing project alternatives</li><li>Recommending actions to support implementation of the Physical Master Plan, with a focus on exterior spaces and site design</li><li>Preparing feasibility studies and physical development plans for campus components such as stormwater management, infrastructure, transportation systems, pedestrian access, and other general planning and development needs</li><li>Develop, monitor, and update project budgets to ensure alignment with project scope and funding requirements</li><li>Verify budget adequacy for scope of work and review/approve consultant invoices in accordance with University standards</li><li>Establish and maintain comprehensive project schedules and documentation, including meeting minutes key stakeholder communications</li></ul> 442010 - Campus Planning https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233942 2026-08-18T13:23:18-04:00 2026-08-18T13:23:19-04:00 Journey Utilities Plant Operator - Dayshift <div>Behind every class, laboratory, residence hall, and campus event is a team making sure NC State has the reliable utilities it needs to operate. As a <strong>Utility Plant Operator</strong>, you’ll play a critical role in keeping the university running by operating and monitoring the central utility systems that provide electricity, steam, hot water, and chilled water across campus.</div><div><br>This is an opportunity to work with a variety of utility equipment and gain valuable experience in a central utility environment. You’ll work alongside experienced utility professionals to monitor plant operations, troubleshoot equipment, perform inspections, and help maintain safe, efficient, and reliable service. If you’re dependable, safety-minded, mechanically inclined, and interested in learning how large-scale utility systems work, <strong>we want to hear from you. Please apply today!</strong></div><div><br><strong>Working Hours: 12 hr. shift, 3-4 days a week, 6:00 AM - 6:00 PM.<br>Working hours may change to meet the needs of the University.</strong><br><br><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li><strong>Assist in the control and monitoring</strong> of steam boilers, chillers, cogeneration equipment, and auxiliary systems to ensure continuous utility services.</li><li>Maintain safe and efficient operations of steam, hot water, and chilled water production systems.</li><li>Conduct real-time monitoring, make necessary adjustments, and ensure compliance with emissions and regulatory standards.</li><li><strong>Inspect plant equipment, including fuel gas compressors, air filters, pumps, and cooling towers.</strong></li><li>Work under the guidance of a shift supervisor to optimize plant performance and address operational challenges.</li><li>Support the operation of the <strong>Cates and Centennial Cogeneration Facilities</strong> to produce electricity and steam for the campus.</li><li>Lift and transport materials weighing up to 60 pounds with or without reasonable accommodations and perform a wide range of physically demanding tasks in varied outdoor environments, ensuring safe handling practices and maintaining productivity in varying weather and terrain conditions.</li><li>Communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, and the general public, and follow verbal and written instructions related to work assignments and operational needs.</li></ul> 445050 - Utilities and Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/226265 2026-01-07T16:38:15-05:00 2026-01-07T16:38:17-05:00 CARES Coordinator with Outreach Emphasis <div>The CARES Coordinator in NC State University’s Prevention Services (Division of Academic and Student Affairs) is responsible for conducting behaviorally and clinically informed assessments with students to identify and address barriers to personal and academic success, including safety threats. The role provides referrals, coordinates interventions, collaborates with campus partners, and delivers outreach, training, and prevention programming. Coordinators may also hold an area(s) of emphasis based on operational needs.</div><div><strong><br>Student Assessment and Case Management&nbsp;<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Conduct behaviorally and clinically informed one-on-one assessments with students to identify and mitigate barriers to personal and academic success, including threat assessment for self or others.</li><li>Provide timely and appropriate referrals to university and off-campus resources; coordinate intervention plans to minimize or resolve student concerns.</li><li>Monitor and track student progress to ensure compliance with recommendations, ongoing assessment, and identification of emerging concerns.</li><li>Create and manage re-entry strategies for students returning after psychological hospitalization.</li><li>Serve on key cross-campus support and assessment teams:<ul><li>NC State CARES Team – assess and support students of concern.</li><li>Behavioral Assessment Team (BAT) – assist with threat assessments when appropriate.</li><li>Sexual Assault Response Team (SART) – participate as needed.</li></ul></li><li>Participate in after-hours postvention and case review rotation; occasional evening or weekend outreach responsibilities.</li><li>Maintain service schedule of approximately 50% direct student/faculty/staff support and 50% administrative responsibilities.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Data Management, Documentation &amp; Assessment&nbsp;<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Accurately document all correspondence, notes, and action items in the Maxient database.</li><li>Assist in managing and maintaining ethically sound data collection and analysis related to students of concern.</li><li>Use data to support continuous improvement and best-practice development in case management and prevention services.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Outreach, Prevention Education &amp; Training&nbsp;<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Provide comprehensive outreach and consultation to faculty, staff, students, and families through presentations, workshops, and creative multi-media efforts.</li><li>Collaborate with campus partners to ensure consistent, integrated messaging around prevention, well-being, and student support.</li><li>Complete required training (e.g., QPR Suicide Prevention) and deliver training programs to students, faculty, and staff.</li><li>Support university-wide prevention initiatives addressing topics such as suicide prevention, case management, and well-being.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Program Coordination, Areas of Emphasis &amp; Campus Partnerships&nbsp;<br></strong><br></div><div>Coordinators may hold responsibility for one or more <strong>Areas of Emphasis</strong> based on unit needs.<br><br></div><ul><li>Coordinate resources, programming, and direct services within the assigned area(s) of emphasis (e.g., suicide prevention, alcohol and other drugs/recovery, peer education, training, assessment).</li><li>Serve as the primary consultant for campus partners related to the assigned area(s).</li><li>Maintain up-to-date content and resources for the designated emphasis area(s).</li><li>Represent Prevention Services in relevant committees, meetings, or initiatives.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Professional Collaboration, Supervision &amp; Other Duties&nbsp;<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Engage with interns, graduate assistants, and temporary employees; may supervise or mentor these roles.</li><li>Contribute to overall Prevention Services operations and team initiatives.</li><li>Complete other duties as assigned within the scope of a prevention specialist and departmental needs.</li></ul> 244025 - Prevention Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234461 2026-09-01T11:43:48-04:00 2026-09-01T11:43:48-04:00 UTS - Temporary Concur Support Desk Associate at UNC <div>The primary purpose of this role includes but is not limited to: serve as a member of the team. The position will be the central campus resource for questions and assistance regarding the use of the Concur Request &amp; Expense System. This position provides exceptional customer service and acts as an expert travel expense reconciliation resource at the University. This position will ensure compliance with university travel &amp; expense policies and regulations through education efforts and will provide expert guidance on travel expense-related topics.<br><br>Travel Services supports an end-to-end travel program that includes pre-travel approval, travel booking, expense reimbursement, and the reconciliation of university-provided payment methods. The mission of the team is to deliver exceptional customer service, support, and training; provide timely reimbursement processing; and ensure compliance with University Policies, State of North Carolina Policies, and Federal Policies as applicable.<br><strong><br>Remote Position</strong></div> 480502 - UHR UTS/UNC Partnership https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/229675 2026-04-20T09:23:21-04:00 2026-04-20T09:23:22-04:00 CLAWS Manufacturing Systems Engineer <div>We are seeking a motivated Manufacturing Execution System (MES) / Database Engineer to support and develop the digital infrastructure of our advanced semiconductor research foundry. The position focuses on enhancing MES workflows and managing Oracle-based backend systems to improve process traceability, data integrity, and operational efficiency in an R&amp;D fabrication environment.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities:</strong></div><div><strong>MES Workflow Development &amp; Support (40%)</strong></div><ul><li>Create and implement MES workflows to support custom semiconductor fabrication process flows. Support foundry users in establishing new fabrication process flows.</li><li>Build and maintain digital traveler systems for lot tracking, process documentation, and process monitoring.</li><li>Integrate MES with process tools and data acquisition systems.</li><li>Develop intuitive user interfaces and dashboards for tracking materials, fabrication lots, and operating metrics, including process controls (SPC).</li><li>Ensure data consistency and traceability throughout fabrication process flows.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Database Architecture &amp; Management (30%)</strong></div><ul><li>Administer databases storing process metadata, experiment logs, and tool usage history.</li><li>Design and maintain scalable schemas and optimize query performance for large datasets.</li><li>Ensure data security, backup protocols, and system uptime.</li><li>Support analytics and reporting needs from internal teams and external partners<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Data Integration &amp; Automation (20%)</strong></div><ul><li>Automate data collection pipelines from lab equipment, process logs, and measurement tools.</li><li>Support integration with homegrown and third-party web-based applications.</li><li>Develop and maintain APIs, ETL processes, and scheduled jobs to support workflows.</li><li>Enable clean data delivery for analytics, visualization, and AI/ML use cases.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Collaboration &amp; Support (10%)</strong></div><ul><li>Work with process engineers, cleanroom staff, and IT to gather requirements and implement MES/database updates.</li><li>Support continuous improvement through data accessibility and system enhancements.</li><li>Implement and maintain document control protocols within MES.</li><li>Document workflows, user guides, and SOPs for MES and data systems.</li><li>Provide training and troubleshooting for end users.</li></ul> 140110 - CLAWS https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232252 2026-06-30T09:10:09-04:00 2026-06-30T09:10:11-04:00 Electrician <div>The primary purpose of this position is to provide expedient and professional maintenance to the students who live in residence halls, apartments, and fraternity/sorority houses on the campus of NC State.&nbsp;<br><br>The main functions of this position are to construct, install, maintain, and repair electrical systems, equipment, and fixtures in the residence halls, apartments, and fraternity/sorority houses for DASA Facilities.</div> 242290 - Housing Facilities Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228956 2026-03-31T10:57:00-04:00 2026-03-31T10:57:01-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar - BAE <div>The Department of Biological and Agricultural Engineering at North Carolina State University invites applications for a full-time postdoctoral research associate to work with Dr. Joe Sagues in the area of biocarbon energy storage. The position has an initial one-year appointment and the potential for extension based on performance and funding availability.<br><br></div><div>The successful candidate will be expected to conduct and publish peer-reviewed research on sustainable energy storage materials, with a particular emphasis on the synthesis and application of biomass-derived systems. In addition to generating high-quality publications, the candidate will contribute to leading research initiatives aimed at developing next-generation energy storage technologies. A central focus of this work will be the design and implementation of biocarbon electrodes, advancing the use of renewable resources in scalable and effective energy storage solutions while collaborating with faculty, research staff, and students to drive innovation in this emerging field.</div><div><br>You Belong Here! At NC State, our goal is for all employees to reach their fullest potential at work. As you consider this opportunity, we encourage you to review our <a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>Employee Value Proposition</strong></a> (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/</strong></a>) and learn more about what makes NC State the best place to learn and work for everyone.</div> 118101 - Biological And Agricultural Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231731 2026-06-15T15:54:38-04:00 2026-06-15T15:54:40-04:00 Electronics Specialist <div>Join NC State Facilities as an<strong> Electronics Specialist</strong> and play a critical role in supporting the systems that keep our campus running. This position provides advanced technical expertise in the maintenance, troubleshooting, calibration, and repair of sophisticated electronic and digital control systems within our thermal plant operations.<br><br></div><div><strong>As an Electronics Specialist, you will work with the technology and infrastructure that support high-pressure boilers, chillers, cogeneration systems, and other critical utility equipment</strong>. Your work will directly contribute to the safe, reliable, and efficient delivery of essential services across campus. This role offers the opportunity to solve complex technical challenges, work with cutting-edge control systems, and collaborate with a highly skilled team dedicated to operational excellence.</div><div>We are seeking a motivated professional who enjoys hands-on technical work, thrives in a problem-solving environment, and takes pride in maintaining mission-critical systems. The ideal candidate will possess strong troubleshooting abilities, attention to detail, and a commitment to safety and reliability.<br><br></div><div>This is an exciting opportunity to build your career while supporting one of the largest and most dynamic university campuses in the country. If you are ready to apply your technical expertise, contribute to essential campus operations, and join a team that values innovation and continuous improvement, we encourage you to apply today.</div><div><br><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li><strong>Independently troubleshoot mechanical, electromechanical, electronic, and digital control systems for thermal plant equipment, including chillers, boilers, fuel systems, and cooling towers.</strong></li><li>Identify and resolve unusual or complex plant control issues that require in-depth analysis and the application of journey-level trade knowledge.</li><li><strong>Diagnose and repair network communication issues and Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition (SCADA) systems to ensure seamless data flow and equipment monitoring.</strong></li><li>Troubleshoot non-standard electronic devices (controllers, transmitters, sensors, actuators) even in instances where documentation is incomplete or unavailable.</li><li>Independently perform recurring PM on control systems and field devices to prevent equipment downtime and ensure peak efficiency.</li><li>Execute service and maintenance on thermal plant digital control systems, managing both physical hardware and the underlying software interfaces.</li><li>Perform non-recurring tasks, including the installation of meters and specialized control devices, utilizing advanced electronic principles.</li><li><strong>Calibrate, test, and maintain</strong> a variety of field devices to ensure accurate sensing and mechanical response within the utility plant.</li><li>Ensure all installations and repairs meet or exceed State and University codes, as well as Facilities Division standards.</li><li>Conduct thorough inspections prior to, during, and upon completion of work to verify the proper maintenance of utility systems.</li><li>Maintain strict safety standards while working on high-performance utility systems to protect personnel and university assets.</li><li>Gather and interpret system information to understand the nature of complex malfunctions and determine the most efficient course of action.</li><li><strong>Evaluate multiple repair options, considering potential outcomes and operational impacts, before implementing solutions.</strong></li><li>Communicate clearly and effectively with supervisors, team members, and the general public to facilitate smooth daily operations.</li><li>Comprehend and execute verbal and written instructions regarding work assignments, safety protocols, and general workplace matters.</li><li>Lift and transport materials weighing up to 60 pounds with or without reasonable accommodations.</li></ul> 445050 - Utilities and Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233211 2026-07-28T10:59:02-04:00 2026-07-28T10:59:03-04:00 Extension Program Assistant-4-H Youth Development & Family & Consumer Sciences <div>Are you looking for a rewarding career that will let you give back to your community? NC State Extension extends research-based knowledge to all North Carolinians, helping them transform science into everyday solutions that improve their lives and grow our state. We are the model of excellence in non-formal, research-based education that advances agriculture, the environment, human health and well-being, youth and communities, while creating prosperity for all North Carolinians.<br><br></div><div>While our employees are located in NC County offices, they reap the benefits of employment at NC State University. Some of our benefits include:<br><br></div><div>· Paid time off<br><br></div><div>· Paid tuition<br><br></div><div>· Health Insurance<br><br></div><div>· Flexible Scheduling<br><br></div><div>· Retirement Savings<br><br></div><div>· Supplemental Benefits Programs<br><br></div><div>· WolfPerks!<br><br></div><div>You Belong Here! At NC State, our goal is for all employees to reach their fullest potential at work. As you consider this opportunity, we encourage you to review our <a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>Employee Value Proposition</strong></a> (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/</strong></a>) and learn more about what makes NC State the best place to learn and work for everyone.<br><br><strong>Primary Function of Organizational Unit</strong></div><div>North Carolina Cooperative Extension gives our residents easy access to the resources and expertise of NC State University and NC A&amp;T State University. Through educational programs, publications, and events, Cooperative Extension field faculty deliver unbiased, research-based information to North Carolina citizens.<br><br>New Hanover County is located in southeastern North Carolina between the Cape Fear River and the Atlantic Ocean. It is the second smallest county in the state but is home to over 238,000 people. Projections predict its population to increase to almost 300,000 by 2040. <br><br>The City of Wilmington is the county seat and is rich in historical significance and home to many recreational and cultural sites and events. Important contributors to the local economy include the state port, tourism, filmmaking, manufacturing, and financial technology.<br><br>More information about New Hanover County Extension Center, visit their website:<br><a href=\"https://newhanover.ces.ncsu.edu/\"><strong>https://newhanover.ces.ncsu.edu</strong></a></div><div><br><strong>Overview</strong><br>This position supports New Hanover County Cooperative Extension programs, specifically focusing on the delivery, administration, and coordination of positive youth development programs through 4-H and community health education through Family and Consumer Sciences (FCS).<br><strong><br>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong></div><div>● <strong>School Enrichment Coordination:</strong> Lead the coordination and delivery of 4-H school enrichment programs in local schools, with a primary focus on managing the 4-H Embryology program (including teacher training, classroom presentations, and logistics).</div><div>● <strong>4-H Club and Program Support:</strong> Assisting the 4-H Agent, providing administrative and programming support to 4-H community clubs, including volunteer management, enrollment tracking, curriculum distribution, and assisting with 4-H Teen Leaders and county, district and state level presentation events.</div><div>● <strong>Summer Camp Support:</strong> Assist with the planning, registration, marketing, and delivery of 4-H summer camps and workshops hosted at the Arboretum or off-site locations.</div><div>● <strong>Healthy Living Programming:</strong> Collaborate with the FCS Agent to deliver educational programs focused on youth healthy living, physical activity, and nutrition.</div><div>● <strong>FCS Program Support:</strong> Provide programming support for the Family and Consumer Sciences (FCS) program, assisting with workshop registration, food safety or nutrition class preparation, community health outreach, and resource distribution.</div><div>● <strong>Database &amp; Reporting:</strong> Maintain accurate records of youth enrollment, volunteer hours, and program participation; enter monthly demographic and program impact data into the Extension Reporting System (ERS).</div><div>● <strong>Communication &amp; Marketing:</strong> Draft regular newsletter updates, social media posts, and marketing materials to promote 4-H clubs, summer camps, and upcoming FCS classes to families and schools.</div><div>● <strong>Customer Service:</strong> Provide professional, welcoming customer service by handling inquiries from parents, educators, and the public regarding youth enrollment, program schedules, and class registrations.</div><div>● <strong>Compliance &amp; Professionalism:</strong> Maintain professionalism and confidentiality when handling youth medical forms, volunteer background checks, and program participant records, strictly following North Carolina Cooperative Extension and university policies.<br><br></div> 117809 - Southeast District - New Hanov https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223236 2025-09-25T10:52:09-04:00 2025-09-25T10:52:10-04:00 LAS Intern <div>The Laboratory of Analytic Sciences (LAS) requires an intern to complete research in the areas of interest to LAS. Potential areas of research include but are not limited to: social sciences, applied sciences and engineering.<br><br>This posting will remain open as we hire interns throughout the year from institutions other than NC State University.</div> 061801 - Lab for Analytical Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234095 2026-08-24T12:16:10-04:00 2026-08-24T12:16:11-04:00 Food Services Supervisor <div>NC State Dining is seeking a dynamic, hands-on <strong>Food Service Supervisor</strong> to oversee the daily operations of a campus café or market. This is a working supervisory role where you will lead by example, driving exceptional customer service and culinary quality during peak service hours. If you are a collaborative leader dedicated to fostering a safe, high-energy environment for the Wolfpack community, we want you on our team!</div><div><strong><br>Key Responsibilities<br></strong><br></div><ul><li><strong>Daily Operations &amp; Leadership:</strong> Oversee front- and back-of-house operations, including opening/closing procedures, cash handling, register setup, and attractive product displays.</li><li><strong>Team Management:</strong> Interview, hire, schedule, and train student and temporary staff. Mentor employees through cross-training, one-on-one coaching, and daily duty assignments.</li><li><strong>Quality &amp; Inventory Control:</strong> Maintain brand-specific standards for food and beverage quality. Assist with vendor ordering, conduct regular inventories, and utilize the C-Board food system to manage food and labor costs.</li><li><strong>Customer Service Excellence:</strong> Maintain a strong presence to resolve customer issues on the spot, build rapport with guests, and ensure swift, friendly service.</li><li><strong>Sanitation &amp; Safety:</strong> Uphold strict Wake County Health Department and food code rules to maintain a <strong>Grade A sanitation rating (95% or better)</strong>. Ensure all allergen best practices and sanitation logs are strictly followed.</li></ul> 471501 - Dining Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/227524 2026-02-16T15:57:24-05:00 2026-02-16T15:57:25-05:00 Lecturer - Marketing <div>The Department of Marketing is accepting applications for the position of Non-Tenure Track Faculty Lecturer (part-time) in the area of Marketing, Marketing Analytics, or New Product Innovation.<br><br>Professional Faculty positions are one semester or one-year, with the possibility of renewal. Applications will remain on file for one year should openings arise in subsequent semesters.<br><br>Successful candidates will teach 1 or more courses for the Marketing concentration in the BSBA (Bachelor of Science in Business Administration), and/or Marketing or related courses in the MBA (Masters of Business Administration) or other graduate programs in the College.<br><br>Specific hiring needs for the academic year have yet to be determined but could include marketing management, marketing research, new product development, or courses in other marketing topics.</div> 207001 - Marketing-Poole College of Management https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234563 2026-09-02T16:03:52-04:00 2026-09-02T16:03:52-04:00 Temporary Research Technician <div><strong>Duties include:</strong></div><ul><li>Processing large numbers of arthropod samples under the stereoscope.</li><li>Identifying and recording numbers of major insect and mite pests of small fruit crops.</li><li>Labeling and organizing arthropod samples.</li><li>Assisting with greenhouse and field collection of arthropod samples.</li><li>Experience using a stereoscope is preferred.</li><li>Experience with arthropod identification is preferred but is not required; training can be<br>provided.</li><li>Assisting with fieldwork involving the excavation and uprooting of blueberry plants from research plots approximately 2–3 times per month, primarily under outdoor field conditions and direct sunlight.</li><li>Performing physically demanding field work that requires prolonged standing, bending, kneeling, digging, and working in variable weather conditions.</li><li>Safely operating heavy-duty hand tools and field equipment, including shovels, hammers, axes, pruning shears, heavy-duty scissors, sharpened cutting tools, and chainsaws, while following all laboratory and field safety procedures.</li><li>Opening and processing plant root masses in the laboratory to extract, collect, and document beetle larvae using hand tools and following established laboratory protocols.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Schedule:<br></strong>35-40 hours per week, 8 am-5 pm with 1 hour lunch break. Fieldworkdays may be longer 10-11-hour days (1-3 days per week, 1-2 times per month).</div> 111801 - Entomology and Plant Pathology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230715 2026-05-18T08:56:37-04:00 2026-05-18T08:56:38-04:00 Dishwasher <div><strong>NC State Dining</strong> is seeking enthusiastic individuals to Join our Pack as a <strong>Dishwasher/Stock Clerk</strong>. Successful candidates may work in a dining hall, retail operation, food court, or restaurant setting.<br><br>Essential job duties include:</div><ul><li>Washing dishes, glassware &amp; serving utensils, bowls, pots &amp; pans, using conveyor belt machine or by hand, ensuring they are cleaned and sanitized.</li><li>Maintaining the flow of the dish room by having a steady supply of clean dishes, utensils, and glassware ready for use.</li><li>Assist, as needed, with restocking and cleaning the beverage and dining area, maintaining and restocking all public restrooms in the facility and removing all trash, and must sweep and mop at the end of the shift.</li><li>Assist with checking in food stock items, breaking down pallets, putting away food stock in a timely and organized manner, and placing received items in appropriate storage areas.</li><li>Performing necessary cleaning tasks in accordance with maintaining a Grade “A” Sanitation rating.</li><li>Other duties as assigned to ensure NC State Dining business needs are met. Position may be asked to assist in other locations as necessary.</li></ul><div><br>This is an hourly position, that includes a State required 32-day break at the end of 11 months. Superior work performance may provide an opportunity to renew employment and/or advance into a benefits eligible permanent position.<br><br><a href=\"https://dining.ncsu.edu/about/overview/\"><strong>NC State Dining</strong></a> provides campus wide food services to students, staff, and faculty on the North Carolina State University Campus. NC State Dining operates 10 cafes, 3 food courts and 6 dining halls, 4 markets, 1 restaurant, vending, a nutritional counseling service, and a catering department. The department employs approximately 1000 temporary and student employees and approximately 190 permanent EPS and SHRA employees.</div> 471501 - Dining Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232758 2026-07-15T11:31:53-04:00 2026-07-15T11:31:55-04:00 Research Technician <div>The Research Technician position will provide operational support for agricultural research projects. Primary crops include peaches, blueberries, ornamentals, cotton, soybeans, and turfgrass. This position will provide knowledge and skills to support existing research programs through field work, tractor and other farm equipment operation, pesticide application, and cultural crop care. Position is task-oriented with a heavy focus on equipment operation and field work.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Conduct field work activities in support of ongoing agricultural research projects.</li><li>Operate tractors and other farm equipment safely and efficiently.</li><li>Apply pesticides according to established protocols and safety guidelines.</li><li>Provide cultural care for various crops.</li><li>Assist in the coordination and execution of research experiments and trials.</li><li>Maintain accurate records of field activities, observations, and data collection.</li><li>Collaborate with research scientists and other team members to achieve project objectives.</li><li>Lift up to 50 lbs. on a routine basis, with or without reasonable accommodations.</li></ul><div><br>Please note that this position is located in Jackson Springs, NC.</div> 115002 - Sandhills Research Station https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233733 2026-08-11T14:40:12-04:00 2026-08-11T14:40:13-04:00 UTS-Temporary Event Preparation Staff <div><br>NC State Continuing and Lifelong Education (NCSCaLE) is a division of the Provost within the Office of the Vice Provost for Continuing Education. The McKimmon Conference and Training Center (MCTC) reports to the Continuing &amp; Professional Education Director. MCTC has 33,000 square feet of meeting space and assists with set-ups, exhibits, educational event gatherings, catering, and printing services which include shipping and receiving, and can provide 650 spaces of free parking.</div><div>MCTC works with campus partners, private/corporate business industry, and local, state, and federal government. MCTC is dedicated to partnering with our global-class faculty and academic partners, as well as cultivating a culture of continuous lifelong learning by identifying and integrating best practices throughout your journey with us. MCTC can help create a meaningful experience that transforms into a lifelong experience. MCTC strives to enrich the lives of people and stay on the cutting edge of the industry.<br><br></div><div><strong>Primary purpose of the position:</strong></div><div>The Event Preparation Staff plays a crucial role in ensuring the successful execution of various events, including workshops, conferences, and seminars. For example, the Bar Exam, Cyber Security, Engineering Career Fair, and Global Green Energy. Event Staff responsibilities span before and after an event. Here are the key duties:</div><ol><li>Planning and Preparation:<ul><li>The Logistics Manager/Co-Crew Leader will provide the Event Prep set-up instructions via Social Tables, clients, or coordinators for each space.</li><li>Ensure precise collaboration and accuracy in setting up event spaces are complete.</li><li>Manage all logistics and manage all scheduling concerns</li><li>Ensure compliance with safety and health regulations.</li></ul></li><li>Post-Event:<ul><li>Break down all of the equipment and place the equipment (chairs/tables) in the storage area.</li></ul></li></ol><div><strong>Other Work/Responsibilities:</strong> Occasional specialized duties may include Audio/Visual technical assistance and operation of specialized equipment as training allows.</div><div><br>The Event Preparation Staff contributes to exceptional customer service and ensures that events run smoothly, efficiently, and positively reflect on the brand. Event Staff plays a key role in creating memorable experiences for attendees!</div><div><br><br></div> 480501 - UHR Temporary Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223797 2025-10-08T09:54:30-04:00 2025-10-08T09:54:30-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>The Computer Science department at North Carolina State University (NCSU) invites applications for a postdoc. The position will exist within the Secure Software Supply Chain Center (S3C2) that is part of the Secure Computing Institute (SCI) led by Drs. Laurie Williams and William Enck. S3C2 is a large-scale multi-institutional research enterprise and community responding to the rise in software supply chain attacks in the software industry (as emphasized in Section 4 of Executive Order 14028). S3C2 is an international leader in software supply chain security and an essential community catalyst for secure development methods, tools, and metrics that are adopted by practitioners and that re-establish trust in the software supply chain; and educates a varied workforce of technical leaders and practitioners. S3C2 includes NCSU faculty and collaborators at Carnegie Mellon University, the University of Maryland, and Paderborn University (in Germany).<br><br>The postdoc will perform the following Duties:</div><ul><li>Conduct research in the area of secure software supply chain security</li><li>Mentor undergraduate and graduate students conducting research.</li><li>Lead and participate in public engagement and outreach activities.</li><li>Coordination of and participation in quarterly center meetings</li></ul> 141901 - Computer Science Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234363 2026-08-29T16:07:43-04:00 2026-08-29T16:07:43-04:00 Temporary Research Specialist <div>The persontaking this position will support research on developing novel gene drive systems for<br>suppression of the New World screwworm. Their main duties will be rearing fly strains,<br>which will involve cleaning cages, preparing diet, setting cages, collecting eggs and setting<br>larval cultures, autoclaving waste, obtaining supplies, checking fly traps and working on<br>other tasks to support research of the senior staff working on the project. The personwill maintain accurate laboratory notebooks and follow all lab safety requirements andrequirements for 20-containment of genetically modified insects.</div> 111801 - Entomology and Plant Pathology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232278 2026-06-30T13:28:25-04:00 2026-06-30T13:28:26-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>A postdoctoral research scholar position is immediately available. The selected candidate will work under the direct guidance of Dr. Michael Nolan (radiation/cancer biologist and veterinary radiation oncologist). Significant mentorship will also be provided by key collaborators: Drs. Duncan Lascelles (translational pain researcher and oncologic surgeon) and Santosh Mishra (neurobiologist and expert in genetically engineered mouse models).<br><br>We are a growing group of inquisitive young researchers who are passionate about improving quality of life and survivorship for patients. NC State provides an outstanding academic environment for professional growth and development. And we are part of an exceptional community of researchers in neurobiology, as well as cancer, radiation, and pain biology/medicine; there are excellent core facilities and resources both at NC State, and our partnering institutions: Duke University and the University of North Carolina - Chapel Hill.<br><br><strong>This will be a two-year minimum position.</strong><br><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>The scholar will work with other lab members to lead NIH-funded research projects, studying mechanisms of pain, and interactions between nerves and cancer.</li><li>The projects involve bench top cell culture experiments, studies using genetically engineered mouse models, behavioral testing, and potential involvement in co-clinical trials in both humans and dogs with cancer.</li></ul> 195001 - Department of Clinical Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233509 2026-08-04T14:19:10-04:00 2026-08-04T14:19:11-04:00 Temporary Research Technician <div>Support the Small Grains and Official Variety Trial programs by assisting with seed processing, seed inventory, and plot preparation for the 2026–2027 research season. Responsibilities include counting and processing seed, collecting and managing research data, and maintaining accurate records throughout the growing season.<br><br></div><div>Conduct field visits to monitor and document trial conditions, collect agronomic data, and assist with evaluating research plots. Support the installation, planting, maintenance, and harvest of field, greenhouse, and laboratory research projects involving wheat, soybean, corn, rice, and other crops as needed.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>&nbsp;Count, process, package, and organize seed for research trials.&nbsp;</li><li>&nbsp;Assist with data collection, entry, quality control, and management for variety trials.&nbsp;</li><li>&nbsp;Visit research field locations to monitor crop development and document trial conditions.&nbsp;</li><li>&nbsp;Collect agronomic and phenotypic data following established research protocols.&nbsp;</li><li>&nbsp;Assist with planting, irrigation, fertilization, pest management, and general maintenance of field and greenhouse research plots.&nbsp;</li><li>&nbsp;Support harvesting, sample processing, and post-harvest data collection.&nbsp;</li><li>&nbsp;Operate and maintain research equipment and assist with laboratory activities.&nbsp;</li><li>&nbsp;Maintain accurate records and ensure compliance with research protocols and safety procedures.&nbsp;</li><li>&nbsp;Perform other research-related duties as assigned.</li></ul> 110901 - Crop and Soil Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223302 2025-09-25T11:37:57-04:00 2025-09-25T11:37:58-04:00 Catering Event Supervisor <div><a href=\"https://rave.ncsu.edu/\">Rave! Catering</a> is seeking enthusiastic individuals to Join our Pack as a <strong>Catering Events Supervisor</strong>. Rave! Catering proudly employs experienced, certified chefs and catering professionals to provide the highest degree of service possible to ensure successful events.<br><br>The primary purpose of this position is to supervise the prep work and setup for catering events around campus. Essential job duties include, but are not limited to:</div><ul><li>Supervise staff in the the absence of a catering event coordinator ensuring that events are successful.</li><li>Professionally and successfully execute all components of the Banquet Event Order (BEO).</li><li>Prepare silverware and napkins, and perform general stockroom duties.</li><li>Provide food and beverage service for buffets, break services, receptions and plated meals.</li><li>Other duties as assigned to ensure NC State Dining business needs are met. Position may be asked to assist in other locations as necessary.</li></ul><div><br>Dedicated and experienced professional staff are available to provide on-the-job training and assistance so you become a successful member of the catering team. We offer flexible schedules and opportunity for learning, growth, and promotion. Superior work performance may provide an opportunity to renew employment and/or advance into a benefits eligible permanent position. This is an hourly position, that includes a State required 32-day break at the end of 11 months. <br><br><a href=\"https://dining.ncsu.edu/about/overview/\"><strong>NC State Dining</strong></a> provides campus wide food services to students, staff, and faculty on the North Carolina State University Campus. NC State Dining operates 11 cafes, 3 food courts and 6 dining halls, 3 markets, 2 restaurants, vending, a nutritional counseling service, and a catering department. The department employs approximately 1000 temporary and student employees and approximately 190 permanent EPS and SHRA employees.</div> 471501 - Dining Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234271 2026-08-27T14:19:03-04:00 2026-08-27T14:19:05-04:00 Assistant Engineer <div>NC State Athletics is a leader in video and broadcast production, supporting high-profile live events and innovative media experiences across its athletic venues. We are seeking a <strong>Broadcast Engineering professional</strong> to join our team and help ensure the reliable operation and delivery of complex productions across the department.</div><div><br>Reporting to the Director of Engineering, this position plays a hands-on role in maintaining broadcast and A/V systems, supporting ESPN/ACC Network events, preparing production equipment, and helping bring multiple productions together on tight timelines. The ideal candidate is technically capable, highly organized, self-directed, and comfortable working in a fast-paced live production environment where priorities can change quickly.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities</strong></div><ul><li>Assist the Director of Engineering with the maintenance, troubleshooting, configuration, and technical operation of broadcast facilities, A/V systems, video boards, and digital signage throughout athletic venues.</li><li>Provide engineering support for ESPN/ACC Network event broadcasts hosted or supported by NC State facilities.</li><li>Plan and coordinate equipment allocation and deployment for multiple productions.</li><li>Set up, test, and prepare production equipment, often supporting multiple events within tight turnaround windows.</li><li>Perform basic maintenance, configuration, and troubleshooting of broadcast and production equipment.</li><li>Train and mentor student operators in equipment operation, safe handling practices, professional production techniques, and production etiquette.</li><li>Apply knowledge of live broadcast programming concepts, production principles, methodologies, and technical practices to support successful events.</li><li>Supervise student staff as needed, including scheduling, load-ins and strikes, coaching and directing, intern management, and oversight of engineering projects.</li><li>Ability to lift up to 30 pounds frequently and up to 60 pounds occasionally, with or without reasonable accommodations.<br><br></li></ul><div>This role requires someone who can <strong>take initiative, work independently, and stay focused in a fast-moving production environment</strong> while collaborating effectively within a team. The successful candidate will be comfortable managing multiple priorities, responding with urgency when situations demand it, and working through complex technical challenges with appropriate support from colleagues.</div><div><br>If you enjoy live sports, broadcast technology, and the challenge of making complex productions work seamlessly behind the scenes, this is an opportunity to make a direct impact on the fan and broadcast experience at NC State Athletics.</div> 105002 - Athletics - Internal/External Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228435 2026-03-13T15:16:14-04:00 2026-03-13T15:16:15-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>The Machado Lab evaluates and develops computational epidemiological methods integrating disease evolution, animal movement, and spatial processes to estimate transmission and spread. We develop new tools for tracking spread and prescribing non-pharmaceutical interventions.<br><br>This position will co-lead the development of new mathematical disease spread models that analyze the propagation of swine diseases and Highly Pathogenic Avian Influenza (HPAI) at farm and barn levels across multiple states.<br><strong><br>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong><br><br></div><ul><li>Develop multiscale spatial mathematical models (e.g., Pigspread, MHASpread) and calibrate them onto curated data (e.g., animal movement, weekly disease occurrence, genetic sequences, on-farm biosecurity).</li><li>Quantify the role of individual transmission routes to spread between and within commercial poultry farms and swine operations.</li><li>Evaluate controls, biosecurity strategies, and assess the role of ventilation systems as alternative biocontainment control interventions.</li><li>Work with extensive population datasets on swine and large commercial poultry-producing states.</li><li>Prepare peer-reviewed publications and collaborate effectively with animal health departments, USDA, poultry veterinarians, and swine producers.</li></ul> 196001 - Department of Population Health and Pathobiology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234700 2026-09-04T09:17:05-04:00 2026-09-04T09:17:06-04:00 HR Generalist for HR Data and Operations <div>The HR Generalist – HR Data &amp; Operations serves as a front-line HR resource responsible for managing and auditing sensitive employee and position data within university HR systems. This role is fundamental to the integrity of college HR operations and records, ensuring the accuracy and security of personnel information while identifying opportunities to improve HR processes and reduce redundancies in workflows.</div><div><br></div><div>As a vital member of the Wilson College of Textiles, you will support the only college in North America solely focused on textiles. Our faculty and researchers apply “textiles thinking” to solve the world’s grand challenges, from medical innovation to environmental sustainability. As the <strong>HR Generalist – HR Data &amp; Operations</strong>, you provide the critical data integrity and operational stability that allow these world-class innovators to focus on their mission of global advancement.</div><div><br></div><div><strong>HR Data Management &amp; Onboarding&nbsp;<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Independently manage and evaluate HR data transactions associated with new hires, job changes, and other employee lifecycle events, ensuring transactions are accurately processed and consistent with university requirements.</li><li>Administer payroll and leave-related HR processes, including management and tracking of family medical leave and other leave types, and review leave balances and potential overdrawn leave situations to determine appropriate next steps.</li><li>Manage student hiring and graduate appointment processes, including evaluating eligibility and ensuring appointments are processed in accordance with applicable university requirements.</li><li>Process summer salary appointments and related additional compensation transactions.</li><li>Review HR data and documentation to identify discrepancies, determine appropriate next steps within established policies and procedures, and coordinate resolution with central HR or other appropriate resources when necessary.</li><li>Maintain electronic personnel records and serve as the college’s OnBase data custodian, ensuring records are maintained in accordance with applicable record retention, security, and confidentiality requirements.</li><li>Manage I-9 and E-Verify processes, including applicable visa documentation, and identify employment eligibility or compliance issues requiring additional review or escalation to University Human Resources.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong><br>System Auditing &amp; Compliance&nbsp;<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Independently review HR workflows, transactions, and compliance requirements to identify errors, inconsistencies, process gaps, and areas of risk or noncompliance, and coordinate appropriate corrective actions.</li><li>Use HR and business systems to develop, implement, and maintain HR processes and workflow tools that improve efficiency and reduce manual work.</li><li>Analyze recurring data, compliance, and workflow issues to determine underlying causes and recommend appropriate process or system improvements.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong><br>Reporting &amp; Analytics&nbsp;<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Analyze HR data from multiple internal systems and data sources, including PeopleAdmin, PeopleSoft HR/FIN/SIS, and other institutional data sources, to identify trends, discrepancies, and operational issues and support college leadership’s evaluation of student hiring trends, ratios to measure performance, salary equity across job families, and other annual reporting needs.</li><li>Evaluate the reliability and completeness of data sources, determine appropriate methods for compiling information, and develop and produce recurring and ad-hoc reports in response to operational and management needs.</li><li>Interpret findings and communicate relevant trends, data issues, and recommendations to HR leadership and managers to support operational decision-making.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong><br>Support, Guidance &amp;Training<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Serve as a primary HR resource for managers and team members, providing guidance on HR data, position and employee transactions, HR systems, policies, procedures, and related administrative processes.</li><li>Assess and resolve moderately complex HR process and system inquiries, identifying appropriate solutions, resolving issues independently, and escalating matters when appropriate.</li><li>Coordinate HR services and processes with the HR team, college administrative units, and central university service areas, including onboarding, systems access, required training, compliance activities, and candidate interview logistics.</li><li>Develop or deliver guidance for training and process needs on HR systems and processes, including time and leave management, performance management, and other tools that support effective HR administration.</li><li>Participate in college committees and initiatives related to employee engagement, recognition, well-being, culture, and college-wide activities.<br><br></li></ul><div>If you are someone who takes pride in keeping things organized, enjoys solving problems, and approaches your work with care and sound judgment, we invite you to apply. If you value being a dependable resource for others, enjoy finding ways to make processes work better, and take pride in providing accurate, responsive service, we would welcome the opportunity to have you join the Wilson College of Textiles HR team.&nbsp;</div> 180101 - Textiles - Dean's Office https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231167 2026-06-02T13:53:50-04:00 2026-06-02T13:53:51-04:00 Data Science Librarian <div>The Data Science Librarian provides expert consultative support, workshop-based training, and course-integrated instruction for students, faculty, and researchers in the areas of data science and visualization. The position leads DSS's support around tabular data, spreadsheet software and tools, and data visualizations, while also contributing to other data science topics aligned with their skills and patron needs. They design, develop, and deliver workshops, both as part of an open workshop series and in collaboration with faculty for course-integrated instruction. They engage with faculty, campus groups, and external partners to support the university’s data and visualization needs, and conduct relevant outreach and participate in sponsoring events. The position collaborates with colleagues and faculty on data-related initiatives and projects, contributes to departmental projects and initiatives, and may mentor or supervise graduate student consultants in their skill areas.<br><br></div><div>The position deeply considers how their approach to their daily work reflects and advances the <a href=\"https://www.lib.ncsu.edu/strategic-priorities\"><strong>Libraries’ Strategic Plan</strong></a>, creating an environment and community that is welcoming for both patrons and colleagues alike. The position participates in library planning and serves on library-wide committees, task forces, and teams. Librarians are expected to be active professionally and to contribute to developments in the field. This position reports to the Department Head, Data Science Services.</div><div><br></div><div>This position is based in North Carolina. This position is eligible for flexible hours with a hybrid work environment. This position will require frequent onsite work.</div><div><br></div><div>NC State promotes an integrated approach to problem solving that transforms lives and provides leadership for social, economic, and technological development across North Carolina and around the world. NC State’s land grant mission of teaching, research and service is dedicated to the service of North Carolina and its people. Applicants are encouraged to review the institution’s mission, vision and strategic plan, and consider how their background, interest and experience would enable them to support the university.</div> 250101 - Libraries https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232989 2026-07-21T15:36:15-04:00 2026-07-21T15:36:16-04:00 Business Services Coordinator <div>The Department of Nuclear Engineering is seeking a highly organized and customer-focused professional to serve as a key member of its Business Office. In this role, you will support both the department and the Nuclear Reactor Program (NRP) by coordinating business operations, managing financial and administrative processes, and serving as a trusted resource for faculty, staff, students, and external partners. Your work will help ensure the smooth day-to-day operation of the department while contributing to an environment that supports teaching, research, and student success.<br><br></div><div>Key Responsibilities:</div><ul><li>Coordinate administrative and business operations for the Department of Nuclear Engineering and the Nuclear Reactor Program.</li><li>Manage complex financial transactions, purchasing, reconciliations, and related business processes while ensuring compliance with university policies and procedures.</li><li>Maintain academic and graduate student records, supporting departmental programs with accuracy and attention to detail.</li><li>Serve as a primary point of contact for faculty, staff, students, and external vendors by providing timely information, resolving questions, and facilitating effective communication.</li><li>Support program management activities and departmental initiatives by organizing priorities, tracking deadlines, and ensuring business operations run efficiently.<br><br></li></ul><div>This role is a great fit for someone who enjoys variety, values teamwork, and finds satisfaction in creating efficient, well-organized processes. If you're a dependable professional who communicates effectively, adapts to changing priorities, and is committed to providing exceptional service, we encourage you to apply.</div> 141101 - Nuclear Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233950 2026-08-18T14:26:07-04:00 2026-08-18T14:26:08-04:00 Assistant Vice Chancellor, Campus Planning and University Architect <div>The <strong>Assistant Vice Chancellor, Campus Planning and University Architect</strong>, provides leadership for planning and strategic investment within the Facilities Division. This position leads university-wide planning initiatives that guide the long-term development, stewardship, and strategic investment of NC State’s physical environment, aligned with the university’s mission and strategic plan and serves as or supervises the university architect.<br><br></div><div><strong>The position is responsible for the development, implementation, and ongoing stewardship of the university’s Physical Master Plan</strong>, which serves as the framework for campus growth, land use, infrastructure investment, sustainability, and design decision-making across NC State’s campuses and research properties, including the Raleigh campus precincts, Lake Wheeler Field Laboratory, and Reedy Creek Field Laboratory.<br><br></div><div>Reporting to senior Facilities Division leadership, the <strong>Assistant Vice Chancellor, Campus Planning and University Architect </strong>provides leadership for campus planning, infrastructure planning, facilities information systems, and sustainability initiatives. The position collaborates extensively with university leadership, the UNC System, local and regional governments, faculty, staff, students, and community stakeholders to ensure the university’s physical environment supports teaching, research, outreach, student success, and operational excellence.<br><br><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><div><strong><br>Strategic Campus Planning</strong></div><ul><li>Lead the development, implementation, and periodic updates of the university’s Physical Master Plan and Campus Development Process, including the 6-Year Capital Plan and capital budget development.</li><li>Ensure campus planning and capital development initiatives align with the university’s strategic priorities, long-range vision, and design principles.</li><li>Chair the Campus Design Review Panel and provide architectural leadership for campus design standards, aesthetics, development guidelines, wayfinding, and signage systems.</li><li>Collaborate with Design and Construction to guide capital project planning, programming, scope development, and design review.</li><li>Lead site planning, landscape planning, space programming, architectural studies, and environmental planning efforts, including the Urban Forest Management Plan and stewardship of Lake Raleigh Woods.</li><li>Coordinate university engagement with municipal, regional, and community planning initiatives.</li><li>Present planning recommendations and master plan updates to university leadership, the Board of Trustees, and the Buildings and Property Committee.</li></ul><div><strong>Infrastructure Planning</strong></div><ul><li>Lead long-range infrastructure planning, capital renewal strategies, and lifecycle management of university facilities, utilities, and major building systems.</li><li>Direct the assessment, prioritization, and development of data-driven capital renewal plans, investment strategies, dashboards, and reporting tools that support institutional decision-making.</li><li>Coordinate Facilities Division Capital Needs Requests and support development of biennial and long-range capital plans.</li><li>Ensure infrastructure investments support institutional growth, operational reliability, risk mitigation, financial stewardship, and the strategic use of utility and asset information systems.</li></ul><div><strong>Facilities Information Systems and Space Management</strong></div><ul><li>Oversee campus GIS programs, mapping systems, and maintenance of the official University Campus Map.</li><li>Manage building information, floor plans, room inventories, and space data systems to ensure accurate records, reporting, and strategic planning.</li><li>Direct management of Facilities’ physical and digital plan room, including project documentation, as-built drawings, O&amp;M manuals, specifications, real estate records, and hazardous materials reports.</li><li>Provide analytics, reporting, and business intelligence to support space utilization, capital planning, utilities management, research administration, and university operations.</li><li>Maintain building records, digital archives, and campus addressing standards.</li></ul><div><strong>Sustainability Leadership</strong></div><ul><li>Provide leadership for the Sustainability Office and implementation of the university’s Sustainability Strategic Plan.</li><li>Advance sustainability initiatives that promote student engagement, applied learning, climate action, resource stewardship, and environmentally responsible operations.</li><li>Integrate sustainability, energy efficiency, and environmental stewardship into campus planning, design, construction, renovation, and operational practices.</li><li>Champion partnerships and solutions that improve building performance, reduce lifecycle costs, and support institutional sustainability goals.</li></ul><div><strong>Governance and Committee Leadership</strong></div><ul><li>Chair the Campus Planning Subcommittee, Space Working Group, and Campus Design Review Panel.</li><li>Advise and support the Campus Development Committee on capital planning, land use, design, and space allocation decisions.</li><li>Support the Board of Trustees Buildings and Property Committee on matters related to the Physical Master Plan and campus development.</li><li>Serve on university committees and advisory groups related to sustainability, environmental stewardship, stormwater management, GIS, public art, and urban forestry.</li><li>Support the NC State Sustainability Council and serve as an ex officio member of capital project building committees.</li></ul> 442010 - Campus Planning https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/226650 2026-01-20T08:49:18-05:00 2026-01-20T08:49:18-05:00 Temporary Program Specialist of Operations and Financial Analyst <div><strong>The Program Operations and Financial Analyst</strong> <strong>will:</strong></div><ul><li>Manage program budgets to ensure expenditures align with approved grant budgets and do not exceed budget line items.</li><li>Review and verify that purchased materials and services align with estimated costs used in budget development.</li><li>Track and manage inventory of program materials, maintaining accurate and up-to-date inventory records.</li><li>Establish and monitor inventory reorder points based on average usage and historical trends.</li><li>Respond to customer and partner organization requests in a timely, professional, and service-oriented manner; accurately fulfill orders.</li><li>Interact with vendors to obtain and evaluate quotes, ensuring accuracy in pricing, quantities, and materials.</li><li>Place purchase orders with vendors and monitor order fulfillment and delivery timelines.</li><li>Process and track vendor invoices to support timely payment and accurate financial reconciliation.</li><li>Create, complete, and maintain funder performance reports to track program implementation and outcomes.</li><li>Develop and maintain performance dashboards for 55 participating organizations using Excel, Google Sheets, and related tools.</li><li>Create and update internal reports tracking cumulative monthly progress toward annual performance goals.</li><li>Monitor university reporting and tracking systems to ensure participating organizations submit required monthly activity data.</li><li>Create and update monthly reports detailing program elements delivered by each participating organization.</li><li>Compile and organize monthly funder reports, including progress toward performance goals and supporting documentation for expenditures.</li><li>Utilize Excel, Google Suite (Forms, Sheets, Drive), Canva, and other web-based tools to support data management, reporting, and communication.</li><li>Maintain organized, accurate records while demonstrating reliability, attention to detail, and consistent follow-through.</li><li>Collect, organize, and manage participant evaluation forms.</li><li>Enter evaluation results into statistical software program (SPSS), establish variables, value range, and conduct analyses to provide insights into program trends and impacts.</li><li>Assist the PI with other activities related to fiscal management, data analyses and reporting.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Work Schedule:<br></strong>The Unit’s hours of operations are Monday through Friday, 8am to 5pm.&nbsp; The schedule for this position will be up to 20 hours per week with an agreed-upon work schedule.<br><br></div> 111401 - Agricultural and Human Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234485 2026-09-01T15:12:55-04:00 2026-09-01T15:12:56-04:00 Director of Creative Media & Content Strategy <div>Serve as the primary creative director responsible for overseeing all creative content (video, photography, graphic design, social media) ideation, creation, and delivery strategies for the football program.<br><br><strong>Responsibilities and duties include but are not limited to:</strong></div><ul><li>Coordinates the creative direction, ideation, plans, estimates and timetables for football content</li><li>Creation and distribution on social media channels, website, video boards and other external facing channels</li><li>In collaboration with other athletic external units, help direct and guide messaging strategy and storytelling for the football program</li><li>Coordinates the main creative content calendar for football, including sponsored content</li><li>Oversight of all football day to day and game day creative operations and content collection - including but not limited to practices, workouts, team travel, in-game, pre-game &amp; post-game</li><li>Collaborate with coaching staff to produce internal highlight and motivational content</li><li>Collaborate with recruiting staff to produce intentional and innovative content for the player acquisition process</li><li>Coordinates the organization and archiving of all yearly creative content into athletic department storage platforms</li><li>Oversight of the student creative intern program to include<ul><li>selection and onboarding</li><li>expectations and compliance</li><li>mentoring, training and development</li></ul></li><li>Oversee the forecasting, creating and tracking the football creative budget</li><li>Purchasing and inventory of all equipment, technology, materials, and supplies</li><li>Any other duties assigned by the General Manager and Head Coach</li><li>Follow all compliance related rules of NC State University, NCAA &amp; ACC</li></ul><div><br><strong><em>This posting is for current permanent NC State University employees only.&nbsp;</em></strong></div> 101012 - Football https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230026 2026-04-29T13:23:18-04:00 2026-04-29T13:23:19-04:00 Temporary Research Technician <div>The primary duties of the Technician position involve maintaining a clean and organized lab environment, perform equipment maintenance, ensuring safe operations of laboratory facilities in the department, assist researchers in experimental plan execution, completing annual equipment inventory. This position may also assist PIs to supervise undergraduate and graduate student research and use of laboratory equipment. This individual will be responsible for ensuring the overall safety of the lab spaces, coordinating across groups to implement the lab safety plans and good laboratory practice while regularly interacting with faculty, staff, students, and departmental farm staff to maintain smooth operation of the laboratory. Primary responsibilities include lab inventory and supply maintenance, coordinating experiment calendars, feed orders, chemical and biohazard safety protocol implementation, and prepare lab safety protocols.</div> 111901 - Prestage Poultry Science https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223916 2025-10-09T17:43:50-04:00 2025-10-09T17:43:51-04:00 Postdoc Research Scholar - Ferreira Lab <div>A one-year renewable position for a Postdoc is available with the Department of Population Health and Pathobiology at North Carolina State University College of Veterinary Medicine. <br><strong><br>Duties and Responsibilities</strong></div><ul><li>The purpose of the position is to advance swine health, welfare and productivity.</li><li>The candidate will play an integral role in advancing pain management strategies for pigs on farm in the US.&nbsp;</li><li>The ideal candidate must have extensive experience conducting on-farm and identifying pain behaviors in swine. In addition, candidates should have experience analyzing data.&nbsp;<br><br></li></ul><div>The candidate must have earned a PhD degree (in a field related to veterinary medicine or animal sciences with preference for welfare experience) and be able to work independently and collaboratively with colleagues that are part of the Animal Welfare and Behavior Lab at NCSU.<br><br>This position is anticipated to have funding for one year.<br><br></div> 196001 - Department of Population Health and Pathobiology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234375 2026-08-31T08:52:09-04:00 2026-08-31T08:52:11-04:00 Assistant Professor and Extension Specialist in Row Crop Entomology <div>The Department of Entomology and Plant Pathology seeks a tenure-track Assistant Professor (12-month) to provide statewide leadership in arthropod management for North Carolina row crop production, with a focus on sweetpotato, peanut, and soybean. The position is <strong>70% Extension and 30% research</strong>, connecting applied research, grower needs, and emerging technologies to strengthen sustainable pest management across North Carolina agriculture.<br><br></div><div>You’ll have the opportunity to build on your expertise in row crop entomology, integrated pest management, pest ecology, and insecticide resistance while shaping innovative approaches to pest prediction, decision support, and resistance management. Your work may draw on field experimentation, landscape-scale studies, pest monitoring, and data-driven tools, including artificial intelligence, precision agriculture, remote sensing, and drones. Through your research and Extension program, you’ll translate new knowledge into practical solutions that help growers and agricultural professionals make informed, sustainable pest management decisions.</div><div><br><strong>Key responsibilities fall within the following functional areas.<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Extension &amp; Outreach:</strong></div><ul><li>Shape a statewide Extension program that helps North Carolina growers address arthropod pest challenges in major row crops, including sweetpotato, peanut, and soybean.</li><li>Translate research into practical recommendations that help growers, consultants, and industry stakeholders make informed pest management decisions.</li><li>Connect with agricultural professionals and growers through field days, Extension programming, and innovative digital decision-support tools.</li><li>Build strong partnerships with commodity groups, Extension agents, and state and federal partners to address emerging arthropod management challenges.</li><li>Support the growth and development of graduate students and other trainees through mentorship, research guidance, and professional development opportunities.</li><li>Strengthen agricultural workforce capacity by developing training and professional development opportunities for Extension agents, consultants, and agricultural professionals.</li><li>Advance adoption of integrated pest management through innovative technologies and decision-support tools that translate research into practical solutions for North Carolina agriculture.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Research &amp; Discovery:</strong></div><ul><li>Build an applied, externally funded research program that responds to grower needs and strengthens the impact of your Extension work.</li><li>Advance understanding of pest ecology and management through field- and landscape-scale research addressing current and emerging crop protection challenges.</li><li>Share new knowledge and practical solutions through peer-reviewed publications, Extension resources, and other scholarly contributions.</li><li>Secure and steward extramural funding to advance applied research and strengthen the impact of Extension priorities.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Mentorship, Teaching &amp; Service:</strong></div><ul><li>Mentor graduate students and other trainees through research guidance, professional development, and career preparation.</li><li>Contribute to undergraduate and graduate instruction through teaching courses and supporting the department’s educational mission.</li><li>Contribute expertise and perspective through departmental, college, university, and professional service activities.<br><br></li></ul><div>If you are a collaborative entomologist who enjoys connecting research with real-world agricultural challenges, building strong relationships with growers and partners, and developing innovative solutions for sustainable crop protection, we encourage you to apply.</div><div><br></div> 111801 - Entomology and Plant Pathology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228980 2026-03-31T14:00:11-04:00 2026-03-31T14:00:13-04:00 Lecturer <div><strong>Essential Job Duties</strong></div><div>The Department of Education Leadership, Policy, and Human Development (ELPHD) in the College of Education is accepting applications for the position of Lecturer – Professional Track Faculty (part-time) for the 2026 - 2027 Academic Year. Professional track Faculty positions do not carry rank and are one-semester or one-year, with the possibility of renewal.</div><div><br></div><div>Successful candidates will teach 1-3 courses on the undergraduate or graduate curriculum, depending on the department’s needs. Teaching is in one of the following areas:<br><br></div><ul><li>Educational Evaluation and Policy Analysis</li><li>Higher Education</li><li>Community College Leadership</li><li>Adult and Lifelong Education</li><li>Counseling and Counselor Education</li></ul><div><br></div><div>This position will remain open through the end of the academic year; however, we will be screening applicants on an ongoing basis.</div> 131601 - Educational Leadership, Policy & Human Development (ELPHD) https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231763 2026-06-16T13:18:45-04:00 2026-06-16T13:18:45-04:00 UTS - Temporary General Talent Pool at NC State and UNC Chapel Hill <div>University Temporary Services at NC State University and UNC Chapel Hill is currently seeking qualified professionals for a variety of different roles at each campus including: Administrative, HR, Accounting, Program Management, Executive-level support, etc.<strong>Recruitments with UTS tend to change on a daily basis and we are unable to post every opportunity we receive therefore, this application serves as the first step in the process to be considered for our current openings.<br></strong><br></div><div>Please see below for the general categories and pay rate ranges for these positions:<br><br></div><div><strong>Administrative</strong> ($17-30 per hour)<br><br></div><div><strong>Accounting</strong> ($19-30 per hour)<br><br></div><div><strong>Human Resources </strong>($18-30 per hour)<br><br></div><div><strong>Marketing and Communications</strong> ($20-35 per hour)<br><br><strong>Research </strong>($23-35 per hour)<br><br><strong>IT</strong> ($25-33 per hour)<br><br></div><div>Working for University Temporary Services is an excellent way to step into higher education or expand your skillset into other fields. While all the positions are temporary in nature, some roles do have the opportunity to be posted permanently and UTS employees would follow the State policy of applying for the role if they are interested.<br><br></div><div>Here is some more information about potential “perks” of being a temporary employee with University Temporary Services:<a href=\"https://hr.ncsu.edu/benefits-and-wellness/perks/temporary-employee-perks/\"><strong>https://hr.ncsu.edu/benefits-and-wellness/perks/temporary-employee-perks/</strong></a><a href=\"https://onboarding.ncsu.edu/tempperks/\"><strong><br></strong></a><br></div><div>We look forward to working with you!<br><br></div> 480502 - UHR UTS/UNC Partnership https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233226 2026-07-28T14:35:18-04:00 2026-07-28T14:35:19-04:00 ASPSA Academic Mentor <div><strong>Division of Academic and Student Affairs</strong><br>The Division of Academic &amp; Student Affairs (DASA) is where students discover connections and community. It is where students live, where they gather, where they improve themselves, each other, and their university. DASA is where students seek guidance and support, both in and outside of the classroom. The division serves all students at NC State through three integrated components: academic affairs, student affairs, and University College.<br><br>NC State wants employees to achieve their highest potential at work, learn more about our employee value proposition here (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>You Belong Here</strong></a>). The Division of Academic and Student Affairs is the place to work for values-driven faculty and staff who are passionate about promoting the success of the whole student.<br><br><strong>The Office of Academic Support Program for Student-Athletes</strong> (ASPSA) is a comprehensive support program that strives to meet the academic, personal and professional development needs of all student-athletes, promoting excellence and effectiveness in undergraduate and graduate education as well as leadership and civic engagement.<br><br>ASPSA is committed to extending the educational experience of its constituency with particular emphasis on empowering student-athletes to become strong self-advocates. We provide specialized initiatives to facilitate a smooth transition from high school to college and from college to professional life while successfully integrating student-athletes into the campus community. We strive to enhance academic skills for all student-athletes and provide personalized academic support to meet the needs of each student-athlete.<br><br>ASPSA maintains a strong sense of integrity and will continue to strive to be one of the benchmark programs for academic support for all collegiate academic support programs in the nation.<br><br><strong>Mentor Position</strong><br>The primary role of a mentor is to meet with the assigned student-athlete to develop and improve time management skills and productive study habits.<br><br>Additional responsibilities include, but are not limited to:</div><ul><li>Assist student-athletes to set academic goals (short and long term)</li><li>Assist student-athletes with requesting Disability Services Office (DSO) accommodations, if needed</li><li>Effectively communicate in a timely manner</li><li>Participate in group training and supervision meetings</li></ul> 240201 - Academic Support Program for Student Athletes https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223242 2025-09-25T10:55:03-04:00 2025-09-25T10:55:05-04:00 Police Officer <div>The main purpose of the NC State University Police Officer position is to protect and serve the students, faculty, staff and visitors of North Carolina State University through law enforcement and crime prevention. The selected candidate(s) will patrol campus and investigate complaints and violations of State laws and local ordinances. This person also develops and implements preventative patrol techniques and performs specialized duties to deter crime and promote effective safety and security practices.<br><br></div><div>This description covers the duties and responsibilities of officers in the Police Officer band and includes Police Officer I, Police Officer II, and Police Officer III. Selection to and movement between the different levels is outlined in Police Department General Order 100-8.<br><br></div><div><strong>Police Officer I</strong>: Positions at this level are entry-level with 0-2 years of experience in law enforcement, completed North Carolina’s <a href=\"https://ncdoj.gov/law-enforcement-training/criminal-justice/training-certification-programs/basic-law-enforcement-training/\"><strong>Basic Law Enforcement Training</strong></a>, be certified as a Law Enforcement Officer in accordance with the provisions of the North Carolina Criminal Justice Education and Training Standards Commission or have the ability to achieve certification within one year of employment.<br><br></div><div><strong>Police Officer II</strong>: Positions at this level are fully functioning officers with a minimum of 2 years of experience as a sworn local, state, or federal law enforcement officer, have completed North Carolina’s Basic Law Enforcement Training, and have at least a General Certification.<br><br></div><div><strong>Police Officer III</strong>: Positions at this level are veteran officers with a minimum of 4 years of experience as a sworn local, state, or federal law enforcement officers, have completed North Carolina’s Basic Law Enforcement Training, and have attained their General Certification.<br><br><strong>What we can offer:<br></strong><br></div><ul><li><strong>FREE</strong> Tuition to officers seeking to continue their education at any University in the UNC System</li><li><strong>FREE</strong>on campus gym membership</li><li><strong>10% </strong>Night shift Differential Pay</li><li><strong>Permanent </strong>Day or Night Shifts</li><li>14 regular days off per month</li><li>Uniform and equipment provided at no cost</li><li>Training classes available for advancement/promotion</li><li>Individual or Family Health Plans</li><li>Paid Vacation and Sick Time</li><li>State Retirement and 401k Plans</li></ul> 425001 - Police Department https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234105 2026-08-24T15:00:13-04:00 2026-08-24T15:00:14-04:00 Plumber II <div>Are you an experienced plumber looking for an opportunity to work on a varied range of facilities while making a lasting impact on a major university campus?<br><br>As a <strong>Plumber II</strong>, you will perform <strong>skilled installation, repair, maintenance, and modification of complex plumbing and piping systems</strong> that support classrooms, laboratories, offices, residence halls, and other critical campus facilities. This journey-level role offers the opportunity to apply advanced plumbing and pipefitting expertise while expanding your knowledge through exposure to secondary electrical and HVAC-related systems. If you take pride in solving complex problems, delivering quality workmanship, and supporting the infrastructure that keeps a campus community running, we encourage you to apply and join our team.<br><br><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:<br></em></strong><br></div><ul><li><strong>Install, maintain, repair, and modify plumbing and piping systems, including water, waste, steam, gas, chilled water, and compressed air systems.</strong></li><li>Service domestic, lab, and acid waste drainage systems, building sewers, and associated fixtures and equipment.</li><li><strong>Perform pipefitting tasks, including cutting, threading, assembling, testing, and installing piping, valves, supports, and fixtures.</strong></li><li>Conduct scheduled <strong>preventive maintenance</strong> on plumbing and related air, gas, water, and waste disposal systems.</li><li>Diagnose, troubleshoot, and repair boilers, pumps, steam systems, compressors, water softeners, and related equipment.</li><li>Apply working knowledge of <strong>secondary electrical and HVAC-related systems</strong>, controls, and components.</li><li><strong>Ensure all work complies with Federal, State, OSHA, and Facilities Operations safety policies and procedures.</strong></li><li>Identify and address safety hazards and properly use required personal protective equipment.</li><li>Communicate clearly with supervisors, coworkers, and campus customers regarding work assignments and timelines.</li><li>Perform tasks that involve climbing ladders, accessing confined or elevated spaces, occasional lifting up to 60 lbs, and working in varied conditions, including exposure to areas with insects or small animals. All essential duties must be completed with or without reasonable accommodation.</li></ul> 445030 - Building Maintenance & Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/227567 2026-02-17T15:03:19-05:00 2026-02-17T15:03:20-05:00 Lecturer - Finance <div>The Department of Finance is accepting applications for the position of Non-Tenure Track Faculty Lecturer (part-time). Non-Tenure Track Faculty positions are one-semester or one-year in duration, with the possibility of renewal.<br><br>Successful candidates will teach 1-3 courses for the Finance concentration in the BSBA (Bachelor of Science in Business Administration) and/or for the Finance concentration in the MBA (Master of Business Administration).<br><br>Specific hiring needs for the academic year have yet to be determined but could include financial modeling, investments, international finance, personal finance, and corporate finance.</div> 206001 - Finance - PCOM https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234580 2026-09-02T20:59:39-04:00 2026-09-03T08:44:45-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>The Atcha Lab based at NC State is seeking a highly motivated postdoctoral scholar to investigate how mechanical and biochemical cues regulate cell-cell interactions during cardiovascular disease.<br><br>The lab combines mechanobiology, biomaterials, and molecular biology to uncover mechanisms of cardiac disease and identify new therapeutic targets.</div> 147501 - Lampe Joint Department of BME https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230720 2026-05-18T09:14:19-04:00 2026-05-18T09:14:20-04:00 Line Station Cook <div><strong>NC State Dining is seeking</strong> individuals to Join our Pack as a <strong>Line Station Cook</strong>. Successful candidates will receive instructions from the chef and other culinary professionals in a dining hall, food court or restaurant setting.<br><br>Essential job duties include:</div><ul><li>Prepare food as per the standardized recipes and specifications.</li><li>Perform small batch cooking and made to order items, display station set-up and service, transport cooked foods to hot boxes for line service.</li><li>Provide support to the front of house operations with back-up food to ensure that there is no lag time between changing out food items.</li><li>May work as a grill cook or a sushi cook by preparing either cook to order menu items or following standardized recipes ensuring excellent quality, appearance, and taste.</li><li>May work as a prep-cook, by following the cook's directions or according to the recipe, including the preparation of ingredients for recipes and production of food items.</li><li>Portion food products prior to cooking according to standard portion sizes and recipe specifications.</li><li>Check temperatures and proper storage of food items, and follow proper guidelines in the storage of food to prevent contamination and spoilage.</li><li>Operate all kitchen equipment safety and as recommended by manufacturer.</li><li>Perform necessary cleaning of utensils, work space, tables, shelves, and equipment in accordance with maintaining a Grade “A” Sanitation rating.</li><li>Other duties as assigned to ensure NC State Dining business needs are met. Position may be asked to assist in other locations as necessary.</li></ul><div><br>This is an hourly position, that includes a State required 32-day break at the end of 11 months. Superior work performance may provide an opportunity to renew employment and/or advance into a benefits eligible permanent position.<br><br><a href=\"https://dining.ncsu.edu/about/overview/\"><strong>NC State Dining</strong></a> provides campus wide food services to students, staff, and faculty on the North Carolina State University Campus. NC State Dining operates 10 cafes, 3 food courts and 6 dining halls, 4 markets, 1 restaurant, vending, a nutritional counseling service, and a catering department. The department employs approximately 1000 temporary and student employees and approximately 190 permanent EPS and SHRA employees.</div> 471501 - Dining Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232786 2026-07-16T09:52:59-04:00 2026-07-16T09:53:00-04:00 EFNEP Educator, Program Assistant /Associate (Youth and Adult) <div>The Extension EFNEP Program Assistant is an employee of NC Cooperative Extension, NC State University. Under the supervision of the County Extension Director (CED) with guided assistance from the Regional Nutrition Extension Associate (RNEA), the EFNEP PA delivers nutrition and related subject matter to enrolled participants. The individual in this position will recruit and enroll adults and school-aged youth participants into the program, make regular teaching visits to enrollees either in the home or in group settings, teach basic principles of nutrition and food preparation skills, submit required program records as requested, market the program within the assigned area, recruit volunteers from within the community involving them in leadership roles, collaborate with local Cooperative Extension staff and agencies, and assist with securing program resources. The EFNEP PA receives programmatic guidance from the District EFNEP Regional Nutrition Extension Associate (RNEA) with overall supervision provided by the County Extension Director (CED). The EFNEP PA and the RNEA work with the Family and Consumer Science Agent, 4-H Agent and/or CED to meet the needs of the county. Renewal of the position is contingent on the availability of funds.<br><br><br>Adult Expanded Food and Nutrition Education Program (EFNEP) involves the enrollment of limited resource individuals and their children who qualify for federal food assistance programs.<br><br>Under the programmatic guidance of the Extension Associate, the EFNEP PA works with clients in small group settings and individually (as needed).<br><br></div><div>This position is a full-time, 40 hours per week position. This position will serve 75% 4-H Youth and 25% Adult Programming.<br><br>Youth Programing will take place at school settings and at informal educational settings with 4-H and community partner organizations. Although youth participants may range from 5 to 18 years old, they are typically 2nd to 6th grade students.</div> 117624 - South Central District - Gaston https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233780 2026-08-12T15:22:15-04:00 2026-08-12T15:22:16-04:00 Research Technician <div>Support innovative research in reproductive physiology and molecular biology by providing technical expertise and laboratory support for multiple research programs. As a Research Technician, you will play an essential role in maintaining transgenic mouse colonies, conducting molecular biology experiments, and supporting faculty, students, and scientists in advancing high-quality research. This position contributes to the success of the laboratory by ensuring research activities are conducted safely, accurately, and in compliance with institutional and regulatory standards.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Research &amp; Laboratory Support</strong></div><ul><li>Perform laboratory experiments and molecular biology procedures, including genotyping assays, to support ongoing research in reproductive physiology and molecular biology.</li><li>Conduct animal-related procedures, including tail sampling and ear tagging, in accordance with established research protocols.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Animal Colony Management</strong></div><ul><li>Manage the transgenic mouse colony by coordinating breeding, weaning, tissue collection, colony maintenance, and accurate recordkeeping of pedigrees and colony health.</li><li>Ensure compliance with institutional and regulatory requirements governing the care and use of research animals.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Laboratory Operations &amp; Collaboration</strong></div><ul><li>Collaborate with faculty, graduate students, scientists, and research staff to support multiple research projects and laboratory initiatives.</li><li>Maintain laboratory organization by managing research supply inventories, supporting laboratory cleanliness, and implementing laboratory safety procedures, including the proper handling and disposal of hazardous, non-hazardous, and radioactive waste in accordance with Environmental Health and Safety (EHS) protocols.<br><br></li></ul><div>If you are<strong> </strong>a detail-oriented research professional who enjoys working in a collaborative laboratory environment and is eager to contribute to innovative scientific research, we encourage you to apply. If you thrive in a hands-on setting, are committed to laboratory safety and quality, and enjoy learning new techniques while supporting a varied research team, we'd love to have you join us.<br><br></div><div><strong>This is a time-limited position with an expected duration of up to 3 years, depending on funding availability</strong></div> 110701 - Animal Science https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232312 2026-07-01T09:23:24-04:00 2026-07-01T09:23:26-04:00 Environmental Services Technician I <div>Are you looking for a fulfilling career where you can make a tangible difference in creating a clean and welcoming environment? We are seeking a passionate and detail-oriented Environmental Services Technician to join our exceptional Environmental Services team at NC State’s Facilities Division. In this vital role, you will help maintain over 10 million square feet of campus space, ensuring a safe and inviting atmosphere for students, staff, and visitors. With the support of a dynamic team of over 320 professionals, you’ll have the opportunity to grow your skills and take pride in your work while contributing to our vibrant campus community.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key responsibilities and duties include, but not limited to:</strong></div><ul><li>Provide cleaning, sanitizing, and pest control services across 10 million square feet of campus space</li><li>Utilize standard cleaning assignments to sanitize designated areas within buildings</li><li>Report discrepancies and respond to emergencies and inclement weather</li><li>Use standard housekeeping equipment for tasks including vacuuming, mopping, scrubbing, dusting, disinfecting, and replenishing supplies</li><li>Remove recycling from centralized indoor recycling sites</li><li>Periodically strip and refinish floors, as well as shampoo and extract carpets</li><li>Perform periodic pressure washing of entrances, exterior walls, and windows</li><li>Conduct seasonalClean and sanitize assigned areas using standard housekeeping equipment and procedures.</li><li>Perform routine cleaning tasks such as vacuuming, mopping, dusting, disinfecting, and restocking supplies.</li><li>Remove recycling and waste from centralized collection sites.</li><li>Report maintenance issues, safety concerns, or discrepancies to supervisors.</li><li>Support emergency and inclement weather response, including snow and ice removal.</li><li>Periodically strip and refinish floors, shampoo and extract carpets, and pressure wash entrances, walls, and windows.</li><li>Participate in team cleaning and special projects during academic breaks or when areas are accessible.</li><li>Ensure safe, efficient, and high-quality cleaning practices across assigned spaces.</li><li>Able to grasp, push, and pull cleaning equipment; reach overhead; and perform physical cleaning tasks like bending, squatting, lifting up to 60 lbs., and climbing ladders, safely and efficiently, with or without reasonable accommodation.<br><br></li></ul><div>We encourage candidates who take pride in maintaining a clean and safe environment to apply. This role provides the opportunity to contribute to a well-functioning campus community while working in a collaborative and supportive team setting.</div> 443501 - Housekeeping Services - Region 3 https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233521 2026-08-04T16:16:33-04:00 2026-08-04T16:16:34-04:00 Market Supervisor <div>Are you a natural leader with a passion for hospitality, team building, and great food? Step up as a <strong>Temporary Market Supervisor</strong> with NC State Dining!<br><br></div><div>In this role, you’ll do more than manage day-to-day market operations—you’ll set the tone for exceptional campus service, coach a dynamic crew, and gain valuable management experience with one of North Carolina’s premier university dining programs. Whether you are looking to build your resume or take the next step toward a permanent career in hospitality, our experienced leadership team is here to support your growth every step of the way.</div><div><strong><br>What You’ll Do</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Lead &amp; Train:</strong> Supervise daily store operations, coach student and temporary staff, and foster a welcoming, high-energy environment.</li><li><strong>Deliver Hospitality:</strong> Serve as a champion for outstanding customer service, ensuring every guest leaves satisfied.</li><li><strong>Manage Operations &amp; Inventory:</strong> Oversee point-of-sale (Micros POS) transactions, maintain accurate financial paperwork, track inventory, and ensure smooth purchasing/selling workflows.</li><li><strong>Craft Quality Products:</strong> Safely prepare drinks and food to recipe standards—from pulling espresso shots and scooping ice cream to baking fresh cookies and making waffle cones.</li><li><strong>Maintain High Standards:</strong> Execute daily cleaning protocols to uphold our proud Grade \"A\" Sanitation rating.</li><li><strong>Adapt &amp; Support:</strong> Pitch in across other dining locations as needed to keep campus running seamlessly.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Why Join NC State Dining?</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Flexible Scheduling:</strong> Work-life balance built around your availability.</li><li><strong>Real Career Growth:</strong> Gain hands-on leadership experience with on-the-job training from dedicated industry professionals.</li><li><strong>A Pathway to More:</strong> Outstanding performance opens doors for contract renewal and potential advancement into benefits-eligible, permanent staff positions.</li><li><strong>Vibrant Campus Impact:</strong> Be part of a massive, energetic operation serving thousands of students, faculty, and staff daily across 25+ campus locations.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Position Details</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Type:</strong> Hourly / Temporary</li><li><strong>Location:</strong> On-Campus (NC State University)</li><li><strong>Term Limit:</strong> Per State policy, temporary roles include a mandatory 31-day break after 24 months of continuous service. Superior performance offers opportunities to re-hire or transition to permanent roles.</li></ul><div><strong><br>About NC State Dining</strong></div><div>NC State Dining is the heart of campus nourishment, operating 10 cafes, 3 food courts, 6 dining halls, 4 markets, a full-service restaurant, catering, vending, and nutritional counseling. Powered by a dedicated team of 1,000+ student and temporary staff alongside 190 permanent professionals, we are committed to service, quality, and community.</div><div><br><strong>Ready to lead on campus? Apply today and Join our Pack!</strong></div><div><br>If you have general questions about the application process, you may contact Human Resources at (919) 515-2135 or <a href=\"mailto:workatncstate@ncsu.edu\"><strong>workatncstate@ncsu.edu</strong></a>.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div> 471501 - Dining Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223312 2025-09-25T11:47:33-04:00 2025-09-25T11:47:34-04:00 Open Rank Non-Tenure Track Teaching Positions - Graphic Design, Experience Design & Industrial Design <div>The Department of Graphic Design and Industrial Design is accepting applications for part-time non-tenure-track faculty appointments for one-semester, one-year, two-year, or three-year appointments, with the possibility of renewal. Positions encompass the following, with rank to be determined based on education and professional experience: Lecturer, Senior Lecturer, Assistant Teaching Professor, Associate Teaching Professor, or Teaching Professor.</div><div><br>Specific teaching opportunities include studios at all levels, including advanced comprehensive studios and/or special topic seminar courses.</div> 120301 - Graphic & Industrial Design https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234275 2026-08-27T15:12:30-04:00 2026-08-27T15:12:31-04:00 Director of Recruiting, Enrollment Management, and Educational Partnerships <div>The <strong>Director of Recruiting, Enrollment Management and Educational Partnerships</strong> provides strategic leadership for recruitment, enrollment management, and educational partnerships within the Office of Academic Affairs in the <strong>NC State College of Engineering</strong>. This position plays a key role in shaping the college’s enrollment strategy, expanding pathways to engineering degrees, strengthening institutional partnerships, and advancing opportunities for student success.</div><div><br>The Director serves as a senior leader and primary liaison for recruitment and enrollment initiatives, working closely with university partners, academic units, educational institutions, donors, and other stakeholders to achieve enrollment goals and strengthen the College of Engineering’s position as a premier destination for engineering education.</div><div>&nbsp;<br><strong>What you will do:</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Lead recruitment and enrollment strategy</strong>, establishing priorities and initiatives that support enrollment goals and long-term growth.</li><li><strong>Lead recruitment and admissions efforts</strong>, partnering with the Office of Admissions on standards, enrollment targets, and recruitment strategies while promoting the college’s academic programs, research, facilities, and student achievements.</li><li><strong>Oversee engineering pathway programs and educational partnerships</strong>, including 2+2 Regional, 3+2 dual-degree, community college, and other strategic pathways to NC State engineering degrees.</li><li><strong>Lead and develop direct reports</strong>, setting clear priorities and ensuring activities align with university, college, and departmental policies.</li><li><strong>Manage recruitment and partnership budgets</strong>, strategically allocating resources to support programs and initiatives while maintaining sound financial practices.</li><li><strong>Champion the College of Engineering’s strengths</strong>, maintaining a strong understanding of its academic programs, research, facilities, and faculty and student achievements.</li><li><strong>Partner with the Engineering Foundation to expand scholarship support</strong>, cultivating donor relationships, increasing scholarship revenue, and developing effective award processes.</li><li><strong>Oversee incoming undergraduate scholarships</strong>, collaborating with university scholarship programs to maximize opportunities for engineering students.</li><li><strong>Partner on recruitment marketing and strategic planning</strong>, developing materials and initiatives that advance college and university enrollment goals.</li><li><strong>Support the Office of Academic Affairs and college leadership</strong> through relevant committees, initiatives, and other responsibilities as assigned.</li></ul><div><br></div><div>In this role, you will <strong>lead with vision, collaboration, and innovation</strong>, building strong relationships across the university and with external partners. You will bring a proven record of <strong>effective leadership</strong>, the ability to <strong>inspire and engage students and colleagues</strong>, and excellent written and oral communication skills. Success in this position requires <strong>sound judgment, discretion, and integrity</strong>, along with the interpersonal skills and political acumen to build effective bridges, foster collaboration, and advance shared goals.</div> 143201 - Engineering-Academic Affairs https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228482 2026-03-16T14:50:38-04:00 2026-03-16T14:50:39-04:00 Temporary Blueberry Research Internship - Castle Hayne, NC <div>This internship offers students and recent graduates meaningful, hands-on experience in<br>applied horticultural research at the NCSU Horticultural Crops Research Station in Castle<br>Hayne, NC, located near Wilmington.<br><br>Selected candidates will support an active blueberry breeding program focused on developing<br>varieties suited for mechanical harvest. Day-to-day responsibilities span greenhouse, shade<br>house, and field environments and include plant care, pollination, transplanting, pruning,<br>weeding, fertilizing, harvesting, and field data collection. Interns will also contribute to inventory<br>management and office-based tasks such as data entry and image labeling in support of an<br>ongoing machine learning project — making this a well-rounded opportunity for those with<br>interests in both plant science and data.<br><br>Attention to detail, comfort with repetitive tasks, and a willingness to work outdoors in varying<br>conditions are important for success in this role. Scheduling is flexible between 7 am and 6 pm<br>on weekdays. Hours begin at 10–20 per week from September through March, increasing to<br>20 or more per week from April through August. Candidates who can commit through the core<br>blueberry season (May–July) are especially encouraged to apply.<br>10-<br>Preference will be given to candidates pursuing degrees in horticulture, biology, or related fields,<br>and to those who demonstrate a strong commitment to learning and collaboration.</div> 111501 - Horticultural Science https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234702 2026-09-04T09:27:27-04:00 2026-09-04T09:27:28-04:00 Assistant Director of Donor Relations and Stewardship <div>Are you a compelling storyteller with a passion for donor impact? The NC State College of Veterinary Medicine is looking for a dynamic professional to elevate the stewardship program.<br><br>The<strong> Assistant Director of Donor Relations and Stewardship</strong> is responsible for developing and implementing both short- and long-term strategies to advance innovative and comprehensive donor engagement for supporters of the NC State College of Veterinary Medicine and the North Carolina Veterinary Medical Foundation.<br><br></div><div>This role partners closely with the Assistant Dean of Philanthropy and advancement leadership to design, execute, and continuously strengthen stewardship, recognition, and engagement initiatives that deepen relationships and enhance philanthropic impact.</div><div><br><strong>Core Responsibilities</strong></div><ul><li>Develop and execute strategic donor relations and stewardship plans that align with college and foundation priorities and support long-term donor retention and growth.</li><li>Collaborate with advancement leadership to craft compelling language and messaging, stewardship communications, impact reporting, and broader engagement initiatives.</li><li>Plan and execute in-person and virtual donor events both on campus and regionally, in partnership with the development team, ensuring a high-quality, mission-aligned donor experience.</li><li>Coordinate donor meetings on and off campus, supporting donor identification, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship efforts.</li><li>Manage the creation, production, and distribution of stewardship reports and donor communications that clearly articulate the impact of philanthropic support.</li><li>Serve as a key resource to the CVM philanthropy team and university stewardship office to ensure coordinated and consistent donor engagement practices.</li><li>Collaborate in broader strategic planning efforts to strengthen donor and volunteer engagement across the college.</li><li>Oversee and enhance signature recognition programs, including the Walk of Honor and Gallop of Honor, ensuring effective program management and continued relevance.</li></ul> 190101 - College Of Veterinary Medicine https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231240 2026-06-04T12:09:37-04:00 2026-06-04T12:09:38-04:00 UTS -Temporary Environmental Services at NC State (4:00 AM – 12:30 PM) <div>Thank you for applying with University Temporary Services! Please note, this application is the first step in the process of being considered for Environmental Services roles at NC State. This is not a specific position but rather, an application showing your skillset and interest in becoming a candidate for consideration with UTS.<br><br>Main responsibilities:<br>• Performing routine manual housekeeping work while cleaning various areas across campus.<br>• Cleaning, mopping, scrubbing, sweeping, and vacuuming floors, as well as stairwells and hallways.<br>• Cleaning of lavatories, washing windows, moving furniture to thoroughly clean areas, and collecting and removing trash.<br><br>This is an ongoing recruitment.</div> 480501 - UHR Temporary Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233043 2026-07-22T14:20:42-04:00 2026-07-22T14:20:43-04:00 Assistant Professor <div>North Carolina State University (NC State) is seeking a highly motivated and innovative individual for a tenure-track position at the level of Assistant Professor as part of the FRIB Experimental Bridge program. This unique position provides an exceptional opportunity for a dynamic researcher to thrive in a collaborative environment at the forefront of nuclear science.</div><div><br>In this role, your professional efforts will be split between the Facility for Rare Isotope Beams (FRIB) at Michigan State University (MSU) and the local facilities at NC State and the Triangle Universities Nuclear Laboratory (TUNL), located in Durham, North Carolina. This dual appointment offers a distinctive platform for cutting-edge research, leveraging the capabilities and resources of both renowned institutions.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Conduct cutting-edge research at FRIB, using its state-of-the-art facilities to advance the frontiers of rare isotope science.</li><li>Lead an independent research program and engage in collaborative research at TUNL, where unique capabilities such as high-intensity particle beams, polarized photon beams, neutron beams, and advanced detection systems support a broad range of nuclear physics investigations.</li><li>Develop a robust, externally funded research program that integrates work at both FRIB and NC State/TUNL.</li><li>Teach undergraduate and graduate courses at NC State, demonstrating a commitment to excellence in education and mentorship.</li></ul><div>If you thrive in collaborative research environments and are excited by the opportunity to work alongside leading researchers while building your own research program, we invite you to apply.</div> 170601 - Physics and Astronomy https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233981 2026-08-19T11:47:54-04:00 2026-08-19T11:47:55-04:00 Environmental Services Technician I - 40324 <div>Are you looking for a fulfilling career where you can make a tangible difference in creating a clean and welcoming environment? We are seeking a passionate and detail-oriented <strong>Environmental Services Technician</strong> to join our exceptional <strong>Environmental Services team at NC State’s Facilities Division</strong>. In this vital role, you will help maintain over 10 million square feet of campus space, ensuring a safe and inviting atmosphere for students, staff, and visitors. With the support of a dynamic team of over 320 professionals, you’ll have the opportunity to grow your skills and take pride in your work while contributing to our vibrant campus community. Join us and be a part of a team that takes pride in delivering exceptional service to one of the most prestigious campuses in the region. <strong>Apply today and help us keep NC State shining bright!</strong><br><br></div><div><strong>Working Hours: 8:30 am - 5:00 pm, Monday – Friday</strong></div><div><br></div><div><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li><strong>Clean and sanitize</strong> designated areas using standard cleaning assignments to maintain a safe and hygienic environment</li><li><strong>Report any discrepancies, damages, or maintenance needs</strong> within buildings and promptly respond to emergencies and inclement weather</li><li><strong>Operate housekeeping equipment</strong> such as vacuums, mops, and scrubbers to complete tasks like dusting, disinfecting, and replenishing supplies</li><li><strong>Periodically strip and refinish floors, shampoo and extract carpets, and perform pressure washing of entrances, exterior walls, and windows</strong></li><li><strong>Remove and manage</strong> recycling from centralized indoor recycling sites</li><li>Assist with window washing and other seasonal cleaning tasks as needed</li><li>Communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, and the general public, and follow verbal and written instructions related to work assignments and operational needs.</li><li>Perform lifting duties up to 30 pounds frequently and 60 pounds occasionally, with or without reasonable accommodations, and perform physical tasks such as grasping, pushing, and pulling cleaning equipment; reaching overhead; bending, squatting, climbing, and maintaining balance.</li></ul> 445080 - Environmental Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/226684 2026-01-21T12:30:40-05:00 2026-01-21T12:30:41-05:00 Full Stack Developer (Machado) <div>A Full Stack Computer science developer needed to contribute to the development and expansion of RABapp.&nbsp; Developing and implementing frontend and backend software applications. Candidate will be able to contribute with new RABapp tools.</div> 196001 - Department of Population Health and Pathobiology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234489 2026-09-01T15:32:35-04:00 2026-09-01T15:32:36-04:00 Lenovo Esports and Gaming Lab Graduate Assistant <div>The Lenovo Esports and Gaming Lab Assistant will join the team in the Lenovo Esports and Gamin Lab dedicated to fostering a vibrant gaming and esports culture at our university. The lab is a dynamic environment designed to support self-guided learning, community engagement, and competitive gaming. This position supports the Gaming and Esports Program , with a focus on events, live streaming, and space management.<br><br>Responsibilities include:<br>• Support community engagement strategies to introduce users to gaming and esports.<br>• Support collaborate with various colleges within NC State to support classes being instructed in the gaming and esports lab<br>• Staff the Gaming and Esports Lab during Saturday evenings.<br>• Help administer outreach events to increase campus awareness of the esports program.<br>• Collaborate with other staff members to enhance the lab’s offerings and improve user experience.<br>• Bring their own interests and expertise to contribute to the lab’s growth.<br><br>The Gaming and Esports Lab Assistant will report to the Esports Program Director</div> 250101 - Libraries https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230114 2026-05-01T00:00:43-04:00 2026-05-01T00:00:49-04:00 Assistant or Associate Professor <div>NCSU College of Design's Department of Media Arts, Design and Technology (MADTech) seeks a full-time Assistant or Associate Professor of Media Arts, Design and Technology with a focus on Visual Effects for Interactive Media and Virtual Production (Games Design and Development, Virtual and Mixed Reality, 2D and 3D animation, kinetic and immersive installations). With a state-of-the-art Virtual Production Lab running on the Unreal Engine, the successful candidate will be well positioned to develop opportunities for lab funding and engagement locally, nationally and internationally.&nbsp;<br><br>The candidate will engage in research in the use of technology across multiple platforms and have the ability to teach new and emerging concepts and ideas in Media Arts, Design and Technology at the graduate and undergraduate level. We are particularly interested in candidates who can balance visual and aesthetic production with technological and conceptual exploration. Successful candidates may also by asked to teach at the foundation level instructing students on techniques of basic drawing, 2D and 3D design, digital illustration, animation and sculpture. Candidates may also bring other areas of expertise to these classes. Additional topics of interest might include sound design, interaction design, computation design, and/or blended media experiences.<br><br>This position is a full-time tenure-track faculty 9-month appointment. Rank to be determined based upon education and professional experience. Specific teaching opportunities include art studios at all levels, including advanced comprehensive studios and/or special topic seminar courses.</div> 120501 - Art and Design https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233786 2026-08-13T08:10:59-04:00 2026-08-13T08:10:59-04:00 Associate Vice Chancellor, Research Administration <div>The Associate Vice Chancellor, Research Administration&nbsp; (AVC-RA) serves as the university's senior executive leader for sponsored programs and institutional research administration, providing strategic oversight of enterprise-wide pre-award services, sponsored project administration, research contracts, research administration systems, and research administration data governance. Reporting directly to the Vice Chancellor for Research and Innovation, the AVC leads a high-performing leadership team that includes the Director of Institutional Research Systems &amp; Reporting, the Director of Research Administration &amp; Campus Liaison, and additional OSP directors. Working collaboratively with university leadership, colleges, research centers, faculty, federal sponsors, and administrative partners, the AVC ensures a coordinated, compliant, and service-oriented research administration enterprise that advances NC State's research mission while strengthening institutional stewardship and operational excellence.<br><br></div><div>As the AVC RA, you will shape the future of research administration at one of the nation's leading public research universities. Your leadership will influence how investigators secure external funding, how research operations evolve to meet an increasingly complex regulatory environment, and how institutional systems, data, and policies support innovation at scale. Success in this position comes from balancing strategic vision with collaborative leadership—building trusted partnerships across the university, championing continuous improvement, and fostering an environment where faculty and research administrators have the resources, guidance, and infrastructure needed to expand NC State's research impact.<br><br></div><div>Key Responsibilities fall within the following functional areas.</div><div><strong><br>Strategic Leadership &amp; Research Administration:</strong></div><ul><li>Provide strategic leadership for the university's sponsored programs enterprise, aligning research administration priorities with institutional goals and the evolving external funding landscape.</li><li>Advise the Vice Chancellor for Research and Innovation and university leadership on research administration strategy, sponsored programs, regulatory compliance, institutional risk, and operational effectiveness.</li><li>Lead the development and implementation of university-wide policies, governance, and business practices that strengthen research administration and support faculty success.</li><li>Foster a culture of collaboration, service excellence, continuous improvement, and accountability across the Office of Sponsored Programs and the broader research community.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Enterprise Oversight &amp; Risk Management:</strong></div><ul><li>Provide executive oversight of institutional pre-award operations, research agreements, and sponsored programs administration to ensure consistent, compliant, and customer-focused service delivery.</li><li>Safeguard the university's legal, financial, and operational interests through effective risk management, contract oversight, and compliance with federal, state, and sponsor requirements.</li><li>Lead the university's response to significant regulatory changes, audits, investigations, and other matters impacting the research enterprise.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Research Systems, Data &amp; Operational Excellence:</strong></div><ul><li>Provide executive sponsorship for research administration systems, institutional data governance, and modernization initiatives that enhance operational effectiveness and decision-making.</li><li>Champion technology, process improvement, and analytics strategies that strengthen research administration services and support institutional growth.</li><li>Ensure research administration infrastructure evolves to meet the changing needs of faculty, sponsors, and the university.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>University &amp; External Engagement:</strong></div><ul><li>Build trusted partnerships with university leadership, faculty, colleges, and research administrators to advance the institution's research mission.</li><li>Represent NC State in relationships with federal agencies, industry, nonprofit organizations, and national research administration associations.</li><li>Serve as the university's senior institutional representative for complex sponsor negotiations, strategic collaborations, and matters requiring executive-level consultation.<br><br></li></ul><div>If you are a collaborative and strategic leader who is passionate about advancing research, building strong institutional partnerships, and shaping the future of research administration, we encourage you to apply.</div> 060401 - ORI-Ofc of Sponsored Programs https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/224717 2025-11-05T12:29:53-05:00 2025-11-05T12:29:54-05:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>We are seeking a researcher to participate in an initiative in human-centered AI for public microtransit systems. The successful candidate will design and implement AI-driven strategies that enhance system performance by promoting prosocial rider behaviors—such as flexible departure timing to benefit vulnerable users—and by personalizing rider interactions to sustain long-term engagement. This role involves modeling individual travel preferences and adaptive time windows, analyzing the effectiveness of message themes (e.g., pro-sociality vs. system efficiency), and deploying tailored persuasive techniques optimized for varied user profiles. <br><br><strong>Responsibilities will include:</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Interdisciplinary collaboration:</strong> Work closely with faculty from computer science, transportation engineering, operations research, and psychology on integrative research related to microtransit systems.</li><li><strong>AI algorithms and engineering:</strong> Design and implement advanced methods to learn rider preferences and effective persuasive approaches to promote cooperation. Build robust pipelines for simulation and integration with a user interface and other app components.</li><li><strong>User studies:</strong> Develop an experimental platform and run user studies to test developed algorithms and analyze impacts.</li><li><strong>Conference presentations and journal publications</strong>: Lead or contribute to conference and journal papers for top venues.</li></ul> 140301 - Civil Construction & Environmental Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234389 2026-08-31T11:07:21-04:00 2026-08-31T11:07:22-04:00 Grounds Technician - Lonnie Poole Golf Course <div>Working at Lonnie Poole Golf Course means more than just cutting grass—it means maintaining the home turf of the NC State Wolfpack. We are looking for reliable and hardworking individuals to join our Grounds Crew team. We take pride in our facility, and this position is vital in assisting with daily golf course maintenance to ensure high-quality playing conditions and pristine overall course appearance.</div><div><br>There are currently two positions available.</div><div><strong><br>Duties and Responsibilities include but not limited to</strong></div><ul><li>Assist in all areas of golf course groundskeeping</li><li>Perform landscaping, sodding, and general course upkeep</li><li>Apply fertilizers and pesticides on and around the course</li><li>Mow and maintain fairways, roughs, fringes, and greens</li><li>Conduct ornamental and turf pest control</li><li>Maintain and repair sprinkler and irrigation systems</li><li>Assist with fencing installation and maintenance</li><li>Perform tree trimming and debris removal</li><li>Operate and maintain golf course maintenance equipment</li><li>Complete daily assignments independently and according to work schedules</li></ul><div><strong>Work Environment</strong></div><ul><li>Outdoor work environment with exposure to heat, cold, rain, and other weather conditions</li><li>Perform strenuous physical labor for long periods of time, walking on uneven surfaces, bending, crouching, or kneeling, pushing/pulling of levers on equipment and machinery.</li><li>Lifting up to 70 lbs. frequently, with or without reasonable accommodation.</li></ul><div><br></div><div>If you enjoy working outdoors and take pride in seeing the immediate results of your hard work, we encourage you to apply.</div> 470301 - Golf Course https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/229041 2026-04-01T12:54:42-04:00 2026-04-01T12:54:44-04:00 General Shops Member <div>The primary purpose of the General Shops Member position is to perform a variety of semi-skilled and skilled manual tasks in the areas of HVAC maintenance, carpentry, plumbing, and electrical work. The position also provides general labor support to the Facilities Unit within University Housing.<br><br><strong>Tasks include:</strong></div><ul><li>Performing preventative maintenance tasks throughout the residence halls.</li><li>Assisting residential areas with pick up and deliveries.</li><li>Properly disposing of unwanted materials as needed.</li><li>Assisting residential areas with maintenance projects.</li><li>Maintaining condition of department vehicles, tools, supplies, and shop environment.</li><li>Assisting with retrieval and closing of work orders.</li><li>Lifting up to 60 pounds frequently and up to 90 pounds occasionally, with or without reasonable accommodations.</li></ul> 242290 - Housing Facilities Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231807 2026-06-17T13:22:20-04:00 2026-06-17T13:22:21-04:00 Research Assistant <div>The Research Assistant will support a multidisciplinary research program focused on the development of CRISPR-based genetic control technologies for the New World screwworm and related blowfly species. This includes work on gene drive systems and precision-guided sterile insect techniques (pgSIT). The position integrates laboratory operations, insect colony management, biosafety compliance, and hands-on experimental support for genetic and molecular studies.<br><br><strong>Duties Include:</strong></div><ul><li>Maintain fly stocks and provide staff with biological material at different stages of development when requested.</li><li>Support postdoctoral fellows by performing blow fly crosses, examining the progeny and setting additional crosses with selected offspring.</li><li>Purchase supplies.</li><li>Autoclaving insect waste.</li><li>Supervise undergraduate students who support insect rearing.</li></ul> 111801 - Entomology and Plant Pathology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233290 2026-07-29T15:26:08-04:00 2026-07-29T15:26:09-04:00 Equine and Farm Animal Attendant <div>This temporary position will be responsible for maintaining a clean and sanitary hospital through routine stall/cage maintenance, hospital cleaning and disinfection, and waste removal. Restrain and provide basic husbandry for equine and farm animal patients. Small animal hospital backup, same duties.</div> 192001 - CVM Teaching Hospital https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223244 2025-09-25T10:56:22-04:00 2025-09-25T10:56:23-04:00 Master Police Officer I - III <div>The main purpose of the NC State University Master Officer position is to protect and serve the students, faculty, staff and visitors of North Carolina State University through law enforcement and crime prevention. The selected candidate(s) will patrol campus and investigate complaints and violations of State laws and local ordinances. This person also develops and implements preventative patrol techniques and performs specialized duties to deter crime and promote effective safety and security practices.<br><br></div><ul><li><strong>Master Officer I</strong>: Positions at this level are fully functioning officers with a minimum of 6 years of experience as a sworn local, state, or federal law enforcement officer, have attained their Intermediate Certification, and have their high school diploma or GED.</li></ul><div><br></div><ul><li><strong>Master Officer II</strong>: Positions at this level are fully functioning officers with a minimum of 3 years of experience as a Master Officer (9 years total in law enforcement) have attained their Intermediate Certification, and have their high school diploma or GED.</li></ul><div><br></div><ul><li><strong>Master Officer III</strong>: Positions at this level are fully functioning officers with a minimum of 6 years of experience as a Master Officer (12 years total in law enforcement), have attained their Intermediate Certification, and have their high school diploma or GED.</li></ul> 425001 - Police Department https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234113 2026-08-24T17:02:13-04:00 2026-08-24T17:02:14-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>The Department of Crop and Soil Sciences encompasses varied disciplines in research, extension, and teaching. The department’s mission is to develop future leaders in crop, soil, and environmental sciences, improve crop plants and products, devise effective and sustainable crop production systems, devise effective and sustainable soil management techniques in both agriculture and urban settings; and disseminate crop, soil, and environmental science knowledge for the benefit of users and producers of food, feed turf, biofuels and fiber in North Carolina, the nation and the world.<br><br>The successful candidate will conduct independent and collaborative research to develop and elucidate bio-physical-chemical mechanisms that regulate soil carbon sequestration, nitrogen retention, and greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions mitigation in agricultural systems. The primary focus will be on understanding how soil amendments derived from bioenergy production byproducts influence soil processes and ecosystem sustainability.<br><br><strong>Key responsibilities include:</strong></div><ul><li>Collect soil samples and set up microcosm experiments.</li><li>Measure soil fluxes of carbon dioxide and nitrous oxide.</li><li>Conduct metagenomic DNA extraction and shotgun sequencing.</li><li>Perform bioinformatics and statistical data analyses.</li><li>Prepare manuscripts and present research findings in professional meetings.</li><li>Work closely with team members and help train graduate students.</li></ul><div><br></div> 110901 - Crop and Soil Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/227722 2026-02-23T14:18:39-05:00 2026-02-23T14:18:40-05:00 Lecturer <div>The <strong>Department of Communication </strong>at North Carolina State University (NC State) invites applications for part-time <strong>Lecturer </strong>positions. Multiple hires may be made from this posting, with most positions being part-time and requiring in-person instruction. The position(s) are one-semester or one-year, non-tenure-track appointments with the possibility of renewal.<br><br>Primary responsibilities include teaching undergraduate courses in communication as well as coordinating, planning, preparing, delivering, and evaluating classroom instruction and related activities. Other duties may be negotiated with the department head.</div> 160701 - Communication https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234603 2026-09-03T10:13:43-04:00 2026-09-03T10:13:44-04:00 Director of Philanthropic Strategy & Prospect Management <div>The Director of Philanthropic Strategy &amp; Prospect Management serves as the College of Engineering’s strategic advisor on fundraising performance, portfolio optimization, campaign readiness, and prospect strategy. This position partners closely with the Assistant Dean for Philanthropy and frontline fundraisers to maximize philanthropic results through data-informed decision making, fundraising analytics, CRM leadership, and strategic portfolio management.<br><br>Rather than serving solely as a prospect manager, this role functions as a strategic partner to fundraising leadership by helping ensure gift officers are focused on the highest-impact opportunities and equipped with the information, systems, and strategy necessary to maximize fundraising success.<br><br><strong>Core Responsibilities</strong></div><div><br></div><div><strong>1. Philanthropic Strategy</strong></div><div><br></div><div><strong>Provide strategic guidance that strengthens fundraising performance across the College.</strong></div><div><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities</strong></div><div><br></div><ul><li>Serve as strategic advisor to the Assistant Dean and Executive Director on fundraising priorities and campaign readiness.</li><li>Lead portfolio optimization and prospect assignment strategies.</li><li>Facilitate prospect strategy meetings with frontline fundraisers.</li><li>Identify opportunities, risks, and trends within all levels of the major gift fundraising pipeline – focusing on the continuum from major gift discovery to major gifts to principal giving.</li><li>Develop recommendations to maximize fundraising productivity and philanthropic outcomes.</li><li>Support campaign planning and principal gift pipeline development.</li></ul><div><br><br></div><div><strong>2. Prospect Management</strong></div><div><br></div><div><strong>Lead the strategic management of prospect portfolios to maximize fundraiser effectiveness.</strong></div><div><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities</strong></div><div><br></div><ul><li>Oversee portfolio management and assignment processes.</li><li>Monitor prospect movement through the donor pipeline.</li><li>Ensure portfolio health and accountability.</li><li>Conduct regular portfolio reviews with gift officers.</li><li>Coordinate closely with University Advancement Prospect Development on prospect management and research, working closely with analysts on research projects to fill fundraiser portfolios &amp; gaps.</li><li>Develop recommendations for prospect assignment and portfolio balancing.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><strong>3. CRM Strategy &amp; Salesforce (Lobo) Leadership</strong></div><div><br></div><div><strong>Serve as the College’s functional expert for Salesforce (Lobo), ensuring the CRM effectively supports fundraising strategy and frontline success.</strong></div><div><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities</strong></div><div><br></div><ul><li>Serve as the College of Engineering’s primary functional lead for Salesforce (Lobo).</li><li>Partner with University Advancement on CRM implementation, enhancements, testing, and user adoption.</li><li>Develop best practices, workflows, and business processes within Salesforce.</li><li>Train and support frontline fundraisers in the effective use of the CRM.</li><li>Ensure data quality and consistency across fundraising operations.</li><li>Translate fundraising needs into CRM solutions that improve efficiency and effectiveness.</li><li>Identify opportunities to leverage Salesforce to improve fundraising performance and reporting.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><strong>4. Fundraising Analytics &amp; Performance</strong></div><div><br></div><div><strong>Provide meaningful data and analysis that informs leadership decisions and drives fundraising success.</strong></div><div><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities</strong></div><div><br></div><ul><li>Develop executive dashboards and performance reports in Salesforce that track activity, strategy and pipeline health.</li><li>Monitor fundraising KPIs and portfolio metrics.</li><li>Analyze pipeline health and fundraising trends.</li><li>Forecast fundraising performance and campaign progress.</li><li>Track proposal activity and donor movement.</li><li>Produce regular reports that support strategic decision making by the Assistant Dean and fundraising leadership.</li></ul> 143801 - Engineering Advancement https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230760 2026-05-18T15:46:08-04:00 2026-05-18T15:46:09-04:00 Temporary Research Associate <div>The selected candidate will conduct research focused on the development of novel gene drive systems for suppression of the New World screwworm. Responsibilities include designing, planning, conducting, and interpreting molecular genetics experiments in collaboration with Dr. Scott.</div><div>The individual will work closely with members of the screwworm gene drive research team, including Dr. Yadav and Ms. Belikoff, to support project objectives and meet established monthly milestones. Additional responsibilities include maintaining accurate laboratory records and ensuring compliance with all laboratory safety and containment protocols related to gene drive insects.</div> 111801 - Entomology and Plant Pathology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232799 2026-07-16T11:21:36-04:00 2026-07-16T11:27:22-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar- Theriot <div>The mission of the Department of Population Health and Pathobiology is to recruit, train, inspire, and graduate Doctors of Veterinary Medicine of exemplary knowledge, skill, and character. In order to maintain the intellectual and professional climate necessary to accomplish this mission, the department’s faculty will advance veterinary medical science through innovative basic and applied research, inspired mentoring of students (undergraduate, professional, residents, and graduate), excellence in clinical and diagnostic services, and innovative extension and engagement activities.<br><br>North Carolina State University’s College of Veterinary Medicine (CVM) is a dynamic community whose members are dedicated to preparing veterinarians and veterinarian scientists while advancing animal and human health from the cellular level through entire ecosystems. The College has gained international recognition on the strength of its teaching, research, engagement, and patient care efforts and is ranked 3rd among the nation’s 28 colleges of veterinary medicine in the current U.S. News &amp; World Report survey.<br><br>Located on 180 acres near downtown Raleigh, the College encompasses 20 buildings on the main Centennial Biomedical Campus. Instruction is in three departments—Clinical Sciences, Molecular Biomedical Sciences, and Population Health &amp; Pathobiology. In addition to the four-year Doctor of Veterinary Medicine degree, there are programs leading to master and doctoral degrees in several areas with numerous opportunities for specialization.<br><br>A postdoctoral scientist position (1+ years) is available in Prof. Casey Theriot’s group at North Carolina State University College of Veterinary Medicine (NCSU CVM). The successful applicant will characterize bacteria for their ability to alter bile acids, and compete against <em>C. difficile</em> for the same nutrients. The scholar will also bioengineer bacteria to manipulate bile acid and amino acid metabolism in the gastrointestinal tract. The Theriot laboratory (https://theriotlab.org) studies how the gut microbiota is able to provide colonization resistance against enteric pathogens, specifically <em>C. difficile</em>. They seek to define the mechanisms that provide protection by looking at the dynamics between the gut microbiota, metabolome, and host using in vitro, ex vivo, and in vivo models. This will aid in the design of targeted therapeutics against <em>C. difficile</em>, but more importantly the design of precision probiotics to manipulate the gut microbiota.&nbsp;<br><br>Requirements: Applicants are expected to have obtained a PhD in a field related to bioinformatics, microbiology, biochemistry, to have strong computational and communication skills, and to be interested in collaborative research. Applicants with a background in bacterial engineering, anaerobic bacterial physiology, and/or microbiology of infectious diseases are highly encouraged to apply. Experience in bioinformatics and characterizing bacteria for biotechnological applications and knowledge in gut microbiome analysis and other omics is desirable.<br><br>Research efforts will contribute toward publications, presentations and other funding opportunities. The selected individual will be expected to have effective communication skills, both with the PI and students within the laboratory, and will be expected to work well independently and within a team.</div> 196001 - Department of Population Health and Pathobiology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234506 2026-09-02T08:49:10-04:00 2026-09-02T08:49:10-04:00 Large Animal - Client Service Representative <div>Client Services Representative needed to greet and check in clients, manage in-person and phone inquiries, respond accurately and empathetically to complex questions, and clearly explain hospital policy, estimate costs, and deposit requirements. Update medical records by scanning in appropriate documentation. Collect and post all forms of payments.</div> 192001 - CVM Teaching Hospital https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230279 2026-05-06T09:24:33-04:00 2026-05-06T09:24:33-04:00 Building Manager <div><a href=\"https://studentcenters.ncsu.edu/\"><strong>NC State Student Centers</strong></a> create memorable experiences for students, guests, and alumni by providing a clean, safe, and welcoming environment. We support student growth and involvement through exceptional service and facilities in alignment with the university’s mission.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Open/Close Talley Student Union and Witherspoon Student Center each day, ensuring health and safety protocols are met.</li><li>Maintain facility cleanliness, safety, and overall appearance.</li><li>Manage daily building operations and coordinate with event operations for a seamless experience.</li><li>Lead a team of 2-6 for common area setups, rearrangement, removal or replacement.</li><li>Communicate event and facility updates to relevant staff.</li><li>Inspect common areas and assist with suite issues as needed.</li><li>Complete maintenance reports, submit, and follow up on work orders.</li><li>Engage with guests and clients to provide information about NC State and the Student Centers.</li><li>Provide coverage at the information desk and loading dock as needed.</li><li>Serve as the primary initial point of contact for non-routine issues.</li><li>Respond to incidents, injuries, and crises.</li><li>Complete detailed shift reports.</li><li>Work special events and perform other duties as assigned.</li><li>Attend and actively participate in mandatory staff meetings and trainings.</li><li>Adhere to all NC State Student Centers policies and expectations.</li><li>Provide exceptional customer service at all times.</li></ul><div><br>Campus Enterprises is NC State University’s division of retail and hospitality organizations — NC State Dining, NC State Stores, Lonnie Poole Golf Course, NC State Student Centers (Witherspoon and Talley), and the One Card Office. Each department is dedicated to providing service excellence, value, and enrichment of the student experience in support of academic success.<br><br>The Division of Campus Enterprises offers employment opportunities across our retail and hospitality units. We offer flexible schedules and opportunities for learning, growth, and promotion.</div> 477001 - Student Centers Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232462 2026-07-06T11:35:37-04:00 2026-07-06T11:35:39-04:00 Advanced Utilities Plant Operator - Dayshift <div>Are you an experienced <strong>Utility Plant Operator</strong> looking for a meaningful career where your work has a direct impact every day?<br><br> Join our team and play a critical role in powering one of the nation's leading research universities. As a <strong>Utility Plant Operator</strong>, you will operate, monitor, and maintain complex utility systems that provide reliable electricity, heating, and cooling to classrooms, laboratories, residence halls, athletic facilities, and research spaces across campus.<br><br></div><div><strong>In this role, you will work with advanced equipment and systems, including the state-of-the-art Cates Cogeneration Facility and large-scale chilled water production and distribution systems. Your expertise will help ensure the safe, efficient, and continuous operation of the infrastructure that supports thousands of students, faculty, staff, and researchers.</strong></div><div><br>This is an excellent opportunity for individuals who enjoy hands-on technical work, problem-solving, and operating sophisticated mechanical and electrical systems in a dynamic environment. If you are looking for a stable career with opportunities to learn, grow, and contribute to a mission-driven organization, we encourage you to apply today and become part of the team that keeps our campus running.</div><div><br><strong>Working Hours: 12 hr. shift, 3-4 days a week, 6:00 AM to 6:00 PM</strong> <br><strong><br></strong><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li><strong>Control, monitor, and operate</strong> steam boilers, chillers, cogeneration equipment, and auxiliary systems to provide continuous utility services.</li><li>Maintain safe and efficient operation of steam, hot water, and chilled water production systems.</li><li>Perform real‑time monitoring of plant systems, make operational adjustments, and ensure compliance with emissions, safety, and regulatory requirements.</li><li><strong>Inspect, operate, and perform routine maintenance</strong> on plant equipment, including fuel gas compressors, pumps, air filters, cooling towers, and related systems.</li><li><strong>Support the operation of the Cates Cogeneration Facility to produce electricity and steam for campus use.</strong></li><li>Identify and respond to equipment or system issues, escalating concerns to the shift supervisor as appropriate.</li><li>Lift and transport materials weighing up to 60 pounds with or without reasonable accommodations and perform a wide range of physically demanding tasks in varied outdoor environments, ensuring safe handling practices and maintaining productivity in varying weather and terrain conditions.</li><li>Communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, and the general public, and follow verbal and written instructions related to work assignments and operational needs.</li></ul> 445050 - Utilities and Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233526 2026-08-04T17:15:26-04:00 2026-08-04T17:15:27-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>The Department of Crop and Soil Sciences encompasses varied disciplines in research, extension, and teaching. The department’s mission is to develop future leaders in crop, soil and environmental sciences, improve crop plants and products, devise effective and sustainable crop production systems, devise effective and sustainable soil management techniques in both agriculture and urban settings; and disseminate crop, soil, and environmental science, knowledge for the benefit of producers of food, feed, turf, biofuels and fiber in North Carolina.<br><br><strong><br>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>The primary responsibilities of this position are to conduct, perform, and execute cotton genetics and breeding research focused on genomic selection (GS), nested association mapping (NAM), and genome-wide association studies (GWAS) for important agronomic traits in tetraploid cotton.</li><li>The candidate is expected to perform cotton genetics and breeding research in the field, greenhouse, and lab-based settings; develop and evaluate genetic, genomic resources, and analysis of genotypic data (GBS, resequencing, array-based and targeted genotyping panel SNPs) and phenotypic data, and use a varied array of statistical, genetics, and bioinformatics tools and methods.</li><li>This position requires the candidate to summarize results, present them as technical reports and presentations at the meetings, and communicate through peer-reviewed publications.</li><li>The candidate will have the opportunity to participate and cooperatively work with other lab members in the ongoing trait mapping and breeding projects towards dissecting the genetic architecture of agronomic traits and improving cotton productivity through advanced breeding tools.</li><li>Further, the candidate is expected to mentor and supervise the students in the cotton program.</li></ul><div><br><strong>This position is initially for one year but can be extended based on satisfactory research progress and availability of funds.</strong></div> 110901 - Crop and Soil Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223318 2025-09-25T11:55:47-04:00 2025-09-25T11:55:48-04:00 Open Rank Non Tenure Track in School of Architecture <div>The School of Architecture is accepting applications for part-time non-tenure-track faculty appointments for a one-semester or one-year appointment with the possibility of renewal. Rank to be determined based on education and professional experience.</div><div><br>Specific teaching opportunities include architecture studios at all levels, including advanced comprehensive studios and/or special topic seminar courses.</div><div><br>Applications will be reviewed as these part-time non-tenure track teaching opportunities arise.</div> 120201 - Architecture https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234331 2026-08-28T15:18:54-04:00 2026-08-28T15:18:54-04:00 Temporary-Technical/Paraprofessional <div>The Research Operations Coordinator will provide high level operational, administrative, and organizational support to ensure smooth day to day functioning of a growing lab focused on ribosome assembly, DNA mismatch repair, viral RNA processing, and advanced single molecule fluorescence microscopy. The successful candidate will coordinate research and associated outreach activities. The successful candidate will support the day-to-day operations of the lab, including but not limited to coordination and management of the following: lab duties, standard operating procedures, orders, lab safety, data storage and backup, web and social media, team building, lab inventories, lab budget, and a summer research program for high school students.<br><br>The Physics and Astronomy Department provides: (1) High quality Physics instruction to the University community through courses in introductory physics; (2) educational opportunities for undergraduate and graduate students in physics through high quality curricula, faculty, and research facilities; (3) outreach programs that advance scientific knowledge. Our research programs cover a varied spectrum including astrophysics, general relativity, condensed matter theory and experiment, computational physics, biophysics, nanoscale science, physics education, experimental nuclear physics, particle and nuclear theory, experimental optics, and experimental atomic and molecular physics. The department enrolls approximately 120 graduate students and 140 undergrad physics majors. The department size is approximately 57 faculty members. While working in this department, you will be expected to foster a supportive and welcoming environment.<br><br>NC State promotes an integrated approach to problem solving that transforms lives and provides leadership for social, economic, and technological development across North Carolina and around the world. NC State’s land grant mission of teaching, research and service is dedicated to the service of North Carolina and its people. Applicants are encouraged to review the institution’s mission, vision and strategic plan, and consider how their background, interest and experience would enable them to support the university.</div> 170601 - Physics and Astronomy https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234305 2026-08-28T09:31:47-04:00 2026-08-28T09:31:48-04:00 Operations Manager <div>As the Operations Manager, you will connect SPEED’s scientific mission with its day-to-day operations by coordinating facility access, staffing, scheduling, safety, equipment readiness, procurement, and user support. SPEED will serve researchers from universities, national laboratories, government agencies, and industry, and your leadership will help establish the facility as a dependable, accessible, and professionally managed research resource.<br><br></div><div>You will also help shape the operational foundation of a growing national research resource. SPEED will serve researchers from universities, national laboratories, government agencies, and industry, and you will play a key role in establishing SPEED as a dependable, accessible, and professionally managed facility. Through thoughtful coordination, responsive support, and effective operational leadership, you will help create an environment where researchers can focus on advancing their work through remote autonomous experimentation.<br><br></div><div><strong>List of Primary Responsibilities<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Facility &amp; User Operations<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Lead daily facility operations, including laboratory readiness, platform availability, staffing coverage, access, scheduling, and user support.</li><li>Develop and maintain operational procedures for user onboarding, experiment submission, training, platform access, technical assistance, and project closeout.</li><li>Coordinate laboratory capacity and schedules across internal researchers, academic users, government collaborators, and industry partners.</li><li>Monitor facility utilization, experiment throughput, turnaround times, incidents, and user needs to identify and resolve operational issues.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>People &amp; Resource Management<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Lead and support technical and operational personnel, including establishing priorities, schedules, performance expectations, training, and cross-coverage for critical facility functions.</li><li>Manage operational budgets, purchasing, inventory, service contracts, equipment needs, and vendor relationships in coordination with university partners.</li><li>Maintain operational records and monitor staffing, resource needs, project milestones, and facility deliverables.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong><br>Safety, Compliance &amp; Risk Management<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Lead facility-wide safety and risk management activities, including hazard assessments, emergency procedures, incident response, inspections, training, and business-continuity planning.</li><li>Coordinate with Environmental Health and Safety, information technology, research administration, facilities, and other university partners to support applicable safety, compliance, cybersecurity, and research requirements.</li><li>Investigate operational incidents and recurring failures and implement corrective and preventive actions.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Stakeholder Communication &amp; Continuous Improvement<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Serve as the primary operational contact for SPEED leadership, users, university administrators, vendors, and external partners.</li><li>Communicate facility capabilities, access requirements, schedules, limitations, risks, and operational updates to technical and nontechnical audiences.</li><li>Use operational data and user feedback to improve procedures, training, services, facility reliability, and user support.</li><li>Contribute to strategic planning for facility growth, new platform deployment, service expansion, workshops, demonstrations, sponsor visits, and long-term sustainability.<br><br></li></ul><div>If you are an analytical and collaborative leader who enjoys bringing people, technology, and complex operations together, we invite you to apply. This is an opportunity to help build and manage a cutting-edge research facility while supporting researchers, technical teams, and partners from across the university and beyond.</div> 230101 - Interdisciplinary Programs - SVP https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228571 2026-03-18T12:58:58-04:00 2026-03-18T12:58:59-04:00 Temporary STEM Camp Counselor 2026 <div>The Science House (TSH) is the College of Sciences statewide K-12 STEM outreach unit. Established in 1991, TSH is a national model of university collaboration between university departments, businesses, and the K-12 community. TSH’s mission is to collaboratively maintain and develop K-12 teacher and student programs that inspire a passion for STEM learning, research, and discovery. The Science House achieves this through four strategic priorities: 1) Teacher Professional Development centered on equipping teachers to use engaging instructional practices, emerging technologies, guided inquiry, and project-based learning, 2) Student Programs focused on problem-solving, applied learning, collaboration, communication, critical thinking, and competition, 3) Rural Satellite Offices that provide teacher professional development, student programs, and an Equipment and Technology Loan Program in rural, high-need areas, and 4) Broader Impact Support that helps NC State faculty in developing Impact Identities and carrying out broader impacts activities integrated with their research that advance open and participatory science.<br><br>The Science House's Imhotep and Kyran Anderson programs introduce elementary and middle school students to the world of STEM. Programs are designed to enhance students’ enthusiasm and preparation for science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) with a particular emphasis on reaching under-served and students historically underrepresented in STEM disciplines. Students actively discover the wonder of STEM through inquiry-based activities, field trips, presentations, and career exploration activities with STEM professionals.<br><br>Kyran Anderson Academy is a one week daily summer program for rising third through sixth graders. Imhotep Academy offers one week daily summer sessions, as well as Fall and Spring sessions for middle school students on Saturdays from 8:45 a.m. to 1:00 p.m. <strong>Instructors may be hired for either or both camps.</strong><br><br>Camp Counselors provide general program support and direct supervision of campers during summer camps, track-out programs, and Saturday academies.</div> 171788 - The Science House Grads & Temps https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234705 2026-09-04T09:56:55-04:00 2026-09-04T09:56:56-04:00 Crew Leader I <div>Join our dynamic <strong>Environmental Services team</strong> as a<strong> Crew Leader I, </strong>where you’ll lead essential projects, such as power washing entrances, window washing, waxing floors, and more. This role offers an exciting opportunity to guide a dedicated team, ensuring the cleanliness and functionality of campus spaces. You will be responsible for <strong>coordinating daily operations, resolving maintenance issues, and delivering excellent customer service</strong>.</div><div><br></div><div><strong>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited:</strong></div><ul><li>Lead projects, including power washing, window washing, floor waxing, and re-lamping fixtures and dispensers</li><li><strong>Inspect and verify maintenance discrepancies and ensure timely resolution</strong></li><li>Provide support to other zones as needed, filling in for vacant positions or absenteeism</li><li><strong>Collect and submit timesheets</strong> within established timelines</li><li>Perform critical duties during adverse weather or emergencies as assigned</li><li>Complete accident reports within 24 hours of an incident</li><li>Assist with <strong>equipment inventory</strong> and maintain monthly inventory of storage closet supplies</li><li>Submit supply orders for manager approval to ensure adequate stock levels</li><li><strong>Train new employees, ensuring they understand tasks and safety protocols</strong></li><li>Transport employees to various locations for assignments</li><li>Collaborate with maintenance staff inside and outside the unit to resolve issues efficiently</li><li>Address and resolve customer requests via radio communications, work orders, and direct interactions</li></ul><div><br></div><div>If you’re passionate about leadership and committed to maintaining a high standard of cleanliness, this role is for you!</div> 445080 - Environmental Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231413 2026-06-08T13:18:31-04:00 2026-06-08T13:18:33-04:00 Pest Control Supervisor <div>Join our team as a<strong> Pest Control Supervisor</strong> and play a vital role in maintaining a safe, clean, and welcoming campus environment. <strong>This position is responsible for leading and supporting Pest Control Technicians while overseeing pest and wildlife management operations across university facilities</strong>. The Pest Control Supervisor manages a wide range of indoor and outdoor pest control services, including treatment and prevention for pests such as termites, rodents, roaches, ants, spiders, wasps, flies, and other common infestations. This role also supports wildlife control efforts involving animals such as raccoons, squirrels, birds, deer, snakes, bats, and more.<br><br></div><div>The ideal candidate will be dependable, proactive, team-oriented, and comfortable working in a fast-paced environment where problem-solving and communication are essential. This is an excellent opportunity for someone with pest control and leadership experience who enjoys making a direct impact and supporting the daily operations of a dynamic campus community.<br><br></div><div>If you are ready to lead, problem-solve, and grow your career in facilities operations, we encourage you to apply today.</div><div><br><strong>Key responsibilities include, but are not limited to:<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Directly supervise and manage the daily performance of two technicians, including approving timesheets/timecards, managing leave requests, and monitoring staff attendance, as well as administering performance work plans and conducting annual appraisals.</li><li>Coordinate professional development by identifying and submitting requests for necessary staff training and required certifications.</li><li>Update and enhance the <strong>Integrated Pest Management (IPM)</strong> procedures to align with campus-wide needs and environmental standards.</li><li>Drive service expansion to meet growing campus demands and improve overall response capabilities.</li><li><strong>Facilitate official partnerships with University Entomology experts and external contractors to leverage specialized knowledge and resources.</strong></li><li>Manage stakeholder communication, ensuring the campus community is informed of pest control activities and safety protocols.</li><li>Evaluate service quality by collecting customer feedback and <strong>conducting account reviews</strong> in collaboration with service managers.</li><li>Execute specialized treatment using established methods to<strong> control infestations </strong>throughout the campus.</li><li><strong>Conduct independent inspections to identify infestation sources, assess property damage, and implement corrective actions.</strong></li><li>Apply chemical solutions, powders, or gases according to technical specifications and ensure thorough cleanup of work sites.</li><li>Operate and maintain specialized equipment, including trucks equipped with power spraying systems.</li><li><strong>Manage project workflows independently, coordinating with safety committees and clients during on-site inspections.</strong></li><li>Enforce strict adherence to state regulations regarding the mixing, storage, and application of professional-grade chemicals.</li><li><strong>Ensure EPA compliance by following all pesticide label directions and environmental protection mandates.</strong></li><li>Minimize environmental impact by implementing updated,<strong> eco-friendly pest control practices</strong> across the university.</li><li>The ability to communicate with a variety of stakeholders. The ability to explain their pest control process, identify timelines, and address occupants' concerns.</li></ul> 445080 - Environmental Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233070 2026-07-23T10:54:35-04:00 2026-07-23T10:54:36-04:00 Anesthesia Veterinary Technician <div>Are you ready to elevate your clinical skills in a dynamic, cutting-edge teaching environment?<br><br>The Anesthesia Department provides advanced anesthetic support to a varied patient population across the hospital campus, including small, large, and exotic species. This role requires advanced veterinary medical knowledge, strong time-management skills, and the ability to thrive in a fast-paced, teaching-focused clinical environment. The position collaborates closely with specialty services, clinicians, staff, and students to coordinate and deliver high-quality anesthetic and perioperative care. Responsibilities include comprehensive patient assessment, administration of complex anesthetic and medical treatments, continuous monitoring during diagnostic and surgical procedures, post-operative recovery, and strict adherence to infectious disease and safety protocols. This role also supports hospital operations through documentation, workflow coordination, and the training and mentoring of learners and new staff.<br><br><strong>Key Responsibilities:</strong></div><div><br></div><div><strong>Advanced Patient Care &amp; Anesthesia Support:</strong> Provide comprehensive anesthetic and peri-anesthetic care, including pre-anesthetic assessments, high-risk drug calculations, advanced monitoring, life-saving interventions, pain management, and post-operative recovery for small, large, and exotic species. Requires previous experience in a canine / feline veterinary setting.</div><div><strong>Monitoring, Equipment &amp; Technical Proficiency:</strong> Operate, interpret, and maintain advanced anesthetic and physiologic monitoring equipment and specialized patient care devices to ensure patient safety during surgical, diagnostic, and imaging procedures.</div><div><strong>Interdisciplinary Collaboration:</strong> Coordinate care and communicate effectively with specialty services, clinicians, staff, and students across multiple hospital service areas to support complex medical and surgical cases.</div><div><strong>Education, Training &amp; Mentorship:</strong> Orient, teach, and coach new staff, students, interns, and residents on anesthetic procedures, hospital policies, and patient care standards in a teaching-hospital environment.</div><div><strong>Documentation, Safety &amp; Compliance:</strong> Accurately document patient care and treatments within hospital information systems while maintaining a clean, safe work environment and adhering to infectious disease protocols, OSHA, EHS, and radiation safety standards. Must have the ability to work with or around sick animals, potentially hazardous materials, cleaning chemicals, gas anesthetics, zoonotic diseases, and unpleasant odors.</div><div><strong>Physical &amp; Operational Requirements:</strong> Ability to lift up to 50 pounds, with or without reasonable accommodations. Ability to work beyond scheduled work hours when needed.</div> 192001 - CVM Teaching Hospital https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/220224 2025-06-30T12:41:54-04:00 2025-06-30T12:41:55-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>The Geohazards and Earthquake Engineering (<a href=\"https://cabas.wordpress.ncsu.edu/\"><strong>GeoQuake</strong></a>) Research Lab invites applications for a full-time Postdoctoral Scholar position. The GeoQuake Lab takes an interdisciplinary approach to the assessment of seismic hazards (including earthquake ground shaking and ground failure), and their impact on civil infrastructure systems as well as the communities they serve. Our research team examines, processes, and compiles large, global, and regional earthquake ground motion databases, and develops analytical and numerical models of seismic wave propagation through complex media. In doing so, we use geospatial analytics to interrogate different types of data and advance modeling frameworks of geohazards at multiple scales. We also conduct dynamic laboratory testing of soils, incorporate geology, geomorphology and probabilistic frameworks into assessments of the variability and uncertainty associated with geohazards.</div><div>The work conducted at the GeoQuake Lab spans the physics underlying the seismic wave propagation phenomenon and the practical implications on infrastructure performance to inform decision making, based on fundamental understanding of natural hazards and their complex interactions with the built environment. The successful applicant will support GeoQuake’s new projects and grant application writing in the area of physics-based ground motion simulations.</div><div><br>Research activities [80%]: Developing pathways to improve physics-based ground motion simulations in highly uncertain environments with a focus on developing improved characterizations of the effects of shallow and deep geologic structures. This work will be highly collaborative, interdisciplinary and will likely involve GeoQuake graduate students working on related topics.</div><div><br></div><div>Project management and coordination [20%]: Collaborate with GeoQuake Principal Investigator to co-develop a strategic research plan, identifying sources of funding, and supporting grant application writing.<br><br></div> 140301 - Civil Construction & Environmental Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233988 2026-08-19T13:00:41-04:00 2026-08-19T13:00:41-04:00 UTS - Temporary Recycling Collections Operator at NC State <div><strong>&nbsp;Bulk Waste and Electronics Collections</strong> – scheduled route collections average of 180 sites per week for materials and electronics not accepted in the automated or recycling collection system. Related transport and sorting into appropriate materials bins at the Dearstyne Rolloff Site. Bulk material collections include items such as pallets, crates, discarded furniture, scrap metal, electronics, etc.<br><br><strong>Recycling and Reuse Collections</strong> – customer requested collections of reuse, recycling and unwanted building items and hauling to Dearstyne for recycling or disposal. This is on demand service for campus customers and response time is offered within 1-2 business days of the initial request. Common requests include delivery and pick up of carts for office clean outs, pick up and signature collections of confidential recycling, preparing and distributing materials for reuse in other areas or departments.<br> <br><strong>Events and Projects</strong> – Field support to provide special waste/recycling/compost service for campus events and support major activities on campus such as residence hall move in and move out where additional services, monitoring and staff support are needed for collections.<br><strong><br>Repair, Maintenance, Safety</strong> – provide inspection and maintenance of containers related to the collection of waste and recycling on campus. Includes cleaning, proper labeling, delivery, distribution and placement of containers inside and outside of buildings. Inspection and maintenance of assigned trucks and equipment used daily to complete duties and assignments. Perform all work assignments and duties in compliance with established safe work practices. Follow safety and health regulations, policies and procedures. Identifies and resolves potential safety concerns or violations.</div> 480501 - UHR Temporary Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/226758 2026-01-23T15:42:40-05:00 2026-01-23T15:42:41-05:00 ICU Veterinary Technician <div>This role is a key part of the Intensive Care Unit (ICU) within the Randall B. Terry, Jr. Companion Animal Medical Center — the hospital’s 24/7 critical care hub. As an ICU technician, you’ll work hands-on with a wide variety of complex small animal and exotic cases, making this an exceptional opportunity to expand your technical skills in a high‑acuity, team-driven environment.</div><div><br>In this position, you’ll play a direct role in delivering lifesaving care. You will triage, monitor, and treat critically ill and post‑operative patients while collaborating closely with specialty services, clinicians, students, and fellow technicians. Your work may include advanced procedures such as catheter placement, transfusions, ventilator monitoring, anesthesia recovery, and emergency response (CPR). You’ll also manage precise medication dosing, use a range of monitoring and life‑support equipment, and provide continuous observation for patients recovering from surgery or experiencing serious medical conditions.</div><div><br>Beyond patient care, you’ll contribute to the daily operations and teaching mission of the hospital. This includes accurate medical documentation, supporting client communication and visitation, and helping maintain a safe and compliant ICU environment. Teaching and mentoring students, interns, and residents is also an important part of the role. Occasional work beyond scheduled hours may be necessary to meet the needs of the service.</div><div><br>To thrive in this fast‑paced setting, you’ll need strong teamwork, adaptability, clear communication, and excellent clinical judgment — especially under pressure. We’re looking for someone who values continuous learning, enjoys a collaborative environment, and is committed to compassionate, high‑quality patient care.</div><div><strong><br>Essential Job Duties<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Provide high‑level nursing care and close monitoring for ICU patients, including critical, surgical, and infectious cases.</li><li>Perform advanced procedures such as catheterization, oxygen therapy, transfusions, and ventilator support.</li><li>Accurately calculate and administer medications, IV fluids, and pain management therapies.</li><li>Assist clinicians and students with diagnostics, anesthetic recovery, and emergency procedures.</li><li>Maintain meticulous patient records, follow infection control and safety protocols, and support training and mentorship of students and interns.</li><li>Safely lift up to 50 pounds, with or without reasonable accommodations.</li></ul> 192001 - CVM Teaching Hospital https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232833 2026-07-16T16:07:49-04:00 2026-07-16T16:07:50-04:00 Extension Agent, Ag-Hort (Assistant, Associate, or Full) <div>Are you looking for a rewarding career that will let you give back to your community? NC State Extension extends research-based knowledge to all North Carolinians, helping them transform science into everyday solutions that improve their lives and grow our state. We are the model of excellence in non-formal, research-based education that advances agriculture, the environment, human health and well-being, youth and communities, while creating prosperity for all North Carolinians. Working for NC State Extension allows you to teach without being tied to a classroom and school bell. Check out this <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3MptxITpJ54&amp;t=3s\"><strong>video</strong></a> on what it’s like to work for NC State Extension.<br><br></div><div>No two days in Extension are ever the same, there is always something new! Extension Agents are active in their communities and often need to meet the clients when and where they are available. This is not your typical desk job!<br><br></div><div>While our employees are located in NC County offices, they reap the benefits of employment at NC State University. Some of our benefits include:</div><div>· Paid time off</div><div>· Paid tuition</div><div>· Health Insurance</div><div>· Flexible Scheduling</div><div><br></div><div>· Retirement Savings</div><div>· Supplemental Benefits Programs</div><div>· WolfPerks!</div><div><br></div><div>You Belong Here! At NC State, our goal is for all employees to reach their fullest potential at work. As you consider this opportunity, we encourage you to review our <a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>Employee Value Proposition</strong></a> (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/</strong></a>) and learn more about what makes NC State the best place to learn and work for everyone.<br><br><strong>Primary Purpose of Position</strong><br>This position will be responsible for Horticulture programming in Dare County. The Horticulture Agent is responsible for planning, implementing and evaluating an outcome-based program addressing issues and opportunities identified in Dare County. The agent in this position must have technical competence in both consumer and commercial horticulture and in the delivery of non-formal education programs, and the identification of plants and pests and the diagnosis of plant problems. The agent in this position develops and works with stakeholder-based committees that provide direction for Extension programs; works with community organizations to effectively link Extension with local resources and clients; and assists effectively with recruiting, training, and managing Extension Master Gardener and other volunteers to expand outreach efforts. In addition, this agent will provide support to community gardens and work towards developing a youth horticulture program.<br><br><strong>Primary Function of Organizational Unit</strong></div><div><br></div><div>North Carolina Cooperative Extension gives our residents easy access to the resources and expertise of NC State University and NC A&amp;T State University. Through educational programs, publications, and events, Cooperative Extension field faculty deliver unbiased, research-based information to North Carolina citizens.</div><div><br><br>Dare County has a strong and thriving Extension Master Gardener volunteer program. The 75 Master Gardener volunteers regularly assist at the Dare County Extension Center, maintain the Outer Banks Arboretum, and have responded to more than 10,539 contacts in 2024 and provided hours of service equaling over $203,000. The group provides educational programs for its members as well as delivers programs throughout the community.</div><div><br></div><div>Dare County is located in northeastern North Carolina along the Atlantic seaboard. Dare County stretches along almost 110 miles of shoreline known as the Outer Banks. The County seat at Manteo is approximately 200 miles east of Raleigh, the State capital, and 90 miles south of Virginia Beach-Norfolk, Virginia, metropolitan area. The County contains much of what is known as North Carolina’s “Outer Banks” resort and vacation areas and contains approximately two-thirds of the North Carolina coastline. Six municipalities are located within the county: Duck, Kill Devil Hills, Kitty Hawk, Manteo, Nags Head and Southern Shores. Dare County has a permanent population of approximately 38,000 residents.<br><br>For information about Dare County visit <a href=\"https://www.darenc.gov/\"><strong>https://www.darenc.gov/</strong></a></div><div><br></div><div>To learn more about the NCCE Dare County Center go to <a href=\"https://dare.ces.ncsu.edu/\"><strong>https://dare.ces.ncsu.edu/</strong></a></div><div><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities<br></strong>The Agent works with homeowners, community groups, commercial horticultural crop producers, professional landscape and turf professionals, and others to increase their knowledge of horticultural issues and implementation of best management practices.</div><div><br></div><div>Additional responsibilities include assisting with recruiting and training new Master Gardeners, pesticide education as it pertains to horticulture and the green industry, and developing and coordinating youth horticulture programs.</div><div><br></div><div>This person must be self-directed, having the ability to juggle multiple responsibilities and schedules, have effective oral and written communication skills, and be willing to work with broad audiences. Grant writing and project management experience is essential. Computer, organization, interpersonal and networking skills are crucial for success. Ability to effectively deliver information via mass media including television, radio and social media is expected. Candidates must be willing to work with the staff to support the entire Extension program.</div><div><br></div><div>The person in this position must possess a high degree of horticulture subject matter competence and excellent people and communication skills, be computer literate, and have the necessary education and experience to lead a strong Extension education program in the assigned areas.</div> 117706 - Northeast District - Dare https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233810 2026-08-13T13:14:33-04:00 2026-08-13T13:14:34-04:00 Biotechnology Program Coordinator <div>In this role, you will play a central role in advancing the Biotechnology (BIT) Program and supporting the students, faculty, and partners who make it successful. You will serve as the program expert and a trusted advisor to students, helping them navigate their academic programs, connect with opportunities, and achieve their educational and professional goals.<br><br></div><div>You will work closely with BIT leadership, faculty, university offices, students, and external sponsors to coordinate courses, monitor student progress, lead communications and events, and support program reporting. You will also lead data analysis and reporting for externally funded training programs, including NSF REU and NIH T32 grants, helping demonstrate program impact and support continuous improvement.<br><br></div><div>This position offers an opportunity to take ownership of meaningful program activities, build relationships across the university and beyond, and directly shape the student experience and continued growth of a dynamic biotechnology education and training program. This role focuses specifically on BIT program operations and does not oversee ISI-wide education or partnership initiatives.</div><div><br><strong>Key Responsibilities:<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Academic Program Leadership</strong></div><ul><li>Advise BIT undergraduate and graduate students on degree requirements, course planning, enrollment, and academic progression.</li><li>Coordinate courses, instructors, classrooms, catalog updates, and graduate program activities.</li><li>Maintain accurate student records and monitor enrollment, progression, and completion.</li></ul><div><strong>Data, Reporting &amp; Program Operations</strong></div><ul><li>Manage program records, databases, calendars, and operational resources.</li><li>Lead data analysis, longitudinal tracking, and sponsor reporting for BIT programs, including NSF REU and NIH T32</li><li>Monitor program and budget information and provide leadership with insights to support planning and decision-making.</li></ul><div><strong>Communications, Outreach &amp; Events</strong></div><ul><li>Lead BIT communications, website content, marketing, and outreach to showcase program opportunities.</li><li>Coordinate seminars, workshops, recruitment activities, visitors, and other program events.</li><li>Manage announcements, calendars, and communication channels to keep students and stakeholders informed and engaged.</li></ul><div><strong>Program Coordination &amp; Support</strong></div><ul><li>Serve as a primary resource for BIT students, faculty, staff, and partners, helping them navigate program processes and university systems.</li><li>Coordinate meetings, schedules, presentations, reports, and other activities that support effective program operations.</li><li>Take ownership of day-to-day program needs, solve problems, and help advance the BIT program's goals and student experience.<br><br></li></ul><div>This is a visible, multifaceted position that combines <strong>student advising, academic program management, data and reporting, communications, and event coordination</strong>. The role is well suited to a proactive, organized professional who enjoys managing varied responsibilities, solving problems independently, and serving as a trusted resource to students, faculty, leadership, and university partners.<br><br></div><div>Success in this role requires strong communication and organizational skills, attention to detail, sound judgment, and the ability to manage multiple priorities while maintaining a high level of service and accuracy.</div> 237002 - Biotechnology Teaching https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/225670 2025-12-11T12:07:02-05:00 2025-12-11T12:07:03-05:00 Temporary Research Technician <div>Research Techs will work with faculty, graduate students, and postdocs on various entomological research projects that blend laboratory, greenhouse, and field research. Work will support research on field crops and sweet potato insect pests. Research assistants will become familiar with several insect species that affect common North Carolina crops and should be prepared to conduct research in the field under challenging environmental conditions.</div> 111801 - Entomology and Plant Pathology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234409 2026-08-31T14:14:47-04:00 2026-08-31T14:14:48-04:00 HR Project Manager <div>The HR Project Manager, housed within the HR Information Management &amp; Analytics (HRIMA) unit, serves as a consultative advisor and facilitator for cross-functional HR projects, supporting the planning, scoping, and implementation of technical and non-technical initiatives. This role connects HR, campus partners, and the Office of Information Technology to clarify business needs, establish project goals and timelines, and advance projects from concept to completion.<br><br></div><div><br>You will bring people, processes, and technology together to make HR operations more efficient and effective. By evaluating workflows, identifying opportunities for improvement, and translating policy and regulatory changes into clear business requirements, you will help teams navigate complexity and turn ideas into practical solutions. Your ability to build relationships, communicate clearly, and remove roadblocks will be key to delivering HR processes and technology that meet university needs and support compliance.<br><br></div><div><br>Key responsibilities fall within the following functional areas.<br><br></div><div><strong><br>Project Facilitation &amp; Consultative Advising<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Facilitate cross-functional process and project teams by providing consultative guidance that helps campus partners navigate process improvements, technical enhancements, and system configurations.</li><li>Connect UHR, campus divisions, and Enterprise Application System/Office of Information Technology partners to identify systemic and technical barriers, coordinate resolution, and maintain momentum on shared priorities.</li><li>Guide discovery and alignment sessions with campus partners to understand current-state workflows, assess institutional and localized business needs, and establish clear direction for cross-functional project delivery.<br><br></li></ul><div><br><strong>Strategic Insight &amp; Workflow Optimization<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Translate complex legislative changes, Federal and State requirements, and UNC System Office mandates into functional requirements and automated system logic that support accurate, sustainable technical solutions.</li><li>Partner with university leadership and process teams to evaluate and improve business processes and technology, increasing efficiency, strengthening service delivery, and reducing unnecessary rework across the organization.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong><br>Employee Data Governance &amp; Quality Assurance<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Provide leadership and guidance for HR technology business rules, security parameters, and data definition standards to promote consistent, reliable, and well-governed employee data.</li><li>Serve as a subject matter expert on the alignment of HR technology functionality with university policies, regulations, rules, and applicable regulatory requirements, helping ensure system behavior supports institutional standards.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong><br>Operational Excellence &amp; Support<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Oversee complex operational troubleshooting and system optimization for core HR processes. Act as the point of escalation for complex functional issues before they are moved to OIT.</li><li>Partner with OIT/EAS throughout User Acceptance Testing (UAT) and post-implementation reviews to validate business requirements, assess system functionality, and ensure technology solutions deliver the intended operational outcomes.<br><br></li></ul><div><br></div><div>We are looking for someone who can build relationships, navigate complexity, and turn ideas into practical solutions that move HR initiatives forward. If you are a collaborative problem-solver who enjoys bringing people, processes, and technology together to make complex work more manageable, we invite you to apply.</div> 480201 - UHR - Information Management & Analytics https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/229314 2026-04-09T08:44:14-04:00 2026-04-09T08:44:15-04:00 Lecturer-Teacher, Education and Learning Sciences <div>The Lecturer supports the TELS program by delivering high-quality, instruction-focused teaching that prepares future educators for success in K–12 settings. This role emphasizes effective pedagogy, integration of instructional technologies, and application of research-based practices. The Lecturer contributes to student learning, curriculum alignment, and the connection between coursework and field-based experiences.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities:<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Instruction &amp; Teaching:</strong></div><ul><li>Teach 1–3 courses per semester in areas such as elementary education, middle grades education, subject-matter methods (e.g., math, science, literacy), or learning sciences</li><li>Design and deliver engaging, student-centered instruction</li><li>Apply research-based teaching strategies and best practices</li><li>Integrate digital tools and instructional technologies to enhance learning</li><li>Connect theory to practice through real-world application</li><li>Facilitate active learning through discussion, collaboration, and applied activities<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Course Design &amp; Curriculum Alignment:</strong></div><ul><li>Develop and update course materials, assignments, and syllabi</li><li>Ensure alignment with program goals and relevant standards (e.g., NC Standard Course of Study)</li><li>Collaborate with faculty and program coordinators to maintain consistency across courses<br><br></li></ul> 131101 - Teacher Education and Learning Sciences (TELS) https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231856 2026-06-18T14:23:31-04:00 2026-06-18T14:23:31-04:00 UTS - Temporary Part-time On-Call CDL Bus Drivers at NC State <div>University Bus Drivers operate activity buses ranging in size from 22 passengers to 44 passengers. A North Carolina State University Bus Driver is responsible for safely transporting students, faculty, staff, and school affiliates to and from designated locations. These locations may be within the same city or different cities. Trips may be field trips or labs to extend the academic experience, sporting events, or other extracurricular activities. Since the University Bus Driver accommodates student/faculty schedules, they generally work unusual hours. He/she may work early morning, late evening, or weekends. The Bus Driver maintains the cleanliness, organization, and safety of the bus itself.<br><br><strong><em>This role is expected to last 11 months with the potential to extend<br></em></strong><br><strong>Essential Duties and Responsibilities:<br></strong>• Picks up and returns passengers at locations specified by reservation<br>• Ensures that all passengers are safely and properly seated on the bus<br>• Observes rules of the road and drives with caution<br>• Ensures timely conveyance of passengers to their destination<br>• Ensures that vehicles are kept neat and clean at all times<br>• Carries out routine pre-trip and post-trip inspection of vehicles in the hour prior to the shuttle pick up; inspects tire pressure, oil levels, windshield wipers, mirrors and other details for any problems. Identify electrical or mechanical faults. May carry out simple maintenance on vehicles. Brings problems or maintenance needs to the attention of the Bus Dispatcher.<br>• Fills bus fuel tank<br>• Read maps and check weather and traffic reports to determine the best route to take<br>• Keep all documents and receipts of the vehicle and maintenance services secure<br>• Maintain a travel log to record travel locations travelled to, travel time, work hours and mileage for billing purposes.<br><br><strong>Knowledge, Skills and Abilities:</strong></div><ul><li>A bus driver must be well-trained. He/she should be a skilled driver and must hold a CDL A/B operating license with passenger endorsement</li><li>He/ she must have specific knowledge of traffic laws and rules of the road. Must adhere strictly to them</li><li>The work of bus driving may involve loading and unloading of luggage, and frequent opening of doors requiring strength and stamina.</li><li>The Bus Driver must be patient and calm in dealing with situations like irate passengers and traffic congestion. Must be capable of acting quickly and calmly in a crisis.</li><li>Must be able to drive for a long period of time and possess a strong sense of direction,</li><li>He/she must be a person who values lives and must think about the safety of his/her passengers and other road users.</li><li>He/she must be focused, confident, and observant while driving. Must demonstrate a quick response time.</li><li>Possess effective communication, leadership and team play skills, as well as the ability to adhere to instructions.</li></ul> 480501 - UHR Temporary Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233383 2026-07-31T09:44:46-04:00 2026-07-31T09:44:48-04:00 Painter <div>Join our passionate Facilities team as a <strong>Painter </strong>in the Trade Shop, where your skills will help keep our campus vibrant, safe, and inviting. <strong>In this role, you’ll use your expertise in painting, finishing, and drywall repair to maintain the beauty and functionality of our buildings and spaces</strong>.<br><br> If you enjoy hands-on work, attention to detail, and seeing the visible results of your craftsmanship, we invite you to apply and be part of the team that helps our campus shine.<br><br><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li><strong>Prepare surfaces by cleaning, sanding, patching, priming, and ensuring optimal conditions for painting</strong></li><li>Paint a variety of surfaces, including walls, ceilings, casework, fixtures, handrails, bollards, and equipment</li><li><strong>Perform</strong> advanced surface preparation, including sanding, applying wood filler, Bondo, caulking, and completing drywall repairs.</li><li>Match paint colors and finishes to maintain consistency throughout campus facilities.</li><li><strong>Mix and tint paints accurately using specialized equipment</strong></li><li>Inspect work areas, materials, and vehicles regularly to <strong>ensure safety and compliance with hazardous material regulations</strong></li><li><strong>Review plans, product specifications, and PPE requirements to ensure proper application and disposal procedures</strong></li><li>Report safety concerns, infractions, accidents, and injuries promptly in accordance with University policy</li><li>Assist with power washing and other maintenance projects across campus as needed</li><li><strong>Collaborate</strong> with other Trade Shops units to support broader maintenance and repair initiatives</li><li>Lift up to 60 pounds frequently and up to 90 plus pounds occasionally, with or without reasonable accommodations.</li><li>Communicate effectively with supervisors and the general public, understand and follow verbal and written instructions and other communications related to work assignments and other matters.</li></ul> 445030 - Building Maintenance & Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223251 2025-09-25T11:00:59-04:00 2025-09-25T11:01:00-04:00 C:LAB Makerspace Technician <div><strong>The Division of Academic &amp; Student Affairs </strong>(DASA) is where students discover connections and community. It is where students live, where they gather, where they improve themselves, each other, and their university. DASA is where students seek guidance and support, both in and outside of the classroom. The division serves all students at NC State through three integrated components: academic affairs, student affairs, and University College.</div><div><br></div><div>NC State wants employees to achieve their highest potential at work, learn more about our employee value proposition here (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>You Belong Here</strong></a>). The Division of Academic and Student Affairs is the place to work for values-driven faculty and staff who are passionate about promoting the success of the whole student.<br><br><strong><em>The Crafts Center</em></strong><em>’s is a place where creative skill and self-expression are fostered through the making and sharing of art and craft. With the intent of enhancing the quality of life, we reach out through academic collaboration, support for student life and through partnerships with other arts organizations. Our studios support the disciplines of woodworking, pottery, photography, fiber arts, lapidary, glass, jewelry, mixed media, and more.<br></em><br></div><div><em>The C:LAB Studio Technician reports to and is accountable to the Director of The Crafts Center</em><strong><em><br></em></strong><br></div><div><strong>Responsibilities</strong></div><ul><li>Upholds the missions and goals of The Crafts Center</li><li>Responsible for initial prioritization, troubleshooting, resolution, escalation, and timely response to user requests and issues</li><li>Provides support for the C:LAB members and students by: answering technical questions, servicing and producing student works [ie. 3D printing, engraving, etc.], offering workflow and data management suggestions, promoting the C:LAB on campus, and supporting instructors</li><li>Maintains, deploys, and supervises all computers, equipment and software in the C:LAB</li><li>Provides technology support for classroom instruction</li><li>Assists with password resets, accounts, permissions, and general network troubleshooting in cooperation with University IT</li><li>Maintains documentation for network, user applications, safety and use, and software</li><li>Works with Director to identify trends, roll out proactive projects and other duties as assigned</li><li>Manages C:LAB inventory, supplies, and materials</li><li>Works with Director and instructors to develop maker projects and expand curriculum</li><li>Provides orientation to [new] instructors and students</li><li>Provides, gathers, manages all documentation relating to user manuals, best practices, and health and safety expectations</li><li>Assures and maintains best practices related to student health and safety by teaching/conducting proper technique and supervision</li><li>Creates a warm and welcoming learning environment for students</li><li>Communicates clearly and professionally in a timely manner</li></ul> 243030 - Crafts Center https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234121 2026-08-25T10:12:31-04:00 2026-08-25T10:12:32-04:00 Administrative Assistant <div>Join NC State University’s Facilities Division as an <strong>Administrative Assistant </strong>on our <strong>Business Operations team</strong> and play an important role in keeping day-to-day operations organized, efficient, and connected. In this position, you’ll provide comprehensive administrative support, assist with special projects for the Assistant Vice Chancellor, and work closely with campus partners to coordinate meetings, events, catering, space reservations, and guest arrangements.<br><br></div><div>Beyond administrative support, you’ll help foster a <strong>positive, collaborative workplace culture</strong> built on teamwork, emotional intelligence, integrity, kindness, and strong working relationships. This is a great opportunity for an organized, proactive professional who enjoys supporting others, solving problems, coordinating details, and making a meaningful contribution to a team that supports the university’s facilities and operations.<br><br></div><div><strong>If you’re ready to bring your organizational skills, professionalism, and collaborative spirit to NC State’s Facilities Division, we invite you to apply today!</strong></div><div><br></div><div><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><div><br></div><ul><li><strong>Coordinate and manage complex schedules and calendars </strong>for both the department and division, ensuring seamless communication and time management</li><li><strong>Draft and prepare memorandums, create detailed spreadsheets, schedule meetings, and maintain essential records for the team</strong></li><li>Maintain and update databases and spreadsheets for special projects assigned by the Assistant Vice Chancellor, conducting in-depth research, reconciling data from multiple systems such as<strong> Interscope </strong>and other project management tools, and producing both standard and customized high-level reports and presentations</li><li><strong>Coordinate travel arrangements and process necessary separation paperwork in a timely manner</strong></li><li>Assist in the development and lead the maintenance of procedures for tracking remote work, ensuring accuracy and consistency.</li><li>Serve as a resource for resolving complex, non-standard issues by coordinating with other work units, facilitating effective solutions, identifying and addressing internal issues that impact customer service and operational efficiency, and collaborating with departmental coordinators to establish standard processes and procedures to enhance overall efficiency.</li><li>Interpret guidelines and policies, respond to inquiries, and provide guidance on processes, services, and operations in non-standard situations.</li><li><strong>Serve as a Facilities Division communications representative by developing and maintaining Standard Operating Procedures and delivering in-person training to departments.</strong></li><li>Maintain and update the Facilities website using <strong>WordPress</strong>, ensuring content is accurate, current, and timely, and develop and distribute communications to keep employees informed of important updates and information.</li><li>Provide comprehensive technical and administrative support for Business Operations by initiating job postings, scheduling interviews, preparing hiring proposals, and processing separation paperwork to ensure a smooth recruitment process</li><li><strong>Manage supply orders and maintain the supply closet, tracking inventory using AiM and the Capital Asset Management System to ensure accuracy.</strong></li></ul> 447010 - Facilities Business Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/227807 2026-02-25T16:27:01-05:00 2026-02-26T14:51:57-05:00 Temporary Dairy Farm Research Technician <div>This position will primarily provides skilled animal care during milking shifts (between the hours of 6am - 11am OR 6pm – 11pm) milking dairy cows in the parlor. Also requires skid steer operation for manure management, moving cows from free stall barn to parlor, maintaining parlor cleanliness, and assisting with other farm chores including cow and calf care.<br><br><strong>Work Schedule:<br></strong>Includes the weekday, weekend and holiday shifts, with the hours ranging from 5:00am – 1:00pm.&nbsp; Hours may be varied due to operational needs of farm. </div> 111102 - Dairy And Process Applications https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234610 2026-09-03T10:38:33-04:00 2026-09-03T10:38:35-04:00 Facilities Maintenance Technician- Advanced <div>Are you an experienced maintenance professional with advanced technical expertise in building systems and operations? We are seeking a skilled <strong>Advanced Facilities Maintenance Technician</strong> to join our <strong>Building Maintenance &amp; Operations</strong> team. In this role, <strong>you will be responsible for maintaining, troubleshooting, repairing, and optimizing HVAC, electrical, plumbing, boiler, and building automation systems that support safe, efficient, and reliable facility operations.</strong><br><br></div><div>This position is ideal for a seasoned technician who enjoys solving complex problems, working with evolving technologies, and serving as a technical resource for others. In addition to hands-on maintenance responsibilities, you will help lead projects, share specialized knowledge, and support the development of team members through training and cross-functional collaboration.<br><br></div><div>​If you are a highly skilled maintenance professional looking for an opportunity to apply your technical expertise, lead challenging projects, and make a meaningful impact on facility operations, <strong>we encourage you to apply today. Bring your experience, leadership, and passion for building maintenance and operations to a team that values excellence, innovation, and continuous improvement.</strong></div><div><strong><br></strong><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li><strong>Perform advanced maintenance, repair, installation, and troubleshooting of HVAC, plumbing, electrical, steam, hot water, boiler, refrigeration, and building control systems.</strong></li><li>Diagnose complex equipment and system failures, identify root causes, and implement effective repairs while ensuring compliance with applicable codes, standards, and safety requirements.</li><li>Maintain and repair temperature controls, sensors, regulators, pneumatic controls, pressure-reducing valves, motorized steam valves, chilled water valves, and other specialized building systems.</li><li>Perform electrical work up to 600 VAC, including maintenance and repair of wiring, panels, breakers, motors, lighting systems, switchgear, and related equipment.</li><li><strong>Support the operation and maintenance of life safety, sprinkler, geothermal, and other advanced building technologies while performing preventive and predictive maintenance activities.</strong></li><li>Demonstrate and enforce proper lockout/tagout procedures, identify system deficiencies, and promote a safe working environment.</li><li>Serve as a technical leader by mentoring team members, cross-training staff, sharing best practices, and providing guidance on advanced repairs across multiple trades.</li><li><strong>Lead or support complex maintenance projects and continuously seek opportunities to improve equipment reliability, operational efficiency, and facility performance.</strong></li><li>Perform tasks requiring standing for extended periods, working in confined spaces, and utilizing various tools. Safely climb and work from different types of ladders to access elevated or below-grade areas (such as roofs, above-ceiling spaces, and utility pits). Be exposed to and work in areas where insects or small animals are occasionally present. All essential tasks must be performed with or without reasonable accommodations.</li><li>Lift, carry, and maneuver materials weighing 60–90 pounds, with or without reasonable accommodations.</li><li>Communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, and the general public, and follows verbal and written instructions related to work assignments and operational needs.</li></ul> 445030 - Building Maintenance & Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230782 2026-05-19T15:20:08-04:00 2026-05-19T15:20:08-04:00 Temporary Fike Agronomy Field Lab Intern <div>The intern will work closely alongside the research technician to develop skills necessary for future work in farm management including equipment maintenance, general field operations including planting, harvesting, cultivation, irrigation and fence maintenance. The intern will also help with several long-term improvement projects related to irrigation, weed identification and crop management plans, and assisting with the design, build, and trialing of three-point attached, custom mechanical cultivators for the Fike Agronomy Field Lab.<br><br><br>As an intern, you'll be involved in all aspects of farm production along with educational programming. You will develop proficiency in the following areas:</div><ul><li>Planting and harvesting commodity crops</li><li>Fence maintenance and installation</li><li>Crop planning</li><li>Soil sensor and flume management</li><li>Soil health assessment</li><li>Safe tractor use and maintenance</li><li>Integrated pest management</li><li>Irrigation maintenance and installation</li></ul><div><br><strong>This position requires some farm experience - at least 6 months is ideal. However, it is expected that the intern will develop skills and knowledge on the job so a good attitude, strong interest in sustainable agriculture, and enthusiasm to learn will be essential for this role.</strong> The intern must also be a clear communicator, able to work with varied individuals, work independently when needed, and able to lift 45 pounds. This position works primarily outdoors, and activities will need to be completed under adverse weather conditions (cold, heat, humidity, rain, pollen) at times. Biting insects, snakes, and poison ivy are sometimes present on our farm.<br><br></div><div><br></div> 110901 - Crop and Soil Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/227014 2026-02-02T10:24:25-05:00 2026-02-02T10:24:26-05:00 Research Scholar, Metabolomics <div><strong>1. Experimental Consultation and Design:</strong></div><ul><li>Serve as the point of contact for client inquiries in the area of small molecule analysis by targeted and untargeted metabolomics, including lipidomics.</li><li>Provide expert advice on experimental design, including selection of sample preparation techniques and analytical methods, to ensure methodological rigor and feasibility.</li><li>Collaborate with other staff members to develop and optimize metabolomics experimental protocols.</li><li>Stay abreast of the latest advances in analytical methods and techniques to integrate into research projects.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>2. Sample Preparation and Acquisition:</strong></div><ul><li>Perform liquid, liquid-liquid, or SPE extraction, derivatization, and other pre-analytical steps to ensure sample integrity and suitability for downstream analysis.</li><li>Operate and maintain LC-MS metabolomics instrumentation (<em>e.g.</em> Thermo TSQ Altis Triple Quadrupole, Exploris 240, Exploris 480, ID-X).</li><li>Develop and implement standard operating procedures (SOPs) for sample handling and preparation to ensure consistency across studies.</li></ul><div><br><strong>3. Data Analysis and Interpretation:</strong></div><ul><li>Perform metabolomics studies and report the results (analyte peak area or concentrations) using relevant software such as Skyline, MS-Dial, and Compound Discoverer.</li><li>Use appropriate statistical and bioinformatics tools to extract meaningful biological insights.</li><li>Perform pathway analysis using specialized software and databases, as needed.</li><li>Interpret complex datasets and collaborate with researchers to contextualize findings within the scope of the project.</li><li>Prepare detailed reports and visualizations of data for internal review, publication in scientific journals, and in grant applications.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>4. Administrative Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Maintain accurate and up-to-date laboratory records, including experimental protocols, data logs, and inventory of samples and reagents.</li><li>Coordinate with the operations manager to ensure adequate supplies; ensure continuity of operations by coordinating maintenance of major instrumentation and associated ancillary equipment.</li><li>Assist in the training and supervision of staff and students in metabolomics techniques and best practices.</li><li>Contribute to the preparation of manuscripts, grant applications, and progress reports for funding agencies.</li><li>Ensure compliance with institutional and regulatory requirements related to biosafety, data management, and ethical standards.</li></ul> 210204 - METRIC Mass Spectrometry Platform Technology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234534 2026-09-02T12:27:48-04:00 2026-09-02T12:27:48-04:00 Financial Operations Supervisor <div>As the Financial Operations Supervisor, you will serve as a strategic financial anchor within the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences (CALS), providing direct leadership and accounting oversight for a centralized financial operations team. Reporting to the Director of Business Services, you will safeguard the integrity, accuracy, and compliance of high-risk transactions across a complex multi-funding environment—including federal (USDA NIFA, Hatch, Smith-Lever), state, and receipt-supported funds within CALS’ unique Ledger 4 structure. In this high-impact role, your expertise will directly mitigate regulatory risk, ensure audit readiness, and maintain the university's trusted financial standing.<br><br></div><div>In this role, you will turn complex accounting requirements into seamless, efficient operations. By fostering a collaborative and accountable team culture, you will empower your staff to master intricate cost principles while serving as a supportive mentor who elevates their professional growth. Your direct influence will bridge the gap between central administration and non-financial department stakeholders, translating dense federal regulations into clear, actionable guidance. Ultimately, you will drive continuous improvement, building robust internal controls that allow the college’s critical research and extension programs to thrive with complete financial confidence.<br><br></div><div>Key responsibilities will fall within the following functional areas:<br><br></div><div><strong>Leadership &amp; Team Supervision</strong>&nbsp;<br><br></div><ul><li>Provide direct supervision, coaching, and performance management, cultivating a collaborative team culture focused on accountability, precision, and service excellence.</li><li>Optimize Capacity: Assign, prioritize, and balance workloads across functional areas (AP, AR, Travel, P-Card) to maximize operational efficiency and maintain compliance standards.</li><li>Design robust onboarding programs to ensure the team thoroughly understands core accounting principles, financial systems, and funding-specific requirements.</li><li>Identify recurring operational bottlenecks and implement standardized workflows, proactively guiding corrective actions to improve team accuracy.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong><br>Financial Oversight &amp; Transaction Review<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Review and approve complex transactions, using critical evaluation to ensure accurate accounting treatment across federal, state, and receipt-supported funds.</li><li>Direct staff analysis of CALS’ Ledger 4 project structures to guarantee correct funding source identification and regulatory framework application.</li><li>Oversee reconciliation processes to ensure&nbsp; data accuracy, applying sound judgment to resolve financial discrepancies promptly.</li><li>Serve as a key decision-maker and escalation point for ambiguous or high-risk financial and compliance issues, applying relevant accounting principles and policy precedents&nbsp;<br><br></li></ul><div><strong><br>Policy, Compliance &amp; Internal Controls&nbsp;<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Interpret and apply complex regulatory guidelines—including USDA NIFA, Hatch, Hatch Multistate, and Smith-Lever requirements—ensuring strict allowability and allocability.</li><li>Oversee and strengthen internal controls, including segregation of duties and documentation standards, to ensure audit readiness and mitigate compliance risk&nbsp;</li><li>Address identified control weaknesses, monitor audit findings, and implement long-term solutions to prevent recurrence.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong><br>Stakeholder Engagement &amp; Advisory<br></strong><br></div><ul><li><br>Communicate intricate policy requirements, financial decisions, and process changes into clear, user-friendly guidance for faculty, staff, and non-financial partners.</li><li>Serve as a diplomatic liaison between central university offices, CORE Support, and academic departments to streamline financial operations and resolve escalated concerns.</li><li>Represent the financial operations unit in campus-wide meetings, championing collaborative solutions for system and workflow enhancements<br><br></li></ul><div>If you are a financial professional who enjoys leading a team, solving complex problems, and helping others navigate challenging financial and regulatory requirements, this role offers an opportunity to make a meaningful impact.. If you are a financial professional who takes pride in leading a team, solving complex problems, and providing thoughtful guidance, we invite you to apply.</div> 110305 - CALS Business Office https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230347 2026-05-07T13:02:26-04:00 2026-05-07T13:02:28-04:00 Crew Leader I - 44637 <div>Join our team as a <strong>Crew Leader I</strong>, where you will lead impactful projects that help maintain the appearance, safety, and functionality of our campus. In this role, you will guide a dedicated team in completing essential work such as power washing entrances, window cleaning, floor care (including waxing), and other facility upkeep tasks that directly support the campus community.</div><div><br><strong>As a Crew Leader, you will coordinate daily operations, prioritize assignments, and ensure work is completed efficiently and to high standards. </strong>You will also play a key role in troubleshooting maintenance issues, supporting your team, and providing excellent customer service to campus partners.<br><br></div><div>This is a great opportunity to step into a leadership role, make a visible impact, and grow your skills in a collaborative and service-focused environment. If you are ready to lead a team and take pride in maintaining a clean and welcoming campus, we encourage you to apply.</div><div><br><strong>Working Hours: 8:00 a.m. to 4:30 p.m., Monday - Friday</strong></div><div><br></div><div><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li><strong>Lead projects</strong>, including power washing, window washing, floor waxing, and re-lamping fixtures and dispensers</li><li><strong>Inspect and verify maintenance discrepancies and ensure timely resolution</strong></li><li>Provide support to other zones as needed, filling in for vacant positions or absenteeism</li><li>Collect and submit timesheets within established timelines</li><li>Perform critical duties during adverse weather or emergencies as assigned</li><li>Complete accident reports within 24 hours of an incident</li><li><strong>Assist with equipment inventory and maintain a monthly inventory of storage closet supplies</strong></li><li>Submit supply orders for manager approval to ensure adequate stock levels</li><li>Train new employees, ensuring they understand tasks and safety protocols</li><li><strong>Transport employees to various locations for assignments</strong></li><li>Collaborate with maintenance staff inside and outside the unit to resolve issues efficiently</li><li>Address and resolve customer requests via radio communications, work orders, and direct interactions</li><li>Ability to lift a minimum of 60 pounds continuously and 90 pounds occasionally with or without reasonable accommodations, climb and balance on a ladder up to 8ft, and distinguish between colors.</li><li>Perform physical tasks such as grasping, pushing, and pulling cleaning equipment; reaching overhead; bending, squatting, climbing, and maintaining balance; and communicate effectively with supervisors and the public.</li></ul> 445080 - Environmental Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232551 2026-07-08T12:12:20-04:00 2026-07-08T12:12:21-04:00 Executive Catering Chef <div>NC State Dining is seeking a visionary, high-energy <strong>Executive Catering Chef</strong> to steer our thriving $4M catering operation and oversee retail food production at the bustling Talley Student Union. In this dynamic administrative and culinary leadership role, you will balance creative artistry with operational excellence—crafting everything from daily retail favorites to high-profile, VIP events like the Chancellor’s dinners.<br><br></div><div>If you are a culinary professional who thrives on mentoring a talented kitchen team, staying ahead of food trends, and delivering unforgettable guest experiences, we want you to lead our kitchen!</div><div><strong><br>What You’ll Do</strong></div><div><strong><br>Culinary Artistry &amp; Innovation</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Set the Standard:</strong> Develop, test, and standardize innovative menus and recipes that incorporate modern food trends and elevate the campus dining experience.</li><li><strong>Flawless Execution:</strong> Oversee high-volume production while maintaining fine-dining execution, technical precision (\"just-in-time\" production), and visually stunning plating for VIP events.</li><li><strong>Tech-Driven Kitchen:</strong> Utilize industry software (NetMenu) to manage recipes, forecast accurately, and streamline production schedules.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Kitchen Leadership &amp; Operations</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Inspire the Team:</strong> Direct, schedule, and mentor a talented Back-of-House (BOH) team, including the unit chef, sous chefs, and cooks.</li><li><strong>Smart Financial Management:</strong> Manage labor and food costs to hit financial targets, overseeing inventory, procurement, and budget planning without sacrificing quality.</li><li><strong>Grow Talent:</strong> Lead the hiring, onboarding, and continuous professional development of kitchen staff, building a collaborative, growth-oriented workplace culture.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Gold-Standard Safety &amp; Customer Service</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Maintain Excellence:</strong> Ensure the kitchen consistently achieves a Grade A sanitation rating (95% or better) in alignment with ServSafe and Wake County Health Department guidelines.</li><li><strong>Client-Centric Focus:</strong> Partner closely with Front-of-House (FOH) teams, nutritionists, and campus partners to customize events, resolve issues quickly, and deliver exceptional customer service.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Who You Are</strong></div><ul><li><strong>A Dual-Threat Chef:</strong> You possess a proven track record blending large-scale catering production with elite, fine-dining execution.</li><li><strong>A People Developer:</strong> You love to coach, train, and build high-performing, safe, and positive kitchen environments.</li><li><strong>An Organized Leader:</strong> You can seamlessly pivot from the chopping block to administrative tasks, managing budgets, schedules, and cross-departmental communication with ease.</li></ul> 471501 - Dining Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233579 2026-08-05T16:04:43-04:00 2026-08-05T16:04:44-04:00 Community Access Clinical Veterinarian <div>Are you passionate about breaking down barriers to veterinary care? We are seeking a dedicated <strong>Clinical Veterinarian</strong> to lead our mobile outreach initiative. This role is designed for a clinician who thrives on variety, enjoys community engagement, and is committed to providing high-quality primary care to underserved populations.</div><div><strong><br>The Mission<br></strong><br></div><div>As part of our Community Access team, you will bring the clinic to the client. Operating out of a <strong>state-of-the-art mobile unit</strong>, you will travel to regional areas where veterinary services are limited, ensuring that every pet—regardless of geography—has access to essential medical care.</div><div><strong><br>Key Responsibilities<br></strong><br></div><ul><li><strong>Primary Care Delivery:</strong> Provide comprehensive wellness exams, vaccinations, and parasite prevention (anthelmintics and heartworm) for small animals, primarily dogs and cats.</li><li><strong>Clinical Management:</strong> Diagnose and treat patients presenting with a wide range of acute and chronic clinical illnesses.</li><li><strong>Advanced Diagnostics:</strong> Utilize on-site technology to guide medical decisions, including <strong>point-of-care bloodwork</strong>, <strong>ultrasonography</strong>, and <strong>digital radiography</strong>.</li><li><strong>Community Engagement:</strong> Act as a face of the organization in regional underserved areas, building trust with pet owners and providing education on preventative health.</li></ul><div><strong><br>What We’re Looking For<br></strong><br></div><ul><li><strong>Adaptability:</strong> Comfortable working in a mobile environment and traveling to different regional locations.</li><li><strong>Clinical Proficiency:</strong> Strong skills in internal medicine, preventative care, and diagnostic interpretation.</li><li><strong>Compassion:</strong> A genuine desire to serve varied communities and improve the lives of pets and their people.</li><li><strong>DVM/VMD Degree:</strong> Must be licensed (or eligible for licensure) in the state of practice.</li></ul> 192001 - CVM Teaching Hospital https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223320 2025-09-25T11:56:47-04:00 2025-09-25T11:56:49-04:00 Lecturer - Applied Music Lecturers <div>The Performing Arts and Technology department at North Carolina State University seeks applications for applied music lecturers in all instrument areas to provide individual instruction for students who register for applied lessons. Responsibilities include teaching 13 weekly, 45-minute lessons per student and participation in the end–of–semester juried exams. This position is part-time; the time commitment and compensation are determined by the number of assigned students.<br><br><strong>Please note: This position is based on need and availability. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis. Candidates should specify which instruments they are qualified to provide instruction for.</strong></div> 248401 - Performing Arts and Technology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234333 2026-08-28T15:22:46-04:00 2026-08-28T15:22:47-04:00 TRIO Upward Bound Tutor <div><strong>The Division of Academic &amp; Student Affairs</strong> (DASA) is where students discover connections and community. It is where students live, where they gather, where they improve themselves, each other, and their university. DASA is where students seek guidance and support, both in and outside of the classroom. The division serves all students at NC State.</div><div><br></div><div>NC State wants employees to achieve their highest potential at work, learn more about our employee value proposition here (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>You Belong Here</strong></a>). The Division of Academic and Student Affairs is the place to work for values-driven faculty and staff who are passionate about promoting the success of the whole student.</div><div><br></div><div><strong>TRIO Programs</strong> were established to support the nation’s commitment to provide educational opportunity for all Americans to enter college, graduate, and move on to participate more fully in realizing “America’s Economic and Social Dream.\"<br><br>The primary purpose of this position is to assist high school students in the TRIO Upward Bound Program in academic skill building for core curricula courses especially rigorous courses.<br><br>Posting will be open until positions are filled.</div> 240405 - TRIO Pre-College https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234323 2026-08-28T13:05:33-04:00 2026-08-28T13:05:34-04:00 Student Success Specialist <div><strong>Student/ Customer Service (30%)</strong></div><ul><li>Develop and maintain effective instructional planning relationships with fellow full- and part-time staff members, as well as professional, productive interactions with students enrolled in the Elevate program.</li></ul><div><strong>Information/ Records Administration (20%)</strong></div><ul><li>Apply in-depth knowledge of student level data sources and additional measures of student performance to improve services and overall program effectiveness</li></ul><div><strong>Decision Making (20%)</strong></div><ul><li>Analyze and synthesize multiple sources of data in the context of Program and University policies and procedures, along with a philosophy of inclusion and fostering self-determination and apply solutions to student supports</li></ul><div><strong>Program Management/ Program Improvement (15%)</strong></div><ul><li>Engage in reciprocal mentoring with other full- and part-time staff to improve overall program delivery in support of student success</li></ul><div><strong>Coordination (15%)</strong></div><ul><li>Communicate and coordinate across a large service delivery team, adhering to agreed upon procedures to achieve program outcomes</li></ul> 131103 - IPSE https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228668 2026-03-20T14:01:38-04:00 2026-08-26T09:43:24-04:00 Lecturer or Assistant Teaching Professor in Philosophy <div>The Department of Philosophy and Religious Studies at NC State University invites applications for potential short-term teaching positions in Philosophy during the 2026-27 academic year.&nbsp;<br><br>These positions may involve teaching one or more courses for either a single semester or the full academic year, depending on departmental needs. Courses that may require instructors include Introduction to Philosophy, Contemporary Moral Issues, Issues in Business Ethics, Data Ethics, Biomedical Ethics, Logic, Metaphysics, Epistemology, Philosophy of Science, and other Philosophy (PHI) courses listed in the NC State course catalog.&nbsp;<br><br>Teaching assignments may be for in-person or online distance education courses; however, local residency is required for both. We welcome applications from qualified candidates and will review submissions as teaching needs arise.</div> 160501 - Philosophy & Religious Studies https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234709 2026-09-04T10:45:54-04:00 2026-09-04T10:45:54-04:00 Lecturer of Data Science <div>Join a collaborative community of instructors and help learners across and beyond NC State apply data science and AI in meaningful, real-world ways.</div><div><br></div><div>The Data Science and AI Academy offers courses grounded in our <a href=\"https://datascienceacademy.ncsu.edu/the-adapt-education-model/\">ADAPT</a> (All Campus Data Science and AI Project-based Teaching and Learning) course model. We seek instructors from industry, government and academia to teach permanent and special topics undergraduate courses at the 200 - 295 level (no prerequisites), 400 - 495 level (skills-based prerequisites) and graduate level courses at the 500 - 595 level.</div><div><br></div><div>As an instructor with The Data Science and AI Academy,you will also join a professional teaching community that meets twice monthly to discuss data science and AI teaching and learning research and practice.</div><div><br></div><div>The DSA occasionally offers not-for-credit customized courses through the Data and AI at Work program. We also seek applicants to teach these professional, customized and continuing education courses. For more information see example course topics here: https://datascienceacademy.ncsu.edu/courses/data-at-work/.</div><div><br></div><div>The for-credit courses meet for 50 minutes once a week for a semester and can be delivered in person or online. Data and AI at Work (not-for-credit) courses have a customized schedule.</div><div><br></div><div>For-Credit Course Offerings:</div><ul><li>Introduction to R/Python for Data Science,</li><li>Introduction to Data Visualization,</li><li>Data Communication,</li><li>Introduction to AI Ethics,</li><li>Data Science for Social Good,</li><li>Introduction to Data Science for Cybersecurity</li><li>Measuring Success</li><li>Data Wrangling and Web Scraping,</li><li>Exploratory Data Analysis for Big Data,</li><li>Data Internships Preparation for Social Impact,</li><li>Exploring Machine Learning,</li><li>Predictive Analytics for Improving Services,<br><br></li></ul><div><br></div><div><em>Appointments will be for one or two semesters with the possibility of renewal. Note that this position is only for teaching a few (often just 1) credits, not full-time.</em></div><div><br></div><div>If you are passionate about data science and enjoy helping others build practical skills, we invite you to bring your expertise and perspective to the Data Science and AI Academy.</div> 231001 - Data Science and AI Academy https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231487 2026-06-09T15:27:49-04:00 2026-06-09T15:27:50-04:00 Specialty Service Anesthesia Technician <div>The Anesthesia Department provides advanced anesthetic support to a varied patient population across the hospital campus, including small, large, and exotic species. While this role primarily operates within the Nephrology/Urology and Internal Medicine clinical areas, the successful candidate must be adaptable and willing to float to other service areas throughout the hospital complex as needed.</div><div><br>This position requires advanced veterinary medical knowledge—specifically including previous experience in a canine and feline veterinary setting—strong time-management skills, and the ability to thrive and practice good judgment independently in a fast-paced, teaching-focused clinical environment. The position collaborates closely with specialty services, clinicians, staff, and students to coordinate and deliver high-quality anesthetic and perioperative care.</div><div><br>Responsibilities include comprehensive patient assessment, administration of complex anesthetic and medical treatments, continuous monitoring during diagnostic and surgical procedures, post-operative recovery, and strict adherence to infectious disease and safety protocols. This role also supports hospital operations through electronic documentation, workflow coordination, and a strong desire to actively train, mentor, and teach learners, including fourth-year veterinary students.</div><div><strong><br>Key Responsibilities:</strong></div><div><strong>Advanced Patient Care &amp; Anesthesia Support</strong></div><ul><li>Provide comprehensive anesthetic and peri-anesthetic care, including pre-anesthetic assessments, high-risk drug calculations, advanced monitoring, life-saving interventions, pain management, and post-operative recovery for small, large, and exotic species.</li><li>Deliver independent, high-quality care for all patients, utilizing sound clinical judgment and requesting assistance from senior medical staff when appropriate.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Monitoring, Equipment &amp; Technical Proficiency</strong></div><ul><li>Operate, interpret, and maintain advanced anesthetic and physiologic monitoring equipment and specialized patient care devices to ensure patient safety during surgical, diagnostic, and imaging procedures.</li><li>Demonstrate strong technical proficiency and the ability to work effectively with computers, electronic medical records, and clinical electronics.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Interdisciplinary Collaboration &amp; Customer Service</strong></div><ul><li>Coordinate care and communicate professionally and effectively at all times with specialty services, clinicians, staff, and students across multiple hospital service areas to support complex medical and surgical cases.</li><li>Provide exceptional customer service and maintain strict professionalism and emotional regulation in charged, high-stress, or challenging clinical situations.</li><li>Work effectively as a collaborative member of the veterinary team, serving as an active participant in collective problem-solving and the continuous advancement of the department.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Education, Training &amp; Mentorship</strong></div><ul><li>Orient, teach, and coach new staff, students, interns, and residents on anesthetic procedures, hospital policies, and patient care standards in a teaching-hospital environment.</li><li>Demonstrate a dedicated desire to teach, guide, and evaluate fourth-year veterinary students during their clinical rotations.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Documentation, Safety &amp; Operational Compliance</strong></div><ul><li>Accurately document patient care, vitals, and treatments within hospital information systems.</li><li>Maintain a clean, safe, and organized work environment, strictly adhering to infectious disease protocols, OSHA, EHS, and radiation safety standards.</li><li>Safely work with or around sick animals, potentially hazardous materials, cleaning chemicals, waste anesthetic gases, zoonotic diseases, and unpleasant odors.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Physical &amp; Schedule Requirements</strong></div><ul><li>Ability to frequently lift and position patients or equipment weighing up to 50 pounds, with or without reasonable accommodations.</li><li>Flexibility and willingness to work beyond regularly scheduled hours when patient care needs or emergency cases dictate.</li></ul> 192001 - CVM Teaching Hospital https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233133 2026-07-24T10:39:12-04:00 2026-07-24T10:39:13-04:00 Temporary Research <div>A research technician is needed to assist with&nbsp; work on pharmacokinetic and population pharmacokinetic modeling in food animal drug avoidance research.&nbsp;</div> 196001 - Department of Population Health and Pathobiology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/220659 2025-07-02T13:55:12-04:00 2025-07-02T13:55:14-04:00 Linux Systems Administrator <div>We are seeking a dedicated and passionate <strong>Linux Systems Administrator</strong> to help support the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering's academic courses and cutting-edge research initiatives. This role is ideal for someone with deep expertise in Linux server administration and software installation, who thrives in a collaborative research computing environment.<br><br></div><div>As part of our team, you'll play a vital role in advancing research and innovation through the creation of a robust and efficient IT infrastructure. We offer the opportunity to work on varied and meaningful projects, providing excellent benefits, a supportive work culture, and ongoing opportunities for professional growth and development.<br><strong><br>Job Responsibilities include but are not limited to:</strong></div><ul><li>Manage and maintain approximately 100 Dell / Lenovo / HP servers running Red Hat Enterprise Linux and Ubuntu Linux in our server room, ensuring their stability, security, and performance.</li><li>Install, configure, and maintain commercial and open source software on our NFS network file shares, ensuring accessibility for users via our Module implementation.</li><li>Utilize container technologies such as Docker and Apptainer to provide alternative methods of software deployment to address research and course needs.</li><li>Work with the campus Puppet and Ansible management systems to automate server configuration and deployment tasks, as well as baseline settings to meet security standards such as CIS or NIST.</li><li>Educate users on the usage of conda, PIP, and Jupyter notebooks to manage Python environments and libraries for our growing AI/ML needs within our courses and research.</li><li>Provide technical support to end-users regarding Linux-related issues, troubleshooting hardware and software problems, and resolving technical incidents in a timely manner.</li><li>Create and maintain various new departmental Linux services – for example, a slurm cluster, JuptyerHub pilot, and Open OnDemand based remote access farms – to keep ECE’s research capabilities relevant.</li><li>Collaborate with ECE researchers and students to provide support for their usage of the campus High-Performance Computing (HPC) service; install ECE specific software within the cluster when appropriate. Coordinate this effort with the reorganized campus Office of Research Computing to streamline ECE usage of central resources.</li><li>Assist Linux Desktop Support position with the support of Linux workstations as needed.</li></ul> 140401 - Electrical & Computer Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234004 2026-08-19T14:57:03-04:00 2026-08-19T14:57:03-04:00 UTS - Temporary Proctor at NC State <div>The primary purpose of this role includes but is not limited to: The Center is currently seeking a part-time employee to serve as a proctor for NC State academic exams. Proctors are responsible for ensuring exam integrity and providing superior customer service in a friendly, technology-enhanced environment. Individuals will be expected to be flexible both in hours and day-to-day operations.</div> 480501 - UHR Temporary Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232896 2026-07-17T15:32:21-04:00 2026-07-17T15:32:22-04:00 Executive Director of Gift Planning <div>Reporting to the Assistant Vice Chancellor for University Development, the Executive Director for Gift Planning serves as the university’s senior leader for gift planning, providing strategic direction, oversight, and execution of a comprehensive, campus-wide program. The Executive Director partners across Advancement to integrate gift planning into fundraising strategy, supports complex and deferred gifts, and ensures alignment with institutional priorities and industry best practices.</div> 082001 - University Development https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233873 2026-08-14T15:39:40-04:00 2026-08-14T15:39:41-04:00 UTS - Temporary Landscape Maintenance Technician at NC State <div><strong>Landscape &amp; Turf Operations:</strong> Expertly operate mowers, blowers, weed eaters, hedge trimmers, chainsaws, edgers, and utility vehicles to deliver high-quality mowing, trimming, edging, and general grounds care.</div><div><strong>Plant Care &amp; Grounds Prep:</strong> Prepare planting beds and install varied flora—including annuals, perennials, bulbs, sod, trees, and shrubs—bringing landscape designs to life under the guidance of the Field Lead.</div><div><strong>Turf Management:</strong> Assist in turf establishment and restoration through professional sod installation and seeding techniques.</div><div><strong>Equipment Care &amp; Maintenance:</strong> Perform routine preventive maintenance on tools and machinery, ensuring all equipment remains safe, clean, and fully operational while promptly flagging repair needs.</div><div><strong>Site Sanitation &amp; Cleanup:</strong> Maintain immaculate work sites, vehicles, and campus grounds through diligent leaf, debris, and waste removal.</div><div><strong>Safety &amp; Incident Reporting:</strong> Champion a culture of safety by strictly following safety protocols and proactively reporting any hazards, accidents, or equipment damage.</div><div><strong>Teamwork &amp; Campus Engagement:</strong> Collaborate effectively with crew members and represent the department with clear, courteous, and professional communication across the campus community.</div> 480501 - UHR Temporary Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/225715 2025-12-11T14:08:52-05:00 2025-12-11T14:08:52-05:00 Temporary NCFIL Intern - Kannapolis, NC <div>Assist in product development of plant-based beverages, meat substitutes, bakery and confections; scale up and product commercialization of both food &amp;amp; beverage ingredients and finished products; develop standard operating procedures, research current and future processing techniques, perform quality testing and shelf life studies on food samples, assist in ordering and stocking ingredients, generate and fill out batch records, assist in pilot plant trials, regularly clean and sanitize pilot plant equipment, develop product specifications for food samples, participate in client visits; assist in photography and videography sessions, adhere to cGMP.</div> 110309 - NC Food Innovation Lab https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234421 2026-08-31T15:32:23-04:00 2026-08-31T15:32:24-04:00 Business Officer <div>Reporting to the Director for Student Leadership &amp; Engagement, you will support the financial, administrative, human resources, and operational success of the department. In this highly collaborative role, you will help ensure Student Leadership &amp; Engagement has the resources, processes, and operational support needed to advance student engagement and leadership development initiatives.<br><br></div><div>Working across finance, human resources, office operations, and campus partnerships, you will partner with professional staff, student employees, university departments, and campus stakeholders to maintain efficient operations and provide the administrative infrastructure needed to support the department’s programs and services.<br><br></div><div><strong><br>Key Responsibilities fall within the following functional areas:<br></strong><br></div><div><strong><br>Financial &amp; Business Operations<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Manage budget and accounting records for Student Leadership &amp; Engagement, supporting an annual budget in excess of $1.5 million.</li><li>Coordinate financial reconciliation and the distribution of appropriations funding to student organizations each semester, totaling nearly $200,000 annually, as approved by Student Government, the Student Body Treasurer, and their advisors.</li><li>Oversee monthly financial reconciliation, including PCard management, office projects, accounting activities, and cash assets.</li><li>Manage PCard purchases, Marketplace orders, vouchers, and other purchasing activities in accordance with University policies and procedures.</li><li>Prepare and submit quarterly financial reports to the division finance office.</li><li>Manage departmental deposits and maintain accurate financial records.</li><li>Communicate financial plans, spending guidelines, and budget strategies to the Student Leadership &amp; Engagement team.</li><li>Assist staff with contract and vendor processes, ensuring appropriate procedures are followed.</li><li>Provide ongoing financial training and guidance to professional staff on University purchasing, travel, student employment, and budget planning.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong><br>Human Resources &amp; Employee Coordination<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Coordinate required clearances for department staff and student employees.</li><li>Maintain accurate department rosters and Google Groups to support effective communication and access.</li><li>Support onboarding and offboarding processes for student and professional staff.</li><li>Assist with student employee hiring processes, including the annual PEP application process.</li><li>Communicate HR timelines, requirements, and processes to supervisors.</li><li>Coordinate with appropriate University partners to support timely completion of employment-related processes.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong><br>Office Operations &amp; Administrative Coordination<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Serve as the primary departmental requester for work orders and coordinate facilities-related needs.</li><li>Assist with developing and maintaining office operations, procedures, and administrative policies.</li><li>Support departmental workflows, documentation, and internal reporting processes.</li><li>Manage department storage facilities and ensure resources are organized and maintained.</li><li>Serve as the department surplus manager, coordinating the disposition of surplus property in accordance with University procedures.</li><li>Monitor and maintain office supply inventories to support daily operations.</li><li>Coordinate facility and operational needs with campus partners, including NC Student Centers and DASA Facilities.<br><br></li></ul><div><br>This role is an opportunity to contribute to a collaborative team while helping ensure Student Leadership &amp; Engagement has the financial and operational foundation needed to support students and advance departmental priorities.<br><br></div><div>If you are an organized, detail-oriented professional who enjoys supporting people, managing resources, and improving administrative processes, we encourage you to apply.<br><br></div><div><br><br></div> 240501 - Student Leadership & Engagement https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/229495 2026-04-14T10:46:51-04:00 2026-04-14T10:46:52-04:00 Theriogenology Fellow <div>The Department of Clinical Sciences, College of Veterinary Medicine, North Carolina State University is seeking applicants for a fellowship position in Comparative Theriogenology to begin <strong>July 20, 2026.</strong><br><br>The Theriogenology Fellowship at North Carolina State University is an advanced training program designed for a veterinarian who has already completed a residency in Comparative Theriogenology. Candidates must be either <strong>Board Certified by the American College of Theriogenologists (ACT) or board-eligible</strong>.<br><br><strong><br>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong><br>The primary objective of this fellowship is to refine clinical expertise, contribute to peer-reviewed literature, and support the ongoing academic mission of the university. This fellowship is clinically focused, with a primary emphasis on equine and canine species, complemented by experience in bovine and small ruminant species. The fellowship offers an environment for further professional development, scholarly activity, and specialized clinical practice.<br><br></div><ul><li><strong>Clinical Service:</strong> The Fellow will manage complex hospital reproductive cases and reproductive emergencies within both the equine and small animal hospitals.</li><li><strong>Equine Management:</strong> Clinical duties include overseeing the breeding management, embryo transfer and assisted reproductive techniques (ARTs) of mares and stallions housed at the Theriogenology clinic at the Reedy Creek Farm in Raleigh, NC.</li><li><strong>Teaching:</strong> The Fellow will play an integral role in the didactic and clinical instruction of veterinary students, interns, and residents providing mentorship and teaching from a board-eligible/certified perspective.</li><li><strong>Scholarly Activity:</strong> The Fellow is invited to engage in advanced research under the mentorship of our faculty.</li><li><strong>Faculty Collaboration:</strong> Our team includes 2 full-time faculty members, as well as faculty with board certifications in the American College of Veterinary Internal Medicine (ACVIM) and the American College of Veterinary Surgeons (ACVS), with specific interests in neonatology, urology, and related fields.</li></ul> 195001 - Department of Clinical Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232032 2026-06-23T14:31:38-04:00 2026-06-23T14:31:39-04:00 UTS- Temporary Patient Accounts Representative at UNC Chapel Hill <div>The Patient Accounts Representative is responsible for front-line, patient-facing duties within the Office. This position handles incoming phone calls, assists walk-in patients, processes mail and faxes, and supports patients with billing and account inquiries related to both dental and medical services. The role requires professionalism, attention to detail, and strong communication skills to ensure a positive and efficient patient experience.<br><br><strong>Key Responsibilities</strong></div><ul><li>Greet and assist patients and visitors in a courteous and professional manner</li><li>Answer incoming calls and direct inquiries to the appropriate department or team member</li><li>Respond to patient billing questions, explain statements, and provide basic account information</li><li>Accept and process patient payments; provide receipts and documentation as needed</li><li>Handle incoming and outgoing mail, including scanning, sorting, and distributing faxes</li><li>Log and track patient inquiries, correspondence, and documentation in accordance with office procedures</li><li>Collaborate with billing, clinical, and administrative staff to resolve patient account issues</li><li>Maintain confidentiality of patient information in compliance with HIPAA and institutional policies</li><li>Ensure desk and waiting area remain clean, organized, and professional</li></ul> 480502 - UHR UTS/UNC Partnership https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233409 2026-07-31T16:45:30-04:00 2026-07-31T16:45:31-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>The Department of Entomology and Plant Pathology at NC State University is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Research Scholar to conduct research focused on the isolation, characterization, genomics, and virulence of <em>Orientia</em> spp. bacteria associated with free-living chiggers.<br><br></div><div>The successful candidate will work as part of a collaborative research team investigating <em>Orientia</em> spp. through a combination of bacterial culture, cell-based assays, next-generation sequencing, and genomic bioinformatics. The position will involve laboratory research conducted in a BSL-3 facility, as well as computational analysis of bacterial genomes.<br><br></div><div>Collaborating researchers will collect wild-caught, free-living chiggers in the United States and provide specimens for laboratory analysis. The Postdoctoral Research Scholar will contribute to the culture and characterization of <em>Orientia</em> spp., genomic sequencing and analysis, assessment of bacterial virulence, and preparation of research findings for publication and presentation.</div><div><strong><br>Key Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Culture <em>Orientia</em> spp. bacteria from specimens collected by collaborating researchers and establish and maintain pure bacterial cultures.</li><li>Conduct laboratory studies to characterize <em>Orientia</em> spp. and assess bacterial virulence using appropriate human or animal cell lines.</li><li>Perform bacterial culture and related laboratory research in a BSL-3 facility in accordance with established biosafety and laboratory protocols.</li><li>Prepare purified bacterial DNA for next-generation sequencing and coordinate genomic sequencing with external sequencing service providers.</li><li>Use probe-directed genome sequencing approaches, as appropriate, to obtain genomic data from <em>Orientia</em> spp. present in crude bacterial mixtures.</li><li>Assemble, annotate, and analyze <em>Orientia</em> spp. genome sequences using bioinformatic approaches.</li><li>Compare newly generated genome sequences with known <em>Orientia</em> spp. genomes to determine species identity and characterize genetic relationships.</li><li>Conduct genomic analyses to investigate genetic factors associated with bacterial virulence and other relevant biological characteristics.</li><li>Integrate laboratory and genomic findings to support research objectives.</li><li>Prepare manuscripts, abstracts, presentations, and other scholarly products for dissemination of research findings.</li><li>Present research findings at scientific meetings and conferences.</li><li>Contribute to collaborative research activities, grant proposals, and other scholarly activities as appropriate.</li><li>Maintain accurate records of laboratory experiments, genomic analyses, and research findings.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Other Duties and Responsibilities:<br><br>1. Culture and characterize Orientia spp. bacteria – 50%</strong></div><div>Culture <em>Orientia</em> spp. bacteria from wild-caught, free-living chiggers collected in the United States by collaborating researchers. Establish and maintain pure cultures of <em>Orientia</em> spp. bacteria and conduct laboratory studies to characterize bacterial growth and virulence. Perform bacterial culture and related experimental work in a BSL-3 laboratory using established biosafety protocols. Culture <em>Orientia</em> spp. in appropriate animal cell lines and assess the virulence and biological characteristics of isolated bacterial cultures using human or other animal cell lines. Chiggers will be collected and provided by collaborating researchers in culture media and will not be provided as live specimens.</div><div><strong>2. Obtain genomic sequence data for Orientia spp. – 10%</strong></div><div>Obtain genome sequence data from <em>Orientia</em> spp. bacteria using next-generation sequencing approaches. Prepare purified DNA obtained from bacterial cultures for submission to an external DNA sequencing service. As an alternative approach, collaborate with private sequencing service providers to use probe-directed genome sequencing to obtain genomic data from <em>Orientia</em> spp. present in crude bacterial mixtures derived from free-living chiggers.</div><div><strong>3. Assemble and analyze Orientia spp. genomes – 30%</strong></div><div>Assemble and analyze genomic sequence data from <em>Orientia</em> spp. bacteria. Compare newly sequenced genomes with publicly available genomes from known <em>Orientia</em> spp. to determine species identity and characterize genetic relationships. Use genomic and bioinformatic analyses to identify genetic factors associated with bacterial virulence and other relevant biological characteristics. Interpret and integrate genomic findings with laboratory data to support research objectives and prepare results for publication in scientific journals.</div><div><strong>4. Research communication and scholarly activities – 10%</strong></div><div>Analyze, interpret, and document research findings; prepare manuscripts, abstracts, presentations, and other scholarly materials; contribute to research proposals and grant applications as appropriate; and present research findings at scientific meetings and conferences. Maintain accurate records of experimental procedures, results, and genomic analyses and contribute to collaborative research activities with internal and external research partners.</div> 111801 - Entomology and Plant Pathology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223258 2025-09-25T11:08:49-04:00 2025-09-25T11:08:50-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>NC State Department of Civil, Construction, and Environmental Engineering (CCEE), seeks a postdoctoral researcher in the transportation area related to human-Autonomous Vehicle (AV) interactions. For questions regarding the position please contact Professor Danjue Chen (dchen33@ncsu.edu).<br><br></div><div>The postdoc training and mentoring will be in:<br><br></div><div>1. Helping to advance research in human-automation interaction.</div><div>2. Helping to establish in the professional community.</div><div>3. Helping to get familiar with writing proposals.</div><div>4. Helping &amp; supervising in graduate &amp; undergraduate students advising.<br><br>The research project will study how human users (as a driver or AV user) interact with AVs on the road to investigate a set of problems like (1) how human drivers decide whether to cooperate with AVs (e.g., to yield or not) and (2) how AV users decide whether to accept the cooperation from the algorithmic design? We are interested in learning the influential factors in such human-AV interactions, the decision-making process from the cognitive and/or behavioral economics perspective, and how to develop strategies to promote human-AV cooperation (e.g., via improved communication design, or nudging).<br><br></div><div>We expect to utilize a simulator network consisting of multiple connected driving simulators (i.e., with embedded connectivity).<br><br></div><div>Responsibilities include (1) designing and implementing research experiments, (2) conducting data analysis, and (3) paper writing.</div> 140301 - Civil Construction & Environmental Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234124 2026-08-25T10:36:40-04:00 2026-08-25T10:36:40-04:00 IT Technical Support Analyst <div>The <strong>IT Technical Support Analyst</strong>plays a critical role in delivering advanced IT desktop support and technology solutions to the College of Veterinary Medicine’s faculty, staff, and students. This position requires strong technical expertise, excellent problem-solving abilities, and a high level of customer service in a fast-paced academic and clinical environment. <br><br>As a Tier 2 support position within CVM IT, this role is responsible for resolving complex technical issues, supporting enterprise endpoint management, assisting with infrastructure and technology projects, and serving as an escalation point for Tier 1 staff and student workers. In addition to desktop support responsibilities, the IT Technical Support Analyst assists with Audio/Visual (A/V) support, classroom technologies, and other specialized technology initiatives across the college and veterinary hospital. <br><br><strong>Key Responsibilities</strong>:<strong><br>Advanced IT Desktop Support:</strong></div><ul><li>Provide advanced hands-on and remote technical support for faculty, staff, and students, troubleshooting complex hardware, software, operating system, and network-related issues.</li><li>Serve as an escalation point for Tier 1 support staff and student IT workers, assisting with advanced troubleshooting and issue resolution.</li><li>Deploy, configure, maintain, and troubleshoot Windows and Mac OS devices, printers, mobile devices, and specialized peripherals.</li><li>Utilize enterprise management tools such as SCCM, Jamf, Active Directory, and Group Policy to manage endpoint configurations, software deployments, updates, and security compliance.</li><li>Diagnose and resolve complex endpoint management, authentication, profile, software, and connectivity issues.</li><li>Support and maintain university-supported software, departmental applications, and specialized veterinary and research technologies.</li><li>Document technical issues, resolutions, procedures, and knowledge base articles within the IT ticketing system (ServiceNow).</li><li>Participate in endpoint lifecycle management processes, including imaging, deployments, refresh programs, surplus processing, and asset tracking.</li><li>Collaborate with Systems Administration, Clinical Informatics Support (CIS), Application Development, OIT, vendors, and other stakeholders to resolve technical issues and implement solutions.</li><li>Assist with technology planning, testing, standardization, and implementation of new hardware and software solutions.</li><li>Provide mentorship, guidance, and technical training to Tier 1 staff and student workers.</li><li>Serve as a technical lead for desktop-related networking and telephony support, including troubleshooting endpoint connectivity issues, coordinating network jack activations and moves, supporting VoIP phone deployments and relocations, assisting with switch and wireless troubleshooting, and working closely with NC State OIT/ComTech and vendors to resolve infrastructure-related issues affecting CVM users.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Audio/Visual (A/V) and Technology Project Support:</strong></div><ul><li>Assist with the setup, maintenance, troubleshooting, and support of A/V systems in classrooms, conference rooms, teaching spaces, and event areas.</li><li>Support the A/V Coordinator in implementing classroom technology upgrades, conference room enhancements, and other A/V projects.</li><li>Provide technical assistance for meetings, presentations, live events, and video conferencing systems as needed.</li><li>Assist with diagnosing and resolving classroom and conference room technology issues to minimize disruption to teaching and meetings.</li><li>Participate in technology deployment projects, office moves, Windows upgrades, hardware refresh initiatives, and other departmental IT projects.</li></ul> 190104 - CVM - Dean's Office https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/227960 2026-03-02T15:11:44-05:00 2026-03-02T15:11:44-05:00 Temporary Camp Cook - Ellerbe, NC <div>Assist in the preparation of meals for summer camp. Interact with camp leadership in providing a positive experience for participants.<br><br><strong>Work Schedule:<br></strong>Spring March 27. 2026 – May 25, 2026 as needed. During summer camp Sunday – Friday. Approximately, May 31 – August 7. Hours may vary from week to week.</div> 112413 - Millstone 4-h Camp https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234635 2026-09-03T12:03:37-04:00 2026-09-03T12:03:38-04:00 Assistant Professor <div>The Department of Psychology is seeking an Assistant Professor to advance research, teaching, and service in applied social psychology. This position requires the establishment of an independent research program with demonstrated or potential for external funding, dissemination of scholarship through scholarly outlets, teaching and mentorship of undergraduate and graduate students, and service to the Applied Social Psychology program area, department, college, university, and broader field.<br><br></div><div>As an Assistant Professor, you will conduct a productive program of research that contributes meaningful scholarship to the discipline and strengthens the department’s research profile. You will teach and mentor undergraduate and graduate students, helping them develop as scholars and professionals, while contributing your expertise to the continued growth of the program and the broader discipline.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Develop and sustain an independent, externally funded research program.</li><li>Disseminate research through scholarly publications, presentations, and other professional outlets.</li><li>Teach courses that support undergraduate and graduate education in psychology.</li><li>Mentor undergraduate and graduate students in their academics, research, and professional development.</li><li>Contribute service and professional leadership within the Applied Social Psychology program area, department, college, university, and broader field.<br><br></li></ul><div>If you are an emerging scholar in applied social psychology who is excited to build a meaningful research program, engage students through teaching and mentorship, and contribute to a collaborative academic community, we encourage you to apply.</div> 164501 - Psychology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230867 2026-05-22T11:10:13-04:00 2026-05-22T11:10:14-04:00 Pharmacy Technician <div>The <strong>Pharmacy Technician</strong> plays a critical role in delivering high-quality pharmaceutical care within our Veterinary Hospital (VH). This position ensures the safety and accuracy of medication therapy for a varied range of animal species while supporting the hospital’s clinical and educational missions.<br><br><strong>Key Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Assisting with prescription processing and compounding</li><li>Providing pharmaceutical care for outside prescriptions</li><li>Keeping pharmaceuticals stocked in various protected areas of the hospital for in-house treatment of patients</li><li>Serving as a preceptor for pharmacy technology students rotating from the Wake Technical Community College program</li></ul><div><br>If you are a detail-oriented professional passionate about advancing veterinary medicine and mentoring the next generation of technicians, we invite you to bring your expertise to our team and help us provide world-class care for our patients.</div> 192001 - CVM Teaching Hospital https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234037 2026-08-20T18:45:31-04:00 2026-08-20T18:45:32-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>This position will support an ongoing project with the NCDOT to evaluate road ripples and the position may generally involve supporting other short duration experimental mechanical evaluations of asphalt concrete mixtures. The position will also provide some laboratory and project management support.<br><br><strong>The main duties and responsibilities include:</strong></div><ul><li>Conduct and synthesize literature and agency-practice reviews related to road ripples, including potential formation mechanisms, diagnostic methods, contributing factors, and pavement-performance implications.</li><li>Coordinate and participate in field and forensic evaluations of North Carolina pavements exhibiting road ripples, including site selection, records and data compilation, field testing, sample collection, laboratory evaluation, and comparative analysis.</li><li>Plan, conduct, and analyze laboratory and modeling studies to evaluate residual moisture in asphalt mixtures and the potential short- and long-term effects of road ripples on pavement performance.</li><li>Draft quarterly reports and monthly reports to support the aforementioned research project and/or similar project.</li><li>May be asked to participate in other research projects related to aforementioned topic as projects are awarded.</li><li>Coordinate laboratory management activities in support of the asphalt mixture analysis group tasks. This may include coordinating graduate students for cleaning, ensuring PPE is appropriately worn, ensuring supplies are ordered, and coordinating on calibrations.</li></ul> 140301 - Civil Construction & Environmental Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/227250 2026-02-09T13:53:04-05:00 2026-02-09T13:53:05-05:00 Lecturer, Economics <div>The Department of Economics invites applications for part-time, non-tenure-track Lecturer positions. These appointments are typically for one semester or one academic year, with the possibility of renewal based on departmental needs and performance.<br><br></div><div>Lecturers may be asked to teach one to three courses per semester at the undergraduate and/or graduate level. Instructional needs may include courses in microeconomics, macroeconomics, labor economics, and/or law and economics.<br><br></div><div>Specific teaching needs for the upcoming academic year will be determined as departmental scheduling and enrollment demands are finalized.</div><div><br><strong>Applications will remain on file for one year should openings arise in subsequent semesters.</strong></div> 203001 - Economics - Poole College of Management https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234536 2026-09-02T12:29:38-04:00 2026-09-02T12:29:39-04:00 Senior AI and Applications Developer <div>As a member of the EAS Development team, this position will serve as a Mendix Sr. Enterprise Developer, as well as leading AI development and deployment initiatives within EAS. <br><br><strong>Mendix Development:</strong></div><ul><li>Lead the design, build and/or implementation of practical, cost effective business solutions.</li><li>Complete detailed analysis and solutions development, regression testing, performance testing and quality assurance testing of Mendix applications.</li><li>Develop and maintain technical documentation.</li><li>Assist customers with defining requirements, implementing solutions and testing.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>AI Development and Deployment:</strong></div><ul><li>Evaluate emerging AI technologies and platforms to identify viable solutions for EAS and the broader NC State community; author and formalize a comprehensive strategic plan for embedding AI across software development, deployment, and support life cycles.</li><li>Represent EAS/OIT on collaborative campus IT committees and working groups, aligning AI initiatives and practices across university departments.</li><li>Provide guidance to other team members, across all EAS teams on the proper implementation of AI tools, workflow and security guidelines.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Technical Solutions Development</strong></div><ul><li>Interpret complex business processes and technological functions and provide input to management and customers on the appropriate resource and technology for automated solutions.</li><li>Ensure that application development efforts align closely with business requirements, from design to delivery, and that applications are user friendly, scalable, secure, and can be efficiently maintained once deployed.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Planning and Organizing</strong></div><ul><li>Maintain a strong knowledge of the SDLC at NC State University combined with outstanding organization and communication skills.</li><li>Maintain an understanding of software development project management methodologies and tools (Microsoft Project, QuickBase, SmartSheet, etc) and a thorough understanding of project expectations and procedures.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Technical Support</strong></div><ul><li>Act as a subject matter expert and direct advisor to senior OIT management regarding AI capabilities, risk management, and resource allocation.</li><li>Apply knowledge and skills to troubleshoot complex problems and provide timely resolution.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Customer Service</strong></div><ul><li>Promote positive customer relationships to ensure customer satisfaction.</li><li>Establish a positive and proactive relationship by demonstrating ownership and knowledge of customer business processes and issues.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Technical Solutions Development</strong></div><ul><li>Interpret complex business processes and technological functions and provide input to management and customers on the appropriate resource and technology for automated solutions.</li><li>Ensure that application development efforts align closely with business requirements, from design to delivery, and that applications are user friendly, scalable, secure, and can be efficiently maintained once deployed.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Planning and Organizing</strong></div><ul><li>Maintain a strong knowledge of the SDLC at NC State University combined with outstanding organization and communication skills.</li><li>Maintain an understanding of software development project management methodologies and tools (Microsoft Project, QuickBase, SmartSheet, etc) and a thorough understanding of project expectations and procedures.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Technical Support</strong></div><ul><li>Act as a subject matter expert and direct advisor to senior OIT management regarding AI capabilities, risk management, and resource allocation.</li><li>Apply knowledge and skills to troubleshoot complex problems and provide timely resolution.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Customer Service</strong></div><ul><li>Promote positive customer relationships to ensure customer satisfaction.</li><li>Establish a positive and proactive relationship by demonstrating ownership and knowledge of customer business processes and issues.</li></ul> 515001 - Enterprise Application Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230459 2026-05-11T10:04:08-04:00 2026-05-11T10:04:11-04:00 Crew Leader I - 46995 <div>Join our dynamic Environmental Services team as a <strong>Crew Leader I</strong>, where you’ll take the lead on essential projects like power washing entrances, window washing, waxing floors, and more. In this hands-on leadership role, you’ll guide a dedicated team while helping maintain clean, safe, and welcoming campus spaces.<br><br></div><div><strong>You’ll coordinate daily operations, address maintenance needs, and deliver high-quality service that directly impacts the campus community. </strong>This is a great opportunity for someone who enjoys leading by example, takes pride in their work, and thrives in a fast-paced, team-oriented environment.<br><br></div><div>If you’re ready to step into a leadership role and make a visible impact every day, we encourage you to apply today and be part of a team that keeps our campus running at its best.</div><div><br></div><div><strong>Working Hours: Monday-Friday, 8:00 AM to 4:30 PM</strong></div><div><br></div><div><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li>Lead projects, including power washing, window washing, floor waxing, and re-lamping fixtures and dispensers</li><li>Inspect and verify maintenance discrepancies and ensure timely resolution</li><li>Provide support to other zones as needed, filling in for vacant positions or absenteeism</li><li>Collect and submit timesheets within established timelines</li><li>Perform critical duties during adverse weather or emergencies as assigned</li><li>Complete accident reports within 24 hours of an incident</li><li>Assist with equipment inventory and maintain a monthly inventory of storage closet supplies</li><li>Submit supply orders for manager approval to ensure adequate stock levels</li><li>Train new employees, ensuring they understand tasks and safety protocols</li><li>Transport employees to various locations for assignments</li><li>Collaborate with maintenance staff inside and outside the unit to resolve issues efficiently</li><li>Address and resolve customer requests via radio communications, work orders, and direct interactions</li><li>Ability to lift a minimum of 60 pounds continuously and 90 pounds occasionally with or without reasonable accommodations</li><li>Communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, and the general public, and follow verbal and written instructions related to work assignments and operational needs.</li></ul> 445080 - Environmental Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232616 2026-07-10T08:49:25-04:00 2026-07-10T08:49:27-04:00 Environmental Services Technician I <div>Are you looking for a fulfilling career where you can make a tangible difference in creating a clean and welcoming environment? We are seeking a passionate and detail-oriented Environmental Services Technician to join our exceptional Environmental Services team at NC State’s Facilities Division. In this vital role, you will help maintain over 10 million square feet of campus space, ensuring a safe and inviting atmosphere for students, staff, and visitors. With the support of a dynamic team of over 320 professionals, you’ll have the opportunity to grow your skills and take pride in your work while contributing to our vibrant campus community. Join us and be a part of a team that takes pride in delivering exceptional service to one of the most prestigious campuses in the region. Apply today and help us keep NC State shining bright!</div><div><br></div><div><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li>Clean and sanitize designated areas using standard cleaning assignments to maintain a safe and hygienic environment</li><li>Report any discrepancies, damages, or maintenance needs within buildings and promptly respond to emergencies and inclement weather</li><li>Operate housekeeping equipment such as vacuums, mops, and scrubbers to complete tasks like dusting, disinfecting, and replenishing supplies</li><li>Periodically strip and refinish floors, shampoo and extract carpets, and perform pressure washing of entrances, exterior walls, and windows</li><li>Remove and manage recycling from centralized indoor recycling sites</li><li>Assist with window washing and other seasonal cleaning tasks as needed</li><li>Operate standard cleaning tools and equipment safely and effectively.</li><li>Perform physical tasks such as lifting (up to 30 lbs. frequently, 60 lbs. occasionally), bending, reaching, squatting, and climbing ladders with or without reasonable accommodations.</li><li>Adhere to safety procedures and maintain work areas according to departmental standards.</li><li>Communicate clearly and professionally with team members, supervisors, and the public.</li></ul> 447701 - Housekeeping Services - Region 2 https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233601 2026-08-06T09:55:49-04:00 2026-08-06T09:55:50-04:00 Business Officer <div>Reporting to the NC Agricultural Research Service, The Business Officer supports the administrative, financial, and operational success of the Mountain Horticultural Crops Research and Extension Center (MHCREC) and Mountain Horticultural Crops Research Station (MHCRS). In this highly collaborative role, you will help ensure research, extension, and outreach programs have the resources, facilities, and operational support needed to succeed.<br><br></div><div>Working across finance, operations, communications, facilities, and stakeholder engagement, you will partner with faculty, staff, students, university departments, and external stakeholders to keep the center running efficiently while advancing the mission of agricultural research and education.<br><br></div><div>Key Responsibilities fall within the following functional areas:&nbsp;</div><div><strong>Financial &amp; Business Operations:</strong></div><ul><li>Oversee financial resources from multiple funding sources while ensuring responsible stewardship and compliance with University and College policies.</li><li>Analyze expenditures, develop budgets, and provide financial recommendations that support operational and research priorities.</li><li>Direct procurement activities by cultivating vendor relationships, securing competitive pricing, and managing purchasing processes.</li><li>Administer research station use fees and monitor available resources to support ongoing operations.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Operations, Facilities &amp; Event Coordination:</strong></div><ul><li>Coordinate facility operations, repairs, and renovation projects to maintain a safe, functional, and productive research environment.</li><li>Supervise the Building Environmental Technician, providing direction, prioritizing work, and supporting daily facility operations.</li><li>Oversee scheduling and utilization of meeting spaces, events, and research center resources.</li><li>Support planning and execution of field days, outreach programs, and other center events.</li><li>Coordinate on-site graduate student housing and operational logistics.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Stakeholder Relations &amp; Communications:</strong></div><ul><li>Build collaborative relationships with faculty, staff, students, industry partners, commodity groups, and community stakeholders.</li><li>Represent the research center in interactions with internal and external partners while promoting its programs and accomplishments.</li><li>Develop and maintain departmental communications and online resources that support operational effectiveness and stakeholder engagement.</li><li>Evaluate information requests and provide guidance on departmental procedures, policies, and operations.</li><li>Identify opportunities to strengthen external partnerships through gifts, grants, and other support.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Employee Services &amp; Administrative Coordination:</strong></div><ul><li>Support employee onboarding, timekeeping, leave administration, and required safety training.</li><li>Coordinate administrative processes that contribute to an efficient and well-supported workplace.<br><br></li></ul><div>If you are an organized, relationship-focused professional who enjoys balancing strategic planning with day-to-day operations, we encourage you to apply.&nbsp;</div> 115004 - Mountain Horticultural Crops Research https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223328 2025-09-25T11:59:23-04:00 2025-09-25T11:59:24-04:00 Part-time Music Lecturer <div>The Department of Performing Arts and Technology at North Carolina State University seeks applications for part-time lecturers in the areas of music theory, aural skills, musicology, and ethnomusicology to provide semester-long instruction in a classroom setting. University classes typically meet 2-3 times weekly for 3 hours. Responsibilities include preparing and teaching courses while grading and mentoring students.</div><div><br><strong>Please note: This position is part-time and is based on need and availability. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis. Candidates should specify their academic area(s) of expertise.</strong></div> 248401 - Performing Arts and Technology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234335 2026-08-28T15:25:21-04:00 2026-08-28T15:25:22-04:00 UTS - Temporary Phlebotomist at NC State <div>We are seeking to hire a phlebotomist to perform phlebotomy, prep specimens, and other duties working in a medical laboratory.<br><br>The purpose of our department is to provide appropriate, affordable, accessible, and high-quality primary health care to eligible students, and provide a comprehensive disease-prevention and health promotion program in the NC State University community. The major function of the department is that of providing outpatient care to the 34,000+ NC State students. Some level of medical care is available 24 hours a day, 7-days a week during the academic year beginning in August and ending in May. Hours of service vary during the summer and during holidays and student breaks.&nbsp;<br><br></div> 480501 - UHR Temporary Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234328 2026-08-28T14:13:03-04:00 2026-08-28T14:13:04-04:00 Research Associate-Burclaff Lab <div>The Burclaff Lab focuses on understanding how metabolism and cellular energetics are regulated within intestinal stem cells, and how this dynamic regulation supports homeostasis, identity maintenance, fate decisions, cell cycle regulation, and tissue plasticity following injury or disease. We utilize a mix of animal models and cutting-edge, genetically modified, primary human intestinal stem cell cultures to advance our understanding of the mammalian intestine.</div><div><br>The <strong>Research Associate </strong>will advance research operations, understand project rationales, and execute experiments to test scientific hypotheses across mouse models, cell lines, and primary cell cultures. In this role, you will also assist with laboratory management, including inventory tracking, equipment oversight, and day-to-day operational organization.</div><div><br><strong>Core Duties and Technical Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Primary Cell Culture Management:</strong> Establish, propagate, and maintain mammalian primary cell cultures and established cell lines using aseptic techniques to support research projects.</li><li><strong>Molecular &amp; Biochemical Assays:</strong> Perform routine and advanced molecular biology and biochemical assays, including DNA/RNA/protein extraction, PCR, RT-qPCR, gel electrophoresis, tissue sectioning, immunofluorescence, immunohistochemistry staining, and live image analysis.</li><li><strong>Animal Model Care &amp; In Vivo Research:</strong> Oversee mouse colony operations—including handling, breeding, weaning, genotyping, and tissue collection—and execute experimental procedures in accordance with institutional guidelines.</li><li><strong>Laboratory Operations &amp; Instrumentation:</strong> Maintain, calibrate, and troubleshoot laboratory equipment, manage inventory and data logs, and ensure strict compliance with safety protocols and standard operating procedures.</li></ul><div><br></div><div>If you are a collaborative scientist who enjoys hands-on research, solving problems, and keeping complex laboratory operations running smoothly, we encourage you to apply.</div> 194001 - Department of Molecular Biomedical Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228708 2026-03-24T08:57:50-04:00 2026-03-24T08:57:51-04:00 Lecturer or Teaching Assistant Professor in Political Science <div>The School of Public and International Affairs, Department of Political Science, is accepting applications for the position of Professional Faculty to teach courses in American Politics at the undergraduate level.<br><br>Professional Faculty is responsible for coordinating, planning, preparing, presenting, and evaluating classroom instruction and related activities.<br><br>The selected individual will be hired as a Teaching Assistant Professor or Lecturer depending on qualifications.</div> 160601 - Public & International Affairs https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234711 2026-09-04T12:08:41-04:00 2026-09-04T12:08:41-04:00 Assistant Professor in Elementary Teacher Education <div>The Department of Teacher Education and Learning Sciences (TELS) invites applications for an Assistant Professor in Elementary Teacher Education to be affiliated with undergraduate and graduate studies in elementary teaching and learning. This is a tenure-track appointment at the Assistant Professor level, with teaching, scholarship, service, and outreach expectations.<br><br>We are seeking those who are scholars in elementary teacher education who will lead and contribute to research on approaches and programs that prepare pre-service teachers. You will engage in research primarily focused on elementary teacher preparation programs’ features and impact. This might include elements such as modality (e.g., face-to-face, hybrid, online), structure (e.g., traditional four-year programs, part-time programs for practicing teachers, competency-based education), or pathway (e.g., bachelors degree, masters degree, residency, registered apprenticeship). They will submit proposals for internal/external funding in support of the related research. <br><br>The ideal candidate will teach undergraduate and graduate courses in elementary teacher preparation, including the Master of Arts in Teaching (MAT) program, with a strong preference for those who teach elementary social studies education. As a faculty member,<strong><em> </em></strong>&nbsp;you will provide service and leadership to the Elementary Education program area, the department, college, and university. You will participate in outreach and engagement activities with elementary schools locally and across North Carolina, and collaborate with faculty within the department, college, and across the university.<strong><br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Position Responsibilities</strong></div><ul><li>Maintain a high level of scholarly research, publication, and grant-writing activity that aligns with a tenure track position in a university with high research activity.</li><li>Produce high quality research on pre-service teacher preparation programs, features, and impact.</li><li>Seek external funding for research and development activities.</li><li>Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in elementary education, with a strong preference for social studies pre-service teacher education graduate and undergraduate programs</li><li>Recruit, advise, and mentor graduate students and serve on doctoral committees.</li><li>Engage in collaborative research with other faculty and researchers within the College of Education, including the Friday Institute for Educational Innovation and the Belk Center for Community College leadership, as well as across the university.</li><li>Provide service and leadership in the elementary education program, department, college, university, and professional organizations.</li><li>Participate in outreach and engagement for department and college initiatives with underserved communities and high need areas and engaging with stakeholders across the state of North Carolina.<br><br></li></ul><div>If you are passionate about preparing future elementary educators and interested in teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in elementary teacher preparation, including the Master of Arts in Teaching (MAT) program, with expertise in elementary social studies education, we encourage you to apply and join our faculty.</div> 131101 - Teacher Education and Learning Sciences (TELS) https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231541 2026-06-10T11:22:06-04:00 2026-06-10T11:22:06-04:00 Barista Supervisor (Temp) <div><strong>NC State Dining</strong> is seeking enthusiastic individuals to join our Pack as a <strong>Temporary Barista Supervisor</strong>. Successful candidates may work in a dining hall, retail operation, food court, or restaurant setting.<br><br>Essential Job Duties:</div><ul><li>Provide exemplary customer service to every guest.</li><li>Prepare quality beverages, such as espresso-based beverages, shakes, and smoothies, and other drinks and food products consistently by adhering to all recipe and presentation standards while following all health, safety, and sanitation guidelines.</li><li>Accurately process transactions through the POS (point of sale) by following NC State Dining revenue handling procedures.</li><li>Perform necessary cleaning tasks in accordance with maintaining a Grade “A” Sanitation rating.</li><li>Assist in receiving and stocking store merchandise.</li><li>Assist with opening and closing duties of the unit as assigned by the supervisor.</li><li>Supervise and coordinate the activities of Baristas.</li><li>Other duties as assigned to ensure NC State Dining business needs are met. Position may be asked to assist in other locations as necessary.</li></ul><div><br><a href=\"https://dining.ncsu.edu/about/overview/\"><strong>NC State Dining</strong></a> provides campus-wide food services to students, staff, and faculty on the North Carolina State University Campus. NC State Dining operates 10 cafes, 3 food courts and 6 dining halls, 4 markets, 1 restaurant, vending, a nutritional counseling service, and a catering department. The department employs approximately 1000 temporary and student employees and approximately 190 permanent EPS and SHRA employees.<br><br>This is an hourly position that includes a State-required 32-day break at the end of 11 months. Superior work performance may provide an opportunity to renew employment and/or advance into a benefits-eligible permanent position.</div> 471501 - Dining Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233149 2026-07-24T14:13:55-04:00 2026-07-24T14:13:57-04:00 Advanced Locksmith Level 1 <div><strong>Advanced Locksmith Level 1</strong> position will provide highly skilled technical expertise, project management, and leadership for campus locksmith operations. This role is responsible for ensuring compliance with university standards, accurately scoping projects, and efficiently allocating resources.</div><div><strong><br>Key Responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Serve as a <strong>technical expert</strong>, guiding, training, and coaching journey and contributing level technicians on application and installation techniques.</li><li>Research critical information, including <strong>key bittings array, construction documents, and guidelines</strong>.</li><li>Understand and apply <strong>master key database enrollment process and procedures</strong>.</li><li>Demonstrate the ability to review and verify <strong>construction plans and hardware specifications</strong> according to established specifications and construction guidelines.</li><li>Proficiently work with <strong>hardware specifications</strong>.</li><li>Handle <strong>project management tasks</strong> with a working knowledge of processes and timelines.</li><li><strong>Provide cost estimates and proposals</strong> for work, which includes evaluating and researching necessary requirements from vendor sources.</li><li><strong>Monitor and track all work orders</strong> to ensure timely completion.</li><li>Assist the supervisor in <strong>developing timelines and milestones</strong> by identifying critical and less critical activities.</li><li><strong>Allocate appropriate resources and time</strong> for the completion of own work and that of the team, avoiding scheduling conflicts.</li><li>Communicate effectively with customers regarding the necessary <strong>scope of work</strong>.</li><li><strong>Guide&nbsp;</strong>Journey-level technicians, ensuring that both Journey and Contributing level technicians have sufficient resources to complete jobs.</li></ul> 445030 - Building Maintenance & Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/222688 2025-09-08T16:47:10-04:00 2025-09-08T16:47:11-04:00 Temporary Research Summer Intern 2026 - Mills River, NC <div>This position involves assisting faculty and staff to meet annual plant production goals across a variety of nursery crops <strong>.</strong> Interns will be exposed to a variety of projects and topics including plant breeding and improvement, propagation, tissue culture, and plant production. Specific duties include transplanting, maintenance of tissue culture projects, plot maintenance, flow cytometry, container production, data collection etc. Interns will also participate in independent research projects, field trips, and attend relevant conferences.<br><br></div> 111501 - Horticultural Science https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234661 2026-09-03T12:59:28-04:00 2026-09-03T12:59:29-04:00 Roofing Assistant <div>Are you looking to build a career in facilities maintenance, construction, or roofing? Join our team as a <strong>Roofing Assistant</strong> and play an important role in helping maintain, protect, and extend the life of campus buildings and infrastructure. In this hands-on position, you'll work alongside experienced roofing professionals to support roof maintenance, repairs, waterproofing projects, and related building maintenance activities that keep our facilities safe and operational.<br><br></div><div>This is an excellent opportunity for individuals who enjoy working outdoors, learning skilled trades, and developing valuable experience in roofing systems, waterproofing, facility maintenance, and construction support. You'll gain practical knowledge, work on a variety of projects across campus, and contribute to a team dedicated to providing high-quality service and craftsmanship.<br><br></div><div>​If you're ready to gain valuable hands-on experience, work alongside skilled professionals, and help maintain the facilities that support our campus community, <strong>we encourage you to apply today. Start building your future with a team that values safety, craftsmanship, professional growth, and service excellence.</strong></div><div><strong><br></strong><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li><strong>Perform preventive maintenance on campus roofing systems, including clearing debris from roof surfaces, drains, gutters, and scuppers to ensure proper drainage and system performance.</strong></li><li>Inspect roofs and identify deficiencies, damage, or maintenance concerns, documenting and communicating findings to supervisors.</li><li>Assist roofing technicians with roof repairs, waterproofing projects, material handling, equipment setup, and job site preparation.</li><li><strong>Support the installation, patching, and repair of various roofing systems using industry-approved methods and materials.</strong></li><li>Assist with waterproofing applications for buildings and below-grade structures to help prevent water intrusion and protect campus assets.</li><li>Set up scaffolding, ladders, safety equipment, tools, and materials while maintaining a safe and organized work environment.</li><li>Support related maintenance activities, including <strong>asphalt patching, caulking expansion joints, and installation of campus furnishings such as signs, benches, tables, and fencing.</strong></li><li>Follow established safety procedures and maintain a clean, efficient, and productive work site.</li><li>​Safely climb and work from different types of ladders to access elevated or below-grade areas (such as roofs, above-ceiling spaces, and utility pits). Be exposed to and work in areas where insects or small animals are occasionally present. All essential tasks must be performed with or without reasonable accommodations.</li><li>Perform lifting duties up to 30 pounds frequently and 60 pounds occasionally, with or without reasonable accommodations</li><li>Communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, and the general public, and follows verbal and written instructions related to work assignments and operational needs.</li></ul> 445030 - Building Maintenance & Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230954 2026-05-27T12:50:39-04:00 2026-05-27T12:50:41-04:00 Post Doctoral Research Scholar <div>The Department of Electrical Engineering (College of Engineering) and the College of Sciences (Department of Physics, Department of Mathematics) are searching for two (2) Postdoctoral Research Scholars in quantum computing at NC State University. The two positions will work synergistically on hybrid continuous-discrete-variable quantum computing, broadly defined. One position will focus on condensed matter physics, quantum simulation; the other position will focus on compilation, benchmark, and HPC simulation of quantum circuits.</div><div><br></div><div>The postdocs will work with a cluster of vibrant quantum computing faculty at NC State, including Dr. Bojko Bakalov, Professor (Mathematics), Dr. Dror Baron (Electrical and Computer Engineering), Lex Kemper (Physics), Yuan Liu (Electrical and Computer Engineering, Computer Science, and Physics), Frank Mueller (Computer Science), and Huiyang Zhou (Electrical and Computer Engineering), with possible external collaborations with other universities and national lab.<br><br><strong>Other Duties and Responsibilities</strong></div><ul><li>Explore relevant parameter regions for useful boson-fermi mixed quantum matter; Design, develop, and implement novel quantum algorithms to simulate them.</li><li>Quantify resource requirements on simulation on hybrid CD-DV quantum processors under noise, and compare to qubit-based quantum computers;</li><li>Explore compilation, benchmarking, HPC simulation, error mitigation, and error correction strategies on hybrid CV-DV quantum processors;</li><li>Collaborate with multidisciplinary teams to understand domain-specific requirements and integrate discoveries into the team's mission;</li><li>Help to lead the project and mentor junior researchers;</li><li>Publish research findings in reputable journals and conferences</li></ul> 140401 - Electrical & Computer Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232914 2026-07-17T17:39:50-04:00 2026-07-17T17:39:50-04:00 Extension Agent, Ag, Field Crops and Dairy (Assistant, Associate or Full Agent) <div>Are you looking for a rewarding career that will let you give back to your community?<br><br>NC State Extension extends research-based knowledge to all North Carolinians, helping them transform science into everyday solutions that improve their lives and grow our state. We are the model of excellence in non-formal, research-based education that advances agriculture, the environment, human health and well-being, youth and communities, while creating prosperity for all North Carolinians. Working for NC State Extension allows you to teach without being tied to a classroom and school bell. Check out this <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3MptxITpJ54&amp;t=3s\"><strong>video</strong></a> on what it’s like to work for NC State Extension.<br> <br> <strong>The position may be hired at the Assistant, Associate or Full Agent level if the selected candidate meets the required qualifications.</strong><br> <br> While our employees are located in NC County offices, they reap the benefits of employment at NC State University. Some of our benefits include:<br> <br> · Paid time off<br> <br> · Paid tuition<br> <br> · Health Insurance<br> <br> · Flexible Scheduling<br> <br> · Retirement Savings<br> <br> · Supplemental Benefits Programs<br> <br> · WolfPerks!<br> <br> You Belong Here! At NC State, our goal is for all employees to reach their fullest potential at work. As you consider this opportunity, we encourage you to review our <a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>Employee Value Proposition</strong></a> (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/</strong></a>) and learn more about what makes NC State the best place to learn and work for everyone.<br> <br> <strong>Primary Purpose of Position</strong><br> The agent in this position will provide leadership for field crops and dairy programs in Iredell County and proactively conduct need assessments and program evaluations to provide future direction for the program.<br><br> The County Extension Agent is a member of the faculty of North Carolina State University. The County Extension Agent has primary responsibility for planning, executing, and evaluating that phase of county Extension programs within assigned areas of program responsibility. This position provides leadership for the development, implementation and evaluation of an effective educational program to meet the needs of the county population in the following areas of responsibility: field crops (corn, small grains, cotton, and soybeans), pesticide education, and dairy producer education. This position will serve as the county pesticide coordinator and assist with educational training opportunities for private operators to obtain and maintain their licenses. The agent will be expected to develop specific educational programs and to collaborate with other Extension staff, agencies, groups, and organizations in order to more effectively reach intended audiences throughout the county.<br><br> North Carolina Cooperative Extension gives our residents easy access to the resources and expertise of NC State University and NC A&amp;T State University. Through educational programs, publications, and events, Cooperative Extension field faculty deliver unbiased, research-based information to North Carolina citizens. Iredell County is located in the central Piedmont region of North Carolina, just north of Charlotte and bordering Lake Norman, and has a population of approximately 206,000. Agriculture has a significant economic impact on the County, generating an estimated $662 million annually, including over $165 million in cash gate receipts from more than 1,055 farms. Iredell County is the leading county in the state for dairy production and ranks first in all cattle (both beef and dairy) and first in corn silage production, making it a hub for dairy and livestock operations in North Carolina. Field crops including soybeans, wheat, and corn are also widely grown throughout the county, supporting the local dairy and beef cattle industries as well as regional grain markets. Other agricultural commodities include hay (fifth in the state), poultry, cotton, viniferous grapes, strawberries, and nursery and ornamental plant production. With close proximity to Charlotte and Lake Norman, there is a large and growing suburban presence in the southern half of the county, creating unique challenges and opportunities for preserving working farmland alongside nearby quality of life amenities not usually found in a rural area.<br><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong><br>The person selected for this position will be responsible for educational programming and technical assistance related to all phases of field crop production and pesticide education. The person selected will also be expected to conduct on-farm research work in the areas of corn, soybeans, cotton, and wheat production. In addition, the person selected for this role will be responsible for educational programing and technical assistance related to dairy production, animal waste operator certifications, and youth dairy programing.</div> 117625 - South Central District - Iredell https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233890 2026-08-17T13:33:47-04:00 2026-08-17T13:33:49-04:00 Pastry Sous Chef <div>NC State Dining is seeking a passionate<strong> Pastry Sous Chef </strong>to join our team! The Pastry Sous Chef position will provide day-to-day oversight of the baking production staff for NC State Dining. This position works closely with the Executive Pastry Chef to provide a high-quality baking department. This position will be responsible for maintaining the highest culinary standards and works directly with the bakery staff to provide training, supervision, quality control, and excellent customer service.<br><br></div><div><strong>Responsibilities include but are not limited to:<br></strong><br></div><ul><li><strong>Operational Compliance</strong> — Train staff on NC State Dining guidelines, procedures, food safety, and sanitation standards.</li><li><strong>Event &amp; Cost Management</strong> — Coordinate and participate in special events for The Chancellor while supporting food and labor cost goals.</li><li><strong>Inventory &amp; Safety Oversight</strong> — Manage inventory, storage organization, food safety walkthroughs, and time/temperature controls across bakery service areas.</li><li><strong>Staffing &amp; Daily Operations</strong> — Ensure proper staffing for opening/closing, assist with shift transitions, and verify completion of temp logs and production forms.</li><li><strong>Cross‑Department Communication</strong> — Communicate operational culinary issues with the Executive Pastry Chef and coordinate services with campus partners for successful events</li></ul><div><br>If you take pride in your work, enjoy being part of a collaborative team, and thrive in a dynamic environment where professionalism, adaptability, and customer service matter, this is your opportunity to make a meaningful impact in the heart of campus life</div> 471511 - Dining Dining Halls https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/225817 2025-12-15T12:52:13-05:00 2025-12-15T12:52:14-05:00 Temporary Research Technician <div>Operate drone platforms to collect data for research projects, process and analysis the data in a timely manner, and explore potential applications to boost the efficiency of field management.<strong><br></strong><br></div> 118101 - Biological And Agricultural Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234438 2026-08-31T17:07:06-04:00 2026-08-31T17:07:07-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>The Abolhasani Research Group in the Department of Chemical &amp; Biomolecular Engineering at North Carolina State University is seeking a Postdoctoral Research Scholar to develop next-generation self-driving laboratories and autonomous experimentation technologies as part of the NSF-funded SPEED (Self-driving Platforms for Expedited Experimental co-Design) Programmable Cloud Laboratory.<br><br></div><div>The successful candidate will develop and deploy closed-loop experimental platforms that integrate automated synthesis and experimentation, real-time characterization, data analysis, and AI/ML-based experiment selection. The research will focus on creating modular, reliable, and user-accessible autonomous laboratories for accelerated discovery and optimization in chemistry and materials science.<br><br></div><div>Highly motivated candidates with experience in laboratory automation, autonomous experimentation, robotics, scientific instrumentation, process control, or related areas are encouraged to apply. Experience with Python-based automation and integration of laboratory hardware and analytical instruments is highly desirable.<br><br></div><div>The successful candidate will work in a highly interdisciplinary environment involving chemical engineers, chemists, materials scientists, computer scientists, AI/ML researchers, and external academic and industrial partners.<br><strong><br>Duties and Responsibilities include:</strong></div><ul><li>Design, develop, and deploy self-driving laboratory platforms for autonomous chemical and materials experimentation.</li><li>Integrate synthesis, sample handling, analytical characterization, and data-processing tools into automated experimental workflows.</li><li>Develop Python-based interfaces for laboratory hardware and scientific instruments.</li><li>Implement closed-loop workflows connecting experimental platforms with AI/ML experiment-selection algorithms.</li><li>Develop methods for automated calibration, fault detection, error recovery, and experimental quality control.</li><li>Establish standardized workflows and interfaces that allow experimental platforms to be used by external SPEED users.</li><li>Conduct autonomous and high-throughput experimental campaigns.</li><li>Analyze experimental data and evaluate the performance and robustness of autonomous workflows.</li><li>Collaborate with SPEED researchers in chemistry, materials science, AI/ML, data science, and automation.</li><li>Prepare manuscripts, presentations, technical documentation, and regular project reports.</li><li>Meet regularly with the PI and SPEED team to review progress, milestones, and future research plans.</li></ul> 140201 - Chemical & Biomolecular Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/229518 2026-04-14T15:18:03-04:00 2026-04-14T15:18:04-04:00 Open Rank Lecturer or Teaching Assistant Professor - History <div>The History Department is seeking applicants to teach as either a non-tenure track Lecturer or Teaching Assistant Professor, based on credentials and qualifications. In this role, you will help shape the student learning experience by planning, preparing, delivering, and evaluating instruction that fosters engagement, curiosity, and academic success.<br><br></div><div>You will have the opportunity to design and teach your own course syllabi, bringing your expertise, scholarly perspective, and teaching strengths into the classroom while aligning with program goals.</div><div>Teaching assignments may include day, evening, or weekend sections, depending on instructional needs, offering flexibility and variety in scheduling.<br><br></div><div>Appointments may be offered on a per-course, short-term, and/or part-time basis for the 2026–2027 academic year, with the possibility of renewal based on departmental needs and performance.<br><br></div><div>This is a pooled recruitment maintained to meet future instructional demand. All appointments are temporary and part-time, with opportunities that may arise throughout the 2026–2027 academic year.</div> 160301 - History https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232126 2026-06-25T12:39:03-04:00 2026-06-25T12:39:04-04:00 Lab Technician (Mishra) <div>Lab technician needed to assist with behavioral recordings and quantificaiton using deep lab cut, RNA iosloation and running PCR for gene Explanation of Duties expression.</div> 194001 - Department of Molecular Biomedical Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233420 2026-08-03T09:00:33-04:00 2026-08-03T09:00:33-04:00 UTS - Temporary CMA/RMA at NC State <div>The primary duties of this position include the following:</div><div><br></div><ul><li>Comply with legal and regulatory health care requirements related to HIPAA/FERPA, OSHA, AAAHC, Infection Control, CMA/RMA codes of conduct and NC State University Regulations and Campus Health protocols.</li><li>Perform patient intake and document chief complaint, vital signs, medication reconciliation and medication, material allergies or sensitivities in the electronic health record.</li><li>Administer medications and injections as ordered by medical providers and documented in the electronic health record. Assist providers performing office based medical procedures for GYN and Primary Care visits</li><li>Perform skills based tasks such as ekgs, ear lavage, applying DME, wound care Work with the GYN and Primary Care Nursing Supervisors on assignments such as chart reviews and other work group assignments. Complete unit based training with passing scores at hire, as needed, and annually if applicable</li><li>Attend monthly nursing, nurse provider, and all staff meetings. Stock and prepare medical clinics for direct patient care per unit protocols.</li></ul> 480501 - UHR Temporary Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223267 2025-09-25T11:14:52-04:00 2025-09-25T11:14:53-04:00 Assistant/Associate/Full Clinical or Tenure Track Professor of Radiation Oncology <div>The individual will be able to choose between either a <strong>TENURE TRACK or PROFESSIONAL/CLINICAL TRACK</strong> position, provided they meet the qualifications, with rank and tenure status determined based on experience level.<br><br>Both positions come with similar responsibilities: the incumbent will oversee radiation oncology clinical case management, provide high quality clinical and pre-clinical teaching, demonstrate research productivity through the procurement of external grant funding and the publication of peer-reviewed scientific papers, and participate in college and departmental committees.<br><br>The difference is that faculty in professional/clinical track positions spend a majority of their time in the clinic (34 weeks), with a small proportion of their time (5-10%) dedicated to research. By contrast, faculty in tenure track positions are expected to develop and/or run an independent research program, with a focus in clinical, translational, and/or basic research in the cancer and/or radiation-related sciences.<br><br>Many collaborative research opportunities exist within the NC State University Comparative Medicine Institute, as well as with neighboring institutions including UNC Chapel Hill, Duke University, and Wake Forest University.<br><br>Often, our faculty become eligible for associate membership in these NCI-designated Comprehensive Cancer Centers. Notably, the NC State Radiation Oncology team actively participates in a variety of clinical trials, and we are fortunate in that clinical research efforts are well-supported by our Clinical Studies Core, which provides assistance in study design, coordination and implementation.<br><br>In addition to clinical service and research, the successful candidate will be responsible for participation in professional outreach, committee service, teaching, and curriculum development. Faculty radiation oncologists at NC State participate in resident training, and they assist in teaching general oncology in the professional DVM curriculum, host an elective course in feline oncology, and support elective clinical rotations in radiation oncology for 4th year veterinary students. There are additional opportunities for mentoring graduate students.</div> 195001 - Department of Clinical Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234189 2026-08-26T10:19:04-04:00 2026-08-26T10:19:04-04:00 Extension Agent, Ag-Consumer and Commercial Horticulture <div>Are you looking for a rewarding career that will let you give back to your community? NC State Extension extends research-based knowledge to all North Carolinians, helping them transform science into everyday solutions that improve their lives and grow our state. We are the model of excellence in non-formal, research-based education that advances agriculture, the environment, human health and well-being, youth and communities, while creating prosperity for all North Carolinians. Working for NC StateExtension allows you to teach without being tied to a classroom and school bell.</div><div><br>You Belong Here! At NC State, our goal is for all employees to reach their fullest potential at work. As you consider this opportunity, we encourage you to review our <a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>Employee Value Proposition</strong></a> (https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/) and learn more about what makes NC State the best place to learn and work for everyone.</div><div><br>While our employees are located in NC County offices, they reap the benefits of employment at NC State University. Some of our benefits include:</div><div>· Paid time off</div><div>· Paid tuition</div><div>· Health Insurance</div><div>· Flexible Scheduling</div><div>· Retirement Savings</div><div>· Supplemental Benefits Programs</div><div>· WolfPerks!<br><br>The agent in this position will have educational responsibilities in both home and consumer horticulture and commercial horticulture program areas in Haywood County. The successful candidate must be able to conduct educational programs focused on home and consumer horticulture for homeowners, including (but not limited to) best management practices needed in caring for lawns, gardens, and landscapes. The successful candidate must also be able to conduct educational programs focused on the profitability and sustainability of ornamental and nursery plant production, utilizing best practices and encouraging the adoption of proven technology, business, and financial practices. The successful candidate will also be responsible for training and supporting the Master Gardener program in Haywood County.<br><br><strong>Primary Function of Organizational Unit<br></strong>North Carolina Cooperative Extension gives our residents easy access to the resources and expertise of NC State University and NC A&amp;T State University. Through educational programs, publications, and events, Cooperative Extension field faculty deliver unbiased, research based information to North Carolina citizens.</div><div><br></div> 117107 - West District - Haywood https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228067 2026-03-04T14:43:39-05:00 2026-03-04T14:43:41-05:00 Floor Technician <div>We are seeking a dedicated and detail-oriented <strong>Floor Technician</strong> to join our team at NC State’s Facilities Division. In this role, you will play a key part in maintaining the cleanliness and appearance of over 10 million square feet of campus space. <strong>You will utilize specialized cleaning equipment and techniques to ensure a safe and welcoming environment for students, staff, and visitors.</strong> If you take pride in delivering high-quality floor care and enjoy working in a dynamic and collaborative team setting, this is the perfect opportunity for you!</div><div><br><strong>Working Hours: 8:00 AM – 4::30 PM – Monday – Friday<br><br></strong><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li><strong>Strip and refinish hard surface floors, extract and clean carpets, and conduct routine maintenance using equipment such as burnishers, auto scrubbers, and carpet extractors.</strong></li><li>Complete assigned cleaning tasks, including vacuuming, mopping, shower scrubbing, and floor finish application, to maintain designated areas.</li><li><strong>Adhere to established safety procedures</strong> for handling cleaning products and operating equipment. Wear appropriate <strong>Personal Protective Equipment (PPE)</strong> and post caution signage during cleaning activities.</li><li><strong>Identify and report building discrepancies</strong>, safety hazards, and equipment malfunctions to the manager promptly.</li><li>Support cleaning and maintenance needs during emergencies and inclement weather, ensuring the safety and functionality of campus spaces.</li><li>Accurately complete incident reports, <strong>interpret Safety Data Sheets (SDS)</strong>and apply knowledge of safety protocols in daily operations.</li><li>Lift, carry, and maneuver materials weighing 60–90 pounds, with or without reasonable accommodations.</li><li>Communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, and the general public, and follows verbal and written instructions related to work assignments and operational needs.</li></ul> 445080 - Environmental Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233189 2026-07-27T14:44:22-04:00 2026-07-27T14:44:23-04:00 House Director <div>The <strong>Division of Academic &amp; Student Affairs</strong> (DASA) is where students discover connections and community. It is where students live, where they gather, where they improve themselves, each other, and their university. DASA is where students seek guidance and support, both in and outside of the classroom. The division serves all students at NC State through three integrated components: academic affairs, student affairs, and University College.</div><div><br></div><div>NC State wants employees to achieve their highest potential at work, learn more about our employee value proposition here (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>You Belong Here</strong></a>). The Division of Academic and Student Affairs is the place to work for values-driven faculty and staff who are passionate about promoting the success of the whole student.</div><div><br>The <strong>Department of Fraternity and Sorority Life</strong> advises recognized fraternities and sororities, their governing councils, and manages Greek Village housing. The Department of Fraternity and Sorority Life prepares students for society through a values-based fraternal experience. Our work impacts the student experience in three ways: Chapter Services, Council and Community Development, and Living and Learning Environments. Fraternity and Sorority Life also provides several programs to enhance leadership development and to educate the Greek Community of over 2,800 students.<br><br>The House Director will coordinate the student development and administrative functions of an Organization-Owned Fraternity or Sorority House and the community within. <br><br>This position will be directly responsible for the following:<br><br><strong>Advising:</strong></div><ul><li>Create a sense of community by promoting peer accountability among chapter residents.</li><li>Provide training on university policies reinforcing community standards.</li><li>Maintain a high level of visibility within the residential community for the purpose of serving as an on-site advisor to chapter residents, including but not limited to regular office hours, chapter programming, and chapter meals.</li><li>Meet regularly with organization leadership, advisors, and House Corporation volunteers.</li><li>Advise student residents on the development and implementation of programs, applicable policies and procedures, risk management, issues management, leadership development and organization management.</li><li>Serve as a mediator to assist residents in addressing community conflicts when they arise.</li><li>Maintain an environment conducive to student academic success and safety.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Leadership:</strong></div><ul><li>Provide leadership, administrative operations, programming, and facilities management in a residential house composed of about 40 residents with an additional 100 out of house student members.</li><li>Make informed decisions based on a clear understanding of the impact and consequences involved.</li><li>Create and maintaining an open community within the house.</li><li>Serve as a communication link between Fraternity and Sorority Life, House Corporation, and residents.</li><li>Exercise good judgment and remaining unbiased and impartial while addressing house issues.</li><li>Participate in department decision-making regarding policies, procedures, programs, and facilities management.</li><li>Oversee daily operations including facilities, overall resident management, occupancy management, financial budget management to ensure a positive, healthy and safe living-learning environment for our residents.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Policy Enforcement:</strong></div><ul><li>Respond appropriately to student concerns and addressing student issues.</li><li>Follow all University Policies, Regulations, and Rules, as well as other University/Fraternity and Sorority Life Procedures, and Guidelines including but not limited to those pertaining to safety, civility, information security, and non-discrimination.</li><li>Consulate with the Associate Director to refer cases to the Organizational Standards Board, Department of Fraternity and Sorority Life, CARES, Bias Incident Response or Behavior Assessment Teams, or the Office of Student Conduct.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Crisis Management and On-Call Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Make appropriate referrals to University support services ie, Student Health, Counseling Services, Behavior Assessment Team, and/or Student Ombuds.</li><li>Respond to emergencies and providing leadership in crisis situations, collaborating with campus partners when appropriate.</li><li>Must be onsite overnight except when prior arrangements have been made for time away.</li><li>Demonstrate ability to work effectively under pressure.</li><li>Practice effective communication with University administration, Organizational Leadership, and, if applicable, law enforcement or emergency personnel.</li><li>Serve as a resource or referral agent for students experiencing personal concerns or crisis.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Administration:</strong></div><ul><li>Coordinate fall and spring student check-in and check-out system.</li><li>Coordinate and direct the opening and closing of the house, as well as occupancy management of the facility through StarRez.</li><li>Manage room assignment and room change process for the facility.</li><li>Initiate change, developing, and implementing new ideas and concepts as appropriate.</li><li>Participate in departmental and division programs, meetings, and committees as needed.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Facilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Coordinate inspections as required by the University, state, federal, or local agencies.</li><li>Tour building and grounds on a regular basis to identify facility needs.</li><li>Solicit building improvement ideas from staff and residents to share with appropriate organizational leadership and House Corporation. Working in conjunction with Fraternity and Sorority Life staff and Organizational Leadership to ensure the community is well maintained and a safe environment for residents.</li><li>Work with vendors and maintenance workers to promote compliance with written agreements between the chapter and vendor; report issues of concern regarding outsourced services to the House Corporation.</li><li>Assist the House Corporation representative in locating local vendors and contractors as needed and requested by the House Corporation.</li><li>Work with organization leadership and meal plan vendor to establish menus for the chapter on a weekly and monthly basis. Consult with University Nutritionist as appropriate.</li><li>Work with a meal vendor or catering service (when applicable) to promote compliance with written agreement between the organization and the service provider; report issues of concern regarding meal vendor or catering service to the House Corporation.</li><li>Communicate periodic feedback regarding vendor provided services to the House Corporation Board and/or service vendor liaisons.</li></ul> 242301 - Fraternity and Sorority Life https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/222813 2025-09-11T15:55:26-04:00 2025-09-11T15:55:26-04:00 Temporary Research Technician <div>Measuring soil responses to conservation agricultural management with physical, chemical, and biological techniques.</div> 110901 - Crop and Soil Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234041 2026-08-21T09:37:45-04:00 2026-08-21T09:37:47-04:00 Transportation Supervisor <div>Are you a logistical mastermind who loves the fast-paced world of catering and events? We are seeking a dynamic and organized <strong>Catering Driver Supervisor</strong> to lead our transportation and beverage/equipment teams. In this role, you will be the backbone of our event execution, ensuring that all catering equipment, beverages, and supplies are delivered safely, efficiently, and on schedule.<br><br></div><div>Beyond driving, you will manage our vehicle fleet, oversee inventory, and lead a dedicated team to deliver outstanding service across the University. If you are a proactive leader with a knack for logistics and a commitment to excellence, we want you on our team!<br><strong><br>Key Responsibilities<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Team Leadership &amp; Scheduling</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Supervise &amp; Support:</strong> Lead, motivate, and manage the catering transportation and beverage/equipment teams.</li><li><strong>Staff Scheduling:</strong> Utilize specialized departmental software to create, distribute, and monitor employee schedules.</li><li><strong>Clear Communication:</strong> Foster a collaborative team environment and maintain professional communication with multiple levels of University employees, peers, and customers.</li></ul><div><strong>Logistics, Driving &amp; Event Execution</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Delivery Operations:</strong> Develop, distribute, and monitor the daily delivery and transportation schedules.</li><li><strong>Behind the Wheel:</strong> Drive various delivery trucks and vehicles to execute catering and dining deliveries safely.</li><li><strong>Event Support:</strong> Load and unload cargo at each location; actively assist with event setup and breakdown.</li><li><strong>Fast-Paced Adaptability:</strong> Thrive and maintain professionalism under pressure in a high-energy, fast-moving environment.</li></ul><div><strong>Fleet Management &amp; Safety</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Fleet Care:</strong> Ensure a well-maintained, clean, and safe fleet of catering and dining vehicles.</li><li><strong>Training &amp; Records:</strong> Establish and maintain a driver training program and a comprehensive fleet service record-keeping system.</li><li><strong>Preventative Maintenance:</strong> Oversee routine inspections, fueling, and preventative maintenance schedules in strict accordance with the <strong>NCSU Motor Pool</strong> guidelines.</li></ul><div><strong>Inventory &amp; Supply Control</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Inventory Management:</strong> Oversee inventory control, receipt, storage, and issuance of a wide variety of catering supplies, materials, and equipment.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Physical Stamina:</strong> Ability to handle physical demands including bending, twisting, pulling, pushing, and standing for extended periods.</div><div><strong>Lifting Capability:</strong> Must be able to lift 30 pounds above the head, and up to 60 pounds on rare occasions (with or without reasonable accommodation).<br><br></div><div>We offer a collaborative, team-oriented workspace where your leadership directly impacts the success of major University events. If you are ready to drive your career forward, apply today!</div> 471501 - Dining Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/227270 2026-02-09T21:01:16-05:00 2026-02-09T21:01:16-05:00 Temporary Summer Camp Nurse - Reidsville, NC <div>Betsy-Jeff Penn 4-H Educational Center is seeking licensed RNs to join our team for the 2026 Summer Camp season! The Camp Nurse serves as a vital member of the Camp Healthcare Team, and oversees all health care operations for approximately 120 residential campers each week. The ideal candidate will enjoy working with youth of all ages, have the ability to work in unpredictable outdoor conditions, and thrive in a fast-paced dynamic environment outside of the clinical setting. Due to the on-call nature of this position, applicants must be able to reside on-site (housing provided) or within a half-hour commute of camp for the duration of the summer season. If you want to apply your nursing skills to a residential camp setting and work with a team like no other, apply today!</div><div><br><strong>Primary Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Ability to follow all health care policies/procedures as described in facility standing orders, treatment procedures, and Staff Manual</li><li>On call to respond to medical emergencies at all times campers are on-site, including overnight</li><li>Perform basic assessment and first aid for conditions including but not limited to: minor soft tissue and musculoskeletal injuries, insect bites/stings, dehydration, sunburns, general illness; providing follow-up checks as needed</li><li>Facilitate proper medication administration, for both routine prescription and over the counter medications, during all administration periods throughout the day</li><li>Maintain written medical records for all medications and treatments administered according to state and American Camp Association Standards</li><li>Prepare and record insurance and accident report forms</li><li>Oversee Sunday check-in and Friday check-out of all camper medications each week, discussing any medical concerns with parent/guardian as needed</li><li>Review all camper health forms and communicate medical needs to counselors as necessary</li><li>Manage a team of 1-2 Health Care Coordinators who may assist with primary and secondary responsibilities</li><li>Prepare and distribute camper’s medication schedule list to all counselors before dinner on Sundays<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Secondary Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Attend all relevant staff trainings</li><li>Keep camp Infirmary and medical supply storage space clean and organized</li><li>Prepare and distribute all campus first aid kits, conducting routine checks and restocks as necessary</li><li>Perform medical supply/equipment inventory and place restock orders<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Work Schedule:<br>The summer season runs from May 29th - August 7th, 2026. </strong>Workdays will begin at noon on Sunday of each week and end at noon on Friday. These positions demand a high level of responsibility and attention to detail. You will be on call 24/7 including overnight, meal times and during activities to ensure overall health of campers and staff. Lodging (7 days a week, if needed) and Meals (5 days a week) provided.&nbsp;<strong>Potential to modify to a part-time role based on applicant’s needs and experience, please contact us directly to discuss options.<br><br>Child discount for camp is negotiable</strong></div> 112412 - Betsy-Jeff Penn 4-H Camp https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234544 2026-09-02T13:16:18-04:00 2026-09-02T13:16:19-04:00 Production Assistant - Temp <div>Serve as camera operator to work live broadcasts and in-house productions for NC State University Athletics (football, basketball, baseball, soccer, etc.) in Raleigh, NC. Experience with live camera work in sports is highly preferable but not necessary. Other production positions (Graphics, Replay, TD, etc.) are a possibility too based on experience. Help during set up and tear down before and after games is expected.</div> 105002 - Athletics - Internal/External Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230493 2026-05-11T15:33:49-04:00 2026-05-11T15:33:50-04:00 Temporary Dairy Processing Assistant <div>Responsibilities include supporting dairy processing staff with tasks related to the<br>production and handling of finished milk and ice cream products. Significant time will<br>be spent in cold storage areas, including the milk cooler and ice cream freezer,<br>stocking packaged products and assisting with quality control activities such as<br>environmental monitoring and conducting solids and fat content testing in the lab.<br><br><strong>Additional responsibilities include:</strong></div><ul><li>CIP - Clean In Place, operation of the cleaning system for tanks and pipes.</li><li>COP - Clean Out of Place, operation of COP vats for small equipment sanitation.</li><li>Separation Equipment Cleaning - operation of the separator in clean mode, break<br>down, reassemble, and sanitize.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Work Schedule:</strong></div><ul><li>Monday-Friday, hours are flexible but typically 8am-4pm with lunch.</li></ul> 111102 - Dairy And Process Applications https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232686 2026-07-13T15:47:25-04:00 2026-07-13T15:47:27-04:00 Animal Care Technician <div>The Laboratory Animal Resources (LAR) Technician is responsible for the daily care, husbandry, and enrichment of animals used in research and teaching.</div><div><br><strong>Core Duties</strong></div><ul><li>Administer routine medications and prepare specific maintenance or experimental diets.</li><li>Maintain safety and sanitation standards across the animal care facility.</li><li>Keep accurate logs of animal census, daily room statuses, and medical treatments.</li><li>Drive and transport animals as needed.</li><li>Serve as mandatory personnel, which includes working weekends and holidays.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Physical Requirements &amp; Licensure</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Licensure:</strong> Must possess or obtain a valid North Carolina driver’s license within 60 days of the start date and maintain it throughout the duration of employment.</li><li><strong>Physical Demands:</strong> Must be able lift 50 pounds frequently and 90 pounds occasionally with our without accommodations.</li></ul> 193001 - Animal Resources https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233684 2026-08-10T12:35:08-04:00 2026-08-10T12:35:10-04:00 Extension Agent, 4-H (Assistant, Associate or Full Agent) <div>As a Pack member, <a href=\"https://hr.ncsu.edu/work-at-nc-state/employee-value-proposition/\"><strong>you belong here,</strong></a> and can enjoy <a href=\"https://benefits.hr.ncsu.edu/wellness-recognition-perks/wolfperks/\"><strong>exclusive perks</strong></a><strong> </strong>designed to enhance your personal and professional well-being. What we offer: Health Insurance, Tuition waiver for university coursework, <a href=\"https://onboarding.ncsu.edu/other-employee-perks/\"><strong>Attend</strong></a> non-revenue-generating sporting events for free.<br> <br> Attain Work-life balance with our <a href=\"https://hr.ncsu.edu/benefits-and-wellness/child-care/\"><strong>Childcare discounts</strong></a>, <a href=\"https://wellrec.dasa.ncsu.edu/non-student-membership/\"><strong>Wellness &amp; Recreation Membership</strong></a>, and <a href=\"https://hr.ncsu.edu/benefits-and-wellness/wellness/\"><strong>Wellness Programs</strong></a> that aim to build a thriving wolfpack community.<br> <br> Disclaimer: Perks and Benefit eligibility is based on Part-Time or Full-Time Employment status. Eligibility and Employer Sponsored Plans can be found within each of the links offered.<br> <br> The position may be hired at the Assistant, Associate, or Full Agent level if the selected candidate meets the required qualifications<strong>.</strong><br>&nbsp; &nbsp;<br> The 4-H Agent provides leadership for the overall 4-H Youth program, planning, implementing, and evaluating initiatives for youth ages 5 to 19. In this role, the agent establishes and maintains traditional 4-H clubs, school enrichment, summer camps, and after-school programs that engage youth in positive life-skill development. The position will be responsible for working with adult volunteers to design, develop, implement and evaluate programs that meet the needs and interests of youth ages 5-19 to engage them in positive, informal life-skill development activities using a variety of educational methods such as organized 4-H clubs, school enrichment, 4-H summer programs, and after-school programs.<br>&nbsp;<br> This position also recruits, evaluates, trains and coordinates adult and teen volunteers.<br>&nbsp;<br> In addition, this position will be responsible for supervising the overall 4-H program, which includes establishing and maintaining 4-H clubs within Gaston County, recruiting and training present and potential volunteers, managing clubs and special programs, and identifying resources to assist in conducting the 4-H program.<br>&nbsp;<br> Responsibilities will include supervision of program staff and management of programs funded through grants and fees.</div> 117624 - South Central District - Gaston https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223345 2025-09-25T12:08:42-04:00 2025-09-25T12:08:43-04:00 Lecturer, Marketing <div>The Department of Business Management at NC State University is currently accepting applications for a Non-Tenure Track Faculty Lecturer (part-time) position in the areas of Marketing, Marketing Analytics, or New Product Innovation. These Professional Faculty positions are for one semester or one year, with the possibility of renewal. Applications will be kept on file for one year for consideration in future semesters.</div><div><br></div><div>Successful candidates will teach one or more Marketing concentration courses for the BSBA program, and/or Marketing or related courses in the MBA or other graduate programs within the College. Courses will be taught on the main campus in Raleigh and online, with potential opportunities for summer teaching.<br><br>Specific course assignments for the academic year have yet to be determined but may include marketing management, marketing research, new product development, or other marketing topics.</div> 202001 - Business Management - Poole College of Management https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234338 2026-08-28T15:28:12-04:00 2026-08-28T15:28:13-04:00 Research Associate - Digital Learning <div>The Friday Institute at NC State University seeks a Research Associate to help build and scale a growing portfolio of work focused on capacity building in digital learning, including computer science and AI education, and strategic initiatives that support national, state, and local education leaders. This role is pivotal in translating research and partner input into coherent programs, implementation supports, and field-facing deliverables. The successful candidate will be a strong project and portfolio manager; bring experience working at the intersection of research, policy, and practice; excel at building relationships across varied partners; and demonstrate strengths in synthesis, strategic communication, and continuous improvement.<br><br></div><div>In this role, you will help turn emerging ideas, research, and partner needs into strategies and resources that can be implemented and scaled. You will bring structure to complex initiatives, helping teams prioritize work, make informed decisions, and move projects from planning through execution. Your ability to build trusted relationships, synthesize varied perspectives, and communicate clearly will help the Friday Institute strengthen partnerships, improve its work, and extend the reach and impact of digital learning initiatives across the field.<br><br></div><div>Key Impact areas include and are not limited to:</div><div><br></div><ul><li>Shape and advance strategic initiatives by synthesizing research, partner perspectives, and emerging trends into priorities, recommendations, workplans, and opportunities for alignment across projects.</li><li>Strengthen implementation and continuous improvement by tracking meaningful indicators, gathering feedback, documenting lessons learned, and translating evidence into practical improvements.</li><li>Build alignment and engagement by coordinating project teams and partners, facilitating meetings and learning opportunities, and developing clear, publication-ready materials that support implementation and shared decision-making.</li><li>Support growth and sustainability by contributing to proposals, funder communications, project reporting, and opportunity development while helping communicate outcomes, impact, and future priorities.</li></ul><div><br>If you are an adaptable, collaborative professional who thrives in a fast-paced, evolving environment, builds strong relationships across varying partners and audiences, and enjoys working with others to turn ideas into meaningful impact, we encourage you to apply.</div> 139001 - Friday Institute https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228712 2026-03-24T10:48:57-04:00 2026-03-24T10:48:58-04:00 CLAWS III-N RF Integration Engineer <div>We are seeking a highly skilled RF Integration Engineer to join our team and contribute to the development of next-generation RF semiconductor devices. The ideal candidate will have expertise in semiconductor process integration, device and amplifier design, electrical characterization, and device simulation.</div><div><br></div><div>Key Responsibilities</div><ul><li><strong>RF Device Design and Development (30%)</strong><ul><li>Design and optimize GaN RF devices including discrete HEMTs, passive elements, and MMICs.</li><li>Establish a flexible PDK defining the CLAWS RF technologies.</li><li>Utilize TCAD simulation (e.g., Synopsys, Silvaco, etc.) for process and device modeling, electrical characteristics prediction, and performance enhancement.</li><li>Develop and refine device layouts for wafer fabrication, ensuring manufacturability and reliability.</li></ul></li></ul><div><br></div><ul><li><strong>Process Integration and Fabrication (30%)</strong><ul><li>Be responsible for process development and integration enabling the fabrication of GaN devices leveraging NCSU’s nanofabrication facilities.</li><li>Establish hands-on proficiency in relevant fabrication methods, including lithography, metal and dielectric deposition, etching, wafer dicing, and others as needed.</li></ul></li></ul><div><br></div><ul><li><strong>Characterization and Reliability Testing (20%)</strong><ul><li>Define and execute device on-wafer and packaged device characterization plans that include DC, small signal RF, load-pull tests.</li><li>Utilize model extraction tools (e.g., IC-CAP) to generate compact models and from electrical test data.</li><li>Leverage failure analysis and characterization methods to support process development and integration.</li><li>Analyze reliability metrics to assess long-term device stability.<br><br></li></ul></li><li><strong>Cross-Functional Collaboration (20%)</strong><ul><li>Work with process engineers and R&amp;D partners to drive technology improvements.</li><li>Collaborate with circuit designers, applications engineers, and product development teams to translate device-level improvements into system-level advantages.</li></ul></li></ul> 140110 - CLAWS https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234717 2026-09-04T14:12:38-04:00 2026-09-04T14:12:39-04:00 UTS - Temporary Donor Services Specialist at NC State <div>The Donor Services Specialist (Gift Agreements, Namings Opportunities, and Donor Services General Assistance) plays a vital role in supporting the team with researching and assisting with the review of gift agreements, naming opportunities, and related administrative processes. This position ensures the accuracy, consistency, and timeliness of donor documentation while providing general support to facilitate the team’s daily operations.<br><br>We seek a highly organized, detail-oriented individual who thrives in a fast-paced, collaborative environment. The ideal candidate will have experience drafting and copyediting documents (AP style preferred), strong data analysis skills, and proficiency with Excel and other spreadsheet tools. Experience in finance, donor relations, or higher education is a plus. Excellent tracking abilities and typing skills are essential.<br><br><br>In this role, you will support the management and coordination of gift agreements and naming opportunities across the university. It follows a hybrid work model, requiring two in-office days per week.<br><strong><br>Key responsibilities include:</strong></div><ul><li>Conduct research and gather information related to gift agreements.</li><li>Draft, edit, and refine documents, memos, correspondence, and proposals.</li><li>Draft, review, and approve gift agreements to ensure accuracy and alignment with university standards.</li><li>Assist in reviewing gifts and gift agreements for compliance with IRS regulations, CASE standards, university policies, best practices, and Advancement operational guidelines.</li><li>Coordinate the review process with campus partners.</li><li>Develop talking points and briefing materials for front-line fundraisers.</li><li>Track and coordinate all processes related to gift agreements to ensure timely completion.</li><li>Provide support in researching gift agreements, endowment histories, and related records.</li><li>Conduct general research in support of Donor Services initiatives.</li><li>Perform additional duties as assigned to support the overall operations of Donor Services.</li><li>We’re looking for someone with attention to detail, strong writing/communication skills, and a collaborative spirit.</li></ul> 480501 - UHR Temporary Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231666 2026-06-12T11:52:21-04:00 2026-06-12T11:52:22-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar - Oncology <div>The <strong>Postdoctoral Research Scholar</strong> will work under the direct guidance of Dr. Mike Nolan, a veterinary radiation oncologist whose research includes both comparative oncology and cancer neurosciences. Specifically, the Nolan Lab engages in two types of research:<br><br></div><ul><li>Veterinary Clinical Trials: the purpose is to develop novel therapies for pet dogs and cats with cancer; recent endeavors have included nanoparticle-based therapies, small molecule inhibitors that act as radiosensitizers, and immune checkpoint inhibitors.</li></ul><div><br></div><ul><li>Laboratory-Based Translational Research: the lab uses conventional animal modeling and benchtop approaches in cell and molecular biology to study both mechanisms of cancer treatment induced pain, and crosstalk between pain signaling pathways and cancer behavior.</li></ul><div><br>The Lab is situated within the Department of Clinical Sciences, at North Carolina State University’s College of Veterinary Medicine (CVM). The CVM is a dynamic community whose members are dedicated to preparing veterinarians and veterinary scientists while advancing animal and human health from the cellular level through entire ecosystems.<br><br>The candidate will work with other members of the lab team, including two research technicians, a veterinarian who is pursuing a PhD, PhD-level postdoctoral scientists, and several veterinary and undergraduate student researchers. The team also interacts daily with technicians and clinical trial coordinators that work for the College’s Clinical Studies Core. Close on-site faculty collaborators include Dr. Duncan Lascelles (a translational pain researcher, veterinarian, and specialist in small animal surgery with advanced fellowship training in oncologic surgery), and Dr. Santosh Mishra (neurobiologist and itch researcher with particular expertise in mouse modeling), plus a large number of collaborating clinical faculty on the medical, radiation, and surgical oncology services.<br><br>The purpose of this position is to provide the Postdoctoral Research Scholar with training in clinical research including hypothesis generation and testing, data collection, basic statistical analysis, scientific writing and presentation and review of the literature. The position is designed to embed the individual in a large animal (canine/feline) translational and clinical research program, allowing them to acquire skills related to cancer management (diagnosis, treatment, follow-up) and cancer research.<br><br><strong><br>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong><br>With supervision and support from the PI, lab manager and research team, the candidate will learn to execute previously planned/funded canine and feline clinical studies. This will include clinical activities (e.g., physical examinations, prescribing supportive care, providing care to hospitalized patients, interacting with technicians and support staff, medical record keeping, client communication), laboratory activities (not limited to but may include things such as PBMC isolation, cell culture), and documentation of research outcomes (in paper and electronic format). The candidate will learn to organize and analyze data. The candidate will learn to make written and oral presentations of their research, and they will (with help from the team) work to publish research results. Daily, they will interact with faculty, postdoctoral associates, and graduate students within NC State, and at collaborating institutions.</div> 195001 - Department of Clinical Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234223 2026-08-26T14:47:19-04:00 2026-08-26T14:47:20-04:00 OIT Managed Desktop Support Analyst <div>This position provides support, including OS, driver, and application configuration and troubleshooting for workstations (desktop computers, laptops, and other supported devices) and peripherals that are part of OIT’s Managed Desktop Service (OITMD).<br><br>The daily responsibilities include resolution of workstation problems, working either on-site with users, or remotely, while providing exemplary customer service. As part of the support process, this position provides support of hardware, applications, operating systems, and networking. This function requires an in-depth understanding of a wide variety of technologies to effectively support end-users and to provide helpful and accurate information to peers and IT colleagues across campus. As a senior member of the OIT Managed Desktop Support team, this position may participate in in-depth application support, system integration and networking activities. This work requires very strong communication skills, an ability to effectively interact with a broad range of end-users and IT professionals, as well as, an ability to use a variety of resources for diagnosing and resolving technical problems.<br><br>Position Description/General Scope of Duties:<br><br>Workstation Configuration and troubleshooting</div><ul><li>Uses SCCM, Jamf Pro, and other support tools to manage computing systems and increase desktop support efficiencies.</li><li>Images endpoints, deploys applications and provides troubleshooting for non-routine problems that arise.</li><li>Provides updates on work progress to other support staff and the Service Desk using call tracking system.</li><li>Builds test computing environments for enterprise system upgrades for EAS development teams as needed.</li></ul><div><br></div><div>Customer Service/Communication</div><ul><li>Provide excellent customer service through prompt responses and communicating complex ideas in language that customers understand</li><li>Proactively promotes positive customer relationships and mentors others to ensure client satisfaction and organizational success.</li><li>Takes ownership of customer problems and works with a sense of urgency to resolve incidents and problems.</li><li>Communicates status of progress to the customer.</li></ul><div><br></div><div>Consulting/Business Analysis</div><ul><li>Ability to communicate with clients to understand their business needs and provide solutions that meet those needs and still comply with standard operating procedures and campus security requirements.</li><li>Follows up with clients to ensure that questions have been answered and no outstanding issues remain.</li></ul><div><br></div><div>Project Management</div><ul><li>Serves as a productive project team member through timely completion of assigned Tasks.</li><li>Demonstrates initiative in solving problems associated with projects and daily work.</li><li>Demonstrates effective planning and time management of individual tasks.</li><li>May also lead project teams or work groups, serve as a technical expert to management, and/or functions in a project management capacity for projects scoped to the level of the position.</li></ul><div><br></div> 513001 - Technology Support Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228163 2026-03-06T13:11:19-05:00 2026-03-06T13:11:20-05:00 Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Veterinary Academic Excellence <div>North Carolina State University invites applications for the position of <strong>Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Veterinary Academic Excellence</strong> in the College of Veterinary Medicine. This is an exciting opportunity for an individual to advance human and animal health and welfare through the excellence in teaching veterinary and graduate students; application of the most current research techniques addressing complex medical problems; and/or providing cutting edge clinical care at one of the nation’s leading colleges of veterinary medicine. We are seeking a dynamic and transformational academic to join our teams. The applicant will join and strengthen our research, teaching and clinical service teams to provide transformational knowledge in their discipline. The discipline of interest for this position is expected to highlight and expand on already existing strengths within the College of Veterinary Medicine, opening new frontiers in the college. In this role you will enhance the CVM’s abilities and expand our reach in the areas of research, teaching and/or clinical service. It will synergize with existing faculty to build an internationally-recognized program focused in their discipline of interest. Additionally, you will play an active role in training veterinary, house officers and/or graduate students by providing new perspectives in the discipline.<br><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Apply cutting edge scientific knowledge and techniques to develop impactful solutions for complex medical issues in humans and animals.</li><li>Support and encourage leadership in comparative medicine; promote the ability to transfer excellence in research, service and training from animals to human health.</li><li>Leverage faculty access to our state-of-the-art veterinary teaching hospital to foster close collaborations between the CVM and other NC State groups, the medical schools at Duke and the University of North Carolina, and the North Carolina Research Triangle Park.</li><li>Serve as an advocate in enhancing the abilities, visibility, and reach of the CVM’s research, teaching and clinical service programs.</li><li>Synergize with existing faculty to build an internationally-recognized program particularly around the College and University’s current strengths.</li><li>Play an active role in training veterinary, house officer, and graduate students by providing new perspectives.</li><li>Train veterinary, house officer, and/or graduate students. Participate in didactic teaching at the graduate level and augment veterinary student education.</li><li>Secure extramural funding for their independent research program and trainees.</li></ul><div><br><br>If you are on cutting-edge of your field with an outstanding record of achievement as an independent researcher, educator, and/or clinician, we encourage you to apply.</div> 194001 - Department of Molecular Biomedical Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234677 2026-09-03T14:11:59-04:00 2026-09-03T14:12:01-04:00 Facilities Maintenance Technician - Journey <div>Join NC State University’s <strong>Building Maintenance and Operations (BMO)</strong> team as a <strong>Facilities Maintenance Technician – Journey Level</strong> and put your hands-on mechanical skills to work supporting a safe, reliable, and comfortable campus environment. This position performs skilled maintenance, installation, troubleshooting, and repair of <strong>HVAC, piping, insulation, plumbing, steam, and other building mechanical systems</strong>.</div><div>You’ll work with a variety of systems, from domestic and chilled water to steam, gas, air, and waste systems, while also supporting HVAC controls and secondary electrical systems. <br><br>This role is ideal for a skilled trades professional who enjoys <strong>problem-solving, working with their hands, and finding practical solutions to both routine and complex maintenance challenges</strong>. If you take pride in quality workmanship, enjoy collaborating with other trades, and want your skills to make a direct impact on the NC State campus, <strong>we invite you to apply today and become part of the Facilities team!</strong></div><div><strong><br></strong><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include but are not limited to: </em></strong></div><ul><li>Perform maintenance and repairs on <strong>domestic water, chilled water, hot water, steam piping, and ductwork insulation</strong>.</li><li>Perform preventive maintenance on <strong>plumbing and building systems</strong>, including air, gas, water, and waste systems.</li><li>Install, maintain, and repair <strong>plumbing systems and fixtures</strong>, including lab drainage, sinks, toilets, valves, and piping.</li><li>Cut, thread, assemble, and install piping; connect valves; caulk joints; and test installations for leaks and defects.</li><li>Install and maintain gas, water, and sanitary fixtures, including appropriate supports, hangers, and foundations.</li><li><strong>Diagnose and troubleshoot mechanical and plumbing issues</strong>, including complex and non-standard repairs, and develop effective solutions.</li><li>Support troubleshooting and maintenance of <strong>secondary electrical systems up to 600 VAC</strong>, including conduit, breakers, switches, panels, motors, and control circuits.</li><li>Assist with <strong>HVAC preventive maintenance and repairs</strong>, including temperature controls, thermostats, humidity sensors, and regulators.</li><li>Troubleshoot and repair <strong>steam systems and related equipment</strong>, including boilers, pumps, turbines, condensate systems, and air compressors.</li><li>Collaborate with HVAC, electrical, mechanical, and other trades to resolve <strong>multi-system issues and complete maintenance projects safely and effectively</strong>.</li><li>Follow established safety procedures, regulations, and university standards while performing all work.</li><li>Communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, and the general public, and follows verbal and written instructions related to work assignments and operational needs.</li><li>Perform tasks requiring standing for extended periods, working in confined spaces, and utilizing various tools. Safely climb and work from different types of ladders to access elevated or below-grade areas (such as roofs, above-ceiling spaces, and utility pits). Be exposed to and work in areas where insects or small animals are occasionally present. All essential tasks must be performed with or without reasonable accommodations.</li><li>Lift, carry, and maneuver materials weighing 60–90 pounds, with or without reasonable accommodations.</li></ul> 445030 - Building Maintenance & Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231013 2026-05-29T09:24:50-04:00 2026-05-29T09:24:51-04:00 Career Services Coordinator, CALS Career Services <div>The Career Services Coordinator will enhance the career and professional development of CALS students through overseeing the CALS Alumni for Student Success (CASS) Career Coach program and providing programmatic support to all functions of the CALS Career Services office. The Coordinator’s work will contribute to 4,500+ CALS students improving their career readiness, participating in experiential learning, and securing post-graduation opportunities aligning with their interests. The person in this position will report to the Director of CALS Career Services.</div><div><strong><br>Primary Responsibilities include, but are not limited to:<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Program Management</strong></div><ul><li>Serve as lead administrator for the CALS Alumni for Student Success (CASS) Career Coach program that pairs 100+ individual students with individual coaches for seven months of personalized career coaching.</li><li>Collaborate with the CALS Director of Alumni and Engagement and CALS departments to recruit alumni and industry professionals to serve as coaches.</li><li>Collaborate with CALS departments to recruit students to participate in the program and to match students with coaches.</li><li>Coordinate and facilitate program events, including trainings, networking opportunities, and celebrations.</li><li>Conduct program assessments and report outcomes to administration and stakeholders.</li></ul><div><strong>Event Management</strong></div><ul><li>Support the implementation of CALS Career Services events, including career fairs, networking events, trainings, and recognition events.</li><li>Develop comprehensive event plans and task timelines in collaboration with CALS Career Services team members.</li><li>Manage logistics for events both in advance and during the events.</li><li>Create and manage detailed event budgets, track expenditures in real-time, and identify cost-saving alternatives to ensure financial efficiency.</li><li>Collaborate with CALS Career Services team members and CALS Communications to market events to students, faculty, staff, employers, and other external stakeholders.</li><li>Conduct event outcome assessments and contribute to data-based decision making around future directions.</li></ul><div><strong>Programmatic Support</strong></div><ul><li>Assist with CALS Career Services programming as needed, including presenting career and professional development workshops.</li><li>Provide 2-3 hours per week of career coaching/counseling for current students and alumni within one year after graduation.</li><li>Support CALS Rural Works! program through data management and internship site visits.</li><li>Serve as a representative of CALS Career Services as needed.</li></ul> 110101 - CALS - Academic Programs https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232917 2026-07-20T10:19:34-04:00 2026-07-20T10:19:35-04:00 Assistant/Associate Professor in Community Sport Development <div>The Department of Parks, Recreation and Tourism Management (PRTM) in the College of Natural Resources at North Carolina State University invites applications for an Assistant or Associate Professor with expertise in community sport development. We are seeking an innovative scholar and educator whose research explores the role of sport in strengthening communities through sustainable development, cultural engagement, health and well-being, youth development, economic impact, or related areas.&nbsp;<br><br></div><div>In this role, you will shape the future of community sport development through impactful research, meaningful teaching, and collaboration with students, colleagues, and community partners. You'll have the opportunity to develop or expand a nationally recognized research program, mentor the next generation of sport leaders, and translate scholarship into practice through partnerships that improve communities across North Carolina and beyond. As part of NC State's land-grant mission, your work will extend beyond the classroom—creating lasting connections between the university, industry, and communities while contributing to innovative solutions with local, national, and global impact.<br><br></div><div>Key responsibilities include:</div><ul><li>Develop Research by or expanding a high-impact scholarly research program that attracts external funding and contributes to the field of community sport development.</li><li>Teach and Mentor undergraduate and graduate students through engaging instruction, student mentorship, and supervision of research and scholarly activities.</li><li>Publish and Disseminate research through peer-reviewed publications, professional presentations, and other scholarly activities that advance knowledge and practice.</li><li>Collaborate with faculty, community organizations, industry partners, and government agencies to foster interdisciplinary research and strengthen community engagement.</li><li>Shape Academic Programs by contributing to curriculum development, continuous program improvement, and innovative learning opportunities across the department.</li><li>Serve the University and Profession through departmental, college, and university committees, professional organizations, and public service activities that support NC State's land-grant mission.<br><br></li></ul><div>If you are an innovative scholar who thrives on collaboration, values student success, and is committed to advancing community sport development through impactful research and engagement, we encourage you to apply.</div> 150201 - Parks, Recreation & Tourism Management https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233915 2026-08-18T08:41:19-04:00 2026-08-18T08:41:20-04:00 Associate Director of Autonomous Science <div>The <strong>Associate Director of Autonomous Science</strong> will contribute to advancement of the scientific and engineering vision for SPEED’s autonomous laboratories, helping transform how experiments are designed, executed, analyzed, and optimized. This is a highly collaborative leadership role at the intersection of science, robotics, artificial intelligence, and advanced laboratory automation.</div><div><br></div><div>The Associate Director will coordinate the translation of complex scientific questions into closed-loop autonomous experiments that combine AI-driven planning, robotic experimentation, multimodal characterization, data analysis, and scientific reasoning. Working with SPEED scientists, engineers, researchers, and external users, the position will shape new capabilities, guide high-impact research programs, and ensure that autonomous experiments deliver rigorous, reproducible, and scientifically meaningful results.</div><div><br></div><div><strong>Primary Responsibilities:<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Autonomous Science and Engineering Leadership</strong></div><ul><li>Lead design and operation of end-to-end autonomous experimentation workflows across SPEED’s robotic laboratories.</li><li>Translate scientific goals into closed-loop systems spanning experimental design, robotic execution, measurement, analysis, interpretation, and AI-guided experiment selection.</li><li>Define requirements for integrating synthesis, processing, characterization, optimization, and autonomous decision-making.</li><li>Advance Bayesian optimization, active learning, machine learning, scientific models, genetic algorithms, and AI agents in physical laboratory systems.</li><li>Establish standards for controls, validation, uncertainty quantification, reproducibility, metadata, and experimental provenance.</li><li>Evaluate AI-generated recommendations for scientific validity, feasibility, safety, and alignment with research objectives.</li><li>Drive improvements in SPEED’s reliability, throughput, autonomy, and scientific capabilities.</li></ul><div><strong>Scientific Program and User Engagement</strong></div><ul><li>Serve as scientific leader and primary point of contact for researchers using SPEED’s autonomous experimentation capabilities.</li><li>Translate user questions into defined experimental objectives, strategies, and success criteria.</li><li>Assess project feasibility and develop experimental plans, autonomous campaigns, analysis strategies, and validation approaches.</li><li>Partner with researchers and engineers to execute projects, interpret results, and resolve scientific and technical challenges.</li><li>Lead reviews of experimental results, AI recommendations, uncertainty, emerging hypotheses, and next steps.</li><li>Develop sustained research partnerships with academic, government, national laboratory, and industry organizations.</li></ul><div><strong>Expansion of SPEED Scientific Capabilities</strong></div><ul><li>Lead development and integration of new autonomous laboratory capabilities, methods, and application areas.</li><li>Identify opportunities to expand SPEED across chemistry, materials science, catalysis, molecular discovery, formulation, process development, and related fields.</li><li>Define requirements for robotic platforms, analytical systems, sensors, AI tools, and integrated workflows.</li><li>Guide new capabilities from concept and prototyping through commissioning, validation, and deployment.</li><li>Establish benchmarks to evaluate performance, reproducibility, and transferability.</li><li>Evaluate emerging technologies in laboratory robotics, scientific AI, multimodal characterization, digital twins, agentic systems, and autonomous decision-making.</li><li>Develop strategies to transfer methods, models, and experimental knowledge across SPEED platforms and partner laboratories.</li><li>Contribute to strategic planning, equipment investments, partnerships, proposals, publications, and initiatives advancing SPEED’s scientific mission.</li></ul><div><strong>Research Team Leadership and Technical Coordination</strong></div><ul><li>Provide scientific direction and mentorship to researchers, platform scientists, and engineering personnel.</li><li>Coordinate multidisciplinary teams spanning science, robotics, automation, software, data, AI, and facility operations.</li><li>Set project priorities, milestones, deliverables, and performance expectations.</li><li>Review experimental plans, autonomous workflows, technical documentation, data products, and scientific conclusions.</li><li>Lead technical discussions to resolve integration challenges and align scientific and engineering efforts.</li><li>Establish consistent scientific, software, data, and documentation practices across SPEED platforms.</li><li>Develop training, workshops, and technical demonstrations for researchers, users, and partners.</li><li>Represent SPEED at scientific meetings, sponsor reviews, user workshops, advisory activities, and external collaborations.</li></ul> 230101 - Interdisciplinary Programs - SVP https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/225874 2025-12-16T16:26:36-05:00 2025-12-16T16:26:37-05:00 Temporary Food Grade Laboratory Assistant <div>Data entry and analysis, food sample preparation, consumer communications for sensory service center.<br><br></div> 111101 - Food, Bioprocessing & Nutrition Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234443 2026-08-31T17:17:53-04:00 2026-09-01T08:10:53-04:00 Temporary Farm to ECE Extension Coordinator <div>The Farm to Early Care &amp; Education (ECE) Extension Coordinator will work with county-level Extension staff and community partners to assess community needs and coordinate implementation of Farm to ECE programming in Head Start and other ECE settings. The Coordinator will provide hands-on support for establishing and sustaining ECE gardens and garden education programs; provide virtual and in-person coaching and technical assistance to teachers, administrators, and other partners; coordinate delivery and setup of garden supplies and outdoor learning materials; support garden site preparation, planting, maintenance, and troubleshooting; participate in community and Extension meetings; support development of partner action plans; and assist with project research and evaluation activities, including data collection and site visits. Regular travel to participating counties and community sites is required.&nbsp;<br><br>This is a part-time position averaging 10–15 hours per week. Regular travel to participating counties and ECE sites is an essential function of the position. The role includes outdoor, hands-on work related to garden establishment and maintenance and may require work in varying weather conditions, transport of supplies/materials, and assistance with setup or assembly of garden and outdoor learning materials.</div> 111401 - Agricultural and Human Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/229616 2026-04-16T14:31:00-04:00 2026-04-16T14:31:01-04:00 Stock Clerk <div>JOIN THE PACK!<br><br></div><div>NC State Dining offers meaningful opportunities for employment across our retail and hospitality units. We offer competitive pay, flexible schedules, and opportunity for learning, growth, and promotion. Dedicated and experienced professional staff are available to provide on-the-job training and assistance so you become a successful member of the team.<br><br></div><div>We are seeking individuals to Join our Pack as a Stock Clerk.<br><br></div><div>Essential Job Duties:<br><br></div><div>• Assist in checking in food stock items, breaking down pallets, and putting away food stock in a timely fashion.<br>• Place received items in appropriate storage areas.<br>• Pull requisitioned stock items for usage in the units throughout the building.<br>• Record a limited daily inventory of food items plus a complete monthly food inventory for unit.<br>• Other duties as assigned to ensure NC State Dining business needs are met. Position may be asked to assist in other locations as necessary.</div><div><br>This is an hourly position with a mandatory 32-day break at the end of 11 months. Excellent performance during this time may provide an opportunity to renew employment and/or advance.</div> 471501 - Dining Administration https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232183 2026-06-26T16:33:05-04:00 2026-06-26T16:33:06-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>The primary purpose of this position is to lead innovative research in photonic accelerators through the design, heterogeneous integration, and characterization of advanced hybrid III-V/silicon photonic integrated circuits. The position supports the development of next-generation optical computing technologies that deliver ultra-wideband performance and energy-efficient computation by combining III-V material capabilities with scalable silicon photonics platforms. The incumbent will collaborate with multidisciplinary teams to advance concepts from device design through system demonstration and application validation.<br><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities include:</strong><br><br></div><ul><li>Drive the end-to-end lifecycle of hybrid III-V/silicon photonic integrated circuits (PICs), including definition, architectural design, simulation, layout, and comprehensive characterization.</li><li>Conduct high-frequency and active device characterization, performing RF testing beyond 50 GHz, standard laser LIV measurements, optical injection locking, Bit Error Rate (BER) testing, and eye-diagram analysis.</li><li>Guide and mentor a multidisciplinary team of Ph.D., Master’s, and undergraduate student researchers, fostering technical growth and aligning their work with project milestones.</li><li>Author, review, and present high-impact manuscripts and technical papers for publication in top-tier peer-reviewed journals and international academic conferences.</li><li>Partner with internal and external multidisciplinary teams to transition novel photonic concepts into validated hardware prototypes and system demonstrations.</li></ul> 140401 - Electrical & Computer Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233422 2026-08-03T09:10:44-04:00 2026-08-03T09:10:45-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>The Physics Department provides: (1) High quality Physics instruction to the University community through courses in introductory physics; (2) educational opportunities for undergraduate and graduate students in physics through high quality curricula, faculty, and research facilities; (3) outreach programs that advance scientific knowledge. Our research programs cover a wide spectrum including astrophysics, general relativity, condensed matter theory and experiment, computational physics, biophysics, nanoscale science, physics education, experimental nuclear physics, particle and nuclear theory, experimental optics, and experimental atomic and molecular physics. The department enrolls approximately 120 graduate students and 140 undergrad physics majors. The department size is approximately 56 faculty members. While working in this department, you will be expected to foster a supportive and welcoming environment.<br><br>NC State promotes an integrated approach to problem solving that transforms lives and provides leadership for social, economic, and technological development across North Carolina and around the world. NC State’s land grant mission of teaching, research and service is dedicated to the service of North Carolina and its people. Applicants are encouraged to review the institution’s mission, vision and strategic plan, and consider how their background, interest and experience would enable them to support the university. <br><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Predictive modeling and design of protein–nucleic acid interactions, including protein–DNA recognition, protein–RNA interactions, and protein- or small-molecule binding to oligonucleotide aptamers, in close collaboration with experimental groups</li><li>Physics-based and AI-enabled modeling of chromatin organization, RNA conformational dynamics, and RNA–protein phase separation.</li><li>Depending on the scholar’s background, the research may emphasize bimolecular simulation, AI for bimolecular science, or an integrated combination of both.</li><li>Responsibilities will include computational method development, rigorous validation against experimental data, development of reproducible software and workflows, manuscript preparation, and dissemination of research findings.</li><li>Conduct independent and collaborative computational research on bimolecular structure, dynamics, interactions, and functions.</li><li>Develop, implement, and evaluate molecular simulation and/or deep-learning methods; construct and analyze atomistic or coarse-grained models; integrate structural, sequence, thermodynamic, and experimental data; and perform rigorous statistical and mechanistic analyses.</li><li>Research may address protein–DNA and protein–RNA recognition, aptamer binding and design, RNA conformational ensembles and dynamics, RNA–protein phase separation, and chromatin organization.</li><li>Maintain reproducible, well-documented software and workflows, communicate findings through peer-reviewed publications, conference presentations, and research software, and collaborate closely with experimental and computational research partners.</li></ul><div><br><br>The Department of Physics and Astronomy at NC State University seeks to fill the position for a Postdoctoral Research Scholar in the Lin Research Group. The scholar will advance computational studies of bimolecular structure, dynamics, interactions, and functions.<br><br><br>The initial appointment is for one year, with possible renewal for a second year contingent upon satisfactory performance. In the cover letter, candidates should briefly describe the project that best demonstrates their relevant expertise, their independent contributions to method, algorithm, or software development, and the connection of this work to one or more of the position’s research directions.</div> 170601 - Physics and Astronomy https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223277 2025-09-25T11:20:44-04:00 2025-09-25T11:20:44-04:00 Temporary-Professional NonFaculty <div>This position, up to 29 hours per week, will support research activities within the Research Group at ITRE primarily in the areas of roadway monitoring and roadway modeling, among others. This position will assist with research tasks related to web-mapping applications, as well as updating all Python scripts. Additional tasks may be assigned to this position in other relevant areas, based on the candidate’s background and expertise. The candidate should also be proficient in reporting writing, analysis, and communicating with the research team and the project sponsor.</div> 062001 - Institute For Transportation Research & Education https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231688 2026-06-12T15:14:18-04:00 2026-06-12T15:14:19-04:00 Temporary Horticultural Camp Counselor <div>The Horticultural Science Summer Institute Counselors are the primary caregivers for the program campers. The Counselor is responsible for supervising and guiding campers in their personal growth and daily living skills.&nbsp;<br><br></div><ol><li>Assist in the direction, supervision, and organization of campers in their dorms, within activities and throughout the camp in order to meet the intended camper outcomes.</li></ol><ul><li>Apply basic youth development principles in working with campers through communication, relationship development, involvement and empowerment of youth.</li><li>Assure campers are properly supervised at all times.</li><li>Be aware of and implement safety guidelines.</li></ul><div><br></div><div>2. Maintain high standards of health and safety in all activities for campers and staff.</div><ul><li>Provide the daily care of each camper within your supervision including recognition of personal health needs.</li><li>Ensure that campers receive their medications as directed by program coordinator.</li><li>Be alert to campers and staff needs and assist them with personal and/or health problems, and discuss with program coordinator when appropriate.</li><li>Be alert to equipment and facilities to ensure utilization, proper care, and maintenance is adhered to; report repairs needed promptly to program coordinator.</li></ul><div><br></div><div>3. Be a role model to campers and staff in your attitude and behavior.</div><ul><li>Follow and uphold all safety and security rules and procedures.</li><li>Set a good example to campers and others in regard to general camp procedures and practices including sanitation, schedule, and sportsmanship.</li></ul><div>Training will be provided for selected positions.</div> 111501 - Horticultural Science https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233194 2026-07-27T15:18:34-04:00 2026-07-27T15:18:35-04:00 ASPSA Tutor <div><strong>Division of Academic and Student Affairs</strong><br>The Division of Academic &amp; Student Affairs (DASA) is where students discover connections and community. It is where students live, where they gather, where they improve themselves, each other, and their university. DASA is where students seek guidance and support, both in and outside of the classroom. The division serves all students at NC State through three integrated components: academic affairs, student affairs, and University College.<br><br>NC State wants employees to achieve their highest potential at work, learn more about our employee value proposition here (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>You Belong Here</strong></a>). The Division of Academic and Student Affairs is the place to work for values-driven faculty and staff who are passionate about promoting the success of the whole student.<br><br><strong><em>The Office of Academic Support Program for Student-Athletes</em></strong><em> (ASPSA) is a comprehensive support program that strives to meet the academic, personal and professional development needs of all student-athletes, promoting excellence and effectiveness in undergraduate and graduate education as well as leadership and civic engagement.<br><br>ASPSA is committed to extending the educational experience of its constituency with particular emphasis on empowering student-athletes to become strong self-advocates. We provide specialized initiatives to facilitate a smooth transition from high school to college and from college to professional life while successfully integrating student-athletes into the campus community. We strive to enhance academic skills for all student-athletes and provide personalized academic support to meet the needs of each student-athlete.<br><br>ASPSA maintains a strong sense of integrity and will continue to strive to be one of the benchmark programs for academic support for all collegiate academic support programs in the nation.<br></em><strong><em><br></em></strong><strong>Primary Purpose of Position:</strong></div><ul><li>Must be willing to learn about and abide by the North Carolina State University, NCAA, and ASPSA rules and regulations.</li><li>Must be able to teach academic success strategies to a wide range of students in a one-on-one or small group setting.</li><li>Must have the ability to effectively communicate verbally and have skills to develop written reports.</li><li>Must be attentive to detail and accurate in record keeping.</li><li>Ability to work a flexible schedule including nights and weekends.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Hours available - All work is 100% IN PERSON:</strong></div><ul><li>Sundays 12 - 3pm, 5 - 8 pm</li><li>Monday - Thursdays 8am - 9pm</li><li>Fridays 8am - 5pm</li></ul><div><br>Temporary positions are required to take a 31-day break after working 11 months.</div> 240201 - Academic Support Program for Student Athletes https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223021 2025-09-17T17:14:23-04:00 2025-09-17T17:14:24-04:00 Deputy General Counsel <div>The <strong>Deputy General Counsel</strong> plays a pivotal role in advancing the university’s mission by providing high-level legal counsel and strategic guidance on matters impacting North Carolina State University. As a trusted advisor and key member of the Office of General Counsel leadership team, this position reports directly to the Vice Chancellor and General Counsel.</div><div><br></div><div>The Deputy General Counsel will collaborate with a dedicated team of legal professionals to address a wide array of legal, risk management, and compliance issues, ensuring the university operates within legal and regulatory frameworks while supporting its academic and operational goals. This role is responsible for delivering proactive legal strategies, managing complex legal matters, and building strong relationships with university leadership, faculty, and staff.</div><div><strong><br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Key&nbsp; Responsibilities</strong></div><ul><li>Serves as a strategic advisor to the Vice Chancellor and General Counsel, providing expert legal advice and innovative solutions on issues affecting the university.</li><li>Advises senior executives, department leaders, staff and faculty on legal risks and opportunities, ensuring alignment with university goals and policies.</li><li>Oversees and manages legal services, depending on need, in areas such as, but not limited to, employment law, governance, contracts, risk management, litigation, and other areas as assigned by the Vice Chancellor and General Counsel.&nbsp; This may include high-profile legal matters and larger legal projects as necessary. &nbsp;</li><li>Manages, mentors, and supervises Office of General Counsel staff in the delivery of legal services, including project allocation and performance assessment, advising on hiring decisions, identifying and providing training, engaging in ongoing direct supervision, addressing personnel issues and actions, and conducting performance reviews.</li><li>Manages the effective delivery of outside legal services for legal matters when necessary, and manages related costs as assigned.</li><li>Represents the university in negotiations, mediations, and proceedings as necessary, including proceedings before various local, state, and federal agencies and courts. Communicates, and interfaces with attorneys representing potential litigants and complainants having disputes with the University. &nbsp;</li><li>Advises and serves on ad hoc and standing committees as assigned.&nbsp;</li><li>Drafts, reviews, and recommends revisions to University policies.</li><li>Provides training and presentations on legal issues and developments for members of the University community.</li><li>Maintains and improves personal legal expertise by following developments and participating in continuing legal education regarding higher education law.</li><li>In partnership with the Vice Chancellor and General Counsel, handles other legal and administrative duties as assigned.</li></ul> 270101 - Office of General Counsel https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234068 2026-08-21T15:46:44-04:00 2026-08-21T15:46:45-04:00 County Extension Director <div>Are you looking for a rewarding career that will let you give back to your community? NC State Extension extends research-based knowledge to all North Carolinians, helping them transform science into everyday solutions that improve their lives and grow our state. We are the model of excellence in non-formal, research-based education that advances agriculture, the environment, human health and well-being, youth and communities, while creating prosperity for all North Carolinians. Working for NC State Extension allows you to teach without being tied to a classroom and school bell.</div><div><br></div><div>While our employees are located in NC County offices, they reap the benefits of employment at NC State University. Some of our benefits include:</div><ul><li>Paid time off</li><li>Paid tuition</li><li>Health Insurance</li><li>Flexible Scheduling</li><li>Retirement Savings</li><li>Supplemental Benefits Programs</li><li>WolfPerks!</li></ul><div><br></div><div>You Belong Here! At NC State, our goal is for all employees to reach their fullest potential at work. As you consider this opportunity, we encourage you to review our <a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>Employee</strong></a><strong> </strong><a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>Value Proposition</strong></a><strong> </strong>(<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/</strong></a>) and learn more about what makes NC State the best place to learn and work for everyone.<br><br>The County Extension Director is a leadership position with Cooperative Extension at North Carolina State University. The major responsibility of the job is to provide leadership for and coordinate the efforts of the county Extension staff in developing an effective total county Extension educational program in cooperation with North Carolina A&amp;T State University’s Cooperative Extension Program<strong>.</strong></div><div><br></div><div>The position requires a dynamic individual to provide visionary leadership for a comprehensive Extension program to meet the needs of all segments of the population. The County Extension Director serves as the County Extension administrative leader with responsibilities for administration, public policy issues, and community/leadership development. The County Extension Director is responsible for maintaining an effective advisory leadership system representative of all program areas. The County Extension Director provides leadership and expertise in coordinating/securing resource development for all program areas. Responsibilities include marketing Extension programs and impacts.</div><div><br></div><div>The County Extension Director must maintain a strong, collaborative relationship in assisting the staff in delivering educational programs with county government input and support. Also, to meet the needs of county citizens, the position must network and collaborate with agencies and organizations, county departments, schools, town officials, area agencies, and University and Extension personnel. The position will be responsible for coordinating staff development, and training. The individual must demonstrate the ability to plan, organize, and work well with people.</div> 117702 - Northeast District - Bertie https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/227397 2026-02-12T10:53:00-05:00 2026-02-12T10:53:01-05:00 Temporary Summer Camp Staff - Ellerbe, NC <div>Are you ready for a summer of amazing experiences and memories that will last a lifetime? We are looking for responsible, enthusiastic individuals to serve as positive role models and mentors for youth, ages 5 – 17 at our American Camp Association accredited summer camp. Qualified candidates will possess patience, fairness, compassion, resilience, great listening skills, and have a passion for the outdoors, fun and developing young people. You will help provide a fun, caring, safe environment in which young people will learn to appreciate nature, enjoy the challenges of adventure activities while creating lasting friendships and memories. You will deliver age appropriate, predetermined curriculum in a variety of camp activities including, but not limited to: target sports, ecology, crafts, swimming, boating, hiking, team challenges, high ropes, photography, sports and games and/or rock climbing. Applicants are required to reside on site and in cabins with children.<br><br><strong>We have the following positions available:</strong></div><ul><li>Jr Camp Counselors</li><li>Camp Counselors</li><li>Health Care Coordinator</li><li>Wellness Coordinator</li><li>Program Coordinator</li><li>Logistics Coordinator</li><li>Head Counselor</li></ul><div><br><strong>Jr Camp Counselor:</strong></div><div>Jr Camp Counselors are responsible for the daily care and supervision of up to 10 children charged under their care, ranging from ages 5 to 17. Jr Camp Counselors will be paired with a co-counselor to support their supervision of campers. They will foster and maintain positive developmentally sound relationships between and with campers, assist in personal care as needed, be attentive at all times, and assist with a variety of camp activities. These include, but are not limited to, target sports, rock climbing, crafts, and aquatic activities.</div><div>Pay starts at $290.<br><br><strong>Camp Counselor:<br></strong>Camp Counselors are responsible for the daily care and supervision of up to 10 children charged under their care, ranging from ages 5 to 17. Camp Counselors will foster and maintain positive developmentally sound relationships between and with campers, assist in personal care as needed, be attentive at all times, and assist with a variety of camp activities and programs. These include, but are not limited to, target sports, rock climbing, crafts, and aquatic activities.</div><div>Pay starts at $300.<br><br><strong>Health Care Coordinator:<br></strong>The Health Care Coordinator will be responsible for the general healthcare of our campers during the summer. This individual will be responsible for the organization of medical forms, dispensing medications, providing minor first aid and responding to emergency situations by calling in proper medical technicians if needed.</div><div>Pay starts at $425.<br><br><strong>Wellness Coordinator:<br></strong>The Wellness Coordinator supports the mental, emotional, and social well-being of both campers and staff. This role assists in de-escalating high-stress or emotionally charged situations, providing guidance, documenting incidents and ensuring appropriate follow-up. The Wellness Coordinator works closely with the Program Director to document and monitor ongoing camper or staff concerns, helping maintain a safe, supportive, and integrated camp community. <br>Pay starts at $425.<br><br><strong>Program Coordinator:<br></strong>The Program Coordinator is accountable for upholding strong risk-management standards, ensuring that all camp programs are delivered safely, consistent quality checks, efficiently, and in alignment with organizational policies. This position is responsible for overseeing all program materials and ensuring their appropriate use throughout camp operations. <br>Pay starts at $425.<br><br><strong>Logistics Coordinator:<br></strong>The Logistics Coordinator is responsible for managing cabin and cleaning supply inventories, and operational resources. Logistics Coordinator ensures the camp environment remains clean, organized, and fully equipped, enabling smooth and safe operations across all departments. <br>Pay starts at $425. <strong><br><br>Head Counselor:<br></strong>The Head Counselor is responsible for supporting staff management processes, including scheduling, performance communication, and facilitating healthy team dynamics. This role ensures staff members have the support, clarity, and resources needed to perform their duties effectively, contributing to a positive and mission-aligned work culture. <br>Pay starts at $425. <br><br><strong>Work Schedule:<br></strong>Workdays will begin around noon on Sunday of each week and end around noon on Friday. Workdays are subject to fluctuations from time to time. These positions demand a high level of responsibility and attention to detail. You will be with the campers 24/7 for meal times and activities to ensure order, timeliness, and cleanliness throughout the camp week.<br><br><em>Once all applications have been reviewed, you will be assigned a position based on your qualifications for that position. We will then notify you as soon as possible regarding the position to which you are assigned.</em></div> 112413 - Millstone 4-h Camp https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234547 2026-09-02T14:21:40-04:00 2026-09-02T14:21:42-04:00 Research Engineer <ul><li><strong>Reactor Operations and Training:</strong> Receive comprehensive training to obtain licensure as a Senior Reactor Operator from the U.S. Nuclear Regulatory Commission (NRC).</li><li><strong>System Design and Maintenance:</strong> Participate in the design, installation, testing, operation, and maintenance of reactor plant and experimental facility systems.</li><li><strong>Procedure and Protocol Development:</strong> Develop detailed specifications for reactor and experiment system components, including design requirements and operational and maintenance procedures. Create and implement laboratory safety and radiation protection procedures and protocols to support reactor research.</li><li><strong>Computational Modeling and Simulation:</strong> Formulate and execute reactor core and system simulations using high-performance computing (HPC) resources to ensure safe reactor operation.</li><li><strong>Research and Development:</strong> Design experimental facilities and conduct complex research utilizing computational resources. Formulate research methodologies and propose enhancements to improve research quality.</li><li><strong>Documentation and Publication:</strong> Contribute to project documentation and reporting, author scholarly papers, and support the development of proposals for new projects.</li><li><strong>Nuclear System Analysis:</strong> Perform nuclear system simulations, experimental facility design, and nuclear data generation to support the safe operation of the PULSTAR nuclear research reactor. Utilize HPC resources to run atomistic simulation, nuclear data generation, and radiation transport codes.</li></ul> 141201 - Nuclear Reactor Program https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230570 2026-05-13T11:39:37-04:00 2026-05-13T11:39:38-04:00 Next Step Adviser (2026 - 2027 Cohort) <div>The <strong>Division of Academic &amp; Student Affairs</strong> (DASA) is where students discover connections and community. It is where students live, where they gather, where they improve themselves, each other, and their university. DASA is where students seek guidance and support, both in and outside of the classroom. The division serves all students at NC State through three integrated components: academic affairs, student affairs, and University College.</div><div><br>NC State wants employees to achieve their highest potential at work, learn more about our employee value proposition here (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>You Belong Here</strong></a>). The Division of Academic and Student Affairs is the place to work for values-driven faculty and staff who are passionate about promoting the success of the whole student.<br><br>The <strong>Advise NC at NC State</strong> is a postsecondary access program that aims to increase the post-secondary enrollment for high school students across North Carolina. Advise NC at NC State recruits recent college graduates to serve as “near peer” Next Step Advisers in high-need high schools across the state. Our program is dedicated to closing the gap in postsecondary attainment and ensuring all North Carolina students have access to pathways beyond high school.</div><div><br><br></div><div><br></div><div><strong>AmeriCorps Next Step Advisers</strong> serve in a partner high school to increase the post-secondary enrollment among all students.<br><br>Next Step Advisers:</div><ul><li>Provide one-on-one admissions and financial aid advising to any student or family seeking assistance</li><li>Organize group events that encourage students and their families to consider, plan for, and apply to post-secondary opportunities</li><li>Establish productive and strategic relationships with principals, counselors, and teachers within the partner school</li><li>Enhance visibility of the role through school and community outreach</li><li>Document and analyze student interactions to assist in the assessment and long-term sustainability of the program</li><li>Maintain expertise in admissions and financial aid advising</li></ul><div><br><strong>Additional Details</strong></div><div><br></div><div>AmeriCorps Next Step Advisers:</div><ul><li>Report to NC State-based program staff, as well as a high school-based onsite supervisors</li><li>Participate in paid training during the summer and ongoing position-related training throughout the program year</li><li>Participate in ongoing personal professional development throughout the program year</li><li>Commit to one academic year (11 months) of service with a preferred two-year commitment if mutually agreed upon by the program staff and the Next Step Adviser.</li><li>Must be able to relocate to the service area and be involved in the surrounding community</li></ul> 240407 - Advise NC at NC State https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232708 2026-07-14T08:14:19-04:00 2026-07-14T08:14:20-04:00 Lecturer - BAE <div>This position is a 9-month teaching appointment within the Department of Biological and Agricultural Engineering. The successful candidate will teach up to five synchronous courses annually in support of Biological Engineering, Biological &amp; Agricultural Engineering Technology, and Agricultural Institute programs. Course assignments may vary based on departmental needs.<br><br></div><div>Primary teaching responsibilities include lecture and laboratory instruction in areas of engineering circuits, agricultural electrification, agricultural machinery systems, turf and landscape equipment technology, and related applied technical subjects such as shop processes and management, or agricultural health and safety as assigned.<br><br></div><div>Courses include significant hands-on and laboratory-based learning experiences involving electrical circuits, agricultural electrical systems, machinery diagnostics, engine disassembly and reassembly, equipment operation, troubleshooting, maintenance, and safety practices.<br><br></div><div>The position is expected to support student success through effective instruction, mentoring, and academic engagement. Additional responsibilities may include curriculum and laboratory development, coordination of instructional equipment and facilities, maintenance of laboratory safety standards, and service to the department, college, and university.</div> 118101 - Biological And Agricultural Engineering https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233688 2026-08-10T13:12:21-04:00 2026-08-10T13:12:22-04:00 Research Technician (Lab and Field) - DEPP <div>This position plays a key role in planning and executing research protocols, collecting and analyzing data, maintaining research materials, and contributing to scientific reports and project outcomes. The Research Technician will coordinate, conduct, and oversee research activities supporting laboratory and field-based experiments. The Research Technician will also lead research support activities by providing guidance and coordination to other staff involved in research projects.&nbsp; &nbsp;<br><br></div><div><strong><br>Key responsibilities include:<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Coordinate and maintain laboratory and field research trials, ensuring experiments are conducted accurately and according to established protocols.</li><li>Lead the setup, maintenance, monitoring, and harvest of field research plots to support ongoing research initiatives.</li><li>Calibrate, operate, and maintain backpack sprayers and other specialized research equipment.</li><li>Prepare, mix, and apply pesticides, chemicals, and other research materials in compliance with all university, state, and federal regulations.</li><li>Observe, collect, and accurately record research data, including disease incidence, severity, plant injury, defoliation, and crop yield.</li><li>Maintain plants, microbial cultures, and other biological research materials to support laboratory projects.</li><li>Plan and implement experimental methods, adapting research techniques and procedures as project needs evolve.</li><li>Assist with data analysis, report preparation, and communicating research progress to principal investigators and project teams.</li><li>Maintain inventories of laboratory equipment, field supplies, chemicals, and research materials to ensure uninterrupted research operations.</li><li>Provide day-to-day guidance and coordination for research support staff, promoting safe and efficient laboratory and field operations.<br><br></li></ul> 111801 - Entomology and Plant Pathology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223367 2025-09-25T16:36:52-04:00 2025-09-25T16:36:52-04:00 Temporary Sign Language Interpreter <div>The Temporary ASL Interpreter Pool is intended for qualified applicants to be called upon for appointments on an as-needed basis of limited duration. By applying to this pooled position, applicants are not applying for a specific position. Instead, applicants are submitting an application to be considered for temporary, on-call, and some regularly anticipated needs that may arise to serve our students, staff, faculty, and visitors in both the classroom and at events.</div><div><br></div><div>The individual(s) in this position is responsible for providing sign language accommodations under the mandates of Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990 and NC State University policies. Sign language interpreters facilitate communication between Deaf, Hard-of-Hearing, DeafBlind and hearing consumers. To facilitate communication, interpreters use American Sign Language (ASL), Signed English, Contact sign, Oral Transliterating, and/or Cued Speech. Interpreters must be able to adjust to a broad range of consumer needs and preferences and work comfortably with a wide spectrum of interpreting services which requires a high degree of linguistic, cognitive and technical skill. Interpreters must be able to accurately convey the register, tone, intent and content of the consumer’s message.</div> 383401 - Disability Resource Office https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234348 2026-08-28T17:05:33-04:00 2026-08-28T17:05:34-04:00 Feline Health Center Veterinary Technician <div>The position is for an individual that is either a NC Registered Veterinary Technician (RVT) or is eligible for the NC RVT exam. <br><br>The position interacts with internal specialty services and their respective staff, clinicians and students to provide coordination and veterinary care for feline patients.<br><strong><br>Duties include but are not limited to:</strong></div><ul><li>Support the I-131 (hyperthyroidism treatment) program</li><li>Provide excellent patient care by applying full knowledge of anatomy, physiology, disease processes, pharmacology, specialized equipment usage, and specialized drug administration for feline patients.</li><li>Demonstrate and encourage the use of feline-friendly low-stress handling techniques.</li><li>Work directly with clients, rDVM, and hospital personnel to facilitate communication and anticipate, integrate and coordinate services that optimize both internal and external satisfaction and overall patient care.</li><li>Participate in client check in, patient admitting, updates, scheduling, and questions.</li><li>Perform various technical procedures including; anesthesia support and monitoring, Intravenous catheter placement, venipuncture, IV injections, drug administration (IV, Oral, Subcutaneous), inpatient hospitalization care, and various routine diagnostic testing.</li><li>Participate in supervising, coaching, teaching, orienting and evaluating in the principals of feline friendly practices for veterinary students and house officers.</li><li>Support in-house training of staff to comply with feline friendly practices</li><li>Provide support for feline-related clinical studies conducted at the College of Veterinary Medicine by adhering to research protocols, obtaining client consent, collecting and processing patient samples, and collecting clinical trial data.</li><li>Record a variety of data relating to patient care, diagnostic testing and treatments, and client communications.</li><li>Provide quick and accurate information regarding each patient, acting as the liaison between client and veterinarian.</li><li>Enter data into electronic medical records in a proficient manner.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>The position also provides programmatic assistance for the Feline Health Center:</strong></div><ul><li>Organize, coordinate, and develop initiatives to meet program needs and enhance the success of the Feline Health Center and constituents</li><li>Emphasize cooperation with administration to ensure appropriate and effective flow of patients and program initiatives</li><li>Assist with reviewing, updating, and preparing communications, marketing, and program databases. Ensure accuracy of data and compliance with program standards.</li><li>Evaluate, research and assist with procurement of feline clinical supplies and equipment</li><li>Assist with symposium, community outreach and educational events</li><li>Pursue ongoing continuing education to support career growth and maintenance of licensure</li><li>Partner with North Carolina Veterinary Medical Foundation for philanthropic development, including help with stewardship and cultivation of existing and potential donors to the Feline Health Center.</li></ul> 191901 - Research https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228810 2026-03-26T16:45:20-04:00 2026-03-26T16:45:21-04:00 Lecturer (Educational Leadership) <div><strong>Essential Job Duties</strong></div><div>The Department of Education Leadership, Policy, and Human Development (ELPHD) in the College of Education is accepting applications for the position of Lecturer – Professional Track Faculty (part-time) for the 2026 - 2027 Academic Year. Professional track Faculty positions do not carry rank and are one-semester or one-year, with the possibility of renewal.</div><div><br></div><div>Successful candidates will teach 1-3 courses on the undergraduate or graduate curriculum, depending on the department’s needs. Teaching is in Educational Leadership.</div><div><br></div><div>This position will remain open through the end of the academic year; however, we will be screening applicants on an ongoing basis.</div> 131601 - Educational Leadership, Policy & Human Development (ELPHD) https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223291 2025-09-25T11:30:33-04:00 2025-09-25T11:30:33-04:00 Research Assistant This position, up to 40 hours per week, will support research activities within the Research Group at ITRE primarily in the areas of roadway monitoring and roadway modeling, among others. This position will assist with research tasks related to monitoring roadways for safety and operations, as well as modeling roadway networks and processing and analyzing data. Additional tasks may be assigned to this position in other relevant areas, based on the candidate’s background and expertise. The candidate should also be proficient in reporting writing, analysis, and communicating with the research team and the project sponsor. 062001 - Institute For Transportation Research & Education https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234252 2026-08-27T10:00:06-04:00 2026-08-27T10:00:07-04:00 Research Associate, Strategy and Impact <div>The Research Associate will report to the Executive Director of the Friday Institute and will provide direct support to coordinate across initiatives, communicate clearly with internal and external partners, and maintain shared visibility into priorities, progress, and decisions. A core function of this role is extending reach—helping to author, package, publish, and report on its work so that high-quality ideas and programming become usable artifacts, visible impact, and sustained momentum with partners and funders. The primary work centers on connecting the dots across initiatives by identifying patterns, surfacing insights, coordinating collaborators, and ensuring projects move from planning to high-quality execution.<br><br></div><div>In this role, you will help turn complex ideas, research, and partner feedback into clear strategies, actionable plans, and meaningful outcomes. You will connect people and priorities across projects, bringing structure to work that spans multiple initiatives and partners. Your ability to see the big picture while managing important details will help teams make informed decisions, strengthen programs, and continuously improve. You will also help amplify the Friday Institute’s work by creating clear, compelling materials and communications that demonstrate impact, support partnerships, and build momentum for future opportunities.<br><br></div><div>Key responsibilities fall within the following functional areas:</div><div><br></div><div><strong>Program strategy and synthesis:</strong></div><ul><li>Conduct structured inquiry (interviews, listening sessions, document review) to understand needs, constraints, and opportunities across partners and initiatives.</li><li>Synthesize research, practitioner feedback, and implementation data into clear insights, recommendations, and decision points for project teams and leadership.</li><li>Translate strategy into actionable workplans (milestones, dependencies, risks, resourcing) and maintain visibility across the portfolio.</li><li>Identify gaps, overlaps, and opportunities for alignment across initiatives; propose pragmatic ways to streamline and strengthen the portfolio.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Evidence and improvement:</strong></div><ul><li>Define and track meaningful indicators of progress (implementation readiness, participation, adoption signals, quality measures, partner satisfaction).</li><li>Support lightweight evaluation and learning cycles: collect feedback, document lessons learned, and integrate improvements into next iterations.</li><li>Monitor the broader landscape (policy, research, edtech and AI developments) and flag implications for program design and partner needs.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Convenings, coordination, and communications:</strong></div><ul><li>Facilitate and/or contribute to convenings, training, and other partner-facing learning opportunities.</li><li>Coordinate project meetings, working groups, governance structures, and partner communications to support timely decisions and follow-through.</li><li>Prepare, maintain, and update partner-ready project materials for online and print dissemination (briefs, agendas, decision memos, implementation supports), ensuring outputs are clear, consistent, and publication-ready.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Growth and resourcing:</strong></div><ul><li>Contribute to project and grant proposals, including framing problems and possibilities of practice and aligning deliverables.</li><li>Draft and coordinate funder-facing reports and updates that communicate progress, evidence, and impact with clarity and credibility</li><li>Document project activity to capture outcomes, lessons learned, and next-phase recommendations.</li><li>Track and support a pipeline of aligned opportunities; maintain portfolio-level visibility into funding status, commitments, and next steps.</li></ul><div><br>If you are a curious, collaborative, adaptable professional who enjoys connecting ideas, people, and possibilities to move meaningful work forward, we encourage you to apply.</div> 139001 - Friday Institute https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228250 2026-03-09T17:49:17-04:00 2026-03-09T17:49:18-04:00 Temporary Laboratory Assistant <div>Data entry, food sample preparation (food grade lab). This role is crucial for ensuring the smooth operation of fermentation studies, sensory evaluations, and maintaining high-quality data integrity.<br><br></div> 111101 - Food, Bioprocessing & Nutrition Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234685 2026-09-03T16:54:04-04:00 2026-09-03T16:54:05-04:00 Data Science Lead <div>The Data Science Lead will play a pivotal role in advancing NC State’s data and analytics capabilities. Applying advanced analytical and statistical techniques, methods, and tools, this role will develop and implement predictive models and other sophisticated analytics solutions. Working within University Data and Analytics (UDA), the Data Science Lead oversees a specialized team of up to three data scientists dedicated to developing and delivering analytical products and services that provide actionable insights, drive strategic decision-making, and enhance overall institutional effectiveness.</div><div><br>As the Data Science Lead, you will oversee a small team of data scientists responsible for developing advanced analytical solutions that support institutional planning and decision-making at NC State. You will apply statistical methods, predictive modeling, artificial intelligence, and machine learning to analyze complex university data and address institutional needs and uncover insights that support better outcomes. Additionally, you will coordinate data science projects, collaborate with partners across University Data and Analytics (UDA), and provide day-to-day leadership and supervision of the Data Science team.<br><br></div><div><strong>Key Impact Areas include:</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Develop Advanced Analytics Solutions:</strong> Design, develop, and optimize predictive models, artificial intelligence and machine learning solutions, and other advanced analytics to address university needs and support institutional decision-making.</li><li><strong>Supervise and Support the Data Science Team:</strong> Provide day-to-day supervision and leadership for the Data Science team, including coordinating work assignments, setting team priorities, coaching and developing staff, managing performance, and supporting a collaborative environment focused on continuous improvement and high-quality work.</li><li><strong>Collaborate Across UDA: </strong>Partner with Institutional Reporting, Research and Analysis, Business Intelligence, Data Governance, UDA leadership, and other stakeholders to provide data science expertise and support cross-functional initiatives.</li><li><strong>Coordinate Data Science Projects: </strong>Coordinate the execution of data science initiatives by tracking project progress, facilitating collaboration among stakeholders, managing project timelines and risks, and supporting alignment with UDA and institutional priorities.</li><li><strong>Support Responsible and Reliable Data Science Practices: </strong>Apply appropriate practices for model development and evaluation, data governance, security, privacy, and ethical use to support accurate, reliable, and responsible analytical solutions.<br><br></li></ul><div>If you are a person who has strong technical data science expertise, experience working with complex data and analytical problems, and the ability to supervise and support a small team of data scientists, we encourage you to apply.</div> 310101 – University Data and Analytics https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231064 2026-05-29T12:35:48-04:00 2026-05-29T12:35:48-04:00 UTS - Temporary Japanese Language Extension Instructor at NC State <div>The Japanese Language Extension Instructor shall teach varying levels of non-credit Japanese language courses, based on their skill and experience, to a mix of students from the community. Instructors shall teach a minimum of one course per semester, for a total minimum of three courses per year. Each course lasts fifteen weeks, with one class per week and each class lasts 2 hours. There may be exceptions for special courses designed by the instructor and the EECOM that may modify the number of weeks, classes per week, and class duration.<br><br>Based on experience, skill, and interest, instructors will be assigned to courses ranging from beginner level up to preparation for the JLPT N1 exam. In order to stay consistent with for-credit courses and most universities in NC, classes are taught based on the Genki and Quartet series. Based on student demand and Instructor availability, Instructors may teach more than one class per week, but the total number of weekly hours shall be fewer than thirty hours.<br><br><strong>Courses are offered in both in-person and online formats; instructors may be assigned to teach either modality or a combination of both.</strong></div> 480501 - UHR Temporary Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232959 2026-07-21T13:23:17-04:00 2026-07-21T13:23:18-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>We are a dynamic research team focused on elucidating the molecular mechanisms of plant-virus-vector interactions. Our mission is to develop new foundational knowledge of plant viruses and to translate this information into new control strategies and biotechnology tools for crop improvement.<br><br>Grapevine red blotch disease has emerged as a major threat to winegrape production throughout North America in recent decades. We aim to develop tools and protocols for grapevine red blotch virus (GRBV) detection in insects and plants. We will integrate these new technologies with established methods to better understand GRBV localization in the insect vector throughout circulative transmission and make strides in understanding GRBV latency in grapevine and experimental plant hosts. The detection tools developed will also have applications for red blotch diagnostics.<br><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>The postdoctoral research scholar will express GRBV proteins in prokaryotic and eukaryotic (insect and plant) cells and purify proteins for antibody production. (50%)</li><li>Characterize GRBV dissemination and tissue tropism in insect vectors with in situ methods. (50%)</li><li>Molecular analysis of viral genomes, gene expression, and gene function.</li><li>Primer design, DNA/RNA handling, protein expression, Western blotting.</li><li>Develop and test standardized procedures for determining vector competence on candidate vectors.</li></ul> 111801 - Entomology and Plant Pathology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233935 2026-08-18T11:39:28-04:00 2026-08-18T11:39:29-04:00 Agriculture Marketing & Content Creator Internship <div>Capture,edit, prepare and upload agricultural educational content from small grains and Official<br>Variety Trial programs to multi-media platforms. These may include NC State University<br>web portals, social media accounts associated with the program including but not limited to<br>facebook, X and Instagram. Content creation will be both short and long form. Short form<br>to include images with written captions or short video clips with closed captioning. Long<br>form will include 2 to 5 minute video content describing an agricultural concept with closed<br>captioning. Development of written and visual program marketing materials will also be<br>required, along with attendance at grower meetings and larger industry events to deliver<br>those materials to stakeholders.</div> 110901 - Crop and Soil Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/226034 2025-12-18T19:08:56-05:00 2025-12-18T19:08:57-05:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>We are looking for independent and highly motivated candidate to join our active and vibrant laboratory. The primary research focus of our laboratory is to study the host innate immune response as a barrier to cross-species retrovirus transmission in the non-human primates. We employ cutting-edge and novel, innovative approaches to study the replication of chimeric SIV/HIV viruses (SHIVs) in the macaque model of HIV-1 infection. Our laboratory is tackling a fundamental weakness in the HIV-1/AIDS field that affects all downstream aspects of HIV-1 pathogenesis, vaccine, and prevention research by developing more relevant SHIV/macaque models of HIV-1 infection.<br><br></div><div>The fully-funded project available for the postdoctoral fellow is to characterize the viral and host factors that drive selection, adaptation, and pathogenicity of HIV-1 and SHIVs (Sharma et al, PLOS Pathogens 2019, Vilmen et al, mBio 2022). The project will also focus on uncovering the molecular and mechanistic basis of adaptation of SHIVs to the macaque innate and adaptive immune responses (Sharma et al, J Med Primatol., 2015, Boyd et al, PLOS Pathogens 2016). This research project is highly interdisciplinary and built on the conceptual and technical skills from multiple unique fields of research – it combines expertise in HIV-1 transmission biology, translational immunology, and epidemiology with basic training in HIV-1 molecular virology, host cell biology and characterization of viral-host interactions.<br><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Research related to virology.</li><li><strong>80% Research: </strong>Participates in experimental research focusing on studying host innate immune responses as a barrier to cross-species retrovirus transmission in the non-human primates; characterizes the viral and host factors that drive selection, adaptation, and pathogenicity of SHIVs in macaque cells ex vivo; uncover the molecular and mechanistic basis of adaptation of SHIVs to the macaque innate and adaptive immune responses; generates experimental data to support publications, PI’s current active and future grant proposals and seek opportunities for independent funding.</li><li><strong>15% Writing:</strong> Assists in the preparation of research reports, manuscripts, seminars, and presentations for ongoing research progress.</li><li><strong>5% </strong>Other organizational and lab management duties as assigned.&nbsp;</li><li>Successful completion of a University background check is required.&nbsp;</li><li>Requires weekend, nights, and/or holiday hours</li></ul> 196001 - Department of Population Health and Pathobiology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234456 2026-09-01T10:44:44-04:00 2026-09-01T10:44:45-04:00 UTS - Temporary Laborer/Delivery Driver at NC State <div>To assist the Library Operations, Logistics, and Environments department with daily operational tasks, including:<br>- Furniture Moves &amp; Rearrangement<br>- Whiteboard Cleaning &amp; Morning Building Resets<br>- Assist with Mailroom Delivery/Distribution Services.<br>- Assist with event set-up and break-downs as needed.<br><br></div> 480501 - UHR Temporary Services https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/229643 2026-04-17T09:24:44-04:00 2026-04-17T09:24:45-04:00 CLAWS Packaging Engineer <div>We are seeking an experienced semiconductor device Packaging Engineer to join our research foundry team, supporting the development of advanced RF, Power, and Photonic devices and integrated circuits. The ideal candidate will have expertise in the development and integration of packaging processes as well as package design and assembly. The engineer will work closely with device, process, and test engineers to define interconnect strategies, perform thermal and mechanical analysis, and ensure compatibility with wafer-level processes and test infrastructure.<br><br><strong>Key Responsibilities:</strong><br><strong><br>Lead packaging of wide bandgap semiconductor devices (30%)</strong></div><ul><li>RF devices including HEMTs and MMICs</li><li>High power electronic devices including HEMTs, FETs, and diodes</li><li>Visible spectrum optoelectronic devices and Photonic Integrated Circuits.</li></ul><div><strong>Packaging Design and Assembly Workflows (25%)</strong></div><ul><li>Specify package solutions that meet the performance requirements of new technology platforms in collaboration with CLAWS teams.</li><li>Define scalable workflows to realize packaged discrete devices, integrated circuits, or modules.</li><li>Participate in cross-functional design initiatives that improve performance, manufacturability, yield, or reliability.</li></ul><div><strong>Process Integration (25%)</strong></div><ul><li>Lead the development and integration of processes in, for example, die attach, wire bonding, and encapsulation.</li><li>Analyze package and module performance. Inform electrical, thermal, and mechanical simulations with design and characterization data.</li><li>Troubleshoot process integration challenges related to defectivity, yield, and reliability.</li></ul><div><strong>Cross-functional Collaboration (20%)</strong></div><ul><li>Collaborate with circuit designers, applications engineers, and product development teams to realize integrated system-level advantages.</li><li>Stay up to date with emerging technology trends, competitive landscape, and industry benchmarks.</li></ul> 140110 - CLAWS https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232224 2026-06-29T11:26:13-04:00 2026-06-29T11:26:13-04:00 Temporary Lab Technician <div>Daily duties include:</div><ul><li>Assist research scientists with ongoing field projects on local and State water bodies</li><li>Daily travel and field work in all weather conditions.&nbsp;</li><li>Support of general laboratory-based activities</li><li>Maintenance of field equipment, calibration of sensors, and detailed log keeping&nbsp;</li><li>Other laboratory and field duties may be required as deemed by supervisor&nbsp;</li></ul><div><br><strong>Work Schedule:</strong><br>10-15 hours per week between 9am-6pm M-F, with flexibility as needed depending on field conditions</div> 112101 - Applied Ecology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233428 2026-08-03T09:18:36-04:00 2026-08-03T09:18:36-04:00 Temporary Event Technician Pool, University Theatre, Backstage & Front of House <div>The <strong>Division of Academic &amp; Student Affairs </strong>(DASA) is where students discover connections and community. It is where students live, where they gather, where they improve themselves, each other, and their university. DASA is where students seek guidance and support, both in and outside of the classroom. The division serves all students at NC State through three integrated components: academic affairs, student affairs, and University College.</div><div><br></div><div>NC State wants employees to achieve their highest potential at work, learn more about our employee value proposition here (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>You Belong Here</strong></a>). The Division of Academic and Student Affairs is the place to work for values-driven faculty and staff who are passionate about promoting the success of the whole student.</div><div><br><strong>University Theatre</strong> is the campus’ open access community theatre and operates multiple facilities: Stewart Theatre, in the Talley Student Center, and the Titmus Theatre and Kennedy McIlwee Studio Theatre, along with the associated shops and classroom spaces for producing theatre.<br><br></div><div>Our mission is to create a welcoming, student-centered community that inspires meaningful connections grounded in the experience of making and sharing theatre. We present a wide variety of productions, experiences and provide spaces for campus organizations to present events.</div><div><br></div><div><strong><em>Please note</em></strong>: This position is based on need and availability. Applicants will be reviewed on a rolling basis.<br><br><strong>Event Technician </strong>will assist Operations and Event Coordinator, Technical and Assistant Technical Directors in day to day operations of University Theatre venues in support of campus events, rental clients and University Theatre performances as required.<br><br>Duties will include:</div><ul><li>Backstage duties as assigned, including but not limited to lighting operation (lighting consoles, follow spotlights), sound reinforcement (FOH console operation, audio system setup), load-in and load-out assistance, occasional system and facilities maintenance.</li><li>Front of house technicians: position will serve as house manager, responsible for coordinating all Front-Of-House (FOH) operations in the Theatre spaces.</li></ul><div><br>Specific responsibilities include</div><ul><li>Training and supervising ushers</li><li>Communicating with stage managers and technical supervisors during and in advance of performances</li><li>Ensuring the safety of all patrons using theatre spaces.</li><li>Experience in dealing with the public of all ages</li><li>Sensitivity to the needs of patrons</li><li>Upbeat, enthusiastic attitude in public relations</li><li>Ability to enforce department safety regulations and communicate the same to the public</li><li>Attention to detail</li><li>Ability to troubleshoot problems and work for customer satisfaction</li><li>Ability to work independently with limited direct supervision.</li></ul> 248901 - University Theatre https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/231730 2026-06-15T15:47:38-04:00 2026-06-15T15:47:40-04:00 Senior Facilities Maintenance Engineer <div>Join our Facilities team as a <strong>Senior Facilities Maintenance Engineer</strong>, where you will play a critical role in shaping the performance, reliability, and sustainability of campus building systems. This position provides high-level engineering expertise supporting the design, construction, and ongoing operations of complex facilities infrastructure.</div><div><br><strong>In this role, you will apply advanced engineering knowledge to evaluate, troubleshoot, and improve building systems across campus, including HVAC, mechanical, steam, plumbing, life safety, and specialized lab environments.</strong> You will collaborate with cross-functional teams, guide project execution, and help drive solutions for complex renovation and operational challenges that directly impact the campus community.</div><div><br>This is an exciting opportunity for a forward-thinking engineer who enjoys solving complex problems, influencing system design, and ensuring facilities operate at peak efficiency. If you are passionate about engineering excellence, collaboration, and making a tangible impact in a dynamic campus environment, we encourage you to apply.</div><div><strong><br></strong><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include, but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li>Provide expert engineering support for the design, construction, and operation of campus facilities.</li><li>Analyze, troubleshoot, and resolve complex issues across building systems, including HVAC, mechanical, steam, plumbing, life safety, and laboratory environments.</li><li><strong>Lead design reviews of building systems and infrastructure</strong> to ensure compliance with applicable codes, standards, and best practices.</li><li>Develop project scopes, plans, and technical specifications aligned with regulatory and university requirements.</li><li>Guide and support project management efforts, including oversight of junior staff, temporary employees, and interns.</li><li>Manage the Facilities Warranty Program, including reviewing as-built documents and O&amp;M manuals, leading quarterly review meetings, assigning investigations, and resolving warranty-related issues.</li><li>Evaluate system performance and recommend improvements to enhance operational efficiency and reliability.</li><li>Collaborate with Facilities Division units and campus stakeholders to support project planning, execution, and operational needs.</li><li>Manage external designers and consultants; develop in-house schematic drawings as needed.</li><li>Prepare and review project budgets and cost estimates.</li><li>Ensure compliance with applicable building codes and standards within state government and university environments.</li><li><strong>Coordinate resolution of post-construction issues and support training on new systems and equipment.</strong></li><li>Oversee the design review portal, ensuring timely communication and resolution of reported issues.</li><li>Maintain warranty records, track work orders, and ensure timely follow-up on all warranty-related items.</li><li>Manage attic stock inventory and maintain documentation for project checklists and system assets.</li><li>Track and report cost avoidance associated with completed warranty repairs.</li></ul> 445030 - Building Maintenance & Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233203 2026-07-27T16:27:49-04:00 2026-07-27T16:27:52-04:00 Sterile Processing Assistant <div>The primary purpose of this position is to support the NC State Veterinary Hospitals by providing sterile processing services for surgical instrumentation and equipment to ensure safe, efficient, and uninterrupted patient care. This position is responsible for the decontamination, inspection, assembly, sterilization, inventory management, and distribution of surgical instruments and supplies while maintaining compliance with hospital policies and regulatory guidelines. The role also serves as a key support resource for clinical teams by delivering exceptional customer service and helping ensure the operational readiness of surgical services throughout the Veterinary Hospital.<br><br></div><div><strong>Sterile Processing &amp; Instrument Reprocessing:</strong></div><ul><li>Provide sterile processing support for the hospitals through the decontamination, inspection, assembly, sterilization, and distribution of surgical instrumentation and equipment in accordance with hospital and regulatory guidelines.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Customer Service &amp; Clinical Support:</strong></div><ul><li>Deliver exceptional customer service to clinical teams by responding to questions regarding surgical packs, instrumentation availability, equipment operation, and procedural supply needs.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Operating Room Coordination:</strong></div><ul><li>Collaborate closely with Operating Room staff to support surgical scheduling, room turnover, and rapid instrument turnaround to ensure equipment and instrumentation needs are consistently met for procedures.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Inventory &amp; Systems Management:</strong></div><ul><li>Maintain and manage surgical instrumentation, implant inventory, and component lists within ISIP Surgical to ensure accuracy, availability, and operational efficiency.</li></ul><div><strong><br>Department Operations &amp; Purchasing:</strong></div><ul><li>Support departmental operations through coordination of equipment and instrumentation rentals, billing activities, and purchasing of implants and sterile processing supplies to maintain uninterrupted workflow and high-quality service.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Professional Standards &amp; Operations</strong></div><ul><li>Safety &amp; Compliance: Maintain a clean, safe, and functional workspace in accordance with OSHA, EHS, radiation safety, hospital infectious disease policies, and sterile processing standards.</li><li>Team Collaboration &amp; Communication: Participate actively in problem-solving and team advancement while working independently with sound judgment and seeking assistance when appropriate.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Operational Agility &amp; Physical Demands:</strong></div><ul><li>Demonstrate the ability to lift up to 50 pounds with or without reasonable accommodations, work safely around potential hazards including zoonotic diseases, chemicals, and gases, and extend work hours as needed to support patient care and hospital operations.</li></ul> 192001 - CVM Teaching Hospital https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223233 2025-09-25T10:50:48-04:00 2025-09-25T10:50:48-04:00 Temp Project Youth Extension Service Staff Intern Project YES interns provide youth development programs for children and youth of military families throughout the United States and US territories.  Programs may be delivered during Yellow Ribbon events (any phase) and other youth centric activities, i.e. councils, leadership summits, family days, camps, etc.  Staff interns are not required to be North Carolina State University students.  Interns remain at their home university and will travel to event sites to facilitate Project YES programs. Project YES provides high level education in leadership development, facilitation skills, youth development and age-appropriate curriculum.  Project YES interns will also be required to complete a career-enhancing project during the course of the internship.  This will be accomplished with the assistance of an individually matched mentor. Together, the mentor and intern will complete a variety of activities designed to better inform and prepare the intern to transition into the work force.\r\n\r\nWork Schedule - Interns must attend a mandatory Project YES Intern Orientation (22-30 May 2020) and mid-year training (22-25 October 2020) and commit to supporting a minimum of one YRRP weekend per month (Fri-Sun) for the duration of the internship. Following orientation, interns may work 1-2 weekends a month, however interns must commit to a minimum of one weekend per month (Fri-Sun) for the duration of the internship.  Additional opportunities will be presented throughout the year for interns interested in supporting additional weekend events.\r\n\r\n*Please Note - Candidates must also complete an essay and 2 Project YES Letter of Recommendations.  Essay and recommendation form requirements may be found on the Project YES website (http://www.projectyouthextension.com).  .  All documents must be received within the application window.  Incomplete application packages will not be eligible for consideration.* 111401 - Agricultural and Human Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234074 2026-08-21T16:50:58-04:00 2026-08-21T16:50:59-04:00 TRIO Upward Bound Instructor <div><strong>The Division of Academic &amp; Student Affairs</strong> (DASA) is where students discover connections and community. It is where students live, where they gather, where they improve themselves, each other, and their university. DASA is where students seek guidance and support, both in and outside of the classroom. The division serves all students at NC State.</div><div><br></div><div>NC State wants employees to achieve their highest potential at work, learn more about our employee value proposition here (<a href=\"https://news.hr.ncsu.edu/you-belong-here/\"><strong>You Belong Here</strong></a>). The Division of Academic and Student Affairs is the place to work for values-driven faculty and staff who are passionate about promoting the success of the whole student.</div><div><br><strong>The Upward Bound Program</strong> is an early intervention pre-college program that helps students prepare for higher education. Upward Bound brings high school students to college campuses after school, on the weekends, and during the summer to receive instruction in mathematics, laboratory sciences, composition, literature, and foreign languages. Tutoring, counseling, mentoring, cultural enrichment, and work-study programs are additional components to the Upward Bound Program. The NC State University Program serves five high schools (Vance County HS, Louisburg HS, Bunn HS, Franklinton HS, and East Wake HS). The Program has been a part of the University since 1978 and has assisted hundreds of students to enroll in college and gain a college degree.<br><br>The Upward Bound Program is looking for Instructors that will be responsible for:<br><br></div><ul><li>Developing and executing lesson plans in assigned curriculum courses in the following areas:<ul><li>High School Mathematics</li><li>High School English</li><li>High School Science, including physical science and chemistry</li><li>Computer Science, especially AI</li></ul></li><li>Providing written evaluations on each program participant</li></ul> 240405 - TRIO Pre-College https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/227498 2026-02-16T12:38:05-05:00 2026-02-16T12:38:06-05:00 Lecturer-Operations and Supply Chain Management <div>The Department of Information Technology, Analytics, and Operations (ITAO) is accepting applications for the position of Non-Tenure Track Faculty Lecturer (part-time) in the Operations &amp; Supply Chain Management area. Non-Tenure Track Faculty positions are one-semester or one-year, with the possibility of renewal.<br><br>Successful candidates will teach 1-3 courses for the Operations &amp; Supply Chain Management concentration in the BSBA (Bachelor of Science in Business Administration) and/or for the Supply<br>Chain Management concentration in the MBA (Master of Business Administration).<br><br>Specific hiring needs for the academic year have yet to be determined but could include operations modeling, supply management, logistics, supply chain analytics, decision analytics, or operations strategy.</div> 208001 - ITAO - Poole College of Management https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234554 2026-09-02T15:09:30-04:00 2026-09-02T15:09:32-04:00 Facilities Maintenance Technician - Contributing <div>​Join NC State University’s <strong>Building Maintenance and Operations (BMO)</strong> team as a <strong>Facilities Maintenance Technician – Contributing</strong> and build your skills while helping keep our campus facilities safe, reliable, and operating at their best. This hands-on position works within a zone maintenance shop on NC State’s <strong>Original Campus, Centennial Campus, or Centennial Biomedical Campus (Veterinary School)</strong> and supports routine maintenance, operations, and repairs across building systems.</div><div><br>In this role, you’ll develop practical experience across <strong>HVAC, plumbing, electrical, steam, and other building systems</strong> while working alongside experienced mechanics and technicians. You’ll perform preventive maintenance, assist with repairs, operate basic tools and equipment, monitor equipment, identify potential problems, and know when to elevate more complex issues to higher-level mechanics. If you’re dependable, hands-on, eager to learn, and interested in developing your skills in the skilled trades, <strong>we invite you to apply and become part of the NC State Facilities team!</strong></div><div><strong><br></strong><strong><em>Key responsibilities and duties include but are not limited to:</em></strong></div><ul><li>Perform basic maintenance, preventive maintenance, and repairs across <strong>HVAC, plumbing, electrical, steam, and other building systems</strong>.</li><li>Assist higher-level mechanics with maintenance, troubleshooting, repairs, installations, and other assigned work.</li><li>Perform routine tasks such as <strong>lubricating, cleaning, inspecting, re-lamping, repairing plumbing fixtures, and checking equipment</strong>.</li><li>Take readings on boilers, chillers, and other equipment; check chemical levels and report unusual conditions or discrepancies.</li><li>Recognize signs of equipment or system problems, including leaks, unusual noises, temperature changes, electrical outages, and equipment malfunctions.</li><li><strong>Troubleshoot basic maintenance issues and determine potential causes.</strong></li><li>Operate and maintain the basic tools and equipment used in assigned trades and follow appropriate procedures for each task.</li><li>Follow preventive maintenance work orders and maintenance requests, completing assigned work accurately and within established timelines.</li><li>Accurately document work performed, including <strong>time, materials, equipment readings, and identified issues</strong>.</li><li>Develop working knowledge across multiple building systems and cross-train in building equipment operations and maintenance.</li><li>Properly <strong>isolate and lock out equipment and systems</strong> as required before performing maintenance or repairs.</li><li>Maintain a clean, organized, and safe work environment and follow all University safety, health, and established safe-work practices.</li><li>Perform tasks requiring standing for extended periods, working in confined spaces, and utilizing various tools. Safely climb and work from different types of ladders to access elevated or below-grade areas (such as roofs, above-ceiling spaces, and utility pits). Be exposed to and work in areas where insects or small animals are occasionally present. All essential tasks must be performed with or without reasonable accommodations.</li><li>Lift, carry, and maneuver materials weighing 60–90 pounds, with or without reasonable accommodations.</li><li>Communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, and the general public, and follows verbal and written instructions related to work assignments and operational needs.</li></ul> 445030 - Building Maintenance & Operations https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/230612 2026-05-13T16:51:50-04:00 2026-05-13T16:51:51-04:00 Lecturer of Anthropology <div>The Department of Sociology and Anthropology at North Carolina State University (NC State) invites applications for a Lecturer position. Primary teaching responsibilities will be at the undergraduate level for the department’s anthropology programs. Several hires may be made form this posting, with most positions teaching part-time and in person.</div> 168301 - Sociology & Anthropology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/232748 2026-07-14T18:46:17-04:00 2026-07-15T09:29:11-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Scholar - DEPP <div>We are a dynamic research team focused on elucidating the molecular mechanisms of plant-virus-vector interactions. Our mission is to develop new foundational knowledge of plant viruses and to translate this information into new control strategies and biotechnology tools for crop improvement.<br><br>The scientist will be part of a collaborative team using plant rhabdoviruses for crop improvement. Work will focus on optimizing carrying capacity of the virus and delivery of proteins and RNAs to maize using the maize mosaic virus (MMV) vector. The candidate will work with insect-transmitted viruses.<br><br><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong></div><ul><li>Develop weekly research reports for the PI and monthly reports to the funding agency.</li><li>Contribute to proposal writing and disseminating research findings to the broader community.</li><li>Maintenance of virus and insect colonies.</li><li>Training and mentoring other lab members.</li><li>Work will focus on optimizing carrying capacity of the virus and construction of full-length infectious clones of viruses and mutagenesis of regulatory regions (50%)</li><li>Ability to generate infectious clones of plant viruses and/or gene expression experience. Knowledge of genome editing approaches for crop improvement. (50%)</li></ul> 111801 - Entomology and Plant Pathology https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/233724 2026-08-11T11:06:00-04:00 2026-08-11T11:06:01-04:00 Assistant Director, Member Services and Student Employment <div>NC State University is seeking an energetic, innovative, and student-centered leader to serve as the Assistant Director of Member Services and Student Employment. We are looking for a collaborative, proven leader who thrives in a fast-paced, results-oriented environment and is passionate about student success. The ideal candidate will empower both student and professional staff to cultivate world-class wellness and recreation programs, services, and facilities that enrich the student experience inside and outside the classroom.</div><div><br></div><div>The Assistant Director provides strategic leadership and management for Wellness and Recreation member services, point-of-sale (POS) and facility access software, and the department-wide student employment program. Additionally, the candidate will identify and develop alternative revenue streams while adding value to the student experience through special events and programming.</div><div><br></div><div><strong>Specific responsibilities include:<br></strong><br></div><div><strong>Staff Supervision and Human Resource Management&nbsp;</strong></div><ul><li>Direct Supervision: Provide strategic leadership, supervision, and</li><li>performance management for the Coordinator of Member Services and the Student Employment Specialist.</li><li>Indirect Supervision: Indirectly oversee approximately 120 student staff, ensuring the team delivers the highest level of customer service to students and members.</li><li>Department-Wide Student Employment: Manage the comprehensive student employment program (recruitment, hiring, onboarding, orientation, and training) for a department that employs approximately 650 student staff annually.</li><li>Process Optimization: Evaluate current HR workflows to identify efficiencies, develop Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs), and maintain comprehensive training materials for the department.</li><li>Payroll &amp; Timekeeping: Oversee complex department-wide timekeeping and payroll processes to ensure data integrity and strict deadline compliance.</li><li>Student Recognition: Spearhead comprehensive student staff appreciation initiatives, including Student Employee Appreciation Week and university Wellness Days; plan, produce, and host the annual end-of-year awards ceremony for over 250 attendees to celebrate student staff achievements.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Membership Growth and Marketing&nbsp;</strong></div><ul><li>Membership Management: Drive the strategic growth, forecasting, and optimization of Wellness and Recreation Member Services, directly overseeing annual membership and auxiliary revenues exceeding $900k.</li><li>Growth &amp; Retention: Design, implement, and evaluate comprehensive recruitment and retention strategies; establish continuous sales pipelines for alumni, faculty, staff, and external community segments.</li><li>Community Engagement: Build strong relationships with Centennial Campus affiliates and organize special events to boost member satisfaction.</li><li>Marketing &amp; Promotions: Develop high-impact marketing materials, targeted digital promotions, programmatic tours, and data-backed incentives to maximize conversion rates for prospective members.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Revenue Generation and Partnerships&nbsp;</strong></div><ul><li>Auxiliary Revenue Streams: Identify and cultivate alternate revenue streams, including the conceptualization and execution of specialized member events, paid programming, and creative retail or facility rentals.</li><li>Corporate Partnerships: Assist with the design and execution of strategic marketing partnership/sponsorship programs; collaborate with external partners to maximize marketing packages and fundraising opportunities.</li><li>Financial Oversight: Manage the specific and direct fiscal budgets for Member Services, the student staff training program, and the student professional development fund.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Strategic Leadership and Systems Administration&nbsp;</strong></div><ul><li>Leadership: Provide leadership and direction for Wellness and Recreation member services, point of sale and facility access software and student employment program by implementing goals, expectations and processes for programs and revenues.</li><li>Systems Administration: Provide strategic direction for the maintenance, upkeep, configuration, communication, and design of InnoSoft Fusion (the department’s POS and facility access software) and FusionGO (the department’s mobile application for program registration and facility access).</li><li>Compliance: Ensure all operations adhere to University, State, and Federal regulations, as well as specific departmental or governing body requirements.</li><li>Committee Leadership: Lead the Wellness and Recreation student training and development work team, as well as the Fusion department work team.</li><li>Data-Driven Evaluation: Serve on the Wellness and Recreation assessment work team; design satisfaction surveys, evaluate program efforts, and develop data-driven recommendations to implement new programs.</li><li>General Support: Assist as needed with all required departmental functions and university-wide initiatives.</li></ul> 244040 - Wellness and Recreation https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/223547 2025-10-01T09:44:17-04:00 2025-10-01T09:44:18-04:00 CLAWS III-N Power Integration Engineer <div>We are seeking a highly skilled Wide-Bandgap Power Device Engineer to join our team and contribute to the development of next-generation power semiconductor devices. The ideal candidate will have expertise in semiconductor process integration, power device design (diodes, FETs, etc.), electrical characterization, and device simulation to drive innovation in high-efficiency, high-performance wide-bandgap power solutions.<br><br></div><div>Key Responsibilities</div><ul><li><strong>Power Device Design &amp; Development (30%)</strong><ul><li>Design and optimize GaN power devices (HEMTs, Diodes, lateral/vertical FETs) for high-voltage and high-frequency applications.</li><li>Utilize TCAD simulation (e.g., Synopsys, Silvaco, etc.) for process and device modeling, electrical characteristics prediction, and performance enhancement.</li><li>Develop and refine device layouts for wafer fabrication, ensuring manufacturability and reliability.</li></ul></li></ul><div><br></div><ul><li><strong>Process Integration &amp; Fabrication (30%)</strong><ul><li>Work to define and optimize GaN-on GaN, GaN-on-Si, GaN-on-SiC, and other heteroepitaxial structures for power applications.</li><li>Lead the wafer process flow development, including epi structures, etching, metallization, and passivation steps.</li><li>Troubleshoot process integration challenges related to defectivity, yield, and reliability.</li></ul></li></ul><div><br></div><ul><li><strong>Electrical Characterization &amp; Reliability Testing (20%)</strong><ul><li>Define and execute device characterization plans (IV, CV, breakdown, leakage, switching performance).</li><li>Analyze reliability metrics such as TDDB, HTRB, HTOL, and step-stress testing to assess long-term device stability.</li><li>Interface with packaging and application engineers to optimize thermal management and electrical performance in real-world applications.</li></ul></li></ul><div><br></div><ul><li><strong>Cross-functional Collaboration (20%)</strong><ul><li>Work with process engineers and R&amp;D partners to drive technology improvements.</li><li>Collaborate with circuit designers, applications engineers, and product development teams to translate device-level improvements into system-level advantages.</li><li>Stay up to date with emerging GaN technology trends, competitive landscape, and industry benchmarks.</li></ul></li></ul> 140110 - CLAWS https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234360 2026-08-28T18:47:21-04:00 2026-08-28T18:47:21-04:00 Educational Postdoctoral Research Scholar <div>This <strong>Educational Postdoctoral Research Scholar</strong> is designed to bridge the gap between clinical expertise and educational scholarship. By combining research training with hands-on teaching experience, the Postdoctoral Research Scholar will develop the skills necessary to become a leader in veterinary education and communication and professional pedagogy.</div><div><br><strong>Program Overview:</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Duration:</strong> 12-24 months</li><li><strong>Primary Objective:</strong> To equip DVM or PhD professionals with the methodological toolkit required to conduct high-impact research in health communication, communication and educational theory, and professionalism/professional identity.</li><li><strong>Eligibility &amp; Qualifications</strong>: Must hold a DVM (or equivalent) or a PhD in a related field (Education, Communication, Psychology, etc.).</li></ul><div><br><strong>Research &amp; Methodological Training:<br></strong>The Postdoctoral Research Scholar will participate in a structured mentorship program to master mixed-methods research tailored to the veterinary healthcare and educational environment.<br><br><strong>Responsibilities include:</strong></div><ul><li>Quantitative research methodologies, including survey design, statistical analysis of educational outcomes (such as competency-based assessments), and data-driven evaluation of communication interventions</li><li>Qualitative research methods, including thematic analysis of reflective practice, conducting and coding focus groups and semi-structured interviews, and utilizing ethnographic or observational approaches</li><li>Scholarly output through writing and submitting one first-author manuscript per year to a peer-reviewed journal (e.g., <em>Journal of Veterinary Medical Education</em>)</li></ul><div><br><strong>Teaching, Facilitation, and Curriculum:</strong><br>The Postdoctoral Research Scholar will be integrated into the DVM curriculum to ground ongoing research initiatives in active educational practice related to professionalism and communication training.<br><br><strong>Responsibilities Include:</strong></div><ul><li><strong>Communication Lab Facilitation -</strong> Leading small-group sessions using simulated clients (SCs) and providing real-time, constructive feedback to DVM students on clinical communication</li><li><strong>Guest Lecturing - </strong>Developing and delivering didactic content on topics such as conflict<br>management, breaking bad news, or veterinary professional ethics</li><li><strong>Curriculum Development - </strong>Collaborating with faculty to refine and enhance learning modules and assessments across the professionalism curriculum</li></ul> 195001 - Department of Clinical Sciences https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/228950 2026-03-31T09:49:16-04:00 2026-03-31T15:09:15-04:00 Assistant/Associate Professor of Translational Omics <div>North Carolina State University invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position in translational omics at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor. This position is a key component of the Translational Kidney Health Center of Excellence, an initiative designed to integrate clinical nephrology with advanced mechanistic research to accelerate discovery in kidney health across species.<br><br></div><div>This position represents the discovery and biomarker Identification pillar of the Translational Kidney Health Center of Excellence. The successful candidate will develop an innovative, externally funded research program that applies multi-omics technologies to identify molecular pathways and biomarkers associated with kidney disease progression. Areas of emphasis may include, but are not limited to: proteomics, metabolomics, lipidomics, genomics or transcriptomics, and systems biology and integrative bioinformatics.<br><br></div><div>In this role you will leverage NC State’s unique clinical nephrology caseload and biobank of well-characterized samples to identify early diagnostic biomarkers and novel therapeutic targets relevant to both companion animals and humans.&nbsp; As a faculty member, you will establish strong interdisciplinary collaborations with clinicians within the Veterinary Nephrology and Urology Service and with investigators in genomics, bioinformatics, engineering, and systems biology across campus. Additional collaborative opportunities exist with investigators at Duke University and the UNC Kidney Center. This is an exceptional opportunity to build a high-impact research program that integrates clinical observation, molecular discovery, and translational medicine.<br><br></div><div>This is an exceptional opportunity for a creative and forward-thinking investigator to advance both human and animal health through excellence in research and teaching. The successful candidate will contribute to the education of veterinary and graduate students, mentor trainees at multiple levels, and help expand the College’s national and international reputation in comparative and translational medicine. We seek a dynamic scholar whose work will build upon existing institutional strengths while opening new scientific frontiers within the College of Veterinary Medicine.<br><br><strong>Duties and Responsibilities:</strong><br>● Apply cutting-edge scientific knowledge and advanced experimental methodologies to develop impactful solutions for complex kidney diseases affecting both animals and humans.</div><div>● Develop and sustain an innovative, independently funded research program in translational omics and biomarker discovery.</div><div>● Apply advanced multi-omic technologies to identify molecular pathways and biomarkers associated with CKD and AKI.</div><div>● Leverage NC State’s extensive clinical nephrology caseload and biobanking resources to enable high-impact translational research.</div><div>● Collaborate with faculty in molecular physiology, engineering, genomics, and computational biology to integrate omics data with mechanistic studies.</div><div>● Secure extramural funding (e.g., NIH R01, USDA AFRI, foundation, and industry-sponsored research).</div><div>● Mentor DVM students, PhD students, DVM-PhD trainees, postdoctoral fellows, and clinical house officers.</div><div>Participate in graduate and professional teaching in areas such as systems biology, translational omics, renal physiology, and comparative diagnostics.</div><div>● Engage in interdisciplinary center-building initiatives including collaborative program project or center-level grant development.</div><div>● Promote comparative medicine by translating discoveries from veterinary patients to human health applications.</div><div>● Contribute to the national and international visibility of the College of Veterinary Medicine through scholarship and collaboration.</div><div>● Clinical duty within the Nephrology-Urology service if desired (up to 5 weeks per year).<br><br>This is an exceptional opportunity for a creative and forward-thinking investigator to advance both human and animal health through excellence in research and teaching. If you are exited about contributing to the education of veterinary and graduate students, mentor trainees at multiple levels, and help expand the College’s national and international reputation in comparative and translational medicine. We seek a dynamic scholar whose work will build upon existing institutional strengths while opening new scientific frontiers within the College of Veterinary Medicine.</div> 190101 - College Of Veterinary Medicine"
    },
